\mt1 The Book of Genesis, in Hebrew Beresith. בראשית
\is THE ARGUMENT OF THE BOOKE OF GENESIS.
\ip This firſt Booke of holie Scripture, called \it Geneſis\it*, which ſignifieth \it birth\it* or \it beginning\it*, was written by Moyſes, when he was deſigned by God to inſtruct and rule the children of Iſrael: As alſo the \f - \ft Geneſis written by Moyſes.\f* other foure bookes folowing. The Author and authoritie of al which fiue bookes were euer \f - \ft Alwayes authentical.\f* acknowledged by the faithful, both of the old and new Teſtament: and ſo accounted and eſtemed by tradition, til Chriſt and his Apoſtles: who alſo confirmed them by their teſtimonies \f - \ft So knowen by Tradition, confirmed by Chriſt, Alleaged alſo by the Apoſtles.\f* \f - \ft \+it Mat. 19. Heb. 11. Iacob. 2. 1. Pet. 2. 2. Pet. 2.\+it*\f* and allegations of the ſame, as of holie Scriptures. From the creation vntil Moyſes writ (which was aboue two thouſand and foure hundred yeares) the Church exerciſed Religion by Reuelations \f - \ft Religion reueled to ſpecial perſons and ſo obſerued by traditions.\f* made to certaine Patriarches, and by Traditions from man to man, without anie Scriptures or law written. But the peculiar people of God being more viſibly ſeparated from other nations, and manie errors abunding in the world, God would for correction and confutation therof haue his wil \f - \ft \+it S. Aug. ques. vet & noui Teſtam. cap. 3.\+it*\f* made further knowen to his children, and ſo remaine amongſt them in written record, by his faithful ſeruant and Prophet Moyſes. VVho therfore declareth the Author and beginning of al thinges, \f - \ft VVhy Scripture was written.\f* that is, How al creatures were made by God, and of him haue their being, and by him only are conſerued. He teacheth expreſly that there is one onlie God, againſt thoſe that imagined and \f - \ft VVhat Moyſes ſpecially sheweth in this booke.\f* brought into the phantaſies of men manie goddes. That the whole or vniuerſal ſubſtance of heauen and earth, with their ornaments and accidents, were made in time; againſt thoſe that thought the firſt fundation therof had euer benne. That God doth gouerne the ſame; againſt thoſe that ſay, al is ruled by deſtiny or by the ſtarres, and not by the continual prouidence of God. That God is a rewarder of the good, and a punisher of euil, which ſinners ſeme either not to know, or groſly to forgete. And that God created al for mans vſe and benefite, which should make vs grateful. \f - \ft Man moſt\f* VVherfore holie Moyſes more particularly deſcribeth the beginning of man; what he was at firſt; how he fel; how al mankind is come of one man: deducing the Genealogie of Adam, eſpecially to Noe. Then how men being more and more defiled vpon the earth, with wicked, eſpecially \f - \ft The right line from Adam to Noe. particularly deſcribed.\f* carnal ſinnes, were by Gods iuſt wrath drowned with an vniuerſal floud.
\ip Againe, how a few reſerued perſons multiplied the world anew. But this ofſpring alſo falling into manie ſinnes, eſpecially Idolatrie and ſpiritual fornication, as thoſe of the firſt age did to carnal offences, God ſtil conſerued ſome faithful and true ſeruants. Of which Moyſes ſpecially \f - \ft \+it Gen. 10.\+it*\f* purſueth the line of Noe by Sem his firſt begotten ſonne. Then deſcribeth the particular \f - \ft The principal Patriarches from Noe to the 12 ſonnes of Iſrael.\f* vocations, liues, maners, notable ſayings, and noble factes, with ſincere religion of Abraham, Iſaac, Iacob, Ioſeph, and other holie Patriarches: who lived before the written lavv. Likevviſe vpon vvhat occaſion, and in vvhat maner Iacob, otherwiſe called Iſrael, with al his progenie, deſcended from the Land of Canaan into Ægypt, and were there entertayned. So this booke contayneth the hiſtorie of two thouſand three hundred and odde yeares. And it may be diuided into eight partes. The \f - \ft This booke diuided into eight partes.\f* \f - \ft 1.\f* firſt contayneth the Creation of Heauen and Earth, & other Creatures, and laſtly of Man. chap. 1. & 2. The ſecond part is of the trangreſſion & fal of man, & his caſting out of Paradiſe, of \f - \ft 2.\f* multiplication of men, and of ſinne, though ſtil ſome were iuſt, of the general floud, that drowned al except eight perſons, & few other liuing creatures of the earth, from the third chap. to the 8. \f - \ft 3.\f*
\ip The third part is of the new increaſe and multiplication of the ſame, from the 8. chap. to the 11.
\ip The fourth, of the confuſion of tongues and the diuiſion of nations, in the 11. chap. The \f - \ft 4.\f* \f - \ft 5.\f* fift relateth Abrahams going forth of his countrie, Gods promiſe, that in his ſeede al Nations should be bleſſed, & the commandment of Circumciſion, from the 12. chap. to the 21. The \f - \ft 6.\f* ſixth part recounteth the progenie, and other bleſſings, eſpecially the great vertues of Abraham, Iſaac, and Jacob, from the 21. chap. to the 37. The ſeuenth part reporteth the ſelling of Ioſeph \f - \ft 7.\f* into Ægypt, and his aduancement there, from the 37. chap. to the 46. The eighth and laſt \f - \ft 8.\f* part is of Iacob, and his progenies going into Ægypt, their entertainment there, and of Iacobs and finally of Ioſephs death in the fiue laſt chapters.
\is THE SUM AND PARTITION OF THE HOLY BIBLE. With a brief note on the Canonical and Apocryphal Books.
\ip By the uniform conſent of all learned Divines, the holy Bible, or written Word of God, \f - \ft How the holy Scriptures contain all knowledge neceſſary to ſalvation. The old and the new Teſtament shew the ſame God, Chriſt, Church and other Myſteries of Religion. The old more obſcurely, & with leſs helps. The new more expreſſly and yieldeth more grace.\f* \f - \ft \+it S. Hierom Epiſt. ad Paulin. de omnib. S. Scrip. libris. S. Aug. prefat. in ſpecul. li. 2. de doct. Chriſt. ca. 9. li. 1. de Gen. contra\+it*\f* containeth expreſſed or implied all things that man is to believe, to obſerue, and to avoid, for obtaining of eternal ſaluation. That is, all matters of faith and manners, by which we may know and ſerve God, and ſo be ſpiritually ioyned with him in this life, and in eternity. For both the old and new Teſtament propoſe and teſtify vnto us one and the ſame God, the ſame Chriſt, the ſame Church, and other Myſteries of our belief, not differing in ſubſtance, but in manners of uttering; the Old more obſcurely in figures and prophecies fortelling thoſe things, which the New declareth (in great part) as donne and performed. Whereupon ſaith S. Auguſtine: \it In the Old Teſtament the New lieth hidden; and in the New the Old lieth open\it*. And touching their names, wherein appeareth difference, \it the one\it* (ſaith the ſame Doctor) \it is called the old Teſtament, either becauſe it propoſeth promiſes of temporal things\it* (wherewith our old corruptnes is allured) \it or in reſpect of the New, by which it is fulfilled, and in ſome part abolished. The other is called the New, becauſe by it man is renewed, and hath promiſe of eternal life,\it* which shal never waxe old nor decay. Likewiſe S. Gregorie the Great teſtifieth this conformity and correſpondence betwen the Old and New Teſtament, \f - \ft Apocryphal of two ſorts. 1. Not declared canonical. 2. Rejected as erroneous.\f* affirming that the ſame is ſignified by the Prophet Ezechiel's viſion of a wheel, which had four faces, or appearance of foure wheels, the shape whereof \it was, as it were, a wheel in the midſt of a wheel. What is this\it*, ſaith he, \it Niſi quod in Teſtamenti veteris litera Teſtamentum novum latuit per Allegoriam? but that in the letter of the old Teſtament, the New lay hidden by an Allegory?\it*
\ip And as the ſame is the ſum and ſubject of both Teſtaments ſo both are diuided (for the more principal parts thereof) into foure ſorts of Books: \it Legal, Hiſtorical, Sapiential, & Prophetical\it*. \f - \ft In both Teſtaments, are four ſorts of Books.\f* The Legal books of the old Teſtament are the five Books of Moyſes. \it Geneſis, Exodus, Leviticus,\it* \f - \ft Legal.\f* \it Numeri\it*, and \it Deuteronomy\it*; whereto anſwer in the new Teſtament the four \it Goſpels\it* of \it Saint Matthew, S. Mark, S. Luke\it*, and \it S. John\it*. Hiſtorical books of the old Teſtament are the Books of \f - \ft \+it S. Gregr. ho. 6 in Ezech. 1.\+it*\f* \it Ioſue, Judges, Ruth\it*, 'foure books of \it Kings\it*, two of \it Paralipomenon, Eſdras\it*, with \it Nehemias, Tobias, Judith, Heſter, Job\it*, and two of the \it Machabees\it*; unto which in the new Teſtament anſwer \it the Acts of the Apoſtles. Sapiential\it* of the old Teſtament are the \it Proverbs, Eccleſiaſtes, Canticles, Book of\it* \f - \ft Sapiential.\f* \it wiſdom\it*, and \it Eccleſiaſticus\it*; and of like ſort are in the new Teſtament the Epiſtles of S. Paul and of other Apoſtles. \it Prophetical\it* books are \it Dauids Pſalter\it* (which is alſo \it Sapiential\it*, yea likewiſe \f - \ft Prophetical.\f* \it Legal\it* and \it Hiſtorical\it* ) the Books of \it Iſaias, Jeremias\it*, with \it Baruch, Ezechiel, Daniel\it*, the twelve leſs Prophets, \it Oſea, Ioel, Amos, Abdias, Ionas, Michaeas, Nahum, Abacuc, Sophonias, Aggaeus, Zacharias, Malachias\it*. And in the new Teſtament, \it the Apocalips\it* of S. John the Apoſtle.
\ip All theſe Books are vndoubtedly Canonical, as the Authors cited in the inner margent \f - \ft All theſe books recited are Canonical, and of infallible truth.\f* \f - \ft \+it Conc. Carth. An. D. 419. Conc. Laodic. cap. 59. Florent. Inſtruct. Armen. decret. 7. Trident. Seſs. 4. S. Atha. in Synop. S. Aug. li. 2. doct. Chriſt. c. 8. Iſidor. li. 6. Etymol. c. 1. & alibi. Nicep. l. 4. cap. 15. Euſeb. l. 5. c. 8.\+it*\f* teſtify. And conſequently all, and all the parts thereof, are of infallible truth. For otherwiſe, as S. Auguſtin teacheth, if anie part were falſe or doubtful, all were uncertain. \it Once admitting falſehood\it* (ſaith he Epiſt. 8. ad Hieron.) \it in ſuch ſovereign authoritie, no parcel of theſe books should remaine, which anie way should ſeme hard to maners, or incredible to believe, but it might by this moſt pernicious rule be turned to an officious fiction of the author\it*. That is: If any error could be committed by the authors of Scriptures, either through ignorance, obliuion, or any other human frailty, whatſoever were produced, exception might be taken, and queſtion made, whether the author had ered or no. True it is, that \it ſome of theſe books\it* (as we shal particularly diſcuſs in their places) were ſometimes doubted of by ſome Catholics, and called \it Apocryphal\it*, in that ſenſe as the word \f - \ft Hiſtorical.\f* properly ſignifieth \it hidden\it*, or \it not apparent\it*. So \it S. Hierom\it* (in his prologue before the Latin Bible) \it calleth diuers books Apocryphal\it*, being not ſo euident, whether they were Divine Scripture, becauſe they were not in the Jews' Cannon, nor at firſt in the Church's Canon, but were never rejected as falſe or erroneous. In which ſenſe \it the Prayers of Manaſſes\it*, the \it third\it* book of \it Eſdras, and\it* third of \it Machabees are yet called Apocryphal\it*. As for the fourth of Eſdras, and fourth of Machabees there is more doubt. But diuers others, as the \it booke aſcribed to Enoch\it*, the \it Goſpels\it* of \it S. Andrew, S. Thomas, S. Bartholomew\it*, and the like recited by S. Gelaſius (Decreto de libris Eccleſiaſticis diſt. 15. Can. Sancta Romana) S. Innocentius the firſt (Epiſt. 3.) S. Hierom, Ep. ad Laetam, S. Auguſtin. l. 15. cap. 23. de civit. Dei. Origen homi. 2. in Cantica, are in a worſe ſenſe called Apocryphal, and are rejected as containing manifeſt errors, or feigned by Heretics Neither can a Chriſtian Catholic be otherwiſe aſſured, which Books are Divine and Canonical Scriptures, but by declaration of the Catholic Church, which without interruption ſucceedeth the Apoſtles, to whom \it our Saviour\it* \f - \ft The Holy Ghoſt declareth by the Church which Books are Divine Scriptures.\f* \it promiſed, and ſent the Holie-Ghoſt, to teach all truth\it*. For if any thing more than others, \f - \ft \+it Mat. 28. Ioan 14. 16. Act. 2. 20. 1. Tim. 3.\+it*\f* aſſuredly one chief and moſt neceſſary point is, to know and declare which Books are God's holy word, being of moſt ſingular importance.
\is THE SUM OF THE OLD TESTAMENT, as it is diſtinguished from the new.
\ip Notwithſtanding the ſubject, and general argument of both Teſtaments is one and the ſame in ſubſtance, as is already ſaid, yet they differ in time, in maner of uttering of Myſteries, in \f - \ft The old and new Teſtament differ in time. In manner of uttering. Variety of Precepts. Promiſes. Means.\f* variety of precepts and promiſes, alſo in means to obſerve the things exacted, & to attain to the end propoſed. In regard whereof S. Hierom ſaith: \it Lex Moyſi & omne vetus inſtrumentum\it* \f - \ft \+it Tomo. 3. queſt. 10. Algaſiae.\+it*\f* \it elementa mundi intelliguntur, quibus quaſi elementis & Religionis exordijs Deum diſcimus. The law of Moyſes and all the old Teſtament are vnderſtood the elements of the world, by which, as by firſt rudiments and beginnings, we learn to know God\it*. For that in it we haue firſt the law of nature, and afterwards a law written, with premiſes of temporal rewards; as long life, land flowing with milke and honey, and the like; but \it it brought nothing to perfection\it*, as S. Paul ſaith, \it when gifts and\it* \f - \ft \+it Hebr. 7. 9. 10.\+it*\f* \it hoſts were offered, which could not according to conſcience make the obſeruer perfect\it*. For the helps of that time were but \it infirma & egena elementa: Weake & poore elements.\it* Likewiſe in general, \f - \ft \+it Gal. 4.\+it*\f* touching the punishments that ſometimes happened to the people of the old Teſtament, when they tranſgreſſed, the ſame Apoſtle affirmeth, that \it all the ſame chanced to them in figure, and\it* \f - \ft The old Teſtament containeth figure of the new.\f* \f - \ft \+it 1. Cor. 10.\+it*\f* \it are written for our correption, vpon whom the ends of the world are come\it*. So that the old Teſtament or \it Law, was but our pedagogue in Chriſt\it*. Yet it ſetteth forth to us the whole courſe of God's \f - \ft \+it Gal. 3.\+it*\f* Church, for the ſpace of foure thouſand years, that is, from the beginning of the world until Chriſt our Redeemer, which Diuines divide into ſix Ages , wherein was variety and change of her \f - \ft A continual viſible Church from the beginning of the world to Chriſt.\f* \f - \ft Divided into ſix ages.\f* ſtate, three under the Law of nature, and three others under the written Law. The ſeventh \f - \ft The ſame Myſtical body, but\f* and laſt Age being this time of grace (wherein we now are) from Chriſt, to the day of general Judgement: as the world was made in ſix days, and in the ſeventh God is ſaid to have reſted, and therefore ſanctified it, in other ſort than the former ſix. The eighth will be after the Reſurrection, during for all eternity.
\ip Which ſix Ages of the ancient Church and old Teſtament, are thus diſtributed. The firſt \f - \ft \+it Gen. 1. Gen. 8. Gen. 12. Genebrard. Chronolog. S. Aug. l. 15. c. 8. civit. Gal. 3. Exod. 16. 3. Reg. 6. 3. Reg. 7. 1. Eſdra. 1.\+it*\f* \f - \ft The firſt age continued 1656. years.\f* from the Creation to Noe's flood, contained the ſpace of 1656. years. The ſecond from the \f - \ft The ſecond 368. or 398.\f* flood to the going of Abraham out of his country, 368. or counting Cainan (Gen. 11. iuxta 72. and Luc. 3.) 398. years. The third from Abraham his going forth of his country, to the parting of the \f - \ft The third about. 430.\f* children of Iſrael out of Aegypt. Which ſome count to have continued 720. years, others (whom we folow) but 430. And thus farre in the law of nature before the written law. The fourth age dured, \f - \ft The fourth 480.\f* 480. years. from the delivery of the children of Iſrael forth of Aegypt, to the foundation of the Temple in Hieruſalem. The fifth Age was from the foundation of the Temple, to the captivity \f - \ft The fifth 430. different in ſtate.\f* and tranſmigration of the Jews into Babylon, about 430. years. And the ſixth Age dured about \f - \ft The ſixth, near 640.\f* 640. years, from the Captivity of Babylon to Chriſt. In all which times God was acknowledged \f - \ft All the time from the creation to Chriſt about 4000. years.\f* and rightly ſerued, by a continual viſible Church, with true religion; the ſame and no other, which now that Church holdeth, that is called and known by the name of Catholic. As we intend, by God's aſſiſtance, to shew by brief Annotations, concerning diverſe particular points now in Controverſy, as the holy Text giveth occaſion. And eſpecially by way of recapitulation after euerie one of the ſix Ages, when we come to thoſe paſſages in the Hiſtory, where the ſame are ended.
\is OF MOYSES THE AUTHOR OF THE five firſt books.
\ip \it Moyſes\it* (ſo called becauſe he was taken from the water, as the name ſignifieth) was born \f - \ft MOYSES. ſignifieth, taken from the water.\f* \f - \ft \+it Exod. 6. Num. 26. 1. Par. 6. Ioſeph. li. 2. Antiq. cap. 9. S. Aug. ſer. 88. de temp. S. Greg. oratio. in laudem Baſilij magni. S. Aug. li. 18. civit. c. 39.\+it*\f* in Aegypt, the ſon of \it Amram\it*, the ſon of \it Caath\it*, the ſon of \it Levi\it* the Patriarch, and ſo of \it Jacob, Iſaac\it*, and \it Abraham\it*. His marvelous delivery from drowning, his education, excellent form, ſingular wiſdom, heroical virtues, rare dexterity in all affairs, and whole life moſt admirable, are gathered out of holy Scriptures, by S. Gregory Bishop of Nyſſen, into a brief Summe, moſt worthy to be read, but too large for this place. He was born about the year of the world two thouſand foure \f - \ft The excellency of Moyſes.\f* hundred, long before all profane writers, yea before many of the Paynim's falſe gods, as S. Auguſtin declareth in diverſe places of his moſt excellent booke entitled \it of the Citie of God\it*. He lived in this world 120. years: Of which 40. were in Pharao's Court as the adopted ſon of Pharao's daughter: forty in banishment from Aegypt in Madian: and forty more he governed the people of Iſrael. His ſingular prayſes are alſo briefly touched in the laſt chapter of Deuteronomy, added by Ioſue, and in the booke of Eccleſiaſticus. He died in the deſert, and was buried in the vale of \f - \ft \+it Deut. 34. Eccli. 45. Glos. ord.\+it*\f* Moab, ſo ſecretly that no mortal man knew his ſepulchre, leſt the Jews, who were very prone \f - \ft His ſepulchre not known to any man.\f* to Idolatrie, should have adored his body with diuine honour, for the greatneſs and multitude of his miracles, and for the ſingular eſtimation they had of him for the ſame.
\c 1
\cd God createth heauen and earth, and al things therin; diſtinguishing and bewtyfying the ſame; 26. laſt of al the ſixth day he createth man: to whom he ſubiecteth al corporal things of this inferior world. \f - \ft The firſt part. Of the creation of all things. The Church readeth this book in her Office from Septuageſima till Paſſion Sunday. Alſo this firſt chapter & beginning of the ſecond on Eaſter Eve before Maſs.\f*
\p
\v 1 IN \x - \xt Act. 14, 15. 17, 24.\x* THE beginning \sc God\sc* created heauen and earth. \ef - \fr 1.1 \fq In the beginnig. \ft ¹ Holie Moyſes telleth what was done in the beginning of the world, and ſo forward euen til his owne time, writing aboue two thouſand and foure hundreth yeares after the beginning. Al which being incomprehenſible by humaine witte or diſcourſe, he knew partly by Reuelations from God, for he had the gyft of Prophecie in moſt excellent ſorte: partly by Traditions from his elders, who lerned of their fathers. For vntil that time the Church had only Traditions of ſuch things, as were reueled to ſpecial men, wherby we ſee the great authoritie of Traditions, before there were Scriptures. And ſince Scriptures were written they are alſo neceſſarie, for three ſpecial reaſons. ² Firſt for that we are only aſſured by Tradition of the Church, that thoſe bookes are in dede holie Scriptures, which are ſo accounted, and not by the Scripture it ſelfe, for that were to proue the ſame by the ſame, vntil we be aſſured of ſome part, that proueth ſome other partes. And this made S. Auguſtin to ſay plainly, ³ that he could not beleue the Goſpel, except the Church told him which is the Goſpel. ⁴ Secondly holie Scriptures being once knowen to be the word of God, and ſo of moſt eminent authoritie ⁵ of al writings in the world, as S. Auguſtin S. Ierome, & al other Fathers agree, yet for the true vnderſtanding of the ſame, both the Scripture it ſelfe, and the ancient Fathers remitte vs to the Church, namely to thoſe in the Church, that are appointed by Gods ordinance, in the high place that he hath choſen. VVhich were the High Prieſts in the old Teſtament, as appeareth Deut. 17. Mat. 23. Ioan. 11. And in the new Teſtament, S. Peter and his Succeſſors for whom Chriſt prayed that his faith ſhould not faile: and therfore commanded him to confirme his bretheren Luc. 22. ⁶ Thirdly for things not expreſſed in particular in holie Scripture, the Scripture ⁷ and Fathers do likewiſe remitte vs to Traditions, and to the iudgement and teſtimonie of the Church. Chriſt ſaying to his Apoſtles: he that heareth you heareth me. The Apoſtles doubted not to ſay: It ſemed good to the Holie Ghoſt and to vs. And S. Paul willed the Theſſalonians to hold the traditions, vvhich they had lerned, whether it were by word, or by his Epiſtle. \fq ¹ \ft The Church had only Traditions & no Scripture aboue 2400. yeares. \fq ² \ft Traditions necessary for three causes. 1. \fq ³ \ft \+it cont. Epist. fund. c. 5\+it*. \fq ⁴ \ft 2. \fq ⁵ \ft Scripture of most eminent authority. \fq ⁶ \ft 3. \fq ⁷ \ft \+it (a) Luc. 10, 16. Act. 15, 28. 2. Thess. 2\+it*.\ef* \ef - \fr 1.1 \fq In the beginning God made heauen and earth. \ft ¹ Al writers ² ancient and later find ſuch difficulties in theſe firſt chapters, that ſome otherwiſe very lerned haue thought it not poſſible to vnderſtand the ſame according to the proper and vſual ſignification of the wordes, as the letter may ſeme to ſound, but expound al allegorically, as that by the waters aboue the firmament ſhould be vnderſtood the bleſſed Angels, by the waters vnder the firmament wicked ſpirites, and the like. So did Origen and diuers that folow him therein. Yea S. Auguſtin in his bookes vpon Geneſis againſt the Manichees, written ſhortly after his conuerſion, when he could not find as he deſired a good and probable ſenſe agreable to the wordes, in their proper ſignification, expounded them myſtically, but afterwards in his other bookes de Geneſi ad literam, he gratfully acknowledgeth that God had geuen him further ſight therin, and that now he ſuppoſed he could interprete al according to the proper ſignification of the wordes. yet ſo that he durſt not nor would not addict him ſelfe to one ſenſe, but that he was readie to imbrace an other, leſt by ſticking to his owne iudgement he might faile. So likewiſe S. Baſil, S. Chriſoſtom, S. Ambroſe, S. Ierome, S. Bede, and other greateſt Doctors found & confeſſed great difficulties in theſe firſt chapters, which they with much ſtudie endeuored to explicate. And therfore it is a wonder to ſee our Proteſtants & Puritans hold this Paradox, that Scriptures are eaſie to be vnderſtood. VVheras both by teſtimonie of thoſe that haue in deede ſtudied & laboured in them, and by a litle due conſideration, the contrarie is moſt euident. ³ ⁴ For whoſoeuer wil looke into the holie Scriptures, shal find that ſome times in ſhew one place ſemeth contrarie to an other; ſome times the letter & phraſe are obſcure & ambiguous; ſome times the ſentences vnperfect. Againe manie ſpeaches are prophetical, manie parabolical, metaphorical, and vttered vnder other tropes and figures, and that in the literal ſenſe. ⁵ Moreouer there are three ſpiritual ſenſes beſides the literal, very frequent in holie Scripture. Allegorical pertayning to Chriſt and the Church; Moral pertayning to maners; and Anagogical pertayning to the next life. As this word Ieruſalem literally ſignifieth the head citie of Iewrie: Morally the ſoule of man: Allegorically the Church militant: and Anagogically the Church triumphant. And ſometimes this (and the like of others) metaphorically in the literal ſenſe ſignifieth the Church militant, and not the citie of Iewrie, as in the 12. chapter to the Hebrewes: and ſome times the Church triumphant, as in the 21. of the Apocalips. \fq ¹ \ft Scriptures hard. \fq ² \ft \+it (b) Ori. super. Gen. c. 1. Aug. l. 2. de Gen. cont. Manich. ca. 2. lib. 1. c. 18. & li. 8. c. 2. Bas. ho. 9. in Genes. Chriso. epist. 44. Amb. & Beda in examen. Jerome. Ep. ad Eust.\+it* \fq ³ \ft why Scriptures are hard. \fq ⁴ \ft \+it Gen. 1. v. 3. & 14. Exo. 20, 5. & 18, v. 20. Ioan. 8, 25. Rom. 8\+it*. \fq ⁵ \ft Three spiritual senses besides the Literal. Allegorical. Moral. Anagogical.\ef*
\v 2 \x - \xt Pſal. 32, 6. 135, 5. Eccl. 10, 1.\x* And the earth was voide & vacant, and darkenes was vpon the face of the deapth: and the Spirite of God moued ouer the waters. \ef - \fr 1.2 \fq The Spirite of God. \ft In the Hebrew it is ſignified, that the Spirite of God was on the waters to make them fertile, for that fiſhes and birdes were to be procreated therof; the word is merahepheth, incubabat, ſate vpon, to produce fruict (ſaith S. Ierom) from the waters, as a henne by her heate, produceth life in the egges. ¹ And the ſame S. Ierom, ² and before him Tertullian teach, that this was a figure of Baptiſme, which conſiſteth of water and the Holie Ghoſt. For as water in the beginning of the world receiued a certain vital vertue of the Holie Ghoſt to produce liuing creatures: ſo alſo Baptiſme receiueth vertue of the ſame Holie Ghoſt to procreate new men. VVherupon Tertullian calleth Chriſtians fishes, ³ becauſe they are gotten from the waters, and thence haue their firſt ſpiritual life. Let it not therfore ſeme ſtrange (ſaith he) that in Baptiſme VVaters geue life. \fq ¹ \ft A figure of Baptisme. \fq ² \ft \+it Hier. Epist. 83. ad Ocea. Tert. de Baptis.\+it* \fq ³ \ft Christians called fishes.\ef*
\v 3 And God ſaid: Be light made. And light was made.
\v 4 And God ſaw the light that it was good: & he diuided the light from the darkenes. \x - \xt Heb. 11, 3.\x*
\v 5 And he called the light, Day, and the darkenes, Night: and there was euening & morning, that made one day.
\v 6 \x - \xt Job. 38. Jer. 10, 13.\x* God alſo ſaid: Be \f a \fr 1.6 \ft The firmament is al the ſpace from the earth to the hiegheſt ſtarres. the loweſt part diuideth betwene the waters on the earth and the waters in the ayer. S. Aug. li. 11. de Gen. ad lit. c. 4.\f* a firmament made amidſt the waters: and let it diuide betwene waters & waters.
\v 7 And God made a firmament, and diuided the waters, that were vnder the firmament, from thoſe, that were aboue the firmament. And it was ſo done.
\v 8 And God called the firmament, \f b \fr 1.8 \ft Likewiſe heauen is al the ſpace aboue the earth. in whoſe loweſt part are birdes and waters, in the higher part ſtarres. the hiegheſt is the Empyrial heauen. Eſa. 66.\f* Heauen: and there was euening & morning that made the ſecond day.
\v 9 God alſo ſaid: Let the waters that are vnder the heauen, be gathered together into one place: and let the drie land appeare. And it was ſo done.
\v 10 And God called the drie land, Earth: and the gathering of waters together, he called Seas. And God ſawe that it was good.
\v 11 And ſaid: Let the earth ſhootforth grene herbes, and ſuch as may ſeede, & fruite trees yelding fruit after his kinde, ſuch as may haue ſeede in it ſelfe vpon the earth. And it was ſo done.
\v 12 And the earth brought forth grene herbe, ſuch as ſeedeth according to his kinde, and tree that beareth fruite, hauing ſeede eche one according to his kinde. And God ſaw that it was good.
\v 13 And there was euening and morning that made the third day.
\v 14 Againe God ſaid: Be there lightes made in the firmament of heauen, to diuide the day & the night, and let them be \f c \fr 1.14 \ft The lights made the firſt day, are diſpoſed the fourth day in their proper courſes for more diſtinction of times. S. Dionyſ. ca. 4. de diuin. nom. S. Tho. p. 1. q. 67. a. 4. & q. 70. a. 2.\f* for ſignes & ſeaſons, and dayes and yeares:
\v 15 to shine in the firmament of heauen, & to giue light vpon the earth. And it was ſo done.
\v 16 And God made two \f d \fr 1.16 \ft The Sunne & Moone: for though the moone be the leaſt viſible ſtarre except Mercurie, yet it geueth more light on the earth by reaſon it is nerer, and ſo Moyſes ſpeaketh according to the vulgar capacitie and vſe of things. S. Aug. li. 2. de Gen. ad lit. ca. 16.\f* great lights: a greater light, to gouerne the day: and a leſſer light to gouerne the night: and ſtarres. \ef - \fr 1.16 \fq Tvvo great lights, and ſtarres. \ft ¹ Here occurreth an other example of the hardnes of holie Scripture. For if the two great lights (to wit the Sunne & the Moone) and alſo the ſtarres, were made the fourth day, and not before, as it may ſeme by the wordes in this place, then what was that light, and in what ſubiect was it, that was made the firſt day? S. Baſil, S. Gregorie Nazianzen, Theodoret, and ſome others, writing vpon this place do thinke that the light, which was made the firſt day, remayned though an accident without his ſubiect til the fourth day. And albeit moſt other Doctors rather think that the ſubſtance of the Sunne & Moone, & of other planets and ſtarres were created the firſt day, and the fourth day ſet in that order and courſe which now they kepe, with more diſtinction for ſignes and ſeaſons, and dayes and yeares: yet it is clere that the foreſaid ancient Doctors iudged it poſſible, ² that accidents may remaine without their ſubiect. which a Sacramentarie wil be loath to grant, leſt it might be proued poſſible, as both theſe & al other Catholique Doctors beleued and taught, that the accidents of bread and wine remaine in the bleſſed Sacrament of the Euchariſt without their ſubiects. VVhich Proteſtants denie. \fq ¹ \ft Light being an accident remayned without subiect, by the iudgement of some lerned Fathers. \fq ² \ft The accidents of breade and wine can remaine by Gods power without their subiectes.\ef*
\v 17 And he ſet them in the firmament of heauen, to ſhine vpon the earth.
\v 18 and to gouerne the day & the night, and to diuide the light & the darkenes. And God ſawe that it was good.
\v 19 And there was euening and morning that made the fourth day.
\v 20 God alſo ſaid: Let the waters bring forth creeping creature hauing life, and flying foule, ouer the earth vnder the firmament of heauen.
\v 21 And God created huge whales, and al liuing & mouing creature, that the waters brought forth, according to eche ſorte, & al foule according to their kinde. And God ſawe that it was good.
\v 22 And he bleſſed them ſaying: Increaſe and multiplie, and repleniſh the waters of the ſea: and let the birds be multiplied vpon the earth.
\v 23 And there was euening & morning that made the fifth day.
\v 24 God ſaid moreouer: Let the earth bring forth liuing creature, in his kind, cattle, and ſuch as creepe, & beaſtes of the earth according to their kindes: and it was ſo done.
\v 25 And God made the beaſtes of the earth according to their kindes, and cattle, & al that crepeth on the earth in his kind. And God ſaw that it was good,
\v 26 \x - \xt Col. 3, 10.\x* and he ſaid Let vs make Man to our image, & likenes: and let him haue dominion ouer the fishes of the ſea, and the foules of the ayre, and the beaſtes, and the whole earth, and al creeping creature, that moueth vpon the earth. \ef - \fr 1.26 \fq Let vs make man to our Image. \ft For better conſideration of Gods bountie towards vs, and ſturring our ſelues to gratitude towards him, ¹ we may here note tenne prerogatiues beſtowed on vs, by our Lord & maker in our creation aboue al other earthlie creatures. ² Firſt, wheras God by an imperial word of commandment made other creatures, \+it Fiat lux, Fiat firmamentum\+it*: Be there light: Be there a firmament: intending to make man, he procedeth familiarly, by way, as it were, of conſultation, and as to his owne vſe and ſeruice to make man ſaying: \+it Let vs make man to our image and likenes\+it*, that is to ſay, a reaſonable creature with vnderſtanding and free wil, which beaſtes haue not. ³ Secondly, in this worke God firſt inſinuateth the high Myſterie of the B. Trinitie, or pluralitie of Perſons in one God (becauſe man is to beleue the ſame) ſignifying the pluralitie of Perſons by the wordes \+it Let vs make\+it*, and \+it to our\+it*: and the vnitie in ſubſtance, by the wordes \+it Image and likenes\+it*, the firſt in the plural number, the later in the ſingular. ⁴ Thirdly, other creatures were produced by the waters and earth, \+it Let the waters bring forth\+it* (fishe and foule) \+it Let the earth bring forth\+it* (graſſe and cattle, & other beaſtes) but God brought forth man, not by the earth, though of the earth, nor by water, nor by heauen, nor by Angels, but by him ſelfe, geuing him a reaſonable ſoule, not ſenſual only as to beaſtes, and the ſame not produced of anie creature, but created immediatly of nothing. ⁵ Fourthly, God gaue man Paradiſe a moſt pleaſant place to dwel in. ⁶ Fiftly, God gaue man dominion and imperial authoritie ouer al liuing creatures vnder heauen. ⁷ Sixtly, man was created in that innocencie of life, and integritie of al vertues, that his mind was wholly ſubiect to God, his ſenſe to reaſon, his bodie to his ſpirite, and al other liuing creatures obedient to him: euen the terrible Lions, the cruel Tigres, the huge Elephants, and the wildeſt birdes. ⁸ Seuently, God brought them al to man, as to do him homage, and to take their names of him. VVhich by his excellent knowledge he gaue them conformable to their natures. ⁹ Eightly, God gaue man in ſome ſorte an immortal bodie, that if he had kept Gods commandment, he had liued long and pleaſantly in this world, and ſo ſhould haue bene tranſlated to eternal life without dying. ¹⁰ Ninthly, God did not only adorne man with al natural knowledge, and ſupernatural vertues, but alſo with the gift of prophecie. VVherby he knew that Eue was a bone of his bones, and flesh of his flesh, though being a ſlepe he knew not when ſhe was made. ¹¹ Tenthly (which was the chiefe benefite of al) God conuerſed familiarly with man, and that in shape of man, which was a token of his meruelous great loue to man, and a ſingular incitment of him to loue God. Reade more, if you pleaſe, of the dignitie of man, and the benefites of God towards him in his creation, in S. Bernard vpon the 99. Pſalme. And vpon the 61. chapter of Eſaie. \fq ¹ \ft Tenne prerogatiues of man in his creation. \fq ² \ft 1. Made like to God. \fq ³ \ft 2. The Mystery of the B. Trinity insinuated in his creation. \fq ⁴ \ft 3. Produced by God himself. \fq ⁵ \ft 4. placed in Paradise. \fq ⁶ \ft 5. Lord of all earthly creatures. \fq ⁷ \ft 6. innocency. \fq ⁸ \ft 7. excellent knowledge. \fq ⁹ \ft 8. power to live ever. \fq ¹⁰ \ft 9. gift of prophecy. \fq ¹¹ \ft 10. God conversed familiarly with man.\ef*
\v 27 And God created man, to his owne image: to the image of God he created him, male & female he created them.
\v 28 And God bleſſed them, and ſaith: \x - \xt Mat. 19, 4.\x* Increaſe and multiplie, & replenish the earth, and ſubdew it, and rule ouer the fishes of the ſea, and foules of the ayre, & al liuing creatures, that moue vpon the earth. \ef - \fr 1.28 \fq Increaſe and multiplie. \ft ¹ VVhether this be a commandment or no, at leaſt it is a bleſſing, for ſo the wordes before conuince, \+it God bleſſed them and ſaid: Increaſe and multiplie\+it*. He ſaid the ſame alſo to brute creatures, which are not capable of a precept, but by this were made fertile. VVherby we ſee that Gods bleſſing alwayes worketh ſome real effect: as of fertilitie in this and other places, of multiplication of the loaues and fiſhes, Ioan 6. ² And ſome real effect Chriſts bleſſing muſt nedes worke alſo in the bleſſed Sacrament. Mat. 26. VVhich can be no other but changing bread and wine into his bodie & bloud, ſeing him ſelfe expreſſly ſayeth: \+it This is my bodie. this is my bloud\+it*. And though Gods bleſſing in this place, be alſo a precept, yet it is not to al men for euer, ³ but for the propagation of mankind, which being long ſince abundantly propagated, the obligation of the precept ceaſeth the cauſe ceaſing. So S. Cyprian, S. Ierome, S. Auguſtin, and other Fathers expound this place. And confirme the ſame by the text, for immediatly God ſignifying to what end he ſpoke, ſaith: \+it and replenish the earth\+it*. VVhich benig repleniſhed, Gods wil is therin fulfilled. \fq ¹ \ft Gods blessing alwayes effectual. \fq ² \ft Especially in the holie Eucharist. \fq ³ \ft Not al men & wemen commanded to marie.\ef*
\v 29 And God ſaid: Behold I haue giuen you al maner of hearbe that ſeedeth vpon the earth, and al trees that haue in them ſelues ſeede of their owne kinde, to be your meate:
\v 30 and to al beaſtes of the earth, and to euerie foule of the ayre, & to al that moue vpon the earth, and wherein there is life, that they may haue to feede vpon. And it was ſo done.
\v 31 And God ſawe al things that he had made, and \f e \fr 1.31 \ft Euerie creature in nature is good, but al conſidered together make the whole world perfect. moſt apt to mans vſe and Gods glorie. S. Aug. li. 1 de Gen. cont. Manich. ca. 21.\f* they were very good. And there was euening & morning that made the ſixt day.
\c 2
\cd The worke of ſix dayes being finished, God reſted the ſeuenth day & bleſſed it. 8. Then placing man in paradiſe (planted with bewtiful & ſwete trees & watered with foure riuers) 16. commandeth him not to eate of the tree of knowledge of good & euil. 18. & formed a woman of a ribbe of Adam.
\p
\v 1 THE \x - \xt Exod. 20, 11. Deut. 5, 14. Heb. 4, 4.\x* heauens therfore & the earth were fully finiſhed, and al the furniture of them.
\v 2 And the ſeuenth day God ended his woorke which he had made: & reſted \f 1 \fr 2.2 \ft God createth not new kindes of creatures, yet ſtil worketh. Io. 5, 17. conſeruing & gouerning al things, and createth ſoules, grace, and glorie of the ſame kind S. Aug. li, 4 de Gen. ad lit. c. 12.\f* the ſeuenth day, from al woorke that he had done. \ef - \fr 2.2 \fq The ſeuenth day. \ft ¹ Al creatures benig made in their kindes in ſix dayes, complete and perfect, God not neding (as men often do in their workes) to perfect, poolish, or amend the ſame, \+it reſted the ſeuenth day\+it*: and therfore the natural perfection of Gods workes is attributed to the ſeuenth day, and the ſupernatural perfecting of men in eternal life, after the Reſurrection, is attributed to the eight day. as S. Auguſtin and other fathers teach. ² And for this cauſe \+it God bleſſed and ſanctifyed the ſeuenth day\+it*. and after we haue in the Decalogue, or tenne commandments, that this day al should reſt and abſtaine from workes, yea and kepe it feſtiual, occupying them ſelues in ſpiritual exerciſes ſeruice and ſpecial worſhipe of God, as the Iewes did euen til Chriſts, and his Apoſtles time, ³praying and hearing the word of God read and expounded in the Sabboth day. VVherby we ſee that diſtinction of dayes pertayneth to Religion, the people of God thus obſeruing the Sabboth in memorie of the Creation, & diuers other feaſtes in memorie of other benefites. ⁴ And we now kepe the Sunday holie, in memorie of Chriſts Reſurrection, and other feaſtes in gratful remembrance of other Myſteries of Chriſts Natiuitie, the coming of the Holie Ghoſt, and the like. Yea alſo feaſtes of his bleſſed Mother, and other Sainctes, for the benefites receiued from Chriſt by them, and for more honour to Chriſt in them. So this Catholique obſeruation of feaſtes is neither Iudaical (which alſo in the law was good but now is abrogated) nor heathniſh, for we honour not Iupiter, nor Iuno, nor anie falſe god or goddeſſe, but our Lord God Creator & Redemer, & for his ſake, his beſt ſeruants. ⁵ VVherof ſee the Annotations in the Engliſh new Teſtament. 4. chap. to the Galathians. VVherto we here only adde theſe wordes of S. Baſil. ⁶ VVhich may ſerue for a general anſwer to the moſt common obiection. \+it Honor ſeruorum redundat in communem Dominum. The honour of the ſeruantes redoundeth to the common Lord, or Maiſter\+it*. So, ſaith he, the honour of Sainctes is the honour of Chriſt their Lord and ours. \fq ¹ \ft Obseruation of holie dayes by Gods institution. \fq ² \ft \+it in Psal. 6. & 11\+it*. \fq ³ \ft \+it Act. 13, 14. Leuit. 23\+it*. \fq ⁴ \ft Obseruation of festiual dayes is religious, not Iudaical, nor heathnish. \fq ⁵ \ft Honour of Sainctes is to the greater honour of Christ. \fq ⁶ \ft \+it Homil. in 40. Martyres\+it*.\ef*
\v 3 And he bleſſed the ſeuenth day and ſanctified it: becauſe in it he had ceaſed from al his woorke which God created to make.
\v 4 Theſe are the generations of heauen & earth, when they were created in the day, when our Lord God made the heauen, and the earth.
\v 5 and euery plant of the filde, before it shot vp in the earth. And euerie herbe of the ground before it ſprang: for our Lord God had not rayned vpon the earth: and man was not to til the earth:
\v 6 But a ſpring roſe out of the earth, watering al the ouermoſt part of the earth.
\v 7 Our Lord God therfore formed man of the ſlyme of the earth: and \f a \fr 2.7 \ft Mans ſoule is immediatly created by God, not produced of other ſubſtance as the ſoules of beaſtes and plants are.\f* breathed into his face the breath of life, & man became a liuing ſoule. \x - \xt 1. Cor. 15, 45.\x*
\v 8 And our Lord God had \f b \fr 2.8 \ft VVhether this paradiſe be now extant is vncertayne, though it be certaine that Enoch and Elias are yet liuing in earth. \+it S. Aug. li. 2. cont. Pelagi. c. 23. See Perereus. li. 3. q. 5. & li. 7. q. vltima\+it*.\f* planted a Paradiſe of pleaſure from the beginning: wherin he placed man whom he had formed.
\v 9 And our Lord God brought forth of the ground al maner of trees, fayre to behold: and pleaſant to eate of: the tree of life alſo in the middle of Paradiſe: and the tree of knowledge of good & euil.
\v 10 And a riuer iſſued out of the place of pleaſure to water Paradiſe, which from thence is diuided into four heades.
\v 11 The name of the one is Phiſon: that is it which compaſſeth al the land of Heuilath, where gold groweth.
\v 12 And the gold of that land is very good: there is found bdelium, & the ſtone onyx.
\v 13 And the name of the ſecond riuer is Gehon: that is it which compaſſeth al the land of Ethiopia.
\v 14 And the name of the third riuer is Tygris: that ſame paſſeth along by the Aſſirians. And the fourth riuer, the ſame is Euphrates.
\v 15 Our Lord God therfore tooke man, & put him in the Paradiſe of pleaſure, to woorke, & keepe it.
\v 16 And he commanded him ſaying: Of euerie tree of Paradiſe eate thou:
\v 17 But of the tree of knowledge of good & euil eate thou not. For in what day ſoeuer thou ſhalt eate of it, thou ſhalt dye the death. \ef - \fr 2.17 \fq Of the tree of knowledge. \ft ¹ Beſides the law of nature, by which Man was bound to direct al his actions according to the rule of reaſon; and beſides the ſupernatural diuine law, by which he was bound to beleue, and truſt in God, and to loue him aboue al things, hauing receiued the giftes of faith, hope, and charitie: God gaue him an other particular law, that \+it he should not eate of the tree of knowlege of good and euil\+it*. And that for two ſpecial reaſons, which S. Auguſtin ² noteth vpon this place. ³ Firſt, that God might declare him ſelfe to be Lord of man. VVhich was abſolutely neceſſarie for man, and nothing at al profitable to God, who nedeth not our ſeruice, but we without his dominion ſhould vtterly fal to nothing. \+it Nec enim ipſo non creante, &c. For he not creating vs, neither could we haue bene, nor he not conſeruing vs, could we remayne, nor he not gouerning vs, could we liue rightly. VVherfore he onlie is our true Lord, whom not for his, but for our owne profite and ſaluation we ſerue\+it*. ⁴ The other reaſon was, that God might geue man matter wherin to exerciſe the vertue of obedience, and to shew him ſelfe a ſubiect of God. VVhich could not be ſo properly and effectually declared by keping other lawes, nor the enormitie of diſobedience appeare ſo euidently, as by fulfilling of Gods wil commanding him, or by doing his owne wil, moued to the contrarie, in a thing of it ſelfe indifferent, & only made vnlawful, becauſe it was forbid. ⁵ But let vs heare S. Auguſtins owne wordes. \+it Nec potuit melius aut diligentius commendari quantum malum ſit ſola inobedientia, &c. Neither could it\+it* (ſaith this great Doctor) \+it be better, nor more exactly ſignified how bad a thing ſole diſobedience is, then where a man became guiltie of iniquitie, because he touched that thing contrarie to prohibition, which if he, not forbidden, had touched, he had not ſinned at al. For he that ſaith, for example ſake, Touch not this herbe\+it*, ⁶ \+it ſuppoſing it is poyſenful, and doth forwarne one of death, if he touch it, death aſſuredly falleth on the contemner of the precept: yea though no man had prohibited, and he had touched, for he should dye becauſe the ſame thing bereueth him of health and life, whether it had benne forbidden him or no. Alſo when one forbiddeth that thing to be touched\+it*, ⁷ \+it which would not in dede preiudice him that toucheth, but him that forbiddeth, as if one take an others money, being forbid by him, whoſe the money is, it is a ſinne in him that is forbidden, becauſe it is iniurie to him that forbiddeth. But when that thing is touched which neither should hurt him that toucheth, nor any other, if it were not forbid, wherfore is it prohibited, but that the proper goodnes of obedience, and the euil of diſobedience might appeare\+it*? Thus S. Auguſtin ſheweth, that diſobedience is a ſinne, becauſe it is againſt a precept, though otherwiſe the thing that is done were not euil. And amongſt other good notes, ⁸ teacheth that true obedience inquireth not, wherfore a thing is commanded, but leauing that to the Superior, promptly doth that is appointed. \fq ¹ \ft VVhy a particular positiue law besides the general lawes of God & nature, was geuen to man. \fq ² \ft \+it lib. 8. de Gen. ad lit. c. 11. Psal. 15\+it*. \fq ³ \ft first reason. \fq ⁴ \ft 2. reason. \fq ⁵ \ft The sinne of disobedience. \fq ⁶ \ft Ioyned with damage of him that forbiddeth. \fq ⁷ \ft Ioyned with damage of him that forbiddeth. \fq ⁸ \ft True obedience is blind and prompt.\ef* \ef - \fr 2.17 \fq Of the tree eate thou not. \ft This example of our firſt parents tranſgreſſion ſheweth, ¹ ² how friuolous an anſwer it is to ſay; that breaking of commanded faſtes, or eating meates forbidden can not hurt vs, the meate being good and holſome: for ſo the fruite of the tree was good, and ſhould haue hurt no man, if it had not benne forbidden. Euen ſo al meates of their owne nature are good, yet the precept of faſting (foretold by our Sauiour in general, and determined by his Church in particular) and ſo of anie other like law, though it be in things otherwiſe indifferent, ³ proceeding from lawful Superiors, bindeth the ſubiects in conſcience. And the tranſgreſſion is properly diſobedience, what other ſinne ſoeuer may alſo be mixed therwith. \fq ¹ \ft Not meate, but the disobedience hurteth him that transgresseth the precept of abstinence. \fq ² \ft \+it Math. 9. Luc. 10. S. Epiph. in compen. fidei Cat. S. Aug. epist. 80\+it*. \fq ³ \ft Lawes in things indifferent bind in conscience.\ef* \ef - \fr 2.17 \fq Thou shalt dye the death. \ft Againſt the new doctrine, ¹ denying that after ſinne is remitted, ² anie temporal punishment remaineth for the ſame, this place declareth that death (wherof God forewarned Adam, if he ſhould eate of the fruite forbidden) remained due, and was at laſt inflicted vpon him, for his ſinne, which was preſently remitted vpon his repentance. Againe for ſomuch as we are al ſubiect to death, ³ ⁴ it proueth that we were al guiltie of this ſinne, by which death came vpon al men, as S. Paul teacheth. Els God ſhould punish vs without our fault, which is vnpoſſible that his goodnes ſhould do. Eſpecially it appeareth in infants, ⁵ ⁶ who dying before they come to vſe of reaſon, can neuer commit other ſinne. for though they were circumciſed, or had Sacrifice offered, or other remedie vſed for them before Chriſt, or baptiſed ſince Chriſt: ⁷ yet they ſuffer (as S. Auguſtin noteth) both death and manie other penalties, of ſickenes, cold, heate, hunger, and the like, which can neither be to them matter of merite (as to others it may be) nor profite them for auoiding of other ſinnes, ſeing they dye in their infancie. Yea moreouer ⁸ if they dyed without circumciſion, or other remedie of thoſe former times, their ſoules perished from their people; and now without Baptiſme can neuer enter into the kingdome of heauen, which could not ſtand with Gods iuſtice, if they were not guiltie of ſinne. \fq ¹ \ft \+it Caluin l. 3. inst. c. 4. parag. 31. & 32\+it*. \fq ² \ft Temporal punishment due after sinne is remitted. \fq ³ \ft Death due to al for Original sinne. \fq ⁴ \ft \+it Rom. 5\+it*. \fq ⁵ \ft Yea to infants who haue no other sinne. \fq ⁶ \ft \+it lib. 13. de ciuit. c 6. lib. 2. de pec. mer. & remis. c. 34\+it*. \fq ⁷ \ft Also other penalties inflicted vpon infants. \fq ⁸ \ft \+it Gen. 17. Ioan. 3. S. Greg. li. 4. Moral. c. 2\+it*.\ef*
\v 18 Our Lord God alſo ſaid: It is not good for man to be alone: let vs make him a helpe like vnto him ſelfe.
\v 19 Our Lord God therfore hauing formed of clay al beaſtes of the earth, and foules of the ayre, brought them to Adam that he might ſee what to cal them: for al that Adam called any liuing creature, the ſame is his name.
\v 20 And Adam called al beaſtes by their names, and al foules of the ayre, and al cattel of the filde: but vnto Adam there was not found an helper like him ſelfe.
\v 21 Our Lord God therfore caſt a dead ſleepe vpon Adam: and when he was faſt aſleepe, he tooke one of his ribbes, & filled vp fleſh for it.
\v 22 And our Lord God \f c \fr 2.22 \ft As we ſay brick is made of earth, and a houſe is built of bricke: ſo Adam was made of earth, and Eue built of a ribbe of Adam. And that of one ribbe, as if God should build a houſe of one bricke, or as in dede he fedde 500. men with fiue loaues. \+it Chriſ. ho. 15. S. Aug. Tract. 24. in Ioan. S. Tho. p. 1. q. 92. a. 3\+it*.\f*\f 1 \fr 2.22 \ft Mat. 19, 5. Mar. 10, 7. 1. Cor. 6, 16. Eph. 5, 31.\f* built the ribbe which he tooke of Adam into a woman: and brought her to Adam.
\v 23 And Adam ſaid: This now, is bone of my bones, and flesh of my flesh: ſhe ſhal be called woman, becauſe she was taken out of man.
\v 24 Wherfore man shal leaue his father & mother, & shal cleaue to his wife, & they ſhal be \f d \fr 2.24 \ft Not three, nor foure, nor more. for then two were changed to an other number. S. Ier. li. 1. cont. Ioui.\f* two in one flesh.
\v 25 And they were both naked; to wit Adam & his wife: and were not aſhamed.
\c 3
\cd By the craft of the Diuel ſpeaking in a ſerpent, our firſt parents tranſgreſſed Gods commandment. 7. who being ashamed would hide them ſelues. 9 but are reproued by God. 14. and beſides other particular punishements (yet with promiſe of a Redemer) are caſt out of Paradiſe. \f - \ft The ſecond part. Of the fall of man, & propagation of man & of ſin.\f*
\p
\v 1 BVT \f a \fr 3.1 \ft Serpents moſt craftie to eſcape harme, when they hurt men: ſo is the diuel.\f* the ſerpent alſo was more ſubtile then al the beaſts of the earth, which our Lord God had made. Which ſaid to the woman: Why hath God commanded you, that you ſhould not eate of euerie tree of Paradiſe? \ef - \fr 3.1 \fq Why hath God. \ft ¹ ² Here we may ſee how ſinne came firſt amongſt men. For the diuel enuying mans happie ſtate tempted Eue the weaker perſon, beginning with a queſtion, therby to allure her into conference, and by ſuch a queſtion as might bring her into ſuſpition of Gods affection towards man, ſaying: \+it VVhy hath God commanded you, that you should not eate of euerie tree of paradiſe\+it*? inſinuating by theſe words, and withal internally ſuggeſting, that God dealt hardly with them, abridging their libertie without cauſe. ³ And when he had got ſo much of her, that ſhe was diſpleaſed with the precept, which ſhe ſhewed by adding of her owne (to make it ſeme more greuous) that they were forbidden \+it to touch the tree\+it*: and againe by reporting the puniſhment as doubtful, ſaying: leſt perhaps vve dye, then the tempter auouched boldly, and falſly, that they ſhould not dye, and charged God to be enuious of the benefite they should get by eating of that tree, ſaying \+it their eyes should be opened, and they should be as Goddes, knowing good and euil\+it*. Vpon which perſwaſion, and liking alſo ſhe had to the fruite, ſhe did take and eate, and perſwaded Adam alſo to eate. ⁴ ⁵ And forthwith they ſaw that they would not haue ſeene, knew euil which they had better not to haue knowen, were ashamed, and endeuored to couer, and hide them ſelues. Euen thus the diuel dealeth with men euer ſince, aſſaulting the weaker perſons, and weaker part, as the flesh and ſenſualitie, and by them ſetteth vpon the ſtronger and ſuperior part, to get conſent of freewil, without which there is no ſinne. According to that famous ſaying of S. Augustin: ⁶ \+it Pecatum adeo eſt voluntarium, vt nullo modo ſit peccatum, ſi non voluntarium. Sinne is ſo voluntarie, that in no vviſe it can be ſinne, if it be not voluntarie\+it*. wherfore it was no ſinne in Eue to be tempted by the ſerpent, which ſhe could not auoide, nor in Adam to be tempted by Eue, but they ſinned when they conſented to the euil ſuggeſtions. ⁷ ⁸ And now in the regenerate, though concupiſcence remaine, which is the effect of ſinne paſt, & occaſion of ſinne in thoſe that yeld againe to tentations, yet is it not ſinne, but punishment of ſinne, and matter of exerciſe in the iuſt, and if we reſiſt, of merite: and therfore S. Paul exhorteth vs, ⁹ \+it to walke in the ſpirite, and the luſts of the flesh we shal not accomplish\+it*. And in an other place ſheweth, ¹⁰ \+it that he vvhich fighteth lawfully, shal be crowned\+it*. \fq ¹ \ft Sinne entred among men by the enuie & craft of the diuel, man consenting to his suggestions. \fq ² \ft \+it Sap. 2. 24. Ioan. 8, 44. S. Aug. lib. 14. de ciuit. c. 11. Rupert. li. de Trinit. & operibus eius c. 4\+it*. \fq ³ \ft Eue first sinned in thought, then in words, last in deedes. \fq ⁴ \ft Bad sequels of sinne. \fq ⁵ \ft \+it Lib. de vera Religione c. 14. Lib. 1. Retract. c. 13\+it*. \fq ⁶ \ft No sinne can be without freewil. \fq ⁷ \ft Concupiscence no sinne, but the effect, and occasion of sinne. Also occasion of merite. \fq ⁸ \ft \+it S. Aug. lib. 1. de nupt. & con. c. 23\+it*. \fq ⁹ \ft \+it Gal. 5\+it*. \fq ¹⁰ \ft \+it 2. Tim. 2\+it*.\ef*
\v 2 To whom the woman anſwered: Of the fruite of the trees that are in paradiſe, we doe eate:
\v 3 but of the fruite of the tree which is in the middes of paradiſe, God hath commanded vs that we should not eate: and that we ſhould not touch it, leſt perhapes we die.
\v 4 \x - \xt 2. Cor. 11, 3.\x* And the ſerpent ſaid to the woman: No you shal not dye the death.
\v 5 For God doth know that in what day ſoeuer you shal eate therof, your eyes shal be opened: and you shal be as gods, knowing good & euil.
\v 6 The woman therfore ſawe that the tree was good to eate, and fayre to the eyes, and delectable to behold: and ſhe tooke of the fruite therof, and did eate, and gaue to her huſband, who did eate. \x - \xt Eccl. 25. 1. Tim. 2, 14.\x*
\v 7 And the eyes of them both were opened: and when they \f b \fr 3.7 \ft After ſinne they were ashamed, not before. \+it S. Chriſ\+it*.\f* perceiued themſelues to be naked, they ſowed togeather leaues of a figge tree, and made themſelues aprons.
\v 8 And hearing the voice of our Lord God walking in paradiſe at the after none ayre: Adam hid himſelfe and ſo did his wife from the face of our Lord God, amidſt the trees of paradiſe.
\v 9 And our Lord God called Adam, and ſaid to him: Where art thou?
\v 10 Who ſaid: I heard thy voice in paradiſe: and I feared, becauſe I was naked, and I hid me.
\v 11 To whom he ſaid: And who hath told thee that thou waſt naked, but that thou haſt eaten of the tree, whereof I commanded thee that thou ſhouldeſt not eate?
\v 12 And Adam ſaid: The woman, which thou gaueſt me to be my felow companion, gaue me of the tree, and I did eate.
\v 13 And our Lord God ſaid to the woman: Why haſt thou done this? who anſwered: The ſerpent deceiued me, & I did eate.
\v 14 And our Lord God ſaid to the ſerpent: Becauſe thou haſt done this thing, \f c \fr 3.14 \ft All this curſe pertaineth to the devil that ſpake in the ſerpent. St. Auguſtine l. 2. de Gen. ad lit. cap. 36. St. Bede in hunc locum. (earth shalt thou eat)\f* accurſed art thou among al catle, & beaſts of the earth: vpon thy breſt shalt thou goe, & \f d \fr 3.14 \ft Earthly or worldly and carnal men. St. Gregory in Ps. 101.\f* earth shalt thou eate al the dayes of thy life.
\v 15 I wil put enmyties betwen thee & the woman, and thy ſeed and the ſeed of her: ſhe shal bruiſe thy head in peeces, & thou shalt lye in waite \f e \fr 3.15 \ft (of her heel) Though good men reſiſt temptations at the firſt aſſaults, and ſo bruiſe the ſerpent's head, yet he endeavoureth ſtill to deceive, eſpecially in the end of man's life, ſignified by the heel. St. Gregory in cap. 1. Job.\f* of her heele. \ef - \fr 3.15 \fq She shal bruiſe. \ft ¹ Proteſtants wil not admitte this reading, \+it ipſa conteret\+it*, she shal bruiſe, leſt our Bleſſed Ladie should be ſaid anie way to bruiſe the ſerpents head. And Kemniſius amongſt others ſaith, that al ancient Fathers read, \+it ipſum\+it*, not, \+it ipſa\+it*. But he is conuinced of lying by Claudius Marius Victor. \+it lib. 1. in Gen\+it*. Alcimus Auitus \+it lib. 3. carm. c. 6\+it*. S. Chriſoſtom \+it hom. 17. in Genes\+it*. S. Ambroſe \+it lib de fuga ſæculi cap. 7\+it*. S. Auguſtin \+it lib. 2. de Geneſi contra Manichæos, cap. 18 & lib. 11. de Geneſi ad literam cap. 26\+it*. S. Gregorie \+it lib. 1. Moralium cap. 38\+it*. And after them S. Bede, Eucherius, Rabanus, Rupertus, Strabus, and Lira vpon this place, S. Bernard \+it ſer. 2. ſuper Miſſus eſt\+it*. And manie others, who read \+it ipſa\+it* as the Latin text now hath. ² ³ But whether we read, \+it she shal bruiſe\+it*, or, \+it her ſede\+it*, that is her ſonne Chriſt, \+it shal bruiſe\+it* the ſerpents head, we attribute no more, nor no leſſe to Chriſt, nor to our Ladie by the one reading, then by the other: for by the text, \+it I vvil put enmities betwen thee and the woman, betvven thy ſeede, and her ſeede\+it*. It is clere, that this enmitie and battle pertained to the woman and her ſeede on the one partie, and to this diuel, that ſpake by the ſerpent, and al the wicked, on the other partie, and that the victorie should happen to mankind. ⁴ VVhich being captiue by Adams ſinne, occaſioned by a woman, should be redeemed, both ſexes, though in farre different ſorte, concurring therto. And ſo it is moſt true, that Chriſt by his owne proper powre, and his bleſſed mother by her moſt immediate cooperating to his Incarnation (and conſequently to other Miſteries) did bruiſe the ſerpents head, breake and vanquiſh his powre. ⁵ (a) As manie ancient Fathers do excellently diſcourſe: namely S. Bernard, writing vpon theſe wordes in the Apocalips. cap. 12. \+it A great ſigne appeared in heauen, a woman clothed vvith the ſunne\+it*: Albeit (ſaith he) by one man and one woman we were greatly damaged: yet (God be thanked) by one man and one woman al loſſes are repaired, and that not without great increaſe of graces. For the benefite doth farre excede the loſſe. Our merciful father geuing vs for a terreſtrial Adam Chriſt our Redemer, & for old Eue Gods owne mother. Moreouer as the ſame S. Bernard ſheweth, this bleſſed Virgin in ſingular ſorte bruiſed the ſerpents head, ⁶ ⁷ in that ſhe quite vanquiſhed al maner ſuggeſtions of the wicked ſerpent, neuer yelding to, nor taking delight in anie euil moued by him. \fq ¹ \ft The Latin text defended against Kemnisius and other Protestants. \fq ² \ft See Card. Bellarmin. li. 2. c. 12 de verbo Dei. \fq ³ \ft Both readings yeld the same sense. \fq ⁴ \ft As Adam was the cause, and Eue an occasion of mans captiuitie: so Christ is the true cause and his mother an occasion of our restauration. \fq ⁵ \ft * \+it (a) S. Ireneus li. 3. c. 33. & lib. 5. circa med. S. Epiph. Hær. 78. S. Ieron. ep 22. ad Eustoch. S. Aug. (or S. Fulgent) ser. 18 de Sanctis. de fide & Symb. de Agone Christiano\+it*. \fq ⁶ \ft Our B. Ladie resisted al euil suggestions. \fq ⁷ \ft \+it (b) Ser. 2. super Missus est\+it*.\ef*
\v 16 To the woman alſo he ſaid: I wil multiplie thy trauailes, and thy child bearinges: in trauaile shalt thou bring forth children, and thou shalt be vnder thy huſbands power, and he shal haue dominion ouer thee. \x - \xt 1. Cor. 14.\x*
\v 17 And to Adam he ſaid: Becauſe thou haſt heard the voice of thy wife, and haſt eaten of the tree, whereof I commanded thee, that thou ſhouldeſt not eate, curſed is the earth in thy woorke: with \f f \fr 3.17 \ft Al men trauel one way or other: & ſuch as ſuffer wides to ouergrowe (in their ſouls) shal after this life either ſuſtaine the fyre of Purgatorie or eternal paine. \+it S. Aug. li. 2. c\+it*. 20. de Gen. co. Man.\f* much toyling ſhalt thou eate thereof al the dayes of thy life.
\v 18 Thornes and thyſtles shal it bring forth to thee, & thou shalt eate the herbes of the earth.
\v 19 In the ſweat of thy face shalt thou eate bread, til thou returne to earth, of which thou waſt taken: becauſe duſt thou art, and into duſt thou shalt returne. \ef - \fr 3.19 \fq Duſt thou art. \ft By theſe wordes Adam was admoniſhed to humble him ſelfe, conſidering the matter wherof his bodie was made, and into which he should be reſolued againe. ¹ ² (c) wherupon it came to be a ceremonie amongſt penitents, to caſt ashes on their heads. As appeareth in holie Scriptures. for which cauſe the Church now alſo vſeth this ceremonie the firſt day of Lent, putting ashes on her childrens heades: willing them to remember, that duſt they are, and to duſt they shal returne, to moue vs by this meditation to more ſerious penance. \fq ¹ \ft The ceremonie of ashes, on Ashwenesday. \fq ² \ft \+it (c) Iob 42. Esai. 58. Ierem. 6. Ionæ. 3. Mat. 11\+it*.\ef*
\v 20 And Adam called the name of his wife, Eue: for becauſe \f g \fr 3.20 \ft She was mother rather of al the dying: but in figure of our B. Lady who is mother of Chriſt, life it ſelfe, she is called mother of the liuing. S. Epiph. her. 78.\f* ſhe was mother of al the liuing.
\v 21 Our Lord God alſo made for Adam and his wife garments of skynnes, and clothed them.
\v 22 And ſaid: Loe Adam is become as it were one of vs, knowing good & euil: now therfore leſt perhapes he reach forth his hand, and take alſo of the tree of life, & eate, and liue for euer. \ef - \fr 3.22 \fq Leſt perhaps. \ft ¹ Notwithſtanding Gods eternal decree in diſpoſing al thinges, and his omnipotencie which nothing can reſiſt, yet he produceth good, and either auoideth or diſpoſeth of euil which he ſuffereth, by ordinarie meanes, as appeareth Act. 27, v. 31. and that becauſe man hath freewil, with which God concurreth, & deſtroyeth not nor forceth. as S. Auguſtin teacheth.² ³ ⁴ \fq ¹ \ft Gods prouidence concurreth with mans free wil. \fq ² \ft \+it de grat. & liber. arb. c. 6. de corrept. & grat. ad art. falso impos\+it*. \fq ³ \ft Good Angels hinder devils of their desires. \fq ⁴ \ft \+it S. Aug. lib. 11. de Gen. ad lit. c. 40.\+it*\ef*
\v 23 And our Lord God ſent him out of the paradiſe of pleaſure, to worke the earth of which he was taken.
\v 24 And he caſt out Adam: and placed before the paradiſe of pleaſure Cherubins, & a flaming, and a turning ſworde, for to keepe the way of the tree of life. \ef - \fr 3.24 \fq Placed Cherubins. \ft Man being caſt out of paradiſe, the ſame is defended with duble gard. with Angels, that are watchful, wiſe, and potent; and with fire and ſword, moſt terrible armoure to man. ¹ wherby againe we ſee, that God vſeth ordinarie meanes in his prouidence, as the miniſtrie of Angels & humane terror, and would neither deſtroy the tree, ² nor depriue it of the vertue to prolong life, nor bereue man of freewil, by which he might deſire to returne: but conſeruing nature in al creatures, preuenteth inconueniences otherwiſe. Theſe Angels alſo hinder the diuel, that he can not enter paradiſe, leſt he should take of the fruite of the tree, and geue it to men to prolong their liues, and therby draw them to his ſeruice. \fq ¹ \ft Paradise defended by Angels and by fire & sworde. \fq ² \ft God destroyeth not nature\ef*
\c 4
\cd VVicked Cain killeth holie Abel. 9. vvhoſe bloud cryeth for reuenge. 11. Cain a curſed vacabond, 17. hath much iſſue. 25. Adam alſo hath Seth, and Seth Enos.
\p
\v 1 AND Adam knewe Eue his wife: who conceiued and brought forth Cain, ſaying: I haue gotten a man through God.
\v 2 And againe she brought forth his brother Abel. And Abel was a shepehard, & Cain a huſbandman.
\v 3 And it befel after manie dayes that Cain \x - \xt Heb. 11.\x* offred of the fruites of the earth giftes to our Lord. \ef - \fr 4.3 \fq Offered giftes. \ft Either God him ſelfe taught Adam, and he his children, or els they knew by inſtinct of nature, that Sacrifice muſt be offered to God, to acknowledge therby his ſupreme dominion ouer man, and mans due ſubiection to his diuine Maieſtie. And that not only in internal affection, which (as S. Auguſtin, ¹ and al Catholique Doctors teach) is principally required, but alſo in external things, ² becauſe we conſiſt of bodie, and not only of ſoule, and haue, by Gods goodnes, the vſe of corporal things. As here we ſee example in the law of nature: and the ſame was ordained by written precept in the law of Moyſes: ³ the Prophetes alſo foretold, that external Sacrifice ſhould be offered in the law of grace, and new Teſtament, to wit, the ſame which Chriſt inſtituted, and left in his Church, to continew to the end of the world. Moreouer this homage of offering Sacrifice is ſo peculiar to God only, ⁴ that albeit manie other exterior rites and ſeruices are vſed both to God & men, as to be bare head, to bowe, to kneele, & the like before them, \+it either of great humilitie\+it* (ſaith S. Auguſtin) ⁵ \+it or of peſtiferous flatterie\+it*, to ſuch as are \+it homines colendi, venerandi, ſi autem eis multum addatur, & adorandi: men to be vvorshipped, reuerenced, and if much be geuen them, adored\+it* (for this terme of \+it adoring\+it* is alſo applied to men in holie Scriptures Gen. 23. v. 7. 27. v. 29.) yet Sacrifice is due to God only, and to no creature how excellent ſo euer. In ſo much (ſaith the ſame Doctor) that as al nations founde it neceſſarie to offer Sacrifice, ſo none durſt ſacrifice to anie \+it niſi ei, quem Deum aut ſciuit, aut putauit, aut finxit\+it*: but to him whom they either knew, or thought, or fained to be God. \fq ¹ \ft \+it Lib. 10. de ciuit. c. 5\+it*. \fq ² \ft External Sacrifice due to God in euerie Law. \fq ³ \ft \+it Leuit. 1. Dan. 12. Mal. 1. Luc. 22\+it*. \fq ⁴ \ft Sacrifice due to God onlie, and to no creature. \fq ⁵ \ft \+it Lib. 10. ciuit. cap. 4. Aristot. li. 2. Metaphis. Ethic. 9. Polit. 7. c. 8\+it*.\ef*
\v 4 Abel alſo \f a \fr 4.4 \ft A figure of the Lambe that was ſlaine from the beginning of the world. \+it Apoc. 13, v. 8\+it*.\f* offred of the firſt begotten of his flocke, and of their fat: and our Lord had reſpect to Abel, & to his giftes. \ef - \fr 4.4 \fq Had reſpect to Abel. \ft ¹ Both Cain and Abel did wel in offering external Sacrifice, but they differed much in ſinceritie and maner of chooſing or diuiding their oblations, touching Gods part and their owne, as S. Iuſtinus Martyr, S. Hierom, S. Auguſtin and others teach. ² For Abel offered of the beſt things, of \+it the firſt begotten of his flock, and of their fatte\+it*. And therfore God reſpected and approued it. But to Cain and to his giftes he had not reſpect, becauſe he wanted ſincere deuotion. VVhich difference of Gods acceptance appeared doubtles, as S. Hierom and S. Auguſtin ſuppoſed, by ſome external ſigne, otherwiſe Cain had not vnderſtood it. Moſt like it was by fire ſent from God, which inflamed and conſumed Abels Sacrifice, & not Cains. As we read of diuers other Sacrifices in holie Scriptures. \fq ¹ \ft Abels Sacrifice declared acceptable, & not Cains, by some external signe. \fq ² \ft \+it To. 3. q. 4. Quæst. Hebraic. Lib 15. ciuit c. 7. Mala. 1. Hebr. 11. Leuit. 9. Iudic. 6. 2. Par. 7. 3. Reg. 18. 2. Mac. 1\+it*.\ef*
\v 5 But to Cain, and to his giftes he had not reſpect: & Cain was exceeding angrie, and his countenance abated.
\v 6 And our Lord ſaid to him: Why art thou angrie? and why is thy countinance fallen?
\v 7 If thou doe wel, shalt thou not receiue againe: but if thou doeſt il, shal not thy ſinne forthwith be preſent at the dore? but the luſt therof shal be vnder thee, and thou shalt haue dominion ouer it. \ef - \fr 4.7 \fq Shalt thou not receiue:. \ft ¹ Reward of good workes, and punishment of euil are clerly proued by this place. God ſaying to Cain: \+it If thou doeſt vvel, shalt thou not receiue againe\+it*? what els but wel for wel doing? as Abel receiued conſolation of his Sacrifice wel offered. \+it but if thou doeſt il, shal not thy ſinne be preſent forthvvith at the dore\+it*? afflicting thy conſcience, and not ſuffering thy mind to be in quiet, for remorſe of thy wicked fact, and feare of iuſt iudgement. For hence it came that Cains countenance fel, and his ſtomack boyled with angre: puniſhment ſo beginning euen in this life, & much more in the next world our Sauiour wil \+it render\+it* (as him ſelfe ſaith) ² to euery man according to his workes: which the Apoſtle expreſſeth more diſtinctly, \+it eternal life, or vvrath & indignation\+it*. \fq ¹ \ft Reward and punishment according to our workes. \fq ² \ft \+it Mat. 16. Rom. 2\+it*.\ef* \ef - \fr 4.7 \fq Vnder thee. \ft This Text ſo plainly ſheweth freewil in man, ¹ alſo after his falle, that the English Proteſtans to auoid ſo clere a truth, for theſe wordes, \+it the luſt therof\+it* (to wit of ſinne) \+it shal be vnder thee, and thou shalt haue dominion ouer it\+it*, corruptly tranſlate in ſome of their Bibles thus: ² ³ Vnto thee \+it his\+it* deſire shal be ſubiect, and thou shalt rule ouer \+it him\+it*. As if God had ſaid, that Abel ſhould be vnder Cain. As the phantaſtical Manichees peruerted the ſenſe, whoſe abſurditie S. Auguſtin controlleth ⁴ maintayning the true conſtruction of the wordes, \+it Tu dominaberis illius; nunquid fratris? abſit. Cuius igitur niſi peccati? Thou shalt rule ouer: VVhat, ouer thy brother? Not ſo. Ouer vvhat then but ſinne\+it*? In other English Editions, namely in the laſt, which we ſuppoſe they wil ſtand to, it is better, but yet obſcure thus, \+it Vnto thee shal be the deſire therof, and thou shalt haue rule of it\+it*. Let vs therfore examine the ſenſe, and if S. Hierome, ⁵ the great ſcripture Doctor did rightly vnderſtand it, God did ſpeake to this effect to Cain: \+it Becauſe thou haſt freevvil, I vvarne thee, that ſinne haue not dominion ouer thee, but thou ouer ſinne\+it*. ⁶ The Hebrew hath thus: \+it ad te appetitus eius, et tu dominaberis in eum\+it*, or, \+it ei. Vnto thee the appetite therof, and thou shalt rule ouer it\+it*. Thargum Hieroſolomitanum concludeth Gods ſpeach to Cain thus: \+it Into thy hand I haue geuen povvre of thy concupiſcence, and haue thou dominion therof: vvhether thou vvilt to good or to euil\+it*. The Greke hath thus: \+it To thee is the conuerſion therof, and thou shalt beare rule ouer it\+it*: to wit, appetite, luſt, concupiſcence is vnder thy wil. Finally, ⁷ ⁸ al antiquitie vniuerſalitie and vniforme conſent of Chriſtian Doctors, and other lerned Philoſophers, and reaſonable men hold it for certaine and an euident truth, that man yea a ſinner hath freewil. Yet Luther, the father of Proteſtants, ⁹ ſo abhorred this truth, that he could not abide the very word, nor voutſafe (when he writ againſt it) to title his beaſtlie booke, \+it Contra liberum arbitrium, Against freevvil\+it*: but, \+it De ſeruo arbutrio. Of ſeruil arbitriment\+it*. And denieth that man is in aniwiſe free to chooſe, to reſolue, or determine, but in al things ſeruil, tyed, conſtrained, and compelled to whatſoeuer he doth, ſaith, or thincketh. Further, that man in al his actions is like to a hackney, that is, forced to goe whither the rider wil haue him. And knowing the whole world aganſt him, ſhameth not to confeſſe, that he ſetteth them al at naught in reſpect of him ſelfe, concluding thus: \+it I haue not\+it* (ſaith he) \+it conferred vvith anie in this booke, but I haue affirmed, and I do affirme Neither vvil I that anie man iudge hereof, but I counſail al to obey, or yeelde to myn opinion\+it*. ¹⁰ ¹¹ Caluin alſo for his part, conſpireth in this hereſie with Luther, but more faintly rather wiſheth, then imagineth that men be ſo madde as to flee from the name of freewil. \+it I\+it* (ſaith Caluin) ¹² \+it neither myſelfe vvould vſe this vvord, and vvould vvish others, if they aske me counſaile, to abſtaine from it\+it*. But we wil be bold to oppoſe S. Hieromes reaſon againſt Luther, Caluin, al Manichees, and others that denie freewil. \+it God made vs\+it* (ſaith he) ¹³ \+it vvith freevvil, neither are vve dravven by neceſſitie to vertues nor to vices; othervviſe vvhere is neceſſitie, there is neither damnation nor crovvne\+it*. ¹⁴ \fq ¹ \ft Freewil in man also after his falle. \fq ² \ft Heretical translation. \fq ³ \ft \+it Bible 1579.\+it* \fq ⁴ \ft \+it Lib. 15. c. 7. ciuit\+it*. \fq ⁵ \ft \+it Quæst. Hebraic. in Gen\+it*. \fq ⁶ \ft The Hebrew also & Greeke text proue freewil in Cain. \fq ⁷ \ft Freewil testified by antiquitie, vniuersalitie, and consent of lerned & reasonable persons. \fq ⁸ \ft \+it S. Aug. l. de vera Rel. c. 14.\+it* \fq ⁹ \ft Luther abhorred the name of freewil. \fq ¹⁰ \ft \+it li. de servo arbitrio.\+it* \fq ¹¹ \ft Caluin also misliketh the word freewil. \fq ¹² \ft \+it lib. 2. c. 2. par. 8\+it*. \fq ¹³ \ft \+it l. 2. adv. Iovinian.\+it* \fq ¹⁴ \ft VVhere is necessitie there is nether reward nor punishment due.\ef*
\v 8 And Caine ſaid to Abel his brother: Let vs goe forth abroad. And when they were in the filde, Caine roſe vp againſt his brother Abel, and ſlewe him. \x - \xt Sap. 10.\x*
\v 9 And our Lord ſaid to Cain: Where is Abel thy brother? who anſwered: I know not: am I my brothers keper? \x - \xt 1. Io. 3.\x*
\v 10 And he ſaid to him: What haſt thou done? \f b \fr 4.10 \ft VVilful murther is one of the ſinnes that crie to God for reuenge.\f* the voice of thy brothers bloud crieth to me out of the earth.
\v 11 Now therfore curſed shalt thou be vpon the earth, which hath opened her mouth, & receiued the bloud of thy brother at thy hand.
\v 12 When thou shalt til it, it shal not yeld to thee her fruite: a roag and vagabound shalt thou be vpon the earth.
\v 13 And Cain ſaid to our Lord: Myne iniquitie is greater, then that I may deſerue pardon.
\v 14 Loe thou doeſt caſt me out this day from the face of the earth, and from thy face shal I be hid, and I shal be a vagabound & fugitiue on the earth: euerie one therfore that findeth me, ſhal kil me.
\v 15 And our Lord ſaid to him: No, it shal not ſo be: but whoſoeuer shal kil Cain, shalbe punished ſeauen fould. And our Lord put a marke on Cain, that whoſoeuer found him should not kil him.
\v 16 And Cain went forth from the face of our Lord, and dwelt as a fugitiue on the earth at the eaſt ſide of Eden. \ef - \fr 4.16 \fq Cain went forth. \ft It is a marke of Heretikes to make breach, ¹ and goe forth of the Church. And commonly it cometh of enuie. \+it Some runne into hereſies and ſchiſmes\+it* (ſaith S. Cyprian) ² \+it vvhen they enuie Bishops, vvhileſt one either complaineth that him ſelfe vvas not rather ordained, or diſdaineth to ſuffer an other aboue him. Hereupon he kicketh, hereupon he rebelleth. Enuie moued Cain to kil his brother, becauſe his ovvne vvorkes vvere vvicked\+it* and reiected: \+it and his brothers iuſt\+it*, ³ and eſtemed. So going forth became obſtinate, obdurate, and deſperate in his ſinne, and being reprobate of God, began a wicked Citie, oppoſite to the Citie of God. VVherfore Moyſes, as S. Auguſtin noteth, ⁴ intending to deſcribe, and ſhew the perpetual continuance of Gods Citie, the true Church, from Adam, which he doth by the line of Seth to Noe, ſo forward to his owne time, would not omit to tel alſo the progenie of Cain, euen to the floode, wherein al his ofſpring was finally drowned and deſtroyed, that the true Citie of God might appeare more diſtinct, more coſpicuous, & more renowned. And that in deede the ſame only (and not anie broken and interrupted companies or conuenticles) might be knowen to be the true Church of God. \fq ¹ \ft Going forth out of the Church a marke of Heretikes. \fq ² \ft \+it 1. Ioan. 2. Tract. de Zelo & liuore\+it*. \fq ³ \ft \+it 1. Ioan. 3.\+it* \fq ⁴ \ft \+it lib. de Pastore. c. 8. & c. 20\+it*.\ef*
\v 17 And Cain knewe his wife, who conceiued, and brought forth Enoch: And \f c \fr 4.17 \ft By the increaſe of Abrahas ſeede (by the line onlie of Iſaac and Iacob, beſides the iſſues of Iſmael and Eſau) in litle more then 400. yeares to aboue ſixhundreth thouſand men able to beare armes \+it (Num. 1\+it*.) it appeareth that Caines progenie in as manie yeares might ſuffice to people a citie, yea a whole countrie. S. Aug. l. 15. ciuit. c. 8.\f* he built a citie, & called the name therof by the name of his ſonne, Enoch.
\v 18 Moreouer Enoch begat Irad, and Irad begat Mauiael, and Mauiael begat Mathuſael, and Mathuſael begat Lamech.
\v 19 Who tooke \f d \fr 4.19 \ft This Lamech of Cains iſſue, is the firſt that is noted in Scripture, to haue taken two wiues.\f* two wiues, the name of the one was Ada, and the name of the other Sella.
\v 20 And Ada brought forth Iabel, who was the father of them that dwel in tents, and of heardſmen.
\v 21 And his brothers name was Iubal: he was the father of them that ſing on harpe & organes.
\v 22 Sella alſo brought forth Tubalcain, who was a hammerer & worker in al worke of braſſe & iron. And the ſiſter of Tubalcain was Noema.
\v 23 And Lamech ſaid to his wiues Ada and Sella: Heare my voice ye wiues of Lamech, harken to my talke: for I haue ſlaine a man to the wounding of my ſelfe, and a ſtripeling to mine owne drie blowe brewſing. \ef - \fr 4.23 \fq I haue ſlaine. \ft ¹ So hard and obſcure is this place, that S. Hierom required by S. Damaſus Pope to expound it, ² dareth not affirme anie one ſenſe for certaine, but propoſing diuers, which the text may ſeme to beare, wisheth the Pope (who was alſo very lerned) to examine al more at large: putting him in mind that Origen writ his twelfth and thirteenth bookes vpon this onlie place. ³ The moſt probable expoſition ſeemeth to be gathered out of the Hebrews Tradition, that this Lamech of the iſſue of Cain (for there was an other Lamech of Seths progenie) much addicted to hunting, and his eyes decaying, vſed in that exerciſe the direction of a young man his nephew, the ſonne of Tubalcain. VVho ſeing ſomething moue in bushes, ſuppoſing it to be a wild beaſt, willed his grandfather to ſhoote at the ſame: which he did, and ſtroke the marke with a deadlie wound, and approching to take the pray, found it to be old Cain. VVhereupon ſore amazed, afflicted, and moued with great paſſion, did ſo beate the young man, for his il direction, that he alſo died of the drie blowes. After both which mishappes, and his paſſion at laſt caulmed, Lamech lamenteth as the text ſaith, that he had killed a man and ſtripling, to wit, the one with a wound, the other with drie blowes, for which he feared ſeuenfold puniſhment more then Cain ſuffered for killing Abel. Neuertheles S. Hierom & other ⁴ Fathers thinke it probable, that Lamech killing the one of ignorance, the other in paſſion, was not ſo ſeuerly puniſhed as he feared. ⁵ And ſo they vnderſtand the reſt of this paſſage, that ſeuenfold vengance was taken of Cain, by prolongation of his miſerable life til his ſeuenth generation, when one of his owne iſſue ſlew him, and an other of the ſame linage with him. And Lamech was punished ſeuentie ſeuenfold when his ſeuentie ſeuen children (for ſo manie he had, as Ioſephus writeth) and al their ofspring periſhed in the flood. ⁶ Myſtically by ſeuentie ſeuen may be ſignified that the ſinne of mankind should be puniſhed and expiated in Chriſt our Redemer. who was borne in the ſeuentie ſeuenth generation from Adam. \fq ¹ \ft Scripture hard. \fq ² \ft \+it Tom. 3. ad 1. quæst. Damasi\+it*. \fq ³ \ft A probable sense according to the Hebrews Tradition. \fq ⁴ \ft \+it S. Chris. ho. 20. in Gen.\+it* \fq ⁵ \ft \+it Lib. 1. Antiq. ca. 2. Origen in Gen. Luc. 3\+it*. \fq ⁶ \ft Mystical sense.\ef*
\v 24 Seuenfould vengeance shal be taken of Cain: but of Lamech ſeuentie times ſeuen fould.
\v 25 Adam alſo knewe his wife again: and she brought forth a ſonne, and called his name Seth, ſaying: God hath giuen me other ſeede for Abel, whom Cain ſlewe.
\v 26 But to Seth alſo was borne a ſonne, whom he called Enos, this man began to inuocate the name of our Lord. \ef - \fr 4.26 \fq Begane to inuocate. \ft ¹ Seth was a moſt holie man, and ſo brought vp his children, that they were called the ſonnes of God. Gen. 6. Adam alſo and Eue were penitent, and became great confeſſors, and are now Sainctes. And ſo it can not be doubted but amongſt other ſpiritual exerciſes they prayed and inuocated God. And therfore that which is here ſaid: He (to wit Enos) \+it begane\+it* or (as the Hebrew hath) then was begune, \+it to inuocate the name of our Lord\+it*, can not be vnderſtood of priuate, ² but of ſome publique prayer of many meeting togeather, & obſeruing ſome rites & ſet forme in peculiar place dedicated to diuine Seruice, the Church being now growne to a competent multitude. And that beſides Sacrifice, which was alſo before, as appeareth both by Cain & Abel. \fq ¹ \ft \+it Suidas vocabulo Seth. Joseph. l. 1. Anti. S. Aug. epist. 99. ad Evod.\+it* \fq ² \ft Publike prayer besides Sacrifice in the Church of God.\ef*
\c 5
\cd The progenie of Adam, & number of their yeares (vvith the death of the reſt, & tranſlation of Enoch) in the line of Seth, to Noe & his three ſonnes.
\p
\v 1 THIS \x - \xt Sap. 2, 24. Eccl. 17, 1.\x* is the booke of the generation of Adam. In the day, when God created man, to the likenes of God made he him.
\v 2 Male and female created he them; and bleſſed them: and called their name Adam, in the day when they were created.
\v 3 And Adam liued a hundred and thirtie yeares; and begat to his owne image and likenes, and called his name Seth.
\v 4 And the dayes of Adam, after he begat Seth, came to eight hundred yeares: and he begat ſonnes and daughters. \ef - \fr 5.4 \fq Begate ſonnes and daughters. \ft Moyſes in this genealogie reciteth not alwayes the firſt begotten, nor the whole progenie by their names (for then he ſhould haue repeated Cain and Abel, and haue named many others) but thoſe onlie by whom the Church of God continued, ¹ ² ſignifying the reſt in general, whoſe ſucceſſion was cut of by the floud. \fq ¹ \ft The continual succession of Gods Church, and interruption of other communities. \fq ² \ft \+it S. Aug. l. 15. civ. c. 20.\+it*\ef*
\v 5 And al the time that Adam liued, came to nine hundred and thirtie yeares, and he died. \ef - \fr 5.5 \fq And he died. \ft By this Gods word is verified ſaying, that Adam ſhould dye, if he ſhould eate of the forbidden tree. And the diuel is proued a lyer, ſaying, they ſhould not dye. ¹ It is alſo moſt true that Adam dyed that day in which he did eate. For he began that very day to decline to death; and ſo doth al mankind euer ſince, as truly ſaid the woman of Thecua to king Dauid: ² \+it vve doe al die, and as vvaters that returne not, vve fal dovvne on the earth. And vvhat els\+it* (ſaith S. Gregorie) \+it is this daylie decaying of our corruption, but a lingering death\+it*? And none of al theſe that liued longeſt reaching to a thouſand yeares (which with God is as one day) man dyed in that day in which he tranſgreſſed. Morally ancient Fathers here note, that albeit the life of the Patriarkes ſemeth long to vs, ³ yet if we compare the ſame to eternitie it is nothing. Neither by the iudgement of Philoſophers may aniething be counted long, that hath an end: as Tullie bringing Cato wiſely diſputing, ſheweth the longeſt life to be but a ſhort moment. VVhereby againe we may ſee what loſſe we ſuſtaine by ſinne: ſeeing if ſinne had not benne, we ſhould al haue benne tranſlated from earth to heauen, and neuer haue dyed. \fq ¹ \ft How man died the day that he sinned. \fq ² \ft \+it 2. R. 14. ho. 37. in Euangel. Psal. 89. S. Ireneus li. 5. aduer. Heret. Cicero li. de Senec. & q. 1. Tuscul\+it*. \fq ³ \ft Al time is short in respect of eternitie.\ef*
\v 6 Seth alſo liued a hundred fiue yeares, and begat Enos.
\v 7 And Seth liued after he begat Enos, eight hundred and ſeuen yeares, and begat ſonnes and daughters.
\v 8 And al the dayes of Seth came to nine hundred & twelue yeares, and he died.
\v 9 And Enos liued nintie yeares, and begat Cainan.
\v 10 After whoſe birth he liued eight hundred & fiftene yeares, and begat ſonnes and daughters.
\v 11 And al the dayes of Enos came to nine hundred and fiue yeares, and he died.
\v 12 Cainan alſo liued ſeuentie yeares, & begat Malaleel.
\v 13 And Cainan liued after he begat Malaleel, eight hundred & fourtie yeares, and begat ſonnes & daughters.
\v 14 And al the dayes of Cainan came to nine hundred and ten yeares, and he died.
\v 15 And Malaleel liued ſixtie fiue yeares, and begat Iared.
\v 16 And Malaleel liued after he begat Iared, eight hundred and thirtie yeares, and begat ſonnes and daughters.
\v 17 And al the dayes of Malaleel came to eight hundred nyntie fiue yeares, & he died.
\v 18 And Iared liued a hundred ſixtie two yeares, and begat Enoch.
\v 19 And Iared liued after he begat Enoch eight hundred yeares, and begat ſonnes and daughters.
\v 20 And al the dayes of Iared came to nine hundred ſixtie two yeares, & he died.
\v 21 Moreouer Enoch liued ſixtie fiue yeares, & begat Mathuſala.
\v 22 And Enoch \f a \fr 5.22 \ft This Hebrew phraſe \+it walked with God\+it*, ſignifieth that he liued wel & pleaſed God.\f* walked with God: & liued after he begat Mathuſala, three hundred yeares, and begat ſonnes and daughters.
\v 23 And al the dayes of Enoch came to three hundred ſixtie fiue yeares.
\v 24 And he walked with God, and was ſeene no more: becauſe God \f b \fr 5.24 \ft The ſeuentie two Interpreters ſay, God tranſlated him and ſo doth S. Paul. \+it Heb. 11\+it*.\f* tooke him. \ef - \fr 5.24 \fq VVas ſeene no more. \ft ¹ ² That Enoch and Elias are yet aliue is a conſtant knowne truth, in the hartes and mouthes of the faithful, ſaith S. Auguſtin in his firſt booke, \+it de peccat. merit & remiſſ. c. 3\+it*. and confirmeth the ſame in diuers other places. And it is teſtified by very mauy both Greeke and Latin Doctors. S. Ireneus li. 5. S. Iuſtinus Martyr, q. 85. ad Orthodoxos. S. Hippolitus li. de Antichriſto. S. Damaſcen, li. 4. de Orthodoxa fide. S. Hierom. epiſt. 61. ad Pamach. c. 11. S. Ambroſe in Pſalm. 45. S. Chryſoſtom. ho. 21. in Gen. ho. 58. in Mat. ho. 4. in epiſt. 2. ad Theſſ. ho. 22. in ep. ad Heb. S. Greg. li. 14. Moral. c. 11, ho. 12. in Ezech. S. Proſp. li. vlt. de promiſ. S. Bede in c. 9. Marc. Theophilact and Oecumenius in cap. 17. Mat. and others innumerable. Touching Elias it is manifeſt in Scriptures, ³ ⁴ that he shal come, & preach, & be ſlaine with an other witnes of Chriſt, before the terrible day of Iudgement. ⁵ ⁶ Of Enoch Moyſes here maketh the matter more then probable, ſaying of euerie one of the reſt, \+it he dyed\+it*, onlie of Enoch ſaith not ſo, but that \+it he appeared, or vvas ſeene no more\+it*. For which the ſeuentie two interpreters ſay, \+it And he vvas not found, for God tranſlated him\+it*. VVhich can not ſignifie death, but tranſporting, or remouing to an other place. VVhereto agreeth the author of Eccleſiaſticus, ſaying: ⁷ \+it Enoch pleaſed God, and vvas tranſlated\+it*. But moſt clearly S. Paul ſaith. ⁸ \+it Enoch vvas tranſlated, that he should not ſee death. and he vvas not found for God tranſlated him\+it*. VVith what plainer wordes can any man declare, that a ſpecial perſon were not dead, then to ſay: \+it He vvas tranſlated\+it*, or conueyed away, \+it that he should not ſee death\+it*? ⁹ Neither is it a reaſonable euaſion to interprete this of ſpiritual death. For ſo Adam being eternally ſaued (as S. Irenæus li. 3. c. 34. Epiphan. con. hæreſim 46. S. Aguſtin epiſt. 99 ad Euodium, and others teach, and the whole Church beleeueth) was preſerued from that death, and ſo vndoubtedly were Seth, and Enos being moſt holie. and the reſt here recounted, as is moſt probable. Neuertheles for further confutation of the contrarie opinion of Proteſtants, the reader may alſo obſerue the iudgement of S. Chriſoſtom, ¹⁰ ¹¹ who affirmeth that \+it Though it be not a matter of faith, vvhether Enoch be novv in Paradiſe from vvhence Adam and Eue vvere expelled, or in ſome other pleaſant place: Dicunt tamen ſacræ Scripruræ quod Deus tranſtulit eum, & quod viuentem tranſtulit eum, quod mortem ipſe non ſit expertus. The holie Scriptures ſay that God tranſlated him, and that he tranſlated him aliue, that he felt not (or hath not experienced) death\+it*. And S. Auguſtin as expreſly ſaith. \+it Non mortuus, ſed viuus tranſlatus eſt. He\+it* (to vvit Enoch) \+it is tranſlated, not dead but aliue\+it*. Yea he teacheth how his life is ſuſtayned thus many thouſand yeares vpon earth. And ſheweth moreouer that both Enoch and Elias shal dye. \+it For ſeing Enoch and Elias\+it* (ſaith he) \+it are dead in Adam, and carying the ofspring of death in their flesh, to pay that debt, are to returne to this life\+it* (of common conuerſation) \+it and to pay this debt vvhich ſo long is deferred\+it*. ¹² ¹³ Diuers reaſons are alſo alleaged, why God would reſerue theſe two aliue. ¹⁴ Firſt to ſhew by example, that as their mortal bodies are long conſerued from corrupting or decaying, in like ſorte Adam and Eue and al others not ſinning, ſhould haue bene conſerued, and according to Gods promiſe, neuer haue died, but after ſome good time tranſlated to heauen, and indued with immortalitie. ¹⁵ Secondly to giue vs an argument of immortalitie, which is promiſed after the general Reſurrection. For ſeing God doth preſerue ſome mortal, ſo long from al infirmitie, we may aſſuredly beleue that he wil geue immortal & eternal life of bobie and ſoule to his Sainctes, after they haue payed the debt of death, and are riſen againe. ¹⁶ Thirdly theſe two (one of the law of nature, the other of the law of Moyſes) are preſerued aliue, to come amongſt men againe towards the end of the world, to teach, teſtifie, and defend the true faith and doctrin of Chriſt, againſt Antichriſt, when he shal moſt violently oppugne & perſecute the Church. ¹⁷ Of Enoch it is ſaid in the booke of Eccleſiaſticus, that he was tranſlated, \+it vt det gentibus pœnitenntiam, that he geue repentance to the nations\+it*, by his preaching, & reducing the deceiued from Antichriſt. And of Elias Malachie prophicieth, that he \+it shal come before the great and terrible day of our Lord, and shal turne the hart of the fathers\+it* (that is the people of the Iewes) \+it to the ſonnes\+it* (the Chriſtians) \+it and of the ſonnes\+it* (the deceiued Chriſtians) \+it to the fathers\+it*, the ancient true Catholiques. \fq ¹ \ft Enoch & Elias yet liuing in bodie. \fq ² \ft \+it li. 20. ciuit. c 29. lib. 2. de gratia Christi c. 23. tract. 4. in Ioa\+it*. \fq ³ \ft Manifest Scripture that Elias yet liueth, and shal be slaine & an other with him \fq ⁴ \ft \+it Mala. 4. Apo. 11.\+it* \fq ⁵ \ft Likewise that Enoch did not see death. \fq ⁶ \ft \+it Καὶ ὀυχ ἑυρίσκετο ὅτι μετατέθηκεν ἀυτὸν ὁ Θεὸς.\+it* \fq ⁷ \ft \+it Eccl. 44.\+it* \fq ⁸ \ft \+it Heb. 11\+it*. \fq ⁹ \ft These Scriptures speake of temperal not of spiritual death. \fq ¹⁰ \ft The Fathers proue by the scriptures that Enoch is not dead. \fq ¹¹ \ft \+it ho. 21. in Gen. lib. 1. de pec. mer. c. 2. & 3. l. 9. de Gen. ad lit. c. 6. S. Tho. in c. 11. ad Hebreos\+it*. \fq ¹² \ft Causes why Enoch & Elias are reserued aliue. \fq ¹³ \ft \+it S. Chris. ho. 21. in Gen. et in 2. Thes. 2. Theodor. q. 45. in Gen. Aretas, in 11. Apoc. S. Greg. lib. 14. Moral. c. vlt. & ho 12 in Ezech. Eccl. 44. Mala. 4. Eccl. 48\+it*. \fq ¹⁴ \ft 1 \fq ¹⁵ \ft 2 \fq ¹⁶ \ft 3 \fq ¹⁷ \ft See D. Sand. lib. 8. c. 35. de Monar. Eccl. And F. Pererius in c. 12. Danielis.\ef*
\v 25 Mathuſala alſo liued a hundred eightie ſeuen yeares, & begat Lamech.
\v 26 And Mathuſala liued, after he begat Lamech, ſeuen hundred eightie two yeares, and begat ſonnes and daughters.
\v 27 And al the dayes of Mathuſala came to \f c \fr 5.27 \ft This is the longeſt life of al here recited But if we conſider that Adam was as ſtrong of bodie, the firſt day he was created, aſ theſe others were at the age of 60. yeares (before which, none are ſaid to haue begot children) and ſo ſubtract 60. yeares from Mathuſala, then Adam liued in mans ſtate longer then he by 21. yeares.\f* nyne hundred ſixtie nine yeares, & he died.
\v 28 And Lamech liued a hundred eightie two yeares, and begat a ſonne:
\v 29 and he called his name Noe, ſaying: This ſonne shal comfort vs from the workes & labours of our handes on the earth, which our Lord curſed.
\v 30 And Lamech liued, after he begat Noe, fiue hundred nintie fiue yeares, and begat ſonnes and daughters.
\v 31 And al the dayes of Lamech came to ſeuen hundred ſeuentie ſeuen yeares, and he died. And ª \f d \fr 5.31 \ft The ſecond prophecie before Maſſe on Eaſter Eue.\f* Noe when he was fiue hundred yeares olde, begat Sem, Cham, and Iaphat.
\c 6
\cd Mans ſinnes cauſe of the deluge. 4. Giants vvere then vpon the earth. 8. Noe being iuſt vvas commanded to build the Arke, 18. vvherin he vvith ſeuen perſons more, and the ſeede of other liuing things vvere ſaued.
\p
\v 1 AND after that men began to be multiplied vpon the earth, & had procreation of daughters:
\v 2 The \f a \fr 6.2 \ft The profeſſors of true religion were called the ſonnes of God, the folowers of errors the ſonnes of men.\f* ſonnes of God ſeing the daughters of men, that they were faire, tooke to them ſelues wiues out of al, which they had choſen. \ef - \fr 6.2 \fq Sonnes of God. \ft ¹ The progenie of Seth, profeſſing true faith & Religion, were called the ſonnes of God: and thoſe of Cains iſſue and congregation, folowing erronious and wicked opinions, were called the ſonnes of men. VVhich were then the diſtinctiue termes of true and falſe Religion, as afterwardes were the termes of Iewes and Gentiles: after Chriſt, Chriſtians and Paganes: and laſtly true and falſe Chriſtians are diſtinguiſhed, by the names of Catholiques and Heretikes. As S. Auguſtin teacheth, in his queſtions vpon Geneſis, ² & other places. VVhich is confirmed by the like iugement of S. Ciril Alexandrinus li. 9. aduerſ. Iulianum. S. Ambroſe li. de Noe & arca. c. 4. S. Pacianus epiſt. ad Symphorianum. Theodoret. & manie others vpon this place. \fq ¹ \ft Sonnes of God and sonnes of men was then such a distinction, as now Catholiques and Heretiks. \fq ² \ft \+it lib. 15. c. 23. ciuit. De vera relig. c. 7. con. epist. fund. c. 4. Trac. 32. in Ioan\+it*.\ef*
\v 3 And God ſaid: My ſpirit shal not remaine in man for euer, becauſe he is flesh: & his dayes shal be an hũdred & twentie yeares. \ef - \fr 6.3 \fq An hundred and tvventie yeares. \ft ¹ ² Mans life was not here ſhortned to an hundred and twentie yeares, as ſome haue miſunderſtood this place. For after this diuers liued much longer, as appeareth in the genealogie of Sem to Abram in the 11. chapter of Geneſis. And Abraham liued 175. yeares (c. 25.) Iſaac 180. (c. 35.) Iacob 147. (c. 47.) and Ioiadas borne 1500. yeares after, liued 130. yeares (2. Par. 24.) But 120. yeares were granted before the floud for that generation to repent in, as the Chaldee Edition expreſſeth more plainely: \+it Terminus dabitur ei centum viginti annorum ſi forte conuertatur\+it*. The tearme of an hundred and twentie yeares ſhal be geuen them, if perhaps they may conuert. And ſo S. Chriſoſtom. S. Hierom. and S. Auguſtin expound this Scripture. ³Yet whether God cut of 10. of theſe yeares, and brought the floud after a 100 (for Noe had his ſonnes when he was 500. yeares old, & the floud came in the 600. yeare of his age) or that this warning was geuen twentie yeares before anie of his ſonnes were borne, is not ſo eaſely decided by the holie Doctors. How eaſie ſoeuer Proteſtants ſay al Scriptures are. ⁴Though vnder correction of better iudgement, it ſemeth more probable, thar Moyſes by anticipation ioyneth the birth of Noes ſonnes (when he was 500. yeares old) to the reſt of the geneologie of the firſt Patriarkes, in the former chapter, and then telleth of this admonition, geuen 20. yeares before their birth. And ſo God expected the peoples repentance the whole time of 120. yeares preſcribed. \fq ¹ \ft This warning and expectation of repentance sheweth freewil in man. \fq ² \ft \+it Philo. Iosephus. Lactant. Rupert. Tostatus.\+it* \fq ³ \ft \+it ho. 27. in Gen. Tradit. Heb. lib. 15. c. 24. civit.\+it* \fq ⁴ \ft Scriptures not easie.\ef*
\v 4 And Giants were vpon the earth in thoſe dayes. For after the ſonnes of God did companie with the daughters of men, and they brought forth children, theſe be the mightie of the olde world, famous men. \ef - \fr 6.4 \fq Giants vvere vpon the earth. \ft ¹ Some haue thought that theſe giantes were not men, nor begotten by men, but that either diuels, which fel at firſt from heauen, or other Angels allured with concupiſcence, begate them of the daughters of Cain. Philo Iudeus in his booke de Gigantibus, writeth that thoſe whom Moyſes here called \+it Angels\+it*, the Philoſophers called \+it Genios. Qui ſunt animalia aërea, vvhich are liuing creatures vvith ayrie bodies\+it* Ioſephus (li. 1. Antiq.) ſaith that Angels begate theſe giants. Tertullian alſo li. de habitu muliebri) holdeth the ſame error, and diuers more otherwiſe good authors. But S. Ciril of Alexandria (li. 9. aduer. Iulian) S. Chriſoſtom (homil. 22. in Gen) S. Ambroſe (de Noe & arca. c. 4.) S. Auguſtin (li. 15. c. 23. de ciuit) S. Hierom (Ttadit. Hebraic) and other moſt principal Doctors teach it to be vntrue, ² yea vnpoſſible, that theſe giants ſhould haue bene begotten by anie other creatures then by men. ³ For that Angels and diuels are mere ſpirits without al natural bodies. And if they had ayrie bodies (as they haue not) yet they could not haue ſuch generation. ⁴ For the powre or force to engender belongeth to the vegatatiue ſoule, whoſe proper operations are to turne nutriment into the ſubſtance of the ſubiect wherin it is, and to engender new iſſue or ofſpring from the ſame, as Ariſtotle ſheweth (li. 2. de anima, textu. 24.) And in what bodies ſoeuer there is vegetatiue ſoule, it muſt needes be, that the ſame was engendred, and muſt ſome times decay and die, and ſo diuels ſhould be mortal. ⁵ Moreouer if they could haue generation togeather with mankind, then ſuch iſſue ſhould be a diſtinct ſpecies both from man and diuel, as a mule differeth both from horſe and aſſe. ⁶ Againe, if ſpirits had abuſed wemen in aſſumpted bodies, and ſhape of men, yet they did not take them to wiues as the Scripture ſaith they did, who begate theſe giants. ⁷ Finally the holie Scripture here expreſly calleth the giants men. \+it Theſe be the\+it* mightie ones, famous \+it men\+it*. The modeſtie of Scripture terming them famous, whom our common phraſe would cal infamous being more monſtrous in wickednes of mind, ⁸ then in hugenes of bodie. For they were moſt inſolent, laſciuious, couetous, cruel, and in al kinde of vices moſt impious. \fq ¹ \ft Erronious opinions concerning these giants. \fq ² \ft The Principal doctors prove that they were men, and begotten of men. \fq ³ \ft First reason. \fq ⁴ \ft 2 \fq ⁵ \ft 3 \fq ⁶ \ft 4 \fq ⁷ \ft 5 \fq ⁸ \ft Giants most monstruous in bodie and in minde.\ef*
\v 5 And God ſeing the malice of men was much on the earth, and that al the cogitation of their hart was bent to euil at al times, \ef - \fr 6.5 \fq Al the coigtation bent to euel. \ft ¹ Luther (in his 21. article condemned by Leo the tenth) would proue by theſe wordes, and the like folowing, \+it Al flesh had corrupted his vvay vpon earth\+it*, that al workes of men are ſinnes. For (ſaith he) ſeeing the hartes of al men are bent alwaies to euil, and al humane actions proceede from the hart, it muſt needes be that the hart as the fountaine being corrupt, the ſtreames alſo iſſuing from the ſame muſt be corrupted. Againe al flesh hauing corrupted his way vpon earth, there is not any iuſt man (ſaith he) nor any man without ſinne: and with Proteſtantes al ſinnes are mortal. ² But Heretikes arguments are like to that the Poëts feyne of Siſyphus laboring to carie a great ſtone to the toppe of an high hil, which when he hath brought almoſt to the height, it ſtil falleth from him, & tumbleth againe to the bottome. Euen ſo their arguments that make greateſt shew of prouing their opinions, are nothing but vaine traueling, when they come to be tried by the true ſenſe of holy Scripture. In this place Moyſes deſcribeth the enormitie of ſinne that reigned in the world before the floud, for which God ſent that deſtruction. For it was haynous in deede, and that eſpecially in foure reſpects. ³ ⁴ Firſt the malice and wickednes was general, which is ſignified by thoſe wordes, \+it al flesh hath corrupted his vvay vpon earth\+it*. ⁵ Secondly it was great malice, ſignified by the words \+it much, and, al the cogitations of their hart is bent to euil\+it*. For they committed al maner of wickednes in hautines of pride, in al laſciuiouſnes of the flesh, in al crueltie of robbing, ſacking, & murthering, in al impietie, againſt God & man. ⁶ Thirdly, it was of long continuance, and dayly iterated. For Cain once fallen into damnable ſinne neuer repented, and al his progenie was exceding wicked and after that Adam and Seth were dead, and Enoch tranſlated, manie of the faithful fel to the wicked ſorte, and became worſe and worſe \+it omni tempore\+it*, alwaies, or \+it euerie day\+it*. ⁷ Fourthly they were obſtinate and obdurate, not repenting when \+it Noe\+it* built the arke, and \+it preached iuſtice\+it* (as S. Peter teſtifieth) ⁸ and therfore \+it God ſaued him and his familie, bringing in the deluge vpon the vvorld of the impious\+it*. Al which maketh nothing at al for Luther. ⁹ For although the malice of man, and corruption of flesh, was then verie genaral, great, of long continuance, & obſtinate, yet was it not ſo vniuerſal, but that God him ſelfe excepted Noe, ſaying to him \+it I haue found thee iuſt in my ſight in this generation\+it*, whereby it is clere that theſe general termes, \+it al cogitation\+it* and \+it al flesh\+it*, haue exceptions. As likewiſe other as general propoſitions in this ſame chapter, concerning the puniſhment threatned, comprehend not abſolutly al, and euerie one, but almoſt al, very few excepted. I \+it vvil cleane take avvay\+it*, or deſtroy \+it man vvhom I haue created, from the face of the earth. The end of al flesh is come before me\+it*. againe, \+it that I may deſtroy al flesh vvherein is breath of life vnder heauen\+it*. Theſe are very general ſpeaches, that al should be deſtroyed, and yet eight perſons of mankind, that had the ſame natural fleſh, and amongſt other liuing creatures, that had breath, diuers payres were ſaued aliue. So that this place (nor anie other in holie Scripture) wil not proue that Proteſtants paradox, that al mens actions are mortal ſinnes, or that no man in this life is or can be iuſt: but manie ſcriptures tel vs plainly that ſome men were iuſt, ¹⁰ as Noe, Iob, Daniel, Zacharias, Eliſabeth, Simeon and others Of Noe ſee more in the next annotation. \fq ¹ \ft Luthers argument that al mens workes are sinnes. \fq ² \ft Heretiks like to Sisyphus. \fq ³ \ft The sins before the flood very grievous in four respects. \fq ⁴ \ft 1 \fq ⁵ \ft 2 \fq ⁶ \ft 3 \fq ⁷ \ft 4 \fq ⁸ \ft \+it 2. Pet. 2\+it*. \fq ⁹ \ft Luthers argugument answered. \fq ¹⁰ \ft \+it Ezech. 1. Luc. 1 & 2. Apoc. 22\+it*.\ef*
\v 6 it \f b \fr 6.6 \ft God who is immutable, &c. ſubiect to no paſſion, yet by the enormitie of ſinnes ſemeth prouoked to wrath, and to repent that he had made man. \+it S. Amb. li. de Noe & arca. c. 4\+it*.\f* repented him that he had made man on the earth. And touched inwardly with ſorrowe of hart,
\v 7 I wil, ſaith he, cleane take away man, whom I haue created, from the face of the earth, from man euen to beaſtes, from that which creepeth euen vnto the foules of the ayre. for it repenteth me that I haue made them.
\v 8 But Noe found grace before our Lord.
\v 9 Theſe are the generations of Noe: Noe was a iuſt and perfect man in \f c \fr 6.9 \ft In al generations God reſerued ſome iuſt. Much more in the law of Grace.\f*\f 1 \fr 6.9 \ft Eccl. 44. 17.\f* his generations, he did walke with God. \ef - \fr 6.9 \fq Noe vvas a iuſt and perfect man. \ft ¹ Here Noe is not onlie called iuſt, but alſo perfect. The hebrew word \+it tamim\+it* of the verbe \+it tamam\+it* (which ſignifieth to finish or accomplish) sheweth that Noe was a perfect or complete man doing al that he was commanded, and performing the offices of al vertues that pertained to him; and that not in a vulgar and meane ſorte, but in a high degree, & heroical maner, as ſundrie ancient Fathers haue gathered vpon this place. VVe shal cite ſome few of their ſayings for example. S. Hierom (Tradit. Hebraic. in Gen.) diſtinguishing betwen conſummate iuſtice (of the next life) & iuſtice of this generation (or tranſitorie life) ſaith: \+it Noe the iuſt man vvas perfect in his generations: Noe did vvalke vvith God: that is, did folovv his ſteppes\+it*. S. Auguſt. (li. 15. ciuit. c. 26.) ſaith the like, that \+it Noe vvas called iuſt in his generation, to vvit, not as the citizens of Gods citie are to be perfected in that immortalitie, in vvhich they shal be equal to Angels, but as they may be perfect in this pilgramage\+it*. And in his booke de perfectione contra Cæleſtium. he deſcribeth hin to be ² \+it a perfect man, that runneth vvithout blame tovvards perfection, voide of damnable ſinnes, and is not negligent to cleanſe venial ſinnes, by almes, prayers\+it*, and other good workes. S. Ambroſe alſo teſtifieth, (li. de Noe & arca c. 4.) that albeit the world was verie wicked, yet ſome were iuſt, ſaying: \+it By the grace (or fauoure) vvhich Noe found, is shevved that other mens offence doth not obſcure the iuſt man, vvho is prayſed, not by the nobilitie of his birth, but by the merit of his iuſtice and perfection\+it*. S. Chriſoſt. moſt largely (ho. 23. in Gen) ſetteth forth the iuſtice and perfection of Noe. VVhere after he hath shewed that Noe deſerued in deede the name of a man, becauſe he by flying vices, and folowing vertues conſerued the image of man, when others like beaſtes were ledde away and ruled by their wicked luſtes, proceedeth thus in his commendation. \+it Behold\+it* (ſaith he) \+it an other kind of praiſe: Noe is called, iuſt, which denomination comprehendeth al vertue. For this name iuſt we vſe to pronounce of them, that exerciſe al maner of vertue. And that you may lerne, how he ariued to the very toppe which was then alſo required of our nature, the Scripture ſaith, he vvas iuſt, being perfect in his generation. He performed what thinges ſoeuer it behoueth one to doe that embraceth vertue, for ſuch a one is perfect, he intermitted nothing, he halted in nothing, he did not wel in this thing, and ſinned in that thing, but was perfect in euerie vertue, which was requiſite for him to haue. Moreouer to make alſo this iuſt man more conſpicuous to vs in regard of the time, and by comparing him with others, the Scripture ſaith, he vvas perfect in his generation: in that time, in that peruerſe generation, which declined vnto euil, which would not ſo mnch as pretend anie reſemblance of vertue. In that generation therfore, in thoſe times, that iuſt man not only pretended, but arriued to that height of vertue, that he became perfect, and in al thinges abſolute. And that which I ſaid before, to doe wel amongſt the enimies of vertue, amongſt them that forbid vertue, doth alwaies teſtifie a greater poyſe of vertue, ſo by this occaſion the iuſt man got greater prayſes. Neither doth diuine Scripture here make an end of praiſing him, but further ſheweth the excellencie of his vertue, and that he was approued by Gods owne cenſure\+it*, ³ \+it for beſides ſaying: He vvas perfect in his generation, it addeth, that Noe pleaſed God. So great was the renowme of his vertue, that he deſerued to be prayſed of God. For Noe pleaſed God ſaith the Scripture, that you may know that he was approued of God. He pleaſed that eye, that can not be deceiued, by his good workes\+it*. Thus farre S. Chriſoſtom, and much more to the ſame effect. S. Gregorie the great in his fifth booke of Morales, and 36. chapter vpon the third chapter of Iob, recounting certaine principal Patriarches among the reſt ſaith: \+it Noe for that he pleaſed Gods examination vvas ſaued aliue in the vncleane vvorld\+it*. and after a large catalogue of other iuſt men in confirmation of this doctrine, that ſome were iuſt in the law of nature concludeth thus: \+it Neither is it to be beleued\+it* (ſaith he) \+it that only ſo manie vvere iuſt before the lavv vvas receiued, as Moyſes contracteth in his moſt briefe deſcription\+it*. \fq ¹ \ft Noe iust and perfect. \fq ² \ft VVho is perfect in this life. \fq ³ \ft \+it Divino calculo.\+it*\ef*
\v 10 And he begat three ſonnes, Sem, Cham, & Iapheth.
\v 11 And the earth was corrupted before God, and was replenished with iniquitie.
\v 12 And when God had perceiued that the earth was corrupted (for al flesh had corrupted his way vpon the earth)
\v 13 he ſaid to Noe: The end of al flesh is come before me, the earth is replenished with iniquitie from the face of them, & I wil deſtroy them with the earth.
\v 14 Make thee an arke of timber planke: cabinets ſhalt thou make in the arke, and ſhalt pitch it within, and without with bitume.
\v 15 And thus shalt thou make it. The length of the Arke shal be three hundred cubitts: fiftie cubitts the breadth, and thirtie cubitts the height of it. \ef - \fr 6.15 \fq Three hundred cubites. \ft ¹ Apelles an old heretike, ſcholar of Marcian, but after leauing him, and amongſt other new coyned hereſies, reiecting the Law & the Prophetes, would by this place impugne Moyſes, ſaying it was vnpoſſible that in ſo ſmale rowme, as was the arke by this deſcription, the deſigned payres of al kindes of beaſtes, foule, & ſerpents, ſhould be contained, with the eight perſons, and al their prouiſion of meate for a whole yeare. VVherupon he concludeth that this narration (which he calleth a fable) hath no probabilitie, nor poſſibilitie to be true. ² To whom & al ſuch calumniators it may be anſwered, that Moyſes euen in an heretikes owne conceipt, if malice obſcured not his ſenſe, muſt needes be thought wiſe ynough, if he had benne diſpoſed to fayne fables, to frame them probable, or poſſible, eſpecially when he pretended not to ſignifie a miracle, in the ſmalnes of the rowme to receiue ſo much, as he reporteth. ³ Origen to anſwer him ſuppoſeth a cubite here mentioned, to haue contained ſix ordinarie cubites: and ſo doubtles the arke might eaſily containe al thinges that are here ſpoken of, for ſo it were like to a great citie. ⁴ But this opinion neither hath good warrant, that euer the Ægyptians (of whom he ſuppoſeth Moyſes might haue learned it) or any other nation vſed ſuch long cubites, neither can this meaſure of a cubite, be agreable to Moyſes meaning, ⁵ who no doubt ſpeaketh of the like cubites here, as he doth in other places. ⁶ And in Exodus he deſcribeth an Altar to be made fiue cubites long, fiue broade, and three in height. VVhich would be by Origens meaſure (euerie cubite contayning ſix ordinarie cubites, that is nine foote at leaſt) in length, and likewiſe in breadth 45. foote, and 27. foote in height. Againe (Deut. 3.) Moyſes telleth of an iron bed of Og King of Baſan, that was nine cubites long, & foure broad. VVhich make according to Origens meaſure of a cubite, fourſcore and one foote in length, and in breadth 36. foote: which in deede haue no probabilitie. And therfore ⁷ S. Auguſtin and other Doctors, ſuppoſing that Moſes in al theſe bookes, written for inſtruction of the ſame people, whom he brought forth of Ægypt, ſpeaketh of one ſorte of cubites, do likewiſe iudge that he meaneth ordinarie & knowne cubites, which containe a foote & a halfe euerie cubite, as Vitruuius Agricola and others do proue, or a foote and three quarters of a foote, which is the greateſt cubite, that ſemeth to be mentioned in holie Scripture, called a mans cubite, or \+it cubite of a mans hand\+it*. ⁸ And ſo the Arke was at leaſt in length 450. foote, in breadth 75. in height 45. or at moſt in length 525. foote, in breadth 87. and a halfe: in height 52. and a halfe. And either of theſe capacities was ſufficient to receiue al the thinges here mentioned, conſidering the loftes & partions, that were in the whole arke. \fq ¹ \ft Appelles an old Heretike, that denied Christ to haue true flesh. \fq ² \ft A general answer to al calumniators of wise and learned men. \fq ³ \ft \+it ho. 2. in 6. Gen\+it*. \fq ⁴ \ft Origens opinion of long cubites not probable. \fq ⁵ \ft Moyses in other places can not be vnderstood to speake of so long cubites. \fq ⁶ \ft \+it Exod. 27\+it*. \fq ⁷ \ft \+it l. 15. ciuit c. 27\+it*. \fq ⁸ \ft \+it Deu. 3\+it*.\ef*
\v 16 Thou shalt make a windowe in the arke, and in a cubit finish the toppe of it: and the dore of the arke thou shalt ſet at the ſide belowe, middle chambers, and third loftes shalt thou make in it.
\v 17 Behold I wil bring the waters of a great floud vpon the earth, that I may deſtroy al flesh, wherin there is breath of life vnder heauen. Al thinges that are in the earth, shal be conſumed.
\v 18 And I wil eſtablish my couenant with thee: and thou shalt enter into the arke, thou and thy ſonnes, and thy wife, and the wiues of thy ſonnes with thee.
\v 19 And of al liuing creatures of al flesh, thou shalt bring payres into the arke, that they may liue with thee: of the male ſexe, and the female.
\v 20 Of foules according to their kind, and of beaſtes in their kind, & of al that creepeth on the earth according to their kind: payres of al ſortes shal enter in with thee, that they may liue.
\v 21 Thou shalt take therfore with thee of al meates, that may be eaten, and thou ſhalt lay them vp with thee: and they ſhal be meate for thee and them.
\v 22 Noe therfore \f d \fr 6.22 \ft A right example of a iuſt man.\f*\f 1 \fr 6.22 \ft Heb. 11.\f* did al thinges, which God commanded him.
\c 7
\cd Noe vvith his familie, and paires of al kindes of beaſtes and foules, beirg entred into the arke, 12. it raineth fourtie daies and fourtie nights. 21. Al men and other liuing creatures on the earth, without the arke, are deſtroyed.
\p
\v 1 AND our Lord ſaid to him: Get thee in, thou and al thy houſe into the arke: for I haue ſene thee iuſt \f a \fr 7.1 \ft Noe was iuſt not only by the eſtimation of men, but indeede and before God.\f* in my ſight in this generation.
\v 2 Of al beaſts that are \f b \fr 7.2 \ft Obſeruation of cleane and vncleanne beaſtes by tradition, before the law of Moyſes.\f* cleane, thou shalt take ſeauen, and ſeauen, male & female:
\v 3 but of the beaſts that are vncleane two and two, male & female. Yea and of the foules alſo of the ayre ſeauen & ſeauen, male and female: that ſeede may be ſaued vpon the face of the whole earth.
\v 4 For yet a while, and after ſeauen dayes, I wil rayne vpon the earth fourtie dayes and fourtie nights: and I wil cleane deſtroy al ſubſtance, that I haue made, from the face of the earth.
\v 5 Noe therfore did al thinges, which our Lord had commanded him.
\v 6 And he was ſix hundred yeares old, when the waters of the floud flowed ouer the earth.
\v 7 \x - \xt Mat. 24. Luc. 17.\x* And Noe entred and his ſonnes, his wife and the wiues of of his ſonnes with him into the arke, becauſe of the waters of the floud.
\v 8 Of beaſts alſo the cleane and the vncleane, & of foules, and of al that moueth vpon the earth,
\v 9 two & two went to Noe into the arke, male and female, as our Lord had commanded Noe.
\v 10 And after the ſeauen dayes were paſſed, the waters of the floud flowed ouer the earth.
\v 11 In the ſix hundred yeare of the life of Noe, in the ſecond moneth, in the ſeauententh day of the moneth, al the fountaines of the \f c \fr 7.11 \ft The Hebrew word \+it Thehom\+it* ſignifieth a gulfe of water, from whence new fountaynes ſprang, more abundantly then euer ſince or before.\f* greate deapth were broken vp, and \f d \fr 7.11 \ft \+it Arubbah\+it* ſignifieth great pipes or windowes, by which water fell downe in great abundance from the ayre, here called heauen. \+it S. Hier. queſt. Heb. S. Epiph. ad Io. Hieroſolom. S. Chr. ho. 25. in Gen\+it*.\f* the floudgates of heauen were opened:
\v 12 and the raine fel vpon the earth fourtie dayes and fourtie nights.
\v 13 In the verie point of that day entred Noe, and Sem, and Cham, & Iapheth his ſonnes: and his wife, and the three wiues of his ſonnes with them into the arke:
\v 14 they and euerie beaſt according to their kind, and al cattle in their kinde, and al that moueth vpon the earth according to their kind, and al foule according to their kind, al birds, and al that fly
\v 15 went to Noe into the arke, two and two of al flesh, wherin there was breath of life.
\v 16 And ſuch as entred in, male and female of al flesh did enter in, as God had commanded him: and our Lord ſhut him in on the out ſide. \ef - \fr 7.16 \fq Shut him in. \ft God who by his only wil could in a moment haue drowned al the reſt of the world, ſauing whom he pleaſed, not needing in any thing the helpe of his creatures, ¹ yet would vſe both natural, & ſupernatural meanes, as the labour of Noe to build the arke, new fountaines ſpringing, and the heauens powring downe water fourtie dayes togeather, afterwards the winde to dry vp the earh, and becauſe the dore being great (for Elephantsr to enter in) and was to be firmed without (as S. Ambroſe noteth) ² for better induring the forcible waters, could not commodiouſly be cloſed by Noe, \+it our Lord\+it* (by the miniſtrie of Angels) \+it shut him in on the out ſide\+it*, to teach vs by al this, and the like diſpoſition of things, that albeit his Diuine omnipotencie can do what he wil al alone, yet he wil haue his creatures to concurre and cooperate as ſecundarie cauſes, ſometimes naturally, ſometimes ſupernaturally, or miraculouſly, as it pleaſeth his goodnes to impert to them powre and vertue. \fq ¹ \ft God vseth both natural and supernatural meanes, as secundarie causes, in producing, conseruing, gouerning, punishing, & in rewarding his creatures. \fq ² \ft \+it li. de Noe & arca, c. 15\+it*.\ef*
\v 17 And the floud grew fourtie daies vpon the earth: and the waters increaſed, and lifted vp the arke on high from the earth.
\v 18 For they ouerflowed excedingly: and filled al on the face of the earth: moreouer the arke fleeted vpon the waters.
\v 19 And the waters preuailed out of meaſure vpon the earth: and al the hiegh mountaines vnder the whole heauen were couered.
\v 20 Fiftene cubites higher was the water aboue the mountaines, which it couered.
\v 21 And al flesh was conſumed that moued vpon the earth, of foule, of cattle, of beaſts, and of al creepers, that creepe vpon the earth: al men,
\v 22 and al things, wherin there is breath of life on the earth, died. \x - \xt Sap. 10, 4.\x*
\v 23 \x - \xt Eccl. 39.\x* And he cleane deſtroied al ſubſtance, that was vpon the earth, from man euen to beaſt, as wel it that creepeth, as the foules of the ayre: and they were deſtroied from of the earth: but onlie Noe remained, and they that were with him in the arke. \ef - \fr 7.23 \fq But only Noe. \ft As there is not anie thing in al the old Teſtament, from the creation of the world til the coming of Chriſt, more notable, more admirable, or of greater importance, then this hiſtorie of the general floud; ſo was there nothing (though al, or moſt chanced to them in figure) ¹ ² that euer more aptly, more liuely, or more exactly prefigured Chriſt and his Church, with the reſt of al mankind, then did Noe and the arke, & the drowning of the reſt of the world in that deluge. VVhich S. Auguſtin declareth in many places, ³ but moſt eſpecially and of purpoſe in his twelfth booke againſt Fauſtus the Manichee, ftom the 14. chapter to the 22. and in his fifteenth booke of the citie of God, in the two laſt chapters: where he ſheweth at large both the certaintie of the hiſtorie, and that as certainely it was a figure of things in the new Teſtament, and withal the great congruitie betwen the figure & the things figured. ⁴ The ſame did Origen explicate (homil. 2. in Gen.) S. Gregorie (homil 12. in Ezech.) Rupertus (li. 4. comment. in Gen. c. 71. & ſequent.) and diuers other ancient Doctors, confirming their expoſitions by S. Peters teſtimonie, ſaying: In the arke \+it a fevv, that is eight ſoules\+it* (or perſons) \+it vvere ſaued\+it* (from drowning) \+it by vvater, vvhervnto Baptiſme being of the like forme novv ſaueth you alſo\+it*. And by our Sauiours wordes ſaying: ⁵ \+it As in the dayes of Noe, ſo shal alſo the comming of the ſonne of man be\+it*. In ſumme the Doctors teach, that Noe ſignifying \+it reſt\+it* was a figure of Chriſt, ⁶ the very \+it reſt\+it* of mans ſoule. VVhom who ſoeuer foloweth shal find reſt for their ſoules. The arke ſignifieth the Church, ⁷ the forme therof being ſix times ſo long as broad, and tenne times ſo long as hiegh, reſembleth the proportion of mans bodie, lying prone or proſtrate. The dore in the ſide repreſenteth the wound in Chriſts ſide, from whence flowed the holie Sacraments, by which the faithful enter into the Church, ⁸ and are ſanctified. The timber wherof the arke is made, & the water bearing it vp, ſignified the Croſſe of Chriſt and Baptiſme. \+it For as Noe\+it* (ſaith S. Auguſtin) ⁹ \+it vvith his, vvas deliuered by the vvater and the vvoode, ſo the familie of Chriſt, by Baptiſme ſigned vvith Chriſts Paſſion on the Croſſe\+it*. ¹⁰ Likewiſe the ſquarnes of the timber which both ſuſtayned the burden of al contayned in the arke, and reſiſted the boyſtrous waues of the floud beating without, did ſignifie ſuch men in the Church as be conſtant & ſtand firmly in al ſortes of tentations: eſpecially godlie & lerned Doctors and Paſtors, ¹¹ who by worde and example vphold and confirme the faithful people in al afflictions within, and withſtand and conuince al Heretikes, and other Infidels that oppugne the Church without. Againe the hiegher & lower rowmes with the midle chambers & third loftes, & other diſtinctions of cabinets, and partitions, and al ſortes of liuing creatures cleane and vncleane, receiued therin, did ſignifie the varieties of al ſtates & functions, ¹² and diuerſitie of maners and merites in the Church, in which are perſons of al degrees, Clergie and Laitie, Potentates, Princes, ſubiectes, good and euil. ¹³ The moſt ſtrong kind of glew called \+it bitumen\+it*, ſignified the permanent or euerlaſting ſtabilitie, ¹⁴ and vnſeparable conexion of the Church, by the grace and continual aſſiſtance of the Holie Ghoſt conſeruing the ſame. The conſummation of the arke in one cubite ſignified the vnitie of the ſame Church, which is one in al times, and places. ¹⁵Neither would God almightie haue manie arkes, for Noe and his ſonnes or other creatures, nor manie chiefe rulers (though he would that of them ſhould come manie Nations) but one only arke, and one chiefe gouernour therof, ¹⁶ and that al without the ſame ſhould corporally dye, to ſignifie that al which dye without the Church do perish, and are eternally damned. ¹⁷ wherupon S. Hierom, ¹⁸ amongſt other Fathers, ſheweth that al within the Church, that communicate with the Sea Apoſtolique (wherin S. Damaſus ſate then gouernour) are as thoſe in the arke of Noe, and al Schiſmatikes, Heretikes and other Infidels are in like caſe, with the reſt of the world, that were drowned with the floud. The end of the first age. \fq ¹ \ft \+it 1. Cor. 10\+it*. \fq ² \ft Al or most thinges in the old Testament be in figure of the new, & no figure more exact then the floud of Noe. \fq ³ \ft \+it Ep. 99. l. 5. de Baptis. c. 28. de vnitate Eccles. ca. 5. In Psal. 103. & 131. Ser. 69. de tempore\+it*. \fq ⁴ \ft How the Doctors applie the figure to the things figured \fq ⁵ \ft \+it 1. Pet. 3. Mat. 24. Luc. 17\+it*. \fq ⁶ \ft Noe signified Christ. \fq ⁷ \ft The arke the Church. \fq ⁸ \ft Entrance into the Church by Baptisme. \fq ⁹ \ft \+it li. 12. de Baptis. c. 14\+it*. \fq ¹⁰ \ft Vertue of Sacraments cometh from Christs Passion. \fq ¹¹ \ft Doctors and Pastores in the Church. \fq ¹² \ft Varietie of states and orders in the Church. \fq ¹³ \ft Good and euil in the Church. \fq ¹⁴ \ft Perpetuitie o the Church. \fq ¹⁵ \ft Vnitie of the Church. \fq ¹⁶ \ft One chiefe gouernour in the Church \fq ¹⁷ \ft No saluation out of the Church. \fq ¹⁸ \ft \+it Epist. 57. ad Damasum\+it*.\ef*
\v 24 \x - \xt 1. Pet. 3.\x* And the waters held on aboue the earth an hundred fiftie dayes.
\c 8
\cd The waters diminishing by litle and litle, 6. Noe ſendeth forth a crow, 8. after him a doue, thriſe: 18. laſtly goeth forth with al that were with him in the arke. 20. erecteth an Altar, and offereth Sacrifice. \f - \ft The ſecond Age of the world.\f*
\s1 A BRIEF REMONSTRANCE OF THE STATE OF THE CHURCH and face of Religion in the first Age of the world, from the creation to Noe's flood, the space of 1656. years.
\ip Here, according to our purpose mentioned before, we will briefly recite certain principal points of Religion, taught and observed in the first Age: In which the foundations of the true manner of serving God (that should be continued to the end of the world) were laid, and prospered in some, as appeareth in these seven first chapters of Genesis. But first of all, we shall in two words repeat (as it is clearly gathered in the same holy Scripture) the state of man before and immediately after his fall, being the subject to whom all this pertaineth.
\ip After therefore that God had created other things, both in heaven and earth, last of all he made \f - \ft Man made to God's image, and in happy state.\f* Man, to his own image and likeness, with understanding & freewill, therein like to Angels, and superior to all other creatures, and so made him Lord and Master of all earthly things. Neither were these the greatest benefits which God bestowed on man: for his divine goodness endued also this his reasonable creature with innocency & original justice, whereby all things were most rightly ordered within him and about him. His mind, will, and reason were obedient to God; \f - \ft Man obeyed God, and all earthly creatures obeyed man.\f* his senses & inferior part of his soul were subject to reason; his flesh and body obeyed the spirit; and all earthly creatures obeyed him. God also adorned man with excellent knowledge, both natural and supernatural. And albeit his body was of corruptible substance, yet the same, and all his posterity, if they had not sinned, should have been conserved, and without dying have been translated to everlasting life. Thus man was placed in Paradise, and Eve there made of a rib of \f - \ft Man placed in Paradise.\f* his side, to be his mate and unseparable companion, as man and wife joined in Marriage, with God's blessing for increase and multiplication. As appeareth in the two first chapters of this book.
\ip But \it God having made man right, he entangled himself\it* (as holy Scripture speaketh) \it with\it* \f - \ft Man fell by yielding to tentations.\f* \f - \ft \+it Eccl. 7. 30. Sap. 2. 24.\+it*\f* \it infinite questions\it*. For the devil envying man's felicity inveigled our mother Eve with questions and lies, and then by her, first seduced and deceived, allured also Adam to the transgression of God's commandment. And so they lost original justice, which Adam had received for himself and all mankind: and all proceeding from them by natural propagation are born the children of wrath, in original sin contracted from Adam, slaves of the devil, not only subject to temporal death, \f - \ft Original sin.\f* but also are excluded for ever from heavenly bliss and glory: except by Christ's redemption particularly applied, they be restored to grace and justice in this life.
\ip And touching Adam and Eve, whose sin was not original but actual, directly committed by \f - \ft Adam and Eve were penitent.\f* themselves, God's mercy so reclaimed them by new grace, that they despaired not (as Cain, and some others did afterwards) but with hope of remission were sorry and penitent, and accordingly received penance and redemption. For God \it brought Adam from his sin\it* (as holy writ testifieth) \f - \ft \+it Sap. 10.\+it*\f* and the same is collected of Eve, God shewing the like signs of his provident mercy towards them both, of which we shall by and by note some for example.
\ip Now let us see the more principal points of faith and Religion professed and observed by the Church of God before Noe's flood. First, they believed in one Eternal and Omnipotent God, who \f - \ft Faith in one God.\f* made the whole world and all things therein of nothing. Which is easily confessed of all that are not plain Atheists, and may be proved against them by reason. And therefore Adam and other Patriarchs could not err in this Article, nor others be ignorant thereof, except they were very wicked.
\ip The Mystery also of the Blessed Trinity, three Divine Persons in one God, though far above \f - \ft The Blessed Trinity.\f* the reach of man's reason, yet was believed more expressly by some, more implied by others, and conserved from Age to Age by tradition, at least amongst the chief heads and leaders. Whereupon Moyses afterwards insinuated the same great Mystery by diverse words and phrases, writing of God and his works. The two words \it God created\it* if they be rightly considered import so much. For the word \it Elohim, God\it*, in the plural number, signifieth plurality of Persons (for many Gods it cannot signify, seeing there is but one God) and the verb \it bara, created\it*, in the singular number signifieth one God in nature and substance, albeit three Persons. For whatsoever God doth in creatures, is the work of the whole Trinity: though holy Scriptures do oftentimes appropriate some work to one Divine Person, some to another; which also proveth distinction of Persons in God. \f - \ft Though the B. Trinity work jointly in all creatures, yet diverse works are attributed to distinct Persons.\f* So the words \it God created heaven and earth\it* signify the \it Father\it*, to whom \it power\it* is attributed: \it In\it* \f - \ft \+it Gen. 1.\+it*\f* \it the beginning\it*, signify \it the son\it*, to whom \it wisdom\it* is appropriated: and the words, \it The Spirit of God moved over the waters\it*, signify \it the Holy Ghost\it*, by whose bountiful \it goodness\it*, the waters were made fruitful. Likewise God's own words: \it Let us make man\it* signify the plurality of Persons, and \it Image and likeness\it* in the singular number, signify one God.
\ip Men also knew by faith many things pertaining to themselves. As that the body was made of the \f - \ft By faith the state of man past & present was known.\f* slime of the earth: the soul not produced of any thing formerly existing, but created immediately of nothing, and naturally immortal: that the soul of Adam was endued with grace and justice: that he fell from the happy state by yielding to tentation, and breaking God's commandment of abstinence: that for the same sin Adam and Eve were cast forth of Paradise, and all mankind subject to death, and other calamities.
\ip For remedy against sin, & restoration to grace, they believed in \it Christ\it* promised to be born of \f - \ft Belief in Christ to come.\f* the woman's seed, who by his death should conquer the wicked serpent, deliver man from captivity, and restore him to spiritual life. And this is the cause of the perpetual enmity between the woman (especially the most Blessed Virgin-Mother, of whom Christ took flesh) and the serpent, and between her seed, the spiritual children of Christ, and the serpent's seed, the whole company of the wicked. Of this battle and conquest Thargum Hierosolimitanum thus speaketh: \it There shall be remedy and health to the children of women, but to thee, o serpent, there shall be\it* \f - \ft Remedy for men, but none for Angels that fell. Heb. 2. 16.\f* \it no medicine, yea they shall tread thee under their feet, in the latter days, by the power of Christ their King\it*. Likewise God's familiar conversation with diverse men in man's shape (Gen. 2. 3. 4. 6. and 7.) was a sign of Christ's incarnation. And the Sacrifices immolated did prefigurate his death, in respect whereof it is said in the Apocalypse, \it The Lamb was slain from the beginning of the\it* \f - \ft \+it Apo. 13, 8.\+it*\f* \it world\it*. But more expressly S. Paul testifieth, that Abel, Enoch, and Noe believed in Christ, naming them for example of the first Age, and others of other times, and in the end concludeth, that many more \it being approved by the same faith, received not the promise\it* (to wit in their lifetime) \it God providing that they without others\it* (of the new Law) \it should not be consummate\it*, that is, not \f - \ft None admitted into heaven before Christ.\f* \f - \ft \+it Heb. 11.\+it*\f* admitted into heavenly joys & fruition of God, until the way of eternal glory were opened by our Lord's Passion and Ascension.
\ip Neither did the true servants of God in those first days only believe in heart, but they also \f - \ft External Sacrifice.\f* professed their faith & Religion by external Rites, namely in offering of Sacrifice (the most special homage & service to God) which is clearly testified, chap. 4. as well bloody in figure of Christ's Passion, as unbloody in figure of the holy Eucharist. Also the accepting of the one rightly offered by Abel, & rejecting the other not done sincerely by Cain, was declared by external signs, which Cain disdaining and envying his brother's good work, knowing his own to be nought, of mere malice killed his brother.
\ip Besides Sacrifice they had also other Rites in public Assemblies, praying and invocating the name \f - \ft Public prayer with other Rites.\f* of our Lord, in more solemn manner, from Enos' time and so forward, according to that is recorded of him in the end of the fourth chapter. For doubtless Adam, Abel, and Seth did also pray and call upon God, and therefore it was some addition or increase of solemnity in the service of God, which is referred to Enos.
\ip They had moreover other ceremonies: of the seventh day particularly blessed and \f - \ft Ceremonial observations.\f* \f - \ft \+it Gen. 2, 3.\+it*\f* sanctified by God, kept holy by Adam and other Patriarchs, as Abben Ezra witnesseth in his \f - \ft Feasts.\f* commentaries upon the ten commandments: of abstaining from meats, for it seemeth the \f - \ft Abstinence.\f* \f - \ft \+it Gen. 20. Gen. 9.\+it*\f* more godly sort did eat no flesh before the flood, which was after permitted: observation \f - \ft Clean & unclean.\f* \f - \ft \+it Gen. 7, 2.\+it*\f* of clean and unclean beasts for Sacrifice: of peculiar places dedicated to religious uses, \f - \ft Places dedicated to prayer.\f* \f - \ft \+it Gen. 4, 26.\+it*\f* where people met together to pray. Likewise diverse other things in the first Age were figures of Christ's Sacraments : the Spirit of God giving power to the waters (as Tertullian, S. \f - \ft Figures of Christ's Sacraments.\f* \f - \ft \+it Gen. 1.\+it*\f*
\ip Hierom, and others expound it) and the flood of Noe, by S. Peter's testimony, were figures of Baptism . Marriage instituted in Paradise, is the very pattern of holy \f - \ft Baptism.\f* \f - \ft \+it 1. Pet. 3.\+it*\f* \f - \ft Marriage.\f* \f - \ft \+it Mat. 19.\+it*\f* Matrimony, a Sacrament in the Church of Christ, where one man and one wife are only lawful, and not more at once in any wise, Christ reforming that which in Moyses' law was tolerated (for hardness of men's hearts, and for avoiding murder, to put away one wife, and take another) to this first institution as it was in the beginning, \it two in one flesh\it*, not three nor more. The repentance of Adam and Eve was a perfect and examplar figure of the Sacrament of Penance . First, \f - \ft Penance.\f* \f - \ft \+it Gen. 2, 24.\+it*\f* they were ashamed, covering their nakedness, and hiding themselves, which shewed their grief and sorrow for the sin committed . Secondly they confessed their fault , and by \f - \ft Contrition. conserved.\f* \f - \ft \+it Gen. 3.\+it*\f* \f - \ft confession.\f* what means it happened. For God examining Adam, he answered truly and simply saying: \it The woman which thou gavest me, to be my companion, gave me of the tree, and I did eat\it*. Likewise Eve confessed sincerely, saying: \it The serpent deceived me, and I did eat\it*. Thirdly, God gave them \f - \ft Satisfaction.\f* penance (besides death before threatened and other penalties annexed) that Eve \it should in pain and travail bring forth her children\it*; and \it Adam should eat his bread in the sweat of his face\it*, and withal cast them forth of Paradise, but not forth of his favour, as appeared by his making them garments of skins, granting them & their posterity the rest of the earth to live and labour in, especially to serve him & do penance, with admonition to remember, that of dust man was made, and into dust he shall return . All which were signs of love, and that finally he would bring them and many \f - \ft From hence is taken the ceremony of ashes, on Ashwednesday.\f* more to eternal salvation.
\ip The first-born and heads of families were Priests at the time of the law of nature, until the law \f - \ft Priesthood.\f* being changed, God took Priests only of the stock of Aaron, and the rest of the Levites to assist them in that function: \it Aaron & his sons thou shalt appoint, saith our Lord, over the service of\it* \f - \ft \+it Num. 3. 10, 12. 45.\+it*\f* \it Priesthood, for I have taken the Levites of the children of Israel for every first-born\it*. And S. Paul teacheth, that changing of Priesthood and changing of the law go always together, shewing \f - \ft Priesthood & Law stand & change together.\f* evidently that every lawful community or common-wealth under God, hath external Priesthood. So that if there had been no distinct Order of external Priesthood in the law of nature, or now were none in the law of grace (as Protestants say there is not) there were no law at all. See more of \f - \ft \+it Luther lib. de abroganda Missa.\+it*\f* this point in the Annot. chap. 7. ad Hebre. Here we only observe that Abel, Seth, Enos, and other Patriarchs were Priests, & exercised priestly functions: yea Cain also was a Priest (though a bad one) and offered Sacrifice.
\ip But external offices or ministry, without a well disposed mind and sincere virtues producing \it Good works\it*, did never justify any man. And therefore Cain's Sacrifice , offered with a perverse \f - \ft Good works necessary.\f* \f - \ft \+it Gen. 4.\+it*\f* mind, was not respected by God, as Abel's was: whereupon he becoming worse and more malicious, God sharply reproved his anger and envy, conceived without just cause, saying: \it If thou doest well, shalt thou not receive again: but if thou doest ill, shall not thy sin forthwith be present at the door?\it* clearly shewing that every one shall receive according to his works.
\ip This place also evidently sheweth \it Freewill\it*, yea in a wicked man. For this expostulation had \f - \ft Freewill.\f* never been uttered by our most reasonable Lord and Master, if Cain had been deprived of freewill. For he might have excused himself, and must needs have been holden excused, if he had been forced to do as he did. But God charged him as inexcusable, and as one that knew, or ought to know, that he had freewill. And doth further inculcate that he had, and should have power and freewill over his concupiscence, to correct the same if he would, saying: \it The lust thereof shall be under thee, and thou shalt have dominion over it\it*. So that no sinner, be he never so wicked, much less a just man, lacketh freewill. Yet Luther abhorreth the very word, and Calvin wished it out \f - \ft \+it Li. de servo arbit. li. 2. Instit. ca. 2. par. 8. Gen. 3.\+it*\f* of the world.
\ip \it Temporal punishment\it* is proved to be due for sin remitted, by that both death and other penalties \f - \ft Temporal pain due for sin remitted.\f* are inflicted by God's justice upon men, after justification, and by the particular punishments laid upon Adam and Eve, confessing their faults.
\ip \it Purgatory\it* is also proved by the same justice of God. For when any dieth penitent, and yet have \f - \ft Purgatory.\f* not made full satisfaction, they must suffer for that remaineth after death, and be purged, before they can enter into rest. Which remnant of debt our B. Saviour calleth \it The last farthing\it*, and \f - \ft \+it Mat. 5.\+it*\f* saith, it must be payed. The Jews also at this day hold the doctrine of Purgatory by tradition. And consequently they \it Pray for souls departed\it*, not only to God, but also to the ancient Patriarchs \f - \ft Prayer for the dead.\f* (which likewise sheweth \it Invocation of Saints\it* ) in these words: \it Ye fathers which sleep in\it* \f - \ft And to Saints.\f* \f - \ft \+it Officio pro defunctis.\+it*\f* \it Hebron, open to him the gates of Eden\it*, that is of Paradise, which was planted in Eden. And Hebron is the place where Adam was buried, and his sepulcher religiously conserved in the time of \f - \ft Sepulchers of Patriarchs religiously\f* Iosue , above 1500 years after his death. The same is the place which Abraham bought, and \f - \ft \+it Iosue. 14. Gen. 23.\+it*\f* there buried Sara: where also himself, and Isaac, and Jacob were buried: and to which finally the bodies of the twelve sons of Jacob were translated from Sichem, as Iosephus writeth . And \f - \ft \+it l. 2. Antiquit.\+it*\f* Sichem also was specially honoured, because such persons had been buried there, as S. Hierome witnesseth of his own knowledge in his time. \f - \ft \+it Epist. ad Pamach.\+it*\f*
\ip Again by religious care of burying the dead in this first age, \it Enoch\it* was more certainly known \f - \ft Enoch translated alive.\f* to be \it Translated\it* alive, and not to be dead. For the seventy Interpreters, and S. Paul say \it He was not found\it*, which importeth that they sought diligently for him, and that his body could not be \f - \ft \+it Gen. 5. Heb. 11.\+it*\f* found, for God translated him.
\ip By all which we see mutual offices, and communion of good works amongst good men alive and dead, which is called \it Communion of Saints\it*. And herein \it Angels\it* lacked not their offices. \f - \ft Communion of Saints.\f* \f - \ft Ministry of Angels.\f*
\ip For God set Cherubins to keep the gate of Paradise, that neither man should enter, being justly \f - \ft \+it Gen. 3. 24.\+it*\f* expelled for sin, nor devils, as S. Augustin noteth , lest they should take fruit of the tree of life, \f - \ft \+it li. 11. de Gen. ad lit. c. 40.\+it*\f* and giving it to men, allure them to more sin. And now \it Saints\it* being exalted to Angels' glory, have like honourable offices towards other men , as Angels have. Yea the blood of Abel unjustly \f - \ft Honour of Saints.\f* shed by Cain , and justly to be revenged by God, sheweth the peculiar honour, which God \f - \ft \+it Gen. 4.\+it*\f* bestoweth upon his Saints, for their virtues and merits in this life, & especially in their death. For \it Precious in the sight of our Lord, is the death of his Saints\it*. \f - \ft \+it Psal. 115.\+it*\f*
\ip Hence also is proved, that seeing in this life the good are afflicted, and the bad oftentimes prosper temporally, there must needs be another \it Court\it* of exact Justice, and another \it Reckoning day\it*, \f - \ft General Judgement.\f* wherein every one shall receive, according as they have done good or evil. Which was sufficiently intimated by God's discussing, and manifesting Abel's and Cain's deserts, which were hidden before, and in part rewarding them accordingly, yet reserving the full reward of the one, and punishment of the other to the next world. Of the \it Judge\it* and his sentence Enoch (alleged by S. \f - \ft Judge of the world.\f* Jude the Apostle) prophesied clearly, saying: \it Behold our Lord cometh in his holy thousands, to\it* \f - \ft \+it Epis Iudae. v. 14.\+it*\f* \it do judgement against all, and to reprove all the impious of all the works of their impiety, whereby they have done impiously, and of all the hard things which impious sinners have spoken against him\it*. Thus holy Enoch preached touching the wicked, which thought there was no Judgement to come, nor Judge to be feared.
\ip At this Judgment all shall appear in body and soul returning to life. For that \it All men shall rise\it* \f - \ft Resurrection.\f* from death is proved by the immortality of man's soul, which God did not make nor produce of corruptible matter, but immediately \it breathed into his face the breath of life, and man became a living soul\it*. So the soul being immortal, and having a natural inclination to the body, man's natural \f - \ft \+it Gen. 2, 7.\+it*\f* perfection requireth the conjunction of body and soul. For neither soul nor body separated is a man, but both joined in one subsistence are a man, in so much that mankind should perish, except the bodies shall rise again, and live with the soul. And then shall the bodies be qualified according to the state of the souls, happy or miserable for ever.
\ip Of \it Eternal life\it* the translation of Enoch is a figure. For seeing God preserveth his \f - \ft Everlasting life.\f* \f - \ft \+it Gen. 5.\+it*\f* corruptible body so long from death and infirmity, it is a token and manifest sign, that by the same power of God the bodies of men shall at the last day, after that all men are once dead, rise again, and remain with the souls forever: The good in \it Eternal joy\it*, the wicked in \it Eternal pain\it*. \f - \ft The blessed in eternal joy.\f* \f - \ft The wicked in endless pain.\f*
\ip Both signified by the custody of the gate of Paradise by Angels ; who for ever keep out those, \f - \ft \+it Gen. 3, 24.\+it*\f* that are still defiled with sin, and so they depart into fire everlasting, and admit the innocent and just into the Kingdom of heaven, which is everlasting joy and perfect felicity.
\ip Thus we see the face and brief sum of Religion in the beginning of the world, till the flood: and the state of the Church, which was always \it Visible\it*, consisting of men good and bad, with a \f - \ft Church ever visible.\f* continual \it Succession\it* of Rulers , as well spiritual as temporal. For the first-born were both \f - \ft Succession of Patriarchs.\f* Priests and Princes in every family. And amongst the same one ever chief of all . From which \f - \ft One supreme head of the Church.\f* rank Cain was excluded, or rather excluded himself, by \it Going forth from the face of our Lord\it*. \f - \ft \+it Gen. 4, 16.\+it*\f* Whereupon holy Moyses reciteth this Monarchical succession of one chief and \it Supreme Head\it*, \f - \ft \+it Gen. 5.\+it*\f* from \it Adam\it* by the line of \it Seth, Enos, Cainan, Malaleel, Jared, Enoch, Mathusala, Lamech\it*, and \it Noe\it*. Nevertheless he setteth down also the progeny of Cain , the first beginner of a worldly, \f - \ft \+it Gen. 4, 17.\+it*\f* schismatical, and heretical conventicle , opposite to the City of God. He denied God's \f - \ft Cain's negative doctrine.\f* \f - \ft \+it S. Aug. l. de Pastore. c. 8. & 20.\+it*\f* providence (as Thargum Hierosolimitanum testifieth) protesting to Abel, \it That there was no Justice nor Judge, nor other world than this, no reward for virtue, nor punishment for sin, and so desperately he killed Abel\it*. Of these negative principles proceeded other like detestable opinions, and most wicked life, savage and barbarous cruelty, and all kind of impiety. And in process of time albeit many remained in true faith, and unity of the Church , yet by conversation with such \f - \ft True faith still remained in many.\f* miscreants, especially by occasion of Marriages between the faithful and infidels, almost the whole world was corrupted in manners. But Noe was just and perfect . In punishment \f - \ft Some also just and perfect.\f* \f - \ft \+it Gen. 6.\+it*\f* therefore of so great and enormous sins, God sent the general flood, whereby all Cain's progeny, and all other infidels were wholly destroyed and extinguished , and the true Church notably \f - \ft Interruption of heretical Synagogues.\f* purged; only just Noe and his family reserved. By whom the same true Church was continued , and the world again replenished with men. \f - \ft Continuance of the Church.\f*
\p
\v 1 AND \f 1 \fr 8.1 \ft The third part of this book. Of the new increaſe & multiplication of the world.\f* God remembred Noe, and al the beaſts, and al the cattle, which were with him in the arke, and brought a winde vpon the earth, and the waters decreaſed.
\v 2 And the fountaines of the depth, and the floud gates of heauen, were shut vp: and the rayne from heauen was ſtayd.
\v 3 And the waters returned from the earth going & comming: and they begane to decreaſe after a hundred fiftie dayes.
\v 4 And the arke reſted the ſeauenth moneth, the ſeauen & twentith day of the moneth vpon the mountaines of Armenia.
\v 5 But the waters for al that were going and decreaſing vntil the tenth moneth: for in the tenth moneth, the firſt day of the moneth, the topps of the mountaines appeared.
\v 6 And after that fourtie dayes were paſſed, Noe opening the windowe of the arke, which he had made, let forth a crowe:
\v 7 which went forth, and did \f a \fr 8.7 \ft The crowe returned not into the arke, but (as appeareth by the Hebrew text) going and returning reſted vpon the arke.\f* not returne, til the waters were dried vpon the earth.
\v 8 He ſent forth alſo a doue after him, to ſee if the waters were ceaſed yet vpon the face of the earth.
\v 9 Which finding not where her foote might reſt, returned to him into the arke: for the waters were vpon the whole earth: and he ſtretched forth his hand, and caught her, and brought her into the arke.
\v 10 And hauing expected yet ſeauen moe dayes, againe he let forth a doue out of the arke.
\v 11 But she came to him at euentide, carrying a bough of an oliue tree, that had greene leaues in her mouth. Noe therfore vnderſtood that the waters were ceaſed vpon the earth.
\v 12 And he expected yet neuertheles other ſeauen dayes: and he ſent forth a doue, which returned not any more vnto him.
\v 13 Therfore in the ſixt hundred and one yeare, the firſt moneth, the firſt day of the moneth the waters were cleane diminished vpon the earth: and Noe opening the roofe of the arke, looked, and ſawe that the face of the earth was dried.
\v 14 In \f b \fr 8.14 \ft They entred into the arke the 17. day, the ſecond moneth of the other yeare: ſo they remained there 12. monethes and tenne dayes.\f* the ſecond moneth, the ſeuen & twentyth day of the moneth the earth was dried.
\v 15 And God ſpake to Noe, ſaying:
\v 16 Goe forth of the arke, thou & thy wife, thy ſonnes and the wiues of thy ſonnes with thee.
\v 17 Al cattle, that are with thee of al flesh, as wel in foules, as in beaſtes, & al creepers, that creepe vpon the earth, bring out with thee, & goe yee vpon the earth: increaſe and multiplie vpon it.
\v 18 Noe therfore went forth, and his ſonnes: his wife, and the wiues of his ſonnes with him.
\v 19 Yea and al cattle, beaſtes, and creepers that crepe vpon the earth, according to their kinde, went forth out of the arke.
\v 20 And Noe built an Altar to our Lord: and taking of al cattle and foules that were cleane, offered Holocauſts vpon the Altar. \ef - \fr 8.20 \fq Built an Altar. \ft ¹ Noe without expreſs commandment, and without delay, offereth Sacrifice to God, for the benefite receiued in his and his families conſeruation, with the other liuing creatures, in that general deluge of the world, \+it VVel knowing\+it* (ſaith S. Ambroſe) ² \+it that to be true thankſgiving, which is preſented, not commanded\+it* ³ \+it : therefore he made no delay\+it* ⁴ \+it . For the virtue of a grateful mind excludeth doubtful deliberation, and he that expecteth til the debt of thanks be exacted, is an ungrateful perſon.\+it* ⁵ For more ſolemnitie, he dedicated an apt and permanent place, for this peculiar diuine ſervice, \+it building an Altar to our Lord.\+it* The Hebrew word \+it Mizbeach\+it* (of the verbe \+it Zabach\+it*, to kil, or make ſacrifice) and the Greeke \+it Thyſiaſterion\+it*, ſignifie an Altar to ſacrifice on ⁶, not a common table for meate. He offered of the ⁷ cleane and beſt things: becauſe pure and deuout Sacrifice is due to God. Moreouer, it was large and bountiful ⁸, for he offered of all the kinds of cleane beaſts and fowls. Finally, he offered them in \+it Holocauſts\+it* ⁹ \+it ,\+it* where all was burned and conſumed in the honour of God. ¹⁰ How grateful all this was to God, Moyſes ſignifieth ſaying: \+it Our Lord ſmelled a ſvveete ſauour:\+it* not that either anie ſweete corporal ſauour could of it-ſelfe delight God, who is the moſt ſpiritual ſubſtance, or that the burning of flesh, bones, and bowels of beaſts could yeld ſweete ſauour; but the deuout mind declared by ſuch external dutie greatly pleaſed God. For God requireth both, but ſpecially a ſincere heart. As not only diuine Scriptures and holie Fathers ¹¹, but alſo moral Philoſophers teach vs. \+it It were a grievous thing\+it* (ſaith Plato writing of ſacrifices) \+it If God had reſpect rather to the gifts and ſacrifices of men, then to their mind.\+it* lib. peri tes proſeuches. \fq ¹ \ft Noes sacrifice manie wayes commendable. \fq ² \ft \+it li. de Noe & Arca c. 22\+it*. \fq ³ \ft 1. Voluntary. \fq ⁴ \ft 2. Speedy. \fq ⁵ \ft 3. Solemn. \fq ⁶ \ft \+it Heb. 13, 10\+it*. \fq ⁷ \ft 4. Pure. \fq ⁸ \ft 5. Bountiful. \fq ⁹ \ft 6. Holocaust. \fq ¹⁰ \ft Sacrifice is pleasant to God: not for the external things but for the sincere mind. \fq ¹¹ \ft \+it Leuit. 1. Psal. 50. Isaie 1. Amos. 5. Mal. 1. Iust Mar. li 3. q. 4. S. Hiero. Q. Heb\+it*.\ef*
\v 21 And our Lord ſmelled a ſweete ſauour, and ſaid: I wil no more curſe the earth for men: for the ſenſe and cogitation of mans hart are prone to euil from their youth: I wil no more therfore ſtrike euerie liuing ſoule as I haue done.
\v 22 Al the dayes of the earth, \f c \fr 8.22 \ft In the whole yeare of the floud was no ſowing nor reaping, nor pleaſant varietie of times, but al deſolate & miſerable: hence forth God promiſeth more ſeaſonable times S. Amb. li. de Noe & Arca. c. 23.\f* ſeedtime and harueſt, cold and heate, ſommer and winter, night and day ſhal not reſt.
\c 9
\cd God reneweth the bleſſing of multiplication, 3 alloweth the eating of flesh, but not of bloud. 8. promiſeth neuer againe to deſtroy the world by water 22. Cham ſaw, and reported his fathers nakednes, which Sem and Iapheth couered. 24. For which he his curſed, and they are bleſſed.
\p
\v 1 AND God bleſſed Noe and his ſonnes. And he ſaid to them: \f a \fr 9.1 \ft Of this commandment, or rather bleſſing ſee the Annotations. chap. 1. v. 28.\f* Increaſe, & multiplie, and replenish the earth.
\v 2 And your terror and dread be it vpon al the beaſts of the earth, and vpon al the foules of the ayre, with al that moue vpon the earth: al the fishes of the ſea are deliuered to your hand.
\v 3 And al that moueth, and liueth shal be yours for meat: euen as the grene herbes haue I deliuered al to you. \ef - \fr 9.3 \fq Al that moueth. \ft ¹ ² S. Iuſtinus Martyr, S. Chriſoſtom, and other ancient Doctors proue, that flesh was lawful to be eaten before the floud: but being not neceſſarie, becauſe men were ſtronger, and other things alſo of more force, the better ſorte which were of Seths race abſtained from it. But after the floud flesh being more neceſſarie, God altereth that cuſtome of abſtinence, with this limitation and commandment, that they ſhal not eate bloud. \fq ¹ \ft Voluntarie abstinence without commandment. \fq ² \ft \+it Ad. q. 119. Ho. 27. in Gen\+it*.\ef*
\v 4 Sauing that flesh with bloud you shal not eate. \x - \xt Levi. 17.\x* \ef - \fr 9.4 \fq Flesh vvith bloud. \ft ¹ Though this poſitiue precept, of not eating bloud, ſerued wel to make men more abhorre manſlaughter (which is forbid by the law of nature, and the reuenge therof here and in other places ſeuerely threatned) yet it was ſpecially geuen both immediatly after the floud, and in the law of Moyſes ² (with manie the like) to exerciſe men in obedience. And the ſame was renewed, for a time, by the Apoſtles, ³to appeaſe a controuerſie in the primitiue Church. For that the Iewes conuerted to Chriſt, hauing benne long accuſtomed to this obſeruation, could not indure to ſee it broken, by them ſelues, or other Chriſtians, and being no great burden, for the Gentiles, it was decreed that al ſhould kepe it. And ſo peace was made. Neuertheles it was abolished when the cauſe ceaſed. As S. Auguſtin ⁴ declareth againſt Fauſtus the Manichee. Such is the authoritie of the Church to decree, and againe to diſanul an obſeruation of a thing of it ſelfe indifferent. \fq ¹ \ft Abstinence from bloud some times commanded not alwayes. \fq ² \ft \+it Levi. 17.\+it* \fq ³ \ft \+it Act. 15\+it*. \fq ⁴ \ft \+it l. 32. c. 13\+it*.\ef*
\v 5 For I wil require the bloud of your ſoules at the hands of al beaſts: and at the hand of man, at the hand of eech man, and of his brother, wil I require the ſoule of man.
\v 6 Who ſoeuer shal ſheed mans bloud, his bloud shal be shed: for to the image of God man was made.
\v 7 But increaſe you and multiplie, and goe vpon the earth, and fil it.
\v 8 Thus alſo ſaid God to Noe, and to his ſonnes with him:
\v 9 Behold I wil eſtablish my couenant with you, and with your ſeede after you:
\v 10 and with euerie liuing ſoule, that is with you, as wel in al foules as in cattle & beaſts of the earth that are come forth out of the arke, and in al beaſts of the earth.
\v 11 I wil eſtablish my couenant with you, and al flesh ſhal be no more deſtroyed with the waters of a floud, neither shal there be from henceforth a floud to waſt the earth.
\v 12 And God ſaid: This is the ſigne of the couenant which I geue betwen me and you, and betwen euerie liuing ſoule, that is with you, for perpetual generations:
\v 13 \f b \fr 9.13 \ft The rainbow was before, but was not a ſigne, as God ſaith henceforth it should be, for men to remember his promiſe. \+it Seuerianus in Gen. S. Tho. Quodlib. 3. a. 30\+it*.\f* my bowe wil I ſet in the clouds, and it shal be the ſigne of a couenant betwen me and betwen the earth.
\v 14 And when I shal couer the element with cloudes, my bowe shal appeare in the cloudes:
\v 15 and I shal remember my couenant with you, and with euerie liuing ſoule that beareth flesh: and there ſhal no more be waters of a floud, to diſtroy al flesh.
\v 16 And my bowe shal be in the cloudes, and I shal ſee it, and I shal remember the euerlaſting couenant, that was made betwen God and euerie liuing ſoule of al flesh which is vpon the earth.
\v 17 And God ſaid to Noe: This shal be the ſigne of the couenant, which I eſtablished, betwen me & al flesh of the earth.
\v 18 The ſonnes therfore of Noe, that came out of the arke, were Sem, Cham, and Iaphet: and Cham he is the father of Chanaan.
\v 19 Theſe three are the ſonnes of Noe: and \f c \fr 9.19 \ft By this it is clere that Noe had no more children after the floud. \+it S. Chriſoſt. ho. 29. in Gen\+it*.\f* of theſe was al mankind ſpred ouer the whole earth.
\v 20 And Noe a huſbandman began to til the grounde, and planted a vineyard.
\v 21 And drinking of the wine was made drunke, and naked in his tabernacle. \ef - \fr 9.21 \fq Drunke. \ft ¹ Noe ſinned not, by the common iudgement of ancient Fathers, ²in that he was ouercome with wine, becauſe he knew not the force therof, hauing drunck only water al his life before. But this external rebuke, and worldlie diſgrace happened to Noe, in figure of Chriſt naked on the Croſſe. ³As S. Cyprian (Epiſt. 63. ad Cœcilium) S. Auguſtin (li. 16. de ciuit. c. 2. & li. 12. contra Fauſtum Manicheum. c. 23. & 24.) Eucherius, Rupertus and others (vpon this place) do teach. ⁴ And likwiſe that Sem and Iapheth were a figure of the Church conſiſting of Iewes and Gentiles, and Cham of Heretikes, and other Infidels, that deride the infirmities, which our Sauiour ſuſtayned. \+it Goe to now\+it* (yee Manichees, ſaith S. Auguſtin) ⁵ \+it obiect calumnies to the ancient holie Scriptures, doe ſo, yee children of Cham, to whom naked flesh ſemeth vile, by which your ſelues were begotten. For neither could yee by anie meanes haue benne called Chriſtians, except Chriſts as he was fortold by the Prophetes, had come into the world, had drunck that cuppe of his vinyard, which could not paſſe from him, had ſlept in his paſſion, as in drunkennes of folie (which is wiſer then men) and ſo the infirmitie of mortal fleſh (which is ſtronger then men) had become naked, by the ſecret counſel of God, which infirmitie vnles the \+sc WORD OF GOD\+sc* had taken vpon him, the very name Chriſtian, wherof yee alſo glorie, had not benne at al in the earth\+it*. \fq ¹ \ft Noe sinned not in drunkennes: \fq ² \ft \+it S. Chrisost. ho. 29. S. Theo. q. 65. S. Am. c. 30. de Noe & Arca.\+it* \fq ³ \ft Noe a figure of Christ. \fq ⁴ \ft Sem and Iaphet a figure of the Church, Cham of Infidels. \fq ⁵ \ft \+it Li. 12. c. 24. cont. Faust\+it*.\ef*
\v 22 Which when Cham the father of Chanaan, had ſeene, to wit that his fathers priuities were bare, he told it to his two bretheren abroad.
\v 23 But in dede Sem and Iapheth put a cloake vpon their shoulders, and going backward, couered the priuities of their father: and their faces were turned away, and they ſawe not their fathers priuities.
\v 24 And Noe awaking from the wine, when he had learned what his younger ſonne had done to him,
\v 25 he ſaid: Curſed be Chanaan, a ſeruant of ſeruantes shal he be vnto his bretheren. \ef - \fr 9.25 \fq Curſed be Chanaan. \ft ¹ VVhy Chanaan the ſonne is curſed, and not his father Cham, diuers yeld diuers reaſons. ² S. Theodoretus reporteth out of the Hebrew Doctors, that Chanaan a boy firſt ſaw his grandfather's nakednes, and told the ſame to his father, and ſo they both derided that they should haue couered, Chams other ſons not offending: and therefore not his whole progenie but only Chanaan & his poſteritie were here curſed by Noe. ³ S. Chriſoſtome ſuppoſeth that for ſo much as God had bleſſed Noe & his three ſons coming forth of the Arke, he could not preſume to curſe anie of thoſe, whom God had bleſſed, therefore curſed Chanaan who in wickednes was like to his father. S. Gregorie bringeth this for example of wicked men, eſcaping punishment in this life, and are punished in the next, and in their poſteritie folowing their vices ⁴. \+it What meaneth it\+it* (ſaith he) ⁵ \+it that Cham ſinning, Chanaan his ſonne had ſentence of reuenge, but that the ſins of the reprobate proſper here in this life vnreuenged, and are punished aftervvards?\+it* And clear it is that Chanaans moſt wicked poſteritie were ſubdued in the end, and moſt of them deſtroied by the children of Iſrael ⁶ (who were of Sem) vnder the conduct of Joſue, according to Noe's prophetical bleſſing of Sem, and curſing of Chanaan. \fq ¹ \ft VVhy Chanaan is cursed rather then Cham. \fq ² \ft \+it q. 57. in Gen\+it*. \fq ³ \ft \+it Ho. 29\+it*. \fq ⁴ \ft Sinnes punished in the posteritie. \fq ⁵ \ft \+it Li. 26. Moral. c. 18\+it*. \fq ⁶ \ft The effect of blessing and cursing.\ef*
\v 26 And he ſaid: Bleſſed be the Lord God of Sem, Chanaan be his ſeruant. \x - \xt Apoc. 13, 8.\x*
\v 27 God enlarge Iapheth, and dwel he in the tabernacles of Sem, and Chanaan be his ſeruant. \ef - \fr 9.27 \fq God enlarge. \ft ¹ This bleſſing of Iapheth was ² literaly fulfilled, when (according to his name, which ſignifieth Latitude or enlargement) his plentiful iſſue poſſeſſed moſt ample countries, both in the Continent, and Ilandes. ³ But myſtically it had effect (As S. Hierom. S. Auguſtin. Rupertus and others expound it) when the Apoſtles, being Iewes of the race of Sem, firſt builded the Chriſtian Church, wherin the moſt part of that nation refuſed to dwel, contemning Chriſts Goſpel & grace, and the fulnes of Gentiles entred in, and were made inheriters. Finally Chanaan is ſeruant to both Sem and Iapheth, in that Heretikes being vnder the Iuriſdiction of the Church, geathered of Iewes and Gentiles, ⁴ ⁵ ſerue to ſtur vp Catholiques diligence to more exact knowledge of al truth; and their patience to more merite and glorie. \fq ¹ \ft Iapheths blessing. \fq ² \ft Literal. \fq ³ \ft Mystical. \fq ⁴ \ft How Heretikes serue Catholiques. \fq ⁵ \ft \+it Act. 13. Rom. 11. Ephes. 2. Tradit Hebra. Li. 16. ciuit. c. 2. Li 12. c. 24. cont Eaust. Li 4. c. 38. in Gen\+it*.\ef*
\v 28 And Noe liued after the floud three hundred fiftie yeares. \x - \xt Heb. 11.\x*
\v 29 And al his dayes were in the whole nyne hundred fiftie yeares: and he died.
\c 10
\cd The genealogie of Noes children, by whom the world was increaſed againe, after the floud.
\p
\v 1 THESE are the generations of the ſonnes of Noe, Sem, Cham, and Iapheth: and children were borne to them after the floud.
\v 2 The children of Iapheth: Gomer, and Magog, and Madai, and Iauan, and Thubal, and Moſoch, and Thiras.
\v 3 Moreouer the children of Gomer: Aſcenez and Riphath and Thogorma.
\v 4 And the ſonnes of Iauan: Eliſa and Tharſis, the Cetims and the Dodanims.
\v 5 Of theſe were diuided the Iles of Nations in their countries, ech one according to his tongue and their families in their nations.
\v 6 And the ſonnes of Cham: Chus, and Meſraim, and Phut, and Chanaan.
\v 7 And the ſonnes of Chus: Saba, and Heuila, & Sabatha, and Regma, & Sabathaca. The ſonnes of Regma: Saba, and Dadan.
\v 8 Moreouer Chus begat Nemrod: he began to be mightie in the earth, \ef - \fr 10.8 \fq Nemrod. \ft To this Nemrod the ſonne of Chus, firſt builder and king of Babylon, Ioſephus, S. Epiphanius, S. Hierom, S. Auguſtin, and generally al ancient writers aſcribe the firſt tyrannie, ¹ ²and firſt ſetting vp of an earthlie citie oppoſite to the Citie of God after the floud. ³He was a \+it Valiant\+it*, or rather a \+it Violent hunter\+it* a giant hunter (ſaith S. Auguſtin, according to the ſeuentie Interpreters) who by falshood and force brought manie vnder his dominion. For he ſturred vp pride (ſaith Ioſephus) and contempt of God in men, auoching that they were not beholding to God for preſent felicitie, but to their owne vertue; and ſo ſuppoſing men would fal from God to him, if he offered him ſelfe a leader, and helper againſt a new floud, by litle and litle drew al to tyrannie. He was otherwiſe called Saturnus, and was at length amongſt others accounted a god. After him his ſonne Belus Iupiter (as moſt authors affirme) raigned 65. yeares. And then ſucceded Ninus the firſt king of the Aſſirians. \fq ¹ \ft Nemrod king of Babylon, a suttle & cruel giant. \fq ² \ft \+it Li. 1. c. 4. Antiqit. l. Antiq. Chalda. Li. 1. c. 2. in Osee. li. 16 c. 3. 4. & 17. ciuit. Berosus Annian. li. 4. c. 5. Eusebius in chron. & alij\+it*. \fq ³ \ft An Arch-heretike.\ef*
\v 9 and he was a valiaunt hunter \f a \fr 10.9 \ft That is in his ſight, who can not be deceiued.\f* before our Lord. Therof roſe a prouerbe: As it were Nemrod the valiaunt hunter before our Lord.
\v 10 And the beginning of his kingdome was Babylon, and Arach, and Achad, and Chalanne in the land of Sennaar.
\v 11 Out of that land came forth Aſſur, and builded Niniue, and the ſtreets of the citie, and Chale. \ef - \fr 10.11 \fq Aſſur builded Niniue. \ft ¹ Here is great difficultie, and much diſpute amongſt writers, who this Aſſur is. Briefly, we may either ſay with ² Ioſephus, and S. Auguſtin, that Aſſur the ſonne of Sem built a citie, which afterwards Ninus of Chams race enlarged, enriched, and changing the name called it Ninum: or els that this word \+it Aſſur\+it* here ſignifieth (as 4. Reg. 15. Pſal. 82. Eſaiæ 10. & 31.) the king of Aſſirians, to wit Ninus the ſonne of Belus, who as al hiſtories greeke and barbarous reporte (ſaith S. Hierom) was the firſt that raigned ouer al Aſia, & among the Aſſirians built Ninum a citie of his owne name, which the Hebrewes cal Niniue. He ſet vp the Monarchie of the Aſſirians, called the golden kingdom, which ſtood 1240. yeares. And made his father Belus to be honored for a god. To whom the Babilonians, as Plinie teſtifieth, firſt erected ſtatuas, altares, & temples. ³ Of this Belus or Bel of Babylon were alſo deriued other falſe goddes, as Belial the god of Libertines, or without yoke, Beelphegor god of the Moabites, Beelzebub of the Acharonites, Baal in Samaria, Baalim amongſt the Philiſtims: and the like in other nations. ⁴ \fq ¹ \ft Assur, sonne of Sem, or Ninus king of Assirians. \fq ² \ft \+it (a) li. 1. Antiq. c. 4. Li. 16. c. 3. ciuit. Euseb. in chron\+it*. \fq ³ \ft First false goddes. \fq ⁴ \ft \+it Iud. 19. Num. 25. 4. Reg. 1. 3. Reg. 16\+it*.\ef*
\v 12 Reſen alſo betwen Niniue and Chale: this is the great citie.
\v 13 But Meſraim alſo begat the Ludims, & the Anamims, & the Laabims, the Nephthuims,
\v 14 and the Phetruſims, and the Caſluims: of whom came forth the Philiſtims & the Caphtorims.
\v 15 And Canaan begat Sidon his firſt begotten, Hethæus,
\v 16 and Iebuſæus, and Amorrhæus, and Gergeſæus,
\v 17 Heuæus and Aracæus: Sinæus,
\v 18 and Aradius, Samaræus, and Hamathæus: and afterwardes were ſpred the people of the Chananits.
\v 19 And the limitts of Chanaan were from Sidon as we come to Gerara euen to Gaza, vntil thou enter to Sodoma & Gomorrha, and Adama, & Seboim euen to Leſa.
\v 20 Theſe are the children of Cham in their kinreds, and tongues, and generations & lands, and nations.
\v 21 Of Sem alſo \f b \fr 10.21 \ft Hence S. Auguſtin geathereth that the people of Iſrael were called Hebrewes of this Heber. \+it l. 16. c. 3. ciuit\+it*.\f* father of al the children of Herber, the elder brother of Iapheth were borne.
\v 22 The children of Sem: Ælam and Aſſur, and Arphaxad, and Lud, and Aram.
\v 23 The children of Aram: Vs, and Hul, and Gether, and Mes.
\v 24 And Arphaxad alſo begat Sale, of whom was borne Heber.
\v 25 And to Heber were borne two ſonnes: the name of the one was Phaleg, \f c \fr 10.25 \ft Heber hauing a ſonne borne when the tongues were diuided called him Phaleg, which ſignifieth diuiſion \+it S. Aug. li. 16. c. 11. ciuit\+it*\f* becauſe that in his dayes was the earth diuided: and his brothers name was Iectan.
\v 26 The which Iectan begat Elmodad and Saleph, and Aſarmoth, Iare,
\v 27 and Aduram, and Vzal, and Decla,
\v 28 and Ebal, and Abimael, Saba,
\v 29 and Ophir, and Heuila, and Iobab. al theſe were the children of Iectan.
\v 30 And their dwelling was from Meſſa as we goe on as far as Sephar a mountaine in the eaſt.
\v 31 Theſe are the children of Sem according to their kinred and tongues, and countries in their nations.
\v 32 Theſe are the families of Noe, according to their peoples & nations. Of theſe were the nations diuided on the earth after the floud. \ef - \fr 10.32 \fq The Nations. \ft How manie Nations and tongues were in the world immediatly after the towre of Babylon, is more commonly ſuppoſed, then clerly ſhewed by old or late writers. Only it ſemeth certaine and euident, that there were iuſt as manie tongues as Nations. But to finde preciſly (as the common opinion holdeth) ¹ 72, is hard. For in this chapter (where they would count this number) are not mentioned ſo manie. Neuertheles if we adde certaine that begane diſtinct Nations afterwards, til Iacob with his children went into Ægypt (in whom only the Hebrew Nation and Tongue continued to Chriſts time, ² ³ the number wil come right. Of Iapheth were borne 7. ſonnes chiefe of Nations. Againe of Gomer (beſides his ſuppoſed ſucceſſor, who can not be counted beginner of an other diſtinct nation) came 2. other heades. Likwiſe of Iauan (beſides his firſt ſonne) roſe 3. more nations. Of Cham by his firſt ſonne Chus were 6. nephewes princes of nations. Againe of Regma (beſides his ſucceſſor) came one more. And Nemrod beſides his kingdom of Babylon, rayſed vp other 6. By his ſecond ſonne Meſraim came 8. nations, Chams third ſonne Phut made ouly one nation. And Chanaan his ſonnes made 11. more. Of Sem (laſt mentioned for better connecting the maine Hiſtorie, and ſucceſſion of the Church) came the chife and principal Nation the Hebrewes, ⁴deſcending from him by Arphaxad, Sale, Heber, and ſo directly to Iacob. Of Sem alſo were borne 4. other ſonnes beginners of nations. Againe of Aram (beſides his firſt ſonne) were 3 fathers of nations. Likwiſe of Heber (beſides the Hebrewes deſcending by Phaleg) were borne to his other ſonne Iectan 13. heades of nations. Theſe are al that are named in this place: to wit, of Iapheth, 12. of Cham 33. and of Sem 21. which make in al 66. wherunto if we adioyne ⁵ Nachor (Abrahams brother) Moab, and Ammon (Lots ſonnes) alſo Iſmael (Abrahams eldeſt ſonne) and his iſſue by Cetura, and finally Eſau (Iacobs brother) who made 6. more diſtinct nations, the whole number is 72. This probable collection, with the reſt, we ſubmit to better iudgement. \fq ¹ \ft The common opinion of 72. tongues is not clere in Scripture. \fq ² \ft Yet this number of nations and tongues may be probably geathered in this and other places of Genesis. \fq ³ \ft \+it S. Aug. 16. c. 3. civit.\+it* \fq ⁴ \ft The Hebrews chiefe of these nations. \fq ⁵ \ft \+it Gen. 24. 19. 17. 25, 6. 25, 23\+it*.\ef*
\c 11
\cd God hindereth the vaine purpoſe of building a hiegh towre, 7. by confounding mens tongues. 9. wherof it is called Babel. 10. The genealogie of Sem to Abram. \f - \ft The fourth part of this book. Of the diviſion of tongues and nations.\f*
\p
\v 1 AND the earth was of one tongue, and al one ſpeach.
\v 2 And when they remoued from the eaſt, they found a plaine in the land of Sennaar, and dwelt in it.
\v 3 And eech one ſaid to his neighboure: Come, let vs make bricke, and bake them with fire. And they had bricke in ſteed of ſtone, and bitume in ſteed of morter:
\v 4 and they ſaid: Come, let vs make vs a citie and a towre, the toppe wherof may reach to heauen: and let vs renowne our name before we be diſperſed into al lands. \ef - \fr 11.4 \fq Let vs make. \ft ¹ Here we may ſee in Nemrod the common cauſes of hereſies, and the maner of Hertikes ptoceding. For he hauing a ſutle proud, and aſpiring mind, ²firſt detracted from God, perſwading men (as is noted before) not to depend vpon Gods prouidence, and finding ſome others of like humour, they conſpired together, and drew more folowers, by bearing the ſimpler ſorte in hand ³ (for it was vnpoſſible wiſe men ſhould beleue it) that they would make a towre of defence againſt a new floud, if God ſhould thinck to drowne the world againe. But their principal intention was to make themſelues great and ſtrong for the preſent, and famous to poſteritie. ⁴ Al which God ouerthrew neither ſuffering them to build vp their imagined caſtle of ſtrength nor to be praiſed for their worke, but made them infamous to the worlds end. \fq ¹ \ft Pride is cause of schisme and heresie. \fq ² \ft \+it S. Chris. ho. 30. in Gen. Cassian. collat. 4. c. 12\+it*. \fq ³ \ft False pretenses deceiue the simple. \fq ⁴ \ft Heretikes prosper for a time, but are confounded in the end.\ef*
\v 5 And our Lord deſcended to ſee the citie and the towre, which the children of Adam builded,
\v 6 and he ſaid: Behold, it is one people, and one tongue is to al: and they haue begunne to doe this, neyther wil they leaue of from their determinations, til they accomplish them indede.
\v 7 Come ye therfore, let vs goe downe, and there confound their tongue, that none may heare is neighbours voice. \ef - \fr 11.7 \fq Let vs confound. \ft God in diſſipating this vaine worke of men, would vſe the miniſterie of Angels. ¹As not only Philo Iudeus, and Origen, ² but alſo S. Auguſtin, S. Gregorie, and other fathers expound theſe wordes, \+it Come, let vs deſcend, and confound their tongue\+it*. where they alſo note Gods ſingular wiſdome, mercie, and iuſtice, ſo puniſhing the offence, ³ that he turneth it to his owne glorie, and the profite of al men: ſhewing his powre and ſoueraigne Maieſtie by two great miracles. ⁴Firſt by ſo ſuddainly and vtterly depriuing al thoſe builders, of their vſual tongue, that preſently they could neither ſpeake it, nor vnderſtand it. Secondly, by geuing diuers diſtinct languages to ſeueral ſortes or families, which they immediatly vnderſtood, and ſpoke moſt promptly, as if they had long before lerned and vſed the ſame. But to no man was geuen more then one language. And ſo, to the more commoditie of al mankind, they were forced to part into ſundrie coaſtes of the earth, which they inhabited and replenished with diſtinct Nations, hauing the ſame Angels their ſpiritual Patrones and Protectors, which had ſeuerally changed their language. In particular, ⁵ ⁶ it was profitable to the good who being before oppreſſed by the vnited powre of manie wicked, were relecued (as S. Gregorie teacheth) when their perſecutors were diuided. ⁷Theſe good were the familie of Heber, as S. Chriſoſtom, and S. Auguſtin proue. For ſeing the change of tongues was inflicted for puniſhment, ⁸ it appeareth that Heber and his familie were innocent of the vaine attempt, whoſe tongue was not changed, but remained the ſame, and of him was called the Hebrew tongue for diſtinction ſake after there were manie tongues, which before had no diſtinct name being the only tongue of al men. Againe touching the offenders (who were punished in their tongues, that they could not be vnderſtood commanding one an other, becauſe they would not vnderſtand God iuſtly commanding them al) they alſo reaped this ptofite, that they were forced to leaue of that bad worke, and withal to ſeeke more ample habitations, who If \+it they had there more increaſed in number and ſtreingth, vvould vvithout doubt\+it* (ſaith S. Chriſoſtom) \+it haue attempted vvorſe things\+it*. And infinite manſlaughter would haue benne committed, amongſt ſo manie for poſſeſſion of that one citie & towre. Finally the fathers note ⁹that as God wrought here much good by diuiſion of tongues: ſo he wrought much more by communion of tongues, geuen to the Apoſtles, ¹⁰therby inabling them to gether one Church of al Tongues and Nations. \fq ¹ \ft Ministrie of Angels. \fq ² \ft \+it Li. de confus. ling. ho. 11 in Numer. li. 16 c. 6 ciuit. li. 2. Moral. in c 1. Iob\+it*. \fq ³ \ft God turneth the offence of men to good. \fq ⁴ \ft Two miracles In priuation of one tongue and geuing a new. \fq ⁵ \ft Diuision among euil men is profitable. \fq ⁶ \ft \+it Li. 34. Moral. in. c. 41. Iob. ho. 11. in Gen. l. 16. c. 11 & li. 18. c. 39. ci. S. Aug. li. 6. c. 4. ciuit\+it*. \fq ⁷ \ft The member offending is punished. \fq ⁸ \ft Heber and his familie consented not to the building of Babel. \fq ⁹ \ft Others conjecture Cainan should not be in the text of the 70. \fq ¹⁰ \ft \+it Editio quædam vaticanæ.\+it*\ef*
\v 8 And ſo our Lord diſperſed them from that place into al lands, and they ceaſed to build the citie.
\v 9 And therfore the name therof was called Babel, \f a \fr 11.9 \ft He that ſpeaketh ſo confuſedly that he is not vnderſtood is ſaid to bable.\f* becauſe there the tongue of the whole earth was confounded: and from thence our Lord diſperſed them vpon the face of al countries.
\v 10 Theſe are \f b \fr 11.10 \ft Moyſes here ſheweth the ſucceſſion of Patriarches, from Sem to Abraham, as he did before from Adam to Noe. \+it S. Aug. li. 16. c. 10. ci\+it*.\f* the generations of Sem: Sem was an hundred yeares old when he begat Arphaxad, two yeares after the floud.
\v 11 And Sem liued after he begat Arphaxad, fiue hundred yeares: and begat ſonnes and daughters.
\v 12 Moreouer Arphaxad liued thirtie fiue yeares, and begat Sale. \ef - \fr 11.12 \fq Begat Sale. \ft ¹ Here is an intricate difficultie. For the Hebrew and Latin text, both here and in Palalippomenon, ² ſaying Arphaxad begat Sale, the 72. Interpreters and S. Luke ³ place Cainan betwen them, as ſonne of Arphaxad, and father of Sale. Euſebius alſo in his Chronicle, with moſt Greeke Doctors, and S. Auguſtin, ⁴count Cainan in this Genealogie of Sem. VVherupon manie do number him in this ranck, and ſuppoſe that Moyſes omitted him for ſome Myſterie, ⁵and yet writeth truly, that Arphaxad begat Sale, not his proper ſonne, but his ſonnes ſonne: as S. Mathew ſayth, ⁶Ioram begat Ozias, who was his nephewes nephew. But againſt this ſolution it is replied, that then Arphaxad ſhould haue bene a grandfather at 35. yeares of age: which were ſtrange in thoſe daies, how ſoeuer it is now. ⁷ And a greatter difficultie, or rather abſurditie muſt alſo be granted, that Arphaxad begat both Cainan at the age of 35. yeares, according to the 72. Interpreters, and that Sale was alſo begotten the ſame yeare, according to the Hebrew, being both true. VVhich inconuenience is not in the Genealogie written by S. Matthew. Others therfore according to the Hebrew and Latin text, with moſt Latin Doctors, omit Cainan in this place, and Paralipomenon, namely with S. Hierom, who diligently examining and reconciling varieties, betwen the Hebrew ⁸ and the Greeke, maketh no mention at al of this difference. ⁹ VVhich maketh ſome to coniecture, that in S. Hieroms time Cainan was not in the Greeke copies, at leaſt not in thoſe that he had, and held for the beſt. And at this day ſome haue him not. VVhich may be admitted for a probable anſwere touching the Hebrew and Greeke of the old Teſtament. But for ſo much as al copies, both Greeke & Latin, ¹⁰alſo S. Hieroms Edition of S. Lukes Goſpel haue Cainan, ¹¹the difficultie ſtil remaineth betwen Moyſes and S. Luke. How then shal this doubt be ſolued? we can not ſolue it. And no maruel. For venerable Bede could not. VVhoſe wordes are theſe: ¹² \+it S. Luke vſeth rather the Greeke teſtimonies then the Hebrew: wherof hapeneth that I much maruel at, and for dulnes of wit, being ſtriken with great admirrtion, I can not throughly ſcan, ſeeing in the Hebrew veritie are founed only tenne generations from the floud vnto Abraham, by what meanes\+it* S. Luke, \+it who (the Holie Ghoſt gouerning his penne) could in no ſorte write falſe, would rather ſet downe eleuen generations in the Goſpel, Cainan adioyned according to the ſeuentie Interpreters\+it*. ¹³ Thus writeth S. Beda, reuerently admiring that he could not vnderſtand. For being aſſured that the Holie Ghoſt gouerned the pennes both of Moyſes and S. Luke, and that he is not contrarie to him ſelfe, it muſt nedes be true which ech of them writeth, though other learned men can not reach the profoundnes of ſome difficulties that occure. And therfore Beza was extreme ſaucie to daſh Cainan out of S. Lukes Goſpel, ¹⁴and that wittingly and moſt impudently ſaying, in his Annotations: \+it Non dubitauimus expungere: VVe doubted not to put it out\+it*. ¹⁵ ¹⁶ The former English Editions, otherwiſe corrupt in manie places, haue Cainan in the text of S. Lukes Goſpel, but their latter tranſlaters are in this point pure Bezites. The end of the ſecond age. \fq ¹ \ft Scriptures hard \fq ² \ft \+it 1. Par. 1\+it*. \fq ³ \ft \+it Luc. 3. v. 36.\+it* \fq ⁴ \ft \+it Li. 16. c. 10. ciuit\+it*. \fq ⁵ \ft Some think Moyses omitted Cainan for a mysterie. \fq ⁶ \ft \+it Mat. 1.\+it* \fq ⁷ \ft Refutation. \fq ⁸ \ft \+it Quest. Hebræ.\+it* \fq ⁹ \ft \+it Ho. 30. in Gen. S. Greg. ho. 30. in Evan.\+it* \fq ¹⁰ \ft Gift of tongues most profitable to the Church. \fq ¹¹ \ft But neuer anie Catholique (nor heretike before Beza) put Cainan out of S. Lukes Gospel. \fq ¹² \ft \+it Prefat. comment. in Actæ Apost.\+it* \fq ¹³ \ft A memorable sentence of S. Beda. \fq ¹⁴ \ft Beza sacrilegiously proud. \fq ¹⁵ \ft The heretical English Editions differ in this point. \fq ¹⁶ \ft \+it 1552. 1577\+it*.\ef*
\v 13 And Arphaxad liued after he begat Sale, three hundred three yeares: and begat ſonnes and daughters.
\v 14 Sale alſo liued thirtie yeares, and begat Heber.
\v 15 And Sale liued after he begat Heber, foure hundred three yeares: and begat ſonnes and daughters.
\v 16 And Heber liued thirtie foure yeares, and begat Phaleg.
\v 17 And Heber liued after he begat Phaleg, foure hundred thirtie yeares: and begat ſonnes and daughters.
\v 18 Phaleg alſo liued thirtie yeares, and begat Reu.
\v 19 And Phaleg liued after he begat Reu, two hundred nine yeares, and begat ſonnes and daughters.
\v 20 And Reu liued thirtie two yeares, and begat Sarug.
\v 21 Reu liued alſo after he begat Sarug, two hundred ſeauen yeares: and begat ſonnes & daughters.
\v 22 And Sarug liued thirtie yeares, and begat Nachor.
\v 23 And Sarug liued after he begat Nachor, two hundred yeares: and begat ſonnes and daughters.
\v 24 And Nachor liued nine and twentie yeares, and begat Thare.
\v 25 And Nachor liued after he begot Thare, an hundred and nintene yeares: and begat ſonnes and daughters.
\v 26 And Thare liued ſeauentie yeares, and begat Abram, and Nachor, and Aran.
\v 27 And theſe are the generations of Thare: Thare begat Abram, Nachor, & Aran. Moreouer Aran begat Lot.
\v 28 And Aran died before Thare his father, in the land of his natiuitie in \f c \fr 11.28 \ft Vr a citie, or territorie of Chaldea. \+it 70. Interpret. and Ioſephus li. 1. Antiq\+it*.\f* Vr of the Chaldees.
\v 29 And Abram & Nachor maried wiues: the name of Abram his wife was Sarai: and the name of Nachor his Wife, Melcha the daughter of Aran the father of Melcha, and the father of Ieſcha.
\v 30 And Sarai was barren, neither had she children.
\v 31 Thare therfore \f d \fr 11.31 \ft Abram was commanded to goe forth of Chaldea, as appeareth Act. 7. v. 4. Though this iourney is here aſcribed to Thare as the principal perſon.\f* tooke Abram his ſonne, and Lot the ſonne of Aran, his ſonnes ſonne, & Sarai his daughter in law, the wife of Abram his ſonne, and brought them out of Vr of the Chaldees, for to goe into the land of Chanaan: and they came as farre as Haran, and dwelled there.
\v 32 And the dayes of Thare came to two hundred fiue yeares, and died in Haran.
\c 12
\cd Abram commanded by God to leaue his countrie, with promiſe to be bleſſed in his ſeede, 5. taking his wife Sarai, and his nephew Lot, 6. wandreth in the land of Chanaan, 7. erecteth an Altar in Sichem, 8. an other in Bethel. 10. Thence by occasion of famine paſſeth into Ægipt. 14. Where his wife (called his ſiſter) is taken into the kings house, 19. but vntouched is reſtored to him. \f - \ft The beginning of the third Age.\f*
\s1 THE CONTINUANCE OF THE CHURCH AND RELIGION in the second age of the world. From Noe's flood to Abraham's going forth of his country. The space of 368. years.
\ip No man can well doubt nor will deny, that the same Church continued all the second Age \f - \ft Counting Cainan, this Age continued 398. years.\f* which was in the first, considering that Noe lived above 50. years after the birth of Abraham , \f - \ft Noe and Sem lived in Abraham's time.\f* and Sem 150. more: and that these three, and some others of that time are renowned in holy Scripture, for sincere professors of true Religion . But for more manifestation of their faith, and \f - \ft Articles of Religion professed in the second Age.\f* that the Church was then very conspicuous, we shall repeat certain principal points of Religion professed and practised all that time, by a continual known visible company united in one mystical body: though in the mean while, the wicked sprung and grew in number and worldly force, much oppressing yet never suppressing the good.
\ip First, just Noe coming forth of the ark with his family , professed his religious mind to \it One\it* \f - \ft \+it Gen. 8.\+it*\f* \f - \ft One God.\f* \it God\it* almighty, supreme Lord of all, by \it Offering external\it* voluntary, speedy, pure, solemn, and \f - \ft External Sacrifice.\f* bountiful \it Sacrifice\it* of Holocausts, as a \it Priest\it* upon an \it Altar\it*. After which most grateful \f - \ft Priesthood.\f* \f - \ft Altars.\f* office, God making a covenant with him and his seed, never again to destroy the world by water , confirmed the same by the sign of the rainbow, which represented the second Person of \f - \ft \+it Gen. 9.\+it*\f* \f - \ft The B. Trinity.\f* the B. \it Trinity\it*, the \it Son of God, Christ\it* our Lord to be born \it The Son of man\it*, & extended upon the \f - \ft Christ.\f* \it Cross: in whom Noe believing was instituted heir of the justice, which is by faith in our Redeemer\it* \f - \ft Cross.\f*. \f - \ft Redeemer.\f* \f - \ft \+it Heb. 11.\+it*\f*
\ip We have here again \it God's operative blessing\it*, with the effect of increase and multiplication, \f - \ft God's blessing operative.\f* \f - \ft \+it Gen. 9.\+it*\f* the issue of Noe by his three sons, in short time making many Nations. By the way also we have an example of \it Father's\it* solemn \it Blessing\it* and \it Cursing\it* their children. The effects whereof \f - \ft Fathers' blessing & cursing.\f* \f - \ft \+it c. 9. v. 25.\+it*\f* succeeded afterwards accordingly. Likewise in this Age was given a particular precept \it Not\it* \f - \ft Abstinence from blood.\f* \f - \ft \+it c. 9. v. 4.\+it*\f* \it to eat blood\it*. And Noe observed distinction of \it Clean\it* beasts, offering Sacrifice in them only, \f - \ft Clean, and unclean.\f* \f - \ft \+it c. 8. v. 20.\+it*\f* as before the flood he was commanded to take more of them into the ark than of the \it Unclean\it*.
\ip In that so ample mention is made of sin and wickedness, there is no doubt, but Noe, the \it preacher of justice\it*, admonished and exhorted sinners to \it Repentance\it*: yea he \it Punished\it* Cham & \f - \ft Penance preached and inflicted.\f* \f - \ft \+it c. 10, 10. c. 11. 2. Pet. 2.\+it*\f* Chanaan, by his curse in their posterity. And God himself \it Threatened\it* to exact the blood of man unjustly shed . In the mean time \it Inflicted\it* also some \it punishment\it* upon the builders of Babel, by \f - \ft \+it c. 9, 5. c. 11.\+it*\f* confounding their tongues. And that by the \it Ministry of Angels\it*. \f - \ft Ministry of Angels.\f*
\ip \it Which\it* punishment in part, and threats of more import a \it General Resurrection\it*, and \f - \ft Resurrection.\f* \f - \ft Judgement.\f* \it Judgement\it*, where all things \it shall be\it* exactly discussed and \it Judged\it*. And then will follow \it Eternal\it* \f - \ft Eternal joy and pain.\f* \it life\it* to the good: and \it Everlasting pain\it* to the damned.
\ip All these points of Religion (and others mentioned in the former age, and no doubt taught by Noe & his sons) shew clearly a \it Visible Church\it*, consisting of good and bad. \it Noe\it* remaining the \f - \ft Church visible. Good and bad in the Church.\f* \f - \ft \+it Cap. 8. & 9. c. 10, 21.\+it*\f* same man as before the flood; \it Sem\it* and \it Japheth\it* are commended and blessed for well doing; \it Cham\it* blamed, & cursed in his posterity: yet neither he nor any of his sons or daughters fell into heresy, or other infidelity, for any thing that appeareth in Scripture, or other authentical testimony. \f - \ft Always some good.\f* \it Heber also and his family are\it* particularly commended by Moyses, as the right followers, and the spiritual children of Sem (who had innumerable other carnal children) as those that were innocent touching the presumptuous building of others, who for the same fault lost their old tongue, which the family of Heber kept. As S. Chrysostom and S. Augustin do prove . Again, diverse of this \f - \ft \+it Ho. 39. in Gen. li. 16. c. 11. & li. 18. c. 39. civit.\+it*\f* family falling afterwards by little and little to other nations, the \it Family of Thare\it*, saith the same S. Augustin (li. 16. c. 12.) albeit not all, or not always, yet ever some of them, and Abraham continually, with Sem, Heber, Phaleg, & many others, not mentioned by Moyses in his brief description (as S. Gregory doubteth not to suppose) were just, and kept the true faith, and \f - \ft \+it L. 5. c. 35. Moral. in 3. Iob.\+it*\f* undefiled Religion.
\ip But \it Nemrod\it* Cham's nephew, and son of Chus described for a valiant hunter, a violent \f - \ft Nemrod an Arch-heretic\f* \f - \ft \+it Ioseph li. 1. c. 4. Antiq.\+it*\f* giant, and tyrant, was an Arch-heretic, a deviser and teacher of false doctrine, against God and true faith. By subtlety and tyranny he induced many of liking or of fear to follow him, and so in schism he maintained heresy, \it That men were not beholding to God, but to themselves, for temporal\it* \f - \ft His proud heresy.\f* \it prosperity\it*.
\ip Whereof began a new & cruel confederacy, against the City of God, & the second great Sect of Infidels. \it For Barbarism\it* being the first, begun by Cain and ended by the flood: \it The second\it* \f - \ft The third sect was Graecism.\f* \it mother\it* of all Sects beginning after the flood (as S. Epiphanius writeth) was \it Scythism\it*: so \f - \ft \+it L. de heresibus.\+it*\f* \f - \ft The second Scythism.\f* called of the Scythians a most cruel people . Who according to Nemrod's heresy (not thinking \f - \ft Cruelty.\f* themselves beholding to God for temporal happiness, but to their own forces) tyrannized over the weaker, and many wicked banding together extremely oppressed the more peaceable, especially the Church and true servants of God. And this was one special cause of building Babylon, besides their ambitious desire of perpetual fame, and their bearing the simple in hand of a defence against a new flood, to make it indeed, a strong hold for tyrants to offend others, and to defend themselves. Wherefore God (who before destroyed all Infidels by the flood) confounded these builders by dividing their tongues, and so forced them to break and part into many countries.
\ip Thus mankind being divided upon the earth, opinions also were multiplied concerning Religion. \f - \ft Untruths are unconstant.\f* For shortly the persuasion of men's trusting in themselves, and in other mortal men appeared absurd, even the strongest feeling adversities, or failing sometimes of their purposes, saw there was need of supernal help, and that earthly things depended much upon divine will and power. But having forsaken God Almighty, the only maker and conserver of all, they began to imagine and serve false gods, both famous dead men, which had prospered in this world, and diverse other things, by which they received commodity, or feared damage.
\ip Hence therefore rose the third principal Sect called \it Grecism\it*, beginning also in this second age, \f - \ft The first sect of Infidels was Barbarism before the flood.\f* as the same S. Epiphanius writeth. For Ninus the first king of the Assirian great Monarchy, brought to pass that his father \it Belus Jupiter\it* was esteemed and worshipped for the only great God by the Assirians . To him the Babylonians erected first Temples, Altars, and Statuas. Nemrod also by \f - \ft Idolatry.\f* the name of \it Saturnus\it*, as the progenitor of Belus, and first great King or Tyrant of Babylon, \f - \ft \+it Satur deorum.\+it*\f* was accounted a god, and the father of gods. About this time likewise began the Dynastae \f - \ft Dynastae were those that reigned in Aegypt by force and policy: & after were great gods, and little gods.\f* among the Aegyptians, and not sooner, as they vainly brag to have been before the flood, yea much longer than indeed the world hath been. Moreover the Chaldees worshipped the fire. Others the sun, the moon, and innumerable other feigned gods. Against all which (& likewise against all heresies) are two special arguments. First, that they were not from the beginning, as the true God, and all truths are known and received by continual Tradition, but brought in afterwards by men, and commonly by ill men. Secondly, they are not accepted and esteemed for gods, or truths in all places, but with great diversity and dissension, one sort allowing that others despise, as holy Athanasius notably writeth in his oration against Idols in these words: \it Quot sunt gentes\it* \f - \ft Idolatry and Heresy are confuted, by that they begin disorderly; & are at dissension in their imagined Religions.\f* \it totidem Deorum genera confinguntur &c. How many nations\it* (saith he) \it so many kinds of gods are feigned. Also the same country, the same city dissenteth within it-self in superstition of Idols. The Phenicians certes acknowledge not the Aegyptians' gods, neither do the Aegyptians adore the same Idols with the Phenicians: Nor the Scythians receive the gods of the Persians, nor the Persians of the Scythians. The Pelasgies refuse the Thrasian gods, the Thrasians know not the Thebians. The Indians are against the Arabians, the Arabians against the Aethiopians: and in like sort the Aethiopians differ in their religious affairs from the Arabians. The Syrians worship not the gods of the Cilicians, and the nations of Capadocia besides all these have gods of other names. The Bithynians also feigned diverse gods, the Armenians again diverse from them. What need we many words? Those that are in the continent honour other gods from the Island-people. In brief, each city and each village not knowing the gods of their neighbours, setteth forth their own, and esteemeth them only in place of gods\it*. Thus far S. Athanasius. Name we like countries, provinces, cities, and towns in these parts of Europe, where Luther's scholars have set their feet, consider the form of Religion and opinions which \f - \ft Luther's progenies differ as much in opinions of Religion, as Paynims do in their false gods.\f* they hold, and we shall see as unorderly beginnings, & as horrible dissensions in heresies (which S. Hierom calleth the Idols of the New Testament) as the ancient fathers have discryed in \f - \ft \+it in c. 11. Osee.\+it*\f* Paganism. For Lutherans or Protestants having no lawful generation, but proceeding of bastards' race, upstarts of unknown progeny, are no less at discord among themselves, only all agreeing against Catholic, like \it syncretisants\it* against their common enemies, or Herod, Pilate, & the Jews against Christ. And in England alone are diverse Sects without possible means to agree in one. \f - \ft Sects in England diverse from Luther, and each one from the rest.\f* For albeit the civil state endeavoureth prudently and seriously to bring all to uniformity, at least in public shew, yet they are but like many faces under one hood, every sort keeping their own opinions; yea almost every Preacher and mean scholer (to say nothing of artificers and common Ministers) arrogating to be his own Judge, contemneth to stand to Luther or Calvin, to Geneva or Parliament, to Convocation or Synod of their own, but to his own only understanding, and interpretation of holy Scripture. Nor yet to that always; for when he is pressed with that he once said, he will forget it, or eat his own word, if he have not written it, or that you have ready witness against him, so hard it is to make a deceived Protestant or Puritan confess that he is \f - \ft Proud & contentious spirits are hardly persuaded to the truth.\f* convinced, except by very pregnant means you can first cast out of him, or bind fast the spirit of presumption, dissension and contention: whereas the simplest Catholic in the world hath the self same faith in all points, with the whole Church , in which he remaineth, and upon whose \f - \ft Unlearned Catholics believe the same faith in all points with the learned.\f* judgement he dependeth.
\ip To return therefore, from whence we are not unnecessarily digressed, we conclude with S.
\ip Augustin ; When Moyses had shewed the beginning and progress of Nemrod's earthly city, \f - \ft \+it li. 16. c. 10. civit.\+it*\f* leaving it in Babylon, that is \it confusion\it*, as needless to prosecute it further, he returneth to declare the perpetual \it succession\it* of the City of God, the Church, as before the flood from Adam to Noe, by the line of Seth, so after the flood from the same Noe, by the line of \it Sem, Arphaxad, Sale,\it* \f - \ft The succession of Patriarchs from Noe to Abraham.\f* \it Heber, Phaleg, Reu, Sarug, Nachor, Thare\it*, and \it Abraham\it*. The rest of Sem's children, and all the progenies of Japheth and Cham, as not pertaining to this purpose, omitted, so connecting those in order of generations, by whom the succession is directly brought to Abraham, Prince of the elected people, a most special Patriarch , to whom new and great promises are made of multiplication \f - \ft Abraham a principal Patriarch.\f* of his seed, and possession of the land of Chanaan, but especially of Christ our Redeemer, and the same many ways confirmed, as will appear in the next Age.
\p
\v 1 AND \x - \xt Act. 7.\x*\f 2 \fr 12.1 \ft The fifth part of this book. Of Abraham's leaving his country, God's bleſſing of his ſeed, and commandment of Circumciſion.\f* our Lord ſaid to Abram: Goe forth of thy countrie, and out of thy kinred, and out of thy fathers houſe, and come into a land, which I wil shew thee.
\v 2 And I wil make thee into a great nation, and I wil bleſſe thee, and magnifie thy name, and thou shalt be bleſſed.
\v 3 I wil bleſſe them and bleſſe thee, and curſſe them that curſſe thee, and \f a \fr 12.3 \ft In Chriſt borne of Abrahames ſeede al nations are bleſſed. Gal. 3.\f* IN \sc THEE\sc* ſhal al the kindreds of the earth be bleſſed. \x - \xt Heb. 11.\x*
\v 4 Abram therfore went out as our Lord had commanded him, and with him went Lot: ſeauentie fiue yeares old was Abram when he went forth out of Haran.
\v 5 And he tooke Sarai his wife, and Lot his brothers ſonne, and al the ſubſtance which they had poſſeſſed, and the ſoules which they had gotten in Haran: and went forth to goe into the land of Chanaan. And when they were come into it,
\v 6 Abram paſſed through the countrie vnto the place Sichem, as farre as the noble vale: and the Cananite was at that time in the countrie.
\v 7 And our Lord appeared to Abram, and ſaid to him: To thy ſeed wil I giue this land. Who builded there \f b \fr 12.7 \ft Abram dedicated Altars to God eſpecially in thoſe places where he receiued promiſes, or benefites. \+it S. Chriſoſt. ho. 31. in Gen\+it*.\f* an altar to our Lord, that had appeared to him.
\v 8 And marching on from thence to a mountaine, that was on the eaſt ſide of Bethel, there he pitched his tent, hauing Bethel on the weſt, and Hay on the eaſt: he builded there alſo an altar to our Lord, and called vpon his name.
\v 9 And Abram went forward going, and proceding on to the ſouth.
\v 10 And there came a famine in the countrie: and Abram deſcended into Ægypt, to be as a pilgrime there: for the famine was very ſore in the land.
\v 11 And when he was nere to enter into Ægypt, he ſaid to Sarai his wife: I know that thou art a fayre woman:
\v 12 and that when the Ægyptians shal ſee thee, they wil ſay: She is his wife: and they wil til me, and reſerue thee.
\v 13 Say therfore, I pray thee, that thou art my ſiſter: that I may be wel vſed for thee, and that my ſoule may liue for thy ſake. \ef - \fr 12.13 \fq Say therfore. \ft Abraham concealed that Sarai was his wife, and lyed not in ſaying, ſhe was his ſiſter: as he alſo called Lot his brother, ¹being his brothers ſonne, and she his brothers daughter. VVherby he preuented danger of his owne life, vſing ſuch lawful meanes as lay in him, committing his wiues chaſtitie to Gods protection, which him ſelf could not prouide for. ² In which caſe if he had not donne his owne endeuour, he had rather tempted God (ſaith S Auguſtin) ³ then truſted in God. And ſo God preſerued her, though she was in Pharao his houſe. v. 17. \fq ¹ \ft \+it Gen. 13.\+it* \fq ² \ft Men are bond to do their lawful endeuoures, and to commit the rest to God. \fq ³ \ft \+it li. 16. c. 19. ciuit. & q. 26. in Gen\+it*.\ef*
\v 14 When Abram therfore was entred into Ægypt, the Ægiptians ſawe the woman that she was paſſing beautiful.
\v 15 And the princes told Pharao, and praiſed her to him: and the woman was taken into the houſe of Pharao.
\v 16 And they vſed Abram wel for her ſake. And he had ſheepe and oxen and he aſſes, and men ſeruants, and maid ſeruants, and shee aſſes, and Cammels.
\v 17 But our Lord \f c \fr 12.17 \ft God by corporal affliction hindered Pharao and his men from doing violence to Sarai. \+it S. Chriſoſt. ho. 32. in Gen\+it*.\f* ſcourged Pharao with very ſore plagues, and his houſe for Sarai Abrams wife.
\v 18 And Pharao called Abram, and ſaid to him: What is this that thou haſt done to me? Why didſt thou not tel me that she was thy wife?
\v 19 For what cauſe didſt thou ſay, she was thy ſiſter, that I might take her to my wife? Now therfore there is thy wife, take her, and goe thy ways.
\v 20 And Pharao gaue certaine men commandment in the behalfe of Abram: and they conducted him, and his wife, and al that he had.
\c 13
\cd Abram & Lot returne from Ægypt into Chanaan, 6. and being rich ſeparate themſelues, 10, Lot chooſing the countrie about Iordain, Abram dwelleth in Chanaan. 14. where againe God promiſeth him that land, and multiplication of his ſeede. 18. And he erected an other Altar to God.
\p
\v 1 ABRAM therfore aſcended out of Ægypt, he and his wife, and al that he had, and Lot with him to the ſouth coaſt.
\v 2 And he was very rich in poſſeſſion of gold and ſiluer.
\v 3 And he returned by the way, that he came, from the ſouth vnto Bethel, euen to the place where before he had pitched a tabernacle betwen Bethel and Hay:
\v 4 in the place of the altar which he had made before, and there he called vpon the name of our Lord.
\v 5 But Lot alſo that was with Abram, had flocks of sheepe, and heards of beaſts, and tents.
\v 6 Neyther was the land able to receiue them, for to dwel togeather: for their ſubſtance was much, and they could not dwel togeather.
\v 7 Wherupon alſo there aroſe ſtrife amongſt the heardſmen of Abram and of Lot. And that time the Chananite and the Pheriſite dwelled in that countrie.
\v 8 Abram therfore ſaid to Lot: Let there be no brawle I beſeech thee betwen me and thee, and betwen my heardſmen, and thy heardſmen: for we be \f a \fr 13.8 \ft Foure ſortes of bretheren in holie writte: german brothers, as Iacob and Eſau: of kinred, as Abraham and Lot: of the ſame nation, as the Iewes and Samaritanes: in Religion as al Catholiques.\f* brethren.
\v 9 Behold the whole land is before thee: goe aparte from me, I pray thee: if thou wilt goe to the left hand, I wil take the right: if thou chooſe the right hand, I wil paſſe to the left.
\v 10 Lot therfore lifting vp his eyes, ſawe al the countrie about Iordaine, which was watered through out before that our Lord ſubuerted Sodome and Gomorre, as the paradiſe of our Lord, and like as Ægypt as men come vnto Segor.
\v 11 And Lot choſe vnto him the countrie about Iordaine, and he departed from the Eaſt: and they were ſeperated either brother from the other.
\v 12 Abram dwelt in the land of Chanaan: and Lot abode in the townes, that were about Iordaine, and dwelt in Sodome.
\v 13 And the men of Sodome were verie wicked, and ſinners before the face of our Lord out of meaſure.
\v 14 And our Lord ſaid to Abram, after that Lot was ſeperated from him: Lyft vp thyn eyes, and looke from the place, wherin thou now art, to the north and ſouth, to the eaſt and weſt.
\v 15 Al the land, which thou ſeeſt, wil I geue to thee, & to thy ſeed for euer.
\v 16 And I wil make \f b \fr 13.16 \ft Not the children of the flesh but the children of promiſſe are the ſeede. Rom. 9. and are innumerable. Apoc. 7. v. 9.\f* thy ſeede as the duſt of the earth: if any man be able to number the duſt of the earth, thy ſeede alſo shal he be able to number.
\v 17 Ariſe and walke through the land in the length, and in the breath therof: for I wil geue it to thee.
\v 18 Abram therfore remouing his tent, came, and dwelt beſide the vale of Mambre, which is in Hebron: and he builded there an altar to our Lord.
\c 14
\cd The king of Sodom with other foure kings are ouercome in battle, by foure others: 12. where Lot is taken 14. but Abram with 318. perſons proſecuting and ouercoming the victorers, 16. reſcued Lot, with al the captiues and pray. 18. Melchiſedech King and Prieſt bleſſed Abram, 20. Abram payed tithes to him. 21. and rendered the ſpoile to the king of Sodom.
\p
\v 1 AND it came to paſſe in that time, that Amraphel the king of Sennaar, and Arioch the king of Pontus, and Chodorlahomor king of the Elamyts, and Thadal the king of nacions
\v 2 made warre againſt Barra the king of Sodome, and againſt Berſa the king of Gomorra, and againſt Sennaab the king of Adama, and againſt Semebar the king of Seboim, and againſt the king of Bala, the ſame is Segor.
\v 3 Al theſe came together into the Woodland vale, which now is the ſalt ſea.
\v 4 For they had ſerued Chodorlamor twelue yeares, and the thirtenth yeare they reuolted from him.
\v 5 Therfore in the fourtenth yeare came Chodorlahomor, and the king that were with him: and they ſtroke Raphaim in Aſtarothcarnaim, and Suſim with them, and Emim in Sauee of Cariathaim,
\v 6 and the Corrheans in the mountains of Seir, euen to the Champion countrie of Pharan, which is in the wildernes.
\v 7 And they returned, and came as farre as the fountaine of Miſphat, the ſame is Cades: and they ſtroke al the countrie of the Ameleichites, and of the Amorheans, that dwel in Aſſaſonthamar.
\v 8 And they went forth the king of Sodome, and the king of Gomorra, and the king of Adama, and the king of Seboim, moreouer alſo the king of Bala, which is Segor: and they ſet themſelues againſt them in battaile aray in the Woodland vale:
\v 9 to wit againſt Chodorlahomor king of the Elamites, and Chadal king of nacions, and Amraphel king of Sennaar, and Arioch king of Pontus: foure kings againſt fiue.
\v 10 But the Woodland vale had many pitts of bitume. Therfore the king of Sodome, and of Gomorra turned their backes, and were ouerthrowne there: and they that remained fled to the mountaine.
\v 11 And they tooke al the ſubſtance of the Sodomites, and Gomorrheans, and tooke al kind of victuales, and went their way:
\v 12 and Lot alſo and his ſubſtance, the ſonne of Abrams brother, who dwelled in Sodom.
\v 13 And behold one, that had eſcaped, told Abram the Hebrew, that dwelt in the vale of Mambre, the Amorrean brother of Eſchol, and the brother of Aner: for theſe had made a league with Abram.
\v 14 Which when Abram had heard, to witt, that his brother Lot was taken, he numbred of the ſeruantes borne in his houſe, wel appointed three hundred and eightene: and purſued them vnto Dan.
\v 15 And diuiding his companie, he ranne vpon them in the night: and ſtroke them, and purſued them vnto Hoba, which is on the left hand of Damaſcus.
\v 16 And he brought backe al the ſubſtance, and Lot his brother with his ſubſtance, the wemen alſo and the people.
\v 17 And the king of Sodom went forth to meete them, after he returned from the ſlaughter of Chodorlahomor, and of the kinges that were with him in the vale Sauee, which is the kings vale.
\v 18 But Melcheſidech the king of Salem, bringing forth bread and wine, for he was the Prieſt of God moſt highe, \ef - \fr 14.18 \fq Melchiſedech. \ft S. Hierom being earneſtly requeſted by Euagrius to give his iudgement touching Melchiſedech ¹ ², whom a nameles author had endeavoured to proue to be the Holie Ghoſt, plainly confuteth that error: as alſo another error of Origen and Didymus ſaying, he was an Angel. Likewiſe S. Epiphanius \+it (hereſi 55. and 76.)\+it* relateth and condemneth a third error, of ſome that thought him to be the Sonne of God. Theſe two Fathers, and S. Auguſtine \+it (li. de hereſibus her. 34.)\+it* and diuers others whom S. Hierome allegeth, proue euidently, that he was a very man, a Prieſt and a King, yea the High Prieſt (at leaſt of that countrie) Superior to Abraham, and a figure of Chriſt. Beſides theſe hereſies, the ſame S. Hierome relateth two probable opinions. ³ The Jews' Rabins generally hold that Melchiſedech was Sem the ſonne of Noe, from whom Abraham and all the Hebrews deſcended. VVhich they ſeme rather to affirme, as loath to confeſſe that anie man of other Nation then their owne, ſhould haue bene greater and more excellent then Abraham in ſpiritual cauſes, then for anie reaſon they do or can allege. Yet manie, eſpecially latter writers, as Liranus, Toſtatus, Genebrardus, and others, do embrace this opinion as moſt probable. Though S. Hierome ſemeth only to haue added the Hebrews opinion (as he ſaith) becauſe he would intimate all to his friend, when he had firſt cited grauer Authors, S. Ireneus, Hyppolytus, Euſebius Ceſarienſis, Euſebius Emiſſenus, Apolinarius, and Euſtathius, all agreably affirming that Melchiſedech was a Chananite ⁴, King of Salem, which was afterwards called Jeruſalem. To this opinion agreeth Philo Iudæus, continually ſpeaking of him as of a ſtranger to the Jews' Nation. Joſephus alſo a Iew writeth plainly \+it (li. 7. de bello Iudaico. c. 18.)\+it* that he was of Chanaan, and Prince of the Chananites. Alſo S. Dionyſius Ariopagita (Cæleſt. Hier. c. 9.) S. Epiphanius (her. 55. & 67.) Theodoretus (q. 63. in Gen) and Suidas, are of the ſame mind, and manie other Chriſtian Doctors. VVho confirme their aſſertion by that S. Paul ſaith to the Hebrews ⁵: \+it He vvhoſe generation is not numbered among them, tooke tithes of Abraham.\+it* ⁶ For what els can S. Paul meane, but that Melchiſedechs kindred & people, was diuers from the kindred and people of the Jews? Which he could not ſay of Sem, from whom Abraham & all Jews deſcended: as it cannot be ſaid, that Adam and Noe are of diuers generation from anie people that now liueth, becauſe we all come of them. Of this difficultie (not pertaining to anie controuerſie of our time) the ſtudious may ſee more in F. Pererius his commentaries vpon this 14. chap. of Geneſis. diſp. 3. \fq ¹ \ft Old heresies concerning Melchisedech. \fq ² \ft \+it Tomo 3. prope initium\+it*. \fq ³ \ft A probable opinion that Sem and Melchisedech was al one. \fq ⁴ \ft More probable that Melchisedech was a Chananite. \fq ⁵ \ft Proued by S. Pauls wordes. \fq ⁶ \ft \+it Heb. 7. v. 6\+it*.\ef* \ef - \fr 14.18 \fq Bringing forth. \ft Seing the Royal Prophet Dauid, and S. Paul ſay Chriſt is a Prieſt for euer according to the order of Melchiſedech, ¹ ²we demand of Proteſtants, if Chriſt fulfilled not Melchiſedechs figuratiue Sacrifice offered in bread and wine, by offering his owne bodie and bloud at his laſt ſupper in formes of bread & wine, and by inſtituting the ſame to be offered by his Prieſts til the end of the world, what other figuratiue ſacrifice of Melchiſedech they can find performed by Chriſt, by which it may appeare that he is a Prieſt for euer according to that order? Caluin (li. 4. Inſtit. c. 18. para 2.) Kemniſius (par. 2. exam. pag. 740. &. 747) Peter martyr (in 1. Cor. 5.) and moſt Engliſh Proteſtants grant that Melchiſedech was a Prieſt, ³ and that the peculiar function of a Prieſt is to offer Sacrifice, wherfore they hauing no ſacrifice wil haue only miniſters and no Prieſts, but they denie that Melchiſedech offered Sacrifice in bread and wine. VVherupon we ioyne iſſue with them to proue that he did. and that by this place amongſt others of holie Scripture. Kemniſius complayneth that the Latin text hath \+it Obtulit\+it* for \+it Protulit\+it*, \+it Offered\+it*, for \+it Brought forth\+it*. And to diſproue the ſame, he alleageth the Hebrew, Chaldee, Greke, and S. Cyprian. But Catholiques more iuſtly complaine of him, for lying. For al Latin Editions haue Proferens, bringing forth. ⁴ The queſtion therfore in controuerſie is, to what end and vſe Melchiſedech brought forth bread and wine? Caluin and Kemniſius ſay it was only to refresh or feede Abraham and his men, and not for ſacrifice. which their bare ſaying is without reaſon, for that there was ſtore of victuals in the pray (v. 11.) and they had eaten therof. ⁵Moreouer the Hebrew word \+it Hotſi, brought forth\+it*, is a word pertayning to Sacrifice, as in the 6. chap of Iudges. (v. 18. and 19) and importeth that the bread and wine were firſt offered in Sacrifice, and then doubtles they did participat: though they wanted not other ſufficient corporal foode. Againe the wordes folowing \+it For he vvas the Prieſt of God moſt hiegh\+it*, can haue no other ſenſe, ⁶ but that he did the function of a Prieſt in the bread & wine which he brought, otherwiſe if the only cauſe of bringing that prouiſion had benne to releue the campe with victuals, the reaſon would rather haue benne yelded, becauſe he was a bountiful King, a liberal Prince, a ſpecial freind to Abraham, as in deede he was, but none of theſe reaſons, or the like fitted this purpoſe ſo wel, nor touched the cauſe of bringing forth bread and wine, as to ſignifie that he was a Prieſt, whoſe office is to offer Sacrifice. Here againe ſome Proteſtants take exception againſt the Latin text, that the cauſual coniunction \+it Enim, for\+it*, is not agreable to the Hebrew, but should be the copulatiue \+it Et, and\+it*, which is a meere wrangling. ⁷ For the lerned know wel ynough, that the Hebtew particle is better expreſſed in ſuch places, by \+it Enim\+it* or, \+it quia, for\+it* or \+it because\+it*, then by &. And ſo the Engliſh Bible printed in the yeare of our Lord. 1552. readeth: \+it Melchiſedech king of Salem brought forth bread and vvine: for he vvas the Prieſt of the moſt hiegheſt God\+it*. The latter Editions alſo in like places haue not the copulatiue \+it And\+it*, but ſome other word as the ſenſe requireth. Gen. 20. v. 3. \+it Thou art but a dead man, for the vvomans ſake vvhich thou hast taken: for she is a mans vvife\+it*. where the Hebrew phraſe is, \+it And she is maried to a husband\+it*. Gen. 30. v. 27. they read thus: \+it For I haue proued that the Lord hath bleſſed me for thy ſake\+it*, where preciſly conſtruing the Hebrew they ſhould ſay, \+it I diuined\+it* (or coniectured) \+it and the Lord bleſſed me for thy ſake\+it*. Likewiſe Eſaie, 64. v. 5. they read: \+it But Loe, thou haſt bene angrie, for vve offended\+it*, the Hebrew is thus. \+it Loe thou art angrie, and vve haue ſinned\+it*. In the ſame place, they tranſlate, \+it Yet shal vve be ſaued\+it*, vvhich the Hebrew expreſſeth by the copulatiue, \+it And\+it* we shal be ſaued. So when they thinke it conuenient, they tranſlate the Hebrew particle, \+it For, that, yet\+it*, which ſtrictly ſignifieth \+it And\+it*. Now let vs alſo ſee the original in this place. ⁸ In the Hebrew it is thus: \+sc VMALCHI TSEDEC MELEC CHALEM HOTSI LECHEM VAIAIIN. VEHV COHEN LEEL ELION. VAIE VARECHEHV\+sc*, &c. In English word for word thus: \+it And Melchiſedech king of Salem brought forth bread and vvine. And he the prieſt to God moſt hiegh. And he bleſſed him, &c\+it*. where albeit the cauſual word \+it For\+it*, is not expreſſed, yet theſe wordes, \+it And he the prieſt\+it*, further declaring that beſides the office and dignitie of a King (which was ſaid before) Melchiſedec was alſo a Prieſt, muſt nedes ſignifie that he did ſomething about the bread and wine belonging to a Prieſts office. ⁹And what that ſomething was, perhaps the Vniuerſitie of Cambridge wil teſtifie, whoſe late profeſſor of Diuinitie teacheth plainly, that Melchiſedech offered Sacrifice, and was therin a figure of Chriſt \+it (Pag. 6. Reprehen\+it*) ¹⁰ \+it Sacerdotes\+it* (ſaith he) \+it ij vere & proprie ſunt, qui ſacrificia faciunt; qualis fuit Aaron, & Aaronis filij, & Melchiſedechus, & quem illi adumbrabant, Chriſtus. Prieſtes truly and properly are they, that offer ſacrifices, ſuch as vvas Aaron, and the ſonnes of Aaron, and Melchiſedech, and Christ, vvhom they prefigured\+it*. If then both Aaron & Melchiſedech were truly and properly Prieſts, becauſe they offered ſacrifices (according to this Profeſſors definition) and both were figures of Chriſt, it muſt needes be granted that as Chriſt fulfilled the figure of Aarons bloudie ſacrifices, in offering him ſelfe vpon the Croſſe: ſo he alſo fulfilled the figure as wel of vnbloudie ſacrifices of Aaron, as eſpecially of Melchiſedechs Sacrifice in ſome other beſides that on the Croſſe, ſeeing the prophet Dauid and S. Paul ſay, Chriſt is a Prieſt (not according to Aarons order, for that was to haue an end, but) \+it For euer\+it* according to the order of Melchiſedech. ¹¹And what other Sacrifice did our Sauiour offer to remaine perpetual, but of his owne bodie & bloud in vnbloudie maner, vnder the formes of bread and wine, with commandment to his Apoſtles and Prieſts to do the ſame til the end of the world? Let the indifferent reader weigh it wel. And whoſoeuer is not very proud wil for his better inſtruction, or confirmation, eſteme the vniforme iudgement of manie, ancient, godlie, and lerned Fathers writing vpon this place. VVe wil only recite their wordes, without other deduction for breuitie ſake. S. Clemens Alexandrinus (li 4. Strom. verſus finem). writeth thus: Melchiſedech king of Salem, Prieſt of God moſt hiegh, gaue wine & bread ſantified nutriment in type of the Euchariſt. ¹² S Cyprian (Epiſt 63. ad Cæcilium) Chriſt is Prieſt for euer according to the order of Melchiſedech, which order is this coming from that Sacrifice, and thence deſcending, that Melchiſedech was Prieſt of God moſt hiegh, that he offered bread & wine, that he bleſſed Abraham. For who is more a Prieſt of God moſt hiegh, then our Lord Ieſus Chriſt, who offered Sacrifice to God the Father, and offered the ſame, which Melchiſedech had offered, bread and wine, to wit, his owne bodie and bloud. And a litle after: That therfore in Geneſis, the bleſſing might be rightly celebrated, about Abraham, by Melchiſedec the Prieſt, the image of Chriſts Sacrifice conſiſting in bread and wine went before, which thing our Lord perfecting and performing, offered bread and chalice mixt with wine, and he that is the plenitude, fulfilled the veritie of the prefigured image. Euſebius Ceſarienſis. (li 5. Demonſt. Euang. c 3.) Euen as he who was Prieſt of Nations was neuer ſene to offer corporal ſacrifices, but only bread & wine, when he bleſſed Abraham: ſo firſt our Lord & Sauiour him ſelfe, then prieſts that come from him, exerciſing the ſpiritual office of Prieſthood in al nations after the Eccleſiaſtical ordinances, do repreſent the myſteries of his bodie, and healthful bloud in bread & wine. which myſteries Melchiſedech knew ſo long before by diuine ſpirite, and vſed as repreſentations of things to come. S. Ambroſe (li. 5. de Sacramen. c. 1.) VVe know the figure of the Sacraments went before, in Abrahams times, when Melchiſedech offered Sacrifice. Idem. in cap. 5 Heb. It is clere that oblations of cattle are vaniſhed, which were in Aarons order, but Melchiſedechs inſtitution remayneth, which is celebrated al the world ouer in adminiſtration of the Sacraments. S Hierom. (Epiſt. ad Marcellanio vt migret Bethleem.) Haue recourſe to Geneſis, and you shal find Melchiſedech king of Salem, prince of this citie, who euen then in figure of Chriſt offered bread and wine, and dedicated the Chriſtian myſterie in our Sauiours bodie and bloud. Idem Epiſt. ad Euagrium: Melchiſedech offered not bloudie victims, but dedicated the Sacrament of Chriſt in bread and wine, ſimple and pure ſacrifice. Idem. Queſt. in Gen. c. 14: Our myſterie is ſignified in the word of order, not by Aaron in imolating brute victims, but in offering bread and wine, that is the bodie and bloud of our Lord Ieſus. Idem in c. 26. Matthei: Melchiſedech the Prieſt of God moſt hiegh, by offering bread and wine, prefigured the Myſterie of the Euchariſt. S. Chriſoſtom (ho. 35 in Gen.) Seeing the figure, thinck alſo I pray thee, of the veritie ho. 36 After that Melchiſedec king of Salem brought bread and wine (for he was Prieſt of God moſt hiegh) Abraham receiued his oblations. S. Auguſtin (Epiſt. 95.) Melchiſedech bringing forth the ſacrament (or myſterie) of our Lords table, knew how to figurate his eternal ptieſthood. Idem li. 16. c. 22 ciuit. There firſt appeared that ſacrifice, which is now offered to God by Chriſtians in the whole world. Idem li. 17. c. 17. li. 18. c. 35. and vpon the Pſalme. 109. li. 1. contra aduerſ. Leg. & prophet. c. 20. Ser. 4. de Sanctis Innocentibus. But it is bootles or nedles to cite more places, or more authors. for whoſoeuer wil not ſubmit their iudgements to theſe, would not, it is like, beleue, if their owne maiſters, ſhould riſe agane and warne them, leſt they be damned for their incredulitie. ¹³ \fq ¹ \ft Christ is a Priest according to the order of Melchisedech. \fq ² \ft \+it Psal. 109 Heb. 7\+it*. \fq ³ \ft Why the Protestants haue ministers and no Priests. \fq ⁴ \ft The state of the controuersie. \fq ⁵ \ft \+it v. 24. S. Chris. ho. 36. in Gen\+it*. \fq ⁶ \ft Melchisedech offered sacrifice in bread and wine. \fq ⁷ \ft The latin text iustified by other places, yea by Protestants translations. \fq ⁸ \ft The Hebrew text sheweth that Melchisedec did the office of a Priest in bread and wine. \fq ⁹ \ft The proper office of a Priest is to offer Sacrifice. \fq ¹⁰ \ft \+it Guiliel. vvhitak. contra Gregor. Martin\+it*. \fq ¹¹ \ft Christ stil exerciceth the office of Priesthood according to the order of Melchisedech by the ministerie of Priests. \fq ¹² \ft The ancient fathers proue that Melchisedech offered Sacrifice in bread & wine in figure of Christ, and of other Priests of the new Testament. \fq ¹³ \ft Paying of tithes in the law of nature.\ef*
\v 19 bleſſed him, and ſaid: Bleſſed be Abram to God the higheſt, which created heauen and earth: \ef - \fr 14.19 \fq Bleſſed him. \ft Caluin (in cap. 7. v. 9. Heb) Muſculus (locis com. c. de Miſſa Papiſt) and ſome other Proteſtants to auoid the connexion of Melchiſedechs Prieſthood and bringing forth of bread and wine, wil nedes haue theſe wordes, \+it He vvas a Prieſt\+it*, referred only to that which foloweth, \+it he bleſſed Abraham\+it*. ¹ ² And ſome Engliſh tranſlaters for this purpoſe haue corrupted the text, by changing, \+it And\+it* into \+it Therfore\+it*, ſaying thus: \+it And he vvas a Prieſt of the moſt hiegh God, therfore he bleſſed him\+it*. which is alſo a falſe gloſſe. For Melchiſedech did not bleſſe Abram becauſe he was a Prieſt, for Abram was alſo a Prieſt, but becauſe he was a greater Prieſt then Abram. which S. Paul vrgeth ſaying: ³ ⁴ \+it VVithout al contradiction that vvhich is leſſe, is bleſſed of the better\+it*, concluding therupon that Melchiſedech was greatter then Abraham. Agane other Superiors that are not Prieſts may bleſſe their inferiors. As Ioſue and Salomon bleſſed the people, and parents bleſſe their children. ⁵ \fq ¹ \ft Heretical translation. \fq ² \ft \+it Bible 1579.\+it* \fq ³ \ft The greater blesseth the lesse. \fq ⁴ \ft \+it Heb. 7\+it*. \fq ⁵ \ft Iosue 8. &. 22. 3. Reg. 8.\ef*
\v 20 and bleſſed be God the higheſt, by whoſe protection, the enemyes are in thy hands. And he gaue him the tythes of al. \ef - \fr 14.20 \fq Gaue tithes. \ft This is an other prerogatiue of Melchiſedec, ¹ that Abram payed tithes to him, which S. Paul likwiſe explicateth (Heb. 7.) and proueth therby that Chriſts Prieſthood is greatter then the Leuitical. Moreouer this paying of tithes by Abraham ſheweth the antiquitie of this tradition, being practiſed in Abrahams time, that the ſpiritual Superiors receiued tithes of their inferiors. \fq ¹ \ft Calvin contemneth all the ancient fathers. in ca. 9. Heb.\ef*
\v 21 And the king of Sodom ſaid to Abram: Geue me the ſoules, and the reſt take to thee.
\v 22 Who anſwered him: I lift vp my hand to my Lord God moſt hiegh poſſeſſor of heauen and earth,
\v 23 that from the very woofe-thread vnto the ſhoe latchet, I wil not take of al that are thine: \f a \fr 14.23 \ft Abraham enriched by God, would take no more of man, but his ſoldiars ſuſtenance. The proper hire of ſpiritual workmen, S. Chriſoſt. in Gen.\f* leſt thou ſay: I haue enriched Abram:
\v 24 except ſuch thinges, as the young men haue eaten, and the shares of the men, that came with me, Aner, Eſchol, and Mambre: theſe shal take their shares.
\c 15
\cd Abram doubting and lamenting that he should haue no childe, God promiſeth him much iſſue, 6. who beleuing is iuſtified, 9. offereth Sacrifice preſcribed by God, 13. and is forwarned that his ſeede shal be in ſtrange land 400. yeares. 14. shal be deliuered from ſeruitude 18. and poſſeſſe Chanaan.
\p
\v 1 WHEN theſe things therfore were done, the word of our Lord was made to Abram by a viſion ſaying: Feare not Abram, I am thy protector, & thy reward exceding great.
\v 2 And Abram ſaid: Lord God, what wilt thou geue me? I shal goe without children: and the ſonne of the ſtuard of my houſe is this Damaſcus Eliezer.
\v 3 And Abram added: But to me thou haſt not geuen ſeede: and loe my ſeruant borne in my houſe, shal be myn heire.
\v 4 And immediatly the word of our Lord came to him ſaying: He ſhal not be thy heyre: but he that shal come out of thy wombe, him shalt thou haue thin heire.
\v 5 And he brought him forth abroad, and ſaid to him: Looke vp to heauen, and number the ſtarres, if thou canſt. And he ſaid to him: So shal thy ſeed be.
\v 6 \x - \xt Ro. 4. Gal. 3. Iac. 2.\x* Abram beleued God, and it was reputed to him vnto iuſtice. \ef - \fr 15.6 \fq Beleued God. \ft S. Hilarie (li. 9. de Trin) and S. Ambroſe (li. 1. de Abraham. c. 3) by this example teach vs, what maner of faith is reputed to iuſtice. to wit, ¹ ſuch a faith as without tergiuerſation, or requiring of proofe or reaſon, doth ſimply beleue that which God once ſaith, becauſe he is omnipotent, how farſoeuer the thing that is ſaid ſurmounteth our vnderſtanding. For ſo heroical was the act of Abrahams faith, promptly beleuing Gods word in a matter moſt hard to his former conceipt, that for the ſame he receiued ſingular prayſe; and for the like afterwards was called \+it The father of manie nations\+it* (c. 17. v. 5) and by S. Paul. \+it The father of al that beleue\+it* (Ro. 4. v. 11) where the Apoſtle teacheth that Abraham had no iuſtice, nor eſtimation of iuſtice before God, vntil he beleued in Chriſt (v. 18. 19, 20) becauſe al workes before that faith are inſufficient. ²Neyther was this a ſole faith but had other neceſſarie vertues of hope, and charitie, humilite, reuerence, obedience, & the like ioyned with it. wherfore S. Iames teſtifieth that Abraham was iuſtified by workes (that is by workes folowing faith not going before faith. ³ For \+it Faith\+it* (ſaith he) \+it if it haue not vvorkes, is dead in it ſelf\+it*. (c. 2. v. 17) \+it And by vvorkes Abrahams faith vvas conſummat. v. 22\+it*. And concludeth thus: Do yee ſee, that by workes a man is iuſtified and not by faith only. v. 24. \fq ¹ \ft To beleue Gods word without staggering is an act of iustice. \fq ² \ft Not workes before faith but ioyned with faith are meritorious. \fq ³ \ft Onlie faith doth not iustifie.\ef*
\v 7 And he ſaid to him: I am the Lord that brought thee out from Vr of the Chaldees for to giue thee this land, and that thou mighteſt poſſeſſe it.
\v 8 But he ſaid: Lord God, how may I know that I shal poſſeſſe it?
\v 9 And our Lord anſwered, and ſaid: \f a \fr 15.9 \ft Theſe three kindes of beaſtes and two of birdes ſignifie that the Iſraelites ſhould be three generations in a ſtrange land, the fourth in the deſert, the fifth in poſſeſſion of Chanaan, \+it Theod. q. 65. in Gen\+it*.\f* Take me a cowe of three yeares old, and a shee goat of three yeares, and a ramme of three yeares, a turtle alſo, and a pigeon.
\v 10 Who taking al theſe, diuided them by the mydes, and laid ech two peeces arowe one againſt the other: but the birdes he diuided not.
\v 11 And the foules lighted vpon the carcaſſes, and Abram droue them away.
\v 12 And when the ſunne was ſetting, a deepe ſleepe fel vpon Abram, and a great and darkeſome horrour inuaded him.
\v 13 \x - \xt Act. 17.\x* And it was ſaid vnto him: Know and foreknow that a pilgrime shal thy ſeede be in a land not their owne (and they shal bring them vnder bondage, and afflict them) \f b \fr 15.13 \ft Abraham and his ſeed were in ſtrange land 400 and odde yeares, but in ſeruitude and affliction about 140.\f* foure hundred yeares.
\v 14 \x - \xt Exod. 12.\x* But the nation, whom they shal ſerue, I wil iudge: and after this they shal goe forth with great ſubſtance.
\v 15 And thou shalt goe to thy fathers in peace, buried in a good old age.
\v 16 But in the fourth generation they shal returne hither: for \f c \fr 15.16 \ft God deferreth to punish, either that the wicked may amend, or the good be exerciſed by them. S. Aug. Pſal. 54. or becauſe the iniquity is not come to that great meaſure which his wiſdome forſeeth, and wil puniſh in the end, to his owne more glorie, and more good of others. S. Greg. ho. 11. in 3. Ezech.\f* as yet the iniquities of the Amorrheanes are not at the ful vntil this preſent time.
\v 17 Therfore when the ſunne was ſet, there aroſe a darke miſt, and there appeared a fornace ſmoking, and a flake of fire paſſing betwene thoſe diuiſions.
\v 18 That day God made a couenant with Abram, ſaying: To thy ſeede wil I geue this land from the riuer of Ægypt euen to the great riuer Euphrates,
\v 19 the Cineans, & Cenezites, the Cedmonites,
\v 20 and the Hethits, and the Pherezits, the Raphaims alſo,
\v 21 and the Amorreans & the Cananites, and the Gargaſites, and the Iebuſites.
\c 16
\cd Sarai geueth her handmaid Agar as a wife to Abram. 4. Who conceiuing deſpiſeth her myſtreſſe, is therfore afflicted, & flyeth away. 7. But is warned by an Angel to returne and humble herſelfe, 15. which she doth and beareth Iſmael.
\p
\v 1 SARAI therfore, the wife of Abram, had brought forth no children: but hauing an handmaid an Ægyptian named Agar,
\v 2 she ſaid to her huſband: Behold, our Lord hath cloſed me, that I might not beare: Goe in vnto my handmaid, if happely of her at the leaſt I may haue children. And when he agreed to her in this requeſt,
\v 3 she toke Agar the Ægyptian her handmaid tenne yeares after that they firſt dwelled in the land of Chanaan: and gaue her vnto her huſband to wife. \ef - \fr 16.3 \fq To wife. \ft ¹ ² The Manichees did calumniat holie Abraham, and other Patriarches for hauing manie wiues, condemning them of incontinencie and adulterie for the ſame. Luther ³ ⁴ in the contrarie extreme held it not vnlawful, but indifferent, now in the law of grace, for a man to haue more wiues then one at once. ⁵ And ſome English Proteſtants hold, that for adulterie, the innocent partie may marie an other, the firſt liuing. But the Catholique doctrin diſtinguiſhing times and cauſes, ſheweth how pluralitie of wiues was lawful ſometimes, and at other times, eſpecially ſince Chriſt, altogether vnlawful, and vndiſpenſable. The ſumme of which veritie is this. By the firſt inſtitution of Mariage in the ſtate of innocencie, and law of nature, and by the law of Chriſt, it is vnlawful for anie man to haue more wiues, and for anie woman to haue more huſbands, then one: In the one part of which Law notwithſtanding God ſometimes diſpenſed. For there be two kindes of preceptes pertaining to the law of nature. ⁶ ⁷One ſorte are as firſt principles of the law of nature, in which God neuer diſpenſeth, much leſſe anie man. As that one woman may not haue more huſbands then one, becauſe the ſame would rather hinder procreation, and ſo were directly againſt the fruict of mariage. The other ſorte are as concluſions drowne from the firſt principles, in which God ſometimes diſpenſeth, but neuer anie man. As in this preſent example: ſeeing it is againſt natural procreation that one woman ſhould haue manie huſbands, it is conuenient alſo, there being ordinarily as manie men as wemen in the world, that euerie man likewiſe should be reſtrained to one wife, for ſo procreation may rather be increaſed, then if ſome men haue manie wiues, and others by that occaſion haue none at al, except in ſome ſpecial caſe. As after the floud, when there was ſcarſetie of people, God diſpenſed with ſuch men as in deede were like to make greatter procreation by pluralitie of wiues. ⁸VVhich appeareth ſufficiently by that Sarai perſwaded her owne huſband, to marie an other wife, and he a true ſeruant of God agreed therto, not as a new thing but as a lawful practiſe of thoſe times. And Moyſes ⁹ here and in other places ſtil ſpeaketh of it, as of a cuſtome knowne to the people for lawful. \+it If a man\+it* (ſaith he) \+it haue tvvo vviues, one beloued and the other hated, and they haue children by him, and the ſonne of the hated be firſt borne, he can not preferre the ſonne of the beloued\+it*. wherby is clere that two wiues were then lawful, and the children of both legitimate, and that the firſt borne muſt be preferred, without reſpect of firſt or laſt mariage. Yet this diſpenſation either ceaſed before Chriſts time, the cauſe ceaſing, when the world was replenished; or at leaſt our Sauiour tooke it away, reſtoring Matrimonie to the firſt inſtitution of two in one fleſh. ¹⁰ ¹¹who pleaſeth to ſee the Doctors that vnderſtand, and expound the Scriptures to this effect, may read S. Auguſtin li. 22. c. 30. &. 47. con. Fauſt. Manich. li. 16. c. 25. & 38. ciuit. & li. 1 de adulter. coniugijs. S. Chriſtom ho. 56. in Gen. S. Amb. li. de Abraham. c. 4. Alſo S. Chriſoſt. S. Hierom. and S. Bede in 19. Mathei. \fq ¹ \ft Manichees, condemned pluralitie of wiues in the Patriarches. \fq ² \ft \+it S. Aug. l. 22. c. 47. cont. Faust.\+it* \fq ³ \ft Luther alloweth it in Christians. \fq ⁴ \ft \+it Luther propsit. 62. 65. et 66\+it*. \fq ⁵ \ft Other Protestants in some case. \fq ⁶ \ft Two sortes of precepts in the law of nature. \fq ⁷ \ft \+it S. Aug. de bono coniugali. ca. 17\+it*. \fq ⁸ \ft Pluralitie of wiues sometimes allowed. \fq ⁹ \ft \+it Deut. 21\+it*. \fq ¹⁰ \ft By the law of Christ in no case lawful. \fq ¹¹ \ft \+it Math. 19. Gen. 2\+it*.\ef*
\v 4 Who did companie with her, but she \f a \fr 16.4 \ft Some obey whileſt they are rude, or in low ſtate, but hauing got a litle knowlege, or aduancement diſdaine their aduancers. \+it S. Gregorie. li. 21. in 1. Reg. 3\+it*.\f* perceauing that she was with childe, deſpiſed her miſtreſſe.
\v 5 And Sarai ſaid to Abram: Thou doeſt vniuſtly againſt me: I gaue my handmaid into thy boſome, who perceauing herſelf to be with child, deſpiſeth me. Our Lord iudge betwen me and thee.
\v 6 To whom Abram making anſwere: Behold, ſaith he, thy handmaid is in thine owne hand, vſe her as it pleaſeth thee. When Sarai therfore did afflict her, she ranne away.
\v 7 And an angel of our Lord hauing found her, beſide a fountaine of water in the wilderneſſe, which is in the way to Sur in the deſert,
\v 8 he ſaid to her: Agar, the handmaid of Sarai, whence comeſt thou? and whither goeſt thou? who anſwered: From the face of Sarai my miſtreſſe doe I flye.
\v 9 And the angel of our Lord ſaid to her: Returne to thy miſtreſſe, and humble thy ſelfe vnder her hand.
\v 10 And again: Multiplying, ſayth he, wil I multiplie thy ſeed, and it shal not be numbred for the multitude therof.
\v 11 And againe after that: Behold, ſaith he, thou art with child, and thou shalt bring forth a ſonne: and thou shalt cal his name Iſmael, becauſe the Lord hath heard thin affliction.
\v 12 He shal be a wild man: his hand shal be againſt al men, and al mens hands againſt him: and ouer againſt al his bretheren shal he pitch his tents.
\v 13 And she called the name of our Lord that ſpake vnto her: Thou the God which haſt ſene me. For she ſaid: verily here haue I ſene the backe partes of him that hath ſene me.
\v 14 Therfore she called that wel, the Wel of him that liueth and ſeeth me. The ſame is betwen Cadeſſe, and Barad.
\v 15 And Agar brought forth a ſonne to Abram: who called his name Iſmael.
\v 16 Eightie and ſixe yeares old was Abram when Agar brought him forth Iſmael.
\c 17
\cd God renewing his promiſes to Abram, 5. changeth his name, 10. and commandeth Circunciſion. 15. changeth alſo his wiues name, promiſeth a ſonne of her. 20. Likewiſe that Iſmael shal proſper. 23. and the ſame day Abraham circunciſed him ſelfe, and Iſmael, and al the men of his houſe.
\p
\v 1 AND after that he beganne to be nyntie and nyne yeares old, our Lord appeared vnto him: and ſaid vnto him: I am the God almightie: walke before me, and be \f a \fr 17.1 \ft He is perfect in this life, that ſincerely & diligently tendeth towards perfection of the next life. And this God here commanded to Abraham, & Chriſt to al Chriſtians. \+it Math. 5. S. Aug. li. de perfect. cont. Cæleſt\+it*. See Gen 6. v. 9.\f* perfect.
\v 2 And I wil make my couenant betwen me and thee: and I wil multiplie thee exceadingly.
\v 3 Abram fel flat on his face.
\v 4 And God ſaid to him: I am, and my couenant is with thee, & thou shalt be a father of manie nations.
\v 5 Neyther shal thy name be called any more Abram: but thou shalt be called Abraham: becauſe a father of many nations I haue made thee. \ef - \fr 17.5 \fq Manie nations. \ft Abram ſignifying \+it Hiegh\+it* or \+it Noble father\+it*, ¹ changed into Abraham, which ſignifieth \+it Father of manie\+it* (nations) and Sarai ſignifying \+it My Princes\+it*, changed into Sara, which abſolutly ſignifieth \+it Princeſſe\+it*, importe great Myſteries intended by God. For by this changing of names was declared and confirmed, that Abraham by the iſſue that he ſhould haue of Sara, ſhould be the father of manie nations, which S. Paul ² expreſly applieth to his ſpiritual children, thoſe eſpecially that ſhould beleue in Chriſt, of the Gentiles prefigured by \+it Iſaac borne of the freevvoman, by the promiſe\+it*, as the Iewes were prefigured by \+it Iſmael borne of the bondvvoman according to the flesh\+it*, concluding with the prophet Eſai, ³that \+it Manie are the children of the deſolate, that before vvas barren\+it*. ⁴ For albeit Abraham was natural father of foure nations, The Iſmaelites, Madianites, Idumeans, and the Iſraelites, yet he was ſpiritual father of manie more, to wit, of al nations that beleue in Chriſt from his owne time to the end of the world, of which ſome are Iewes, but the greateſt part Gentiles. as the ſame Apoſtle ſheweth. Rom. 4. Gal. 4. Epheſ. 3. Colloſ. 1. and in other places. wherby is clere, that the Church of Chriſt doth alwayes conſiſt of manie nations, ⁵not as Donatiſtes and Proteſtants abſurdly ſay, ſometimes of few inuiſible or vnknowen perſons, for ſo Abraham ſhould ſometimes be father of few or no nations, which derogateth either from Gods omnipotencie if he could not, or from his fidelitie if he ſhould not maintaine his promiſe, made to Abraham for euer. \fq ¹ \ft Changing of names is mystical. \+it S. Aug. li. 16. c. 28 & 39. ciuit. S. Hierom. Tradit. Hebraicis\+it*. \fq ² \ft \+it Gal. 4\+it*. \fq ³ \ft \+it Isa 54.\+it* \fq ⁴ \ft Abraham natural father of four Nations: spiritual father of all that do believe in Christ. \+it Rom. 4, 11.\+it* \fq ⁵ \ft The Church of Christ euer consisteth of many nations.\ef*
\v 6 And I wil make thee encreaſe excedingly, and I wil make thee into nations, and kinges shal come forth of thee.
\v 7 And I wil eſtablish my couenant betwen me and thee, and betwen thy ſeede after thee in their generations by a perpetual couenant: to be thy God, and thy ſedes after thee.
\v 8 And I wil geue to thee, and to thy ſeed the land of thy peregrination, al the land of Chanaan for a perpetual poſſeſſion, and I wil be their God.
\v 9 Againe God ſaid to Abraham: And thou therfore shalt keepe my couenant, and thy ſeed after thee in their generations.
\v 10 This is my couenant which you shal obſerue betwen me and you, and thy ſeede after thee: Al the malekind of you shal be circumciſed: \ef - \fr 17.10 \fq shal be circumciſed. \ft The Sacrament of Circumciſion here firſt inſtituted, about 400. yeares before the Law of Moyſes, ¹ is a perfect figure of Baptiſme, reſembling it in foure things. Firſt by both theſe Sacraments the faithful are diſtinguished from infidels. Secondly proffeſſion of faith is made in them both, either by thoſe that receiue the ſame, if they be of diſcretion, or by others for them, if they be infants. Thirdly by both theſe Sacraments entrance is made into the Church, and to the participation of other Sacraments and ſpiritual rites. Fourrhly both theſe Sacraments induce ſubiection to the iuriſdiction, and lawes of the Church. ² But Baptiſme doth farre excel Circumciſion in that it is more eaſie, or leſſe painful, more vniuerſal, for it pertaineth to al nations, and both ſexes; and eſpecially in vertue and efficacie, for Baptiſme as an inſtrumental cauſe remitteth ſinne and iuſtifieth, Circumciſion was only a ſigne that grace was geuen, & ſinne remitted. Againe Baptiſme imprinteth a character in the ſoule, the other leaueth a marck only in the flesh. Finally Baptiſme openeth the gate of heauen, in vertue of Chriſts paſſion now paſt, which circumciſion could not before Chriſt ſuffered death. Of which both reſemblance and difference S. Auguſtin treateth in manie places, eſpecially li. 3. de doct. chriſt c. 9. Epiſt. 118. and Ianuarium. li: 19. c. 13. cont. Fauſt & in Pſal. 73. where he alſo maketh like compariſon betwen other Sacraments of the old and new Teſtament. \fq ¹ \ft Circumcision a figure of Baptisme. \fq ² \ft Baptisme excelleth Circumcision.\ef*
\v 11 and you shal circumciſe the flesh of your prepuce, that it may be for a ſigne of the couenant betwen me and you.
\v 12 An infant of \f b \fr 17.12 \ft Circumciſion and name receiued the eight day ſignified the aſſociation of Saints in heauen after the ſeuen dayes trauel of this world. \+it Ser. de circum. apud S. Cyprian\+it*.\f* eight daies shalbe circumciſed among you, al malekind in your generations: aſwel the homebred shal be circumciſed, as the bought ſeruant of whoſoeuer he is, not of your ſtocke:
\v 13 and my couenant shal be in your flesh for a perpetual couenant.
\v 14 The male, whoſe flesh of his prepuce shal not be circumciſed, that ſoule shalbe deſtroied out of his people: becauſe he hath broken my couenant. \ef - \fr 17.14 \fq shal be deſtroyed. \ft ¹ Here occurre two difficulties about the true ſenſe of this hard place. Firſt whether this puniſhment belonged to them only, by whoſe fault circumciſion was omitted; or to infants alſo that ſhould be circumciſed and were not. Secondly whether temporal punishment, or eternal was here threatned for tranſgreſſion of this precept. ² Touching the firſt doubt, it ſemeth probable, that this puniſhment pertained only to thoſe, by whoſe negligence themſelues, or others of their charge, were not circumciſed: for that the reaſon why ſuch a one is puniſhed is aleaged, \+it Becauſe he hath broken Gods couenant\+it*. VVhich is only in their powre to kepe or breake, that are of diſcretion, and not in the powre of infants. The indifferencie alſo of the Hebrew and Greeke text fauoreth this ſenſe, for where according to the Latin we read, \+it The male vvhoſe flesh of prepuce is not circumciſed\+it*, The Hebrew and Greke may be tranſlated, \+it The male that doth not circumciſe the flesh of his prepuce\+it*, which can not be ment of an infant of eight dayes old. This expoſition is likewiſe confirmed by example, Exodi. 4. where Moyſes was in danger to be ſlaine by an Angel, becauſe his ſonne was not circumciſed. Neuertheles S. Auguſtin (li. 3. c. 18. cont. Inlian. li. 16. c. 27. ciuit. and in other places) proueth that this commination pertained alſo to infants. whoſe iudgement is confirmed by the 70. Interpreters adding for explication theſe wordes, \+it the eight day\+it*. which neceſſarily include infants, as ſubiect to this puniſhment. not for that they could tranſgreſſe this precept, or commit a new ſinne, but for lack of circumciſion. The reaſon wherof is, for that God now determined this only remedie for original ſinne, in the male ſex of Abrahams ſeede, in place of ſacrifice, or other profeſſion of faith vſed before, and that in caſe it could conueniently be applied for otherwiſe the former remedies were ſtil aualable. ³ Concerning the other difficultie manie ancient fathers expound theſe wordes, \+it shal be deſtroyed out of his people\+it*, of only temporal puniſhment; either death, as the like phraſe ſignifieth, Exodi. 12. \+it VVhoſoeuer shal eate leuened bread, his ſoul shal perish out of Iſrael\+it*; or temporal ſeparation from the people of God, as Num. 19. \+it Euerie one that toucheth the dead corſe, shal perish out of Iſrael\+it*. But S. Auguſtin (li. 3. c. 18. cont. Iulian. li 5. hypog. & alibi) S Gregorie (li. 4. Moral. c. 2. &. 3.) S. Proſper (li. de promiſs. Dei p. 1. c. 14.) S. Beda (li 2. in Lucam. c. 8) and S. Bernard (ſer. 3. de circum) vnderſtand this threatning not only of temporal puniſhment, but alſo of eternal ſeparation from God, and the ſocietie of Saints. And that alſo infants are ſo ſecluded only for lack of this remedie, as now children which dye without Baptiſme, committing no new fault are depriued of the viſion of God, for their original ſinne, not remitted. VVherupon is geathered that albeit Circumciſion was principally inſtituted to diſtinguish the people of God, ⁴which ſhould come of Abrahams ſeede, from other nations, yet it was alſo for remiſſion of original ſinne: not in al, but in thoſe to whom God appointed this particular remedie. \fq ¹ \ft Two difficulties. \fq ² \ft To whom the punishment pertained when circumcision was omitted. \fq ³ \ft what punishment was threatned. \fq ⁴ \ft Circumcision instituted to distinguish the people of God and for remedie of original sinne in some persons, but not in al.\ef*
\v 15 God ſaid alſo to Abraham: Sarai thy wife thou shalt not cal Sarai, but Sara.
\v 16 And I wil bleſſe her, and of her I wil giue thee a ſonne, whom I wil bleſſe, and he shalbe into nations, and kings of peoples shal ſpring of him.
\v 17 Abraham fel vpon his face, & \f c \fr 17.17 \ft Abraham laughed not doubting but reioycing. \+it S. Ambro. li. de Abraham c. 4. S. Aug. li. 16. c. 26. de ciuit\+it*.\f* laughed, ſaying in his hart: Shal troweſt thou to him that is an hundred yeare old a ſonne be borne? and Sara that is nyntie yeares old shal she beare?
\v 18 And he ſaid to God: I would that Iſmael may liue before thee.
\v 19 And God ſaid to Abraham: Sara thy wife shal beare thee a ſonne, and thou shalt cal his name Iſaac, and I wil eſtablish my couenant to him for a perpetual couenant, and to his ſeed after him.
\v 20 Concerning Iſmael alſo I haue heard thee. behold, I wil bleſſe him, and encreaſe, and multiplie him exceadingly: twelue dukes shal he beget, and I wil make him into a great nation.
\v 21 But my couenant I wil eſtablish with \f d \fr 17.21 \ft Temporal bleſſings were common to Iſmael, but ſpiritual pertained properly to Iſaac and Iſrael, & their ſucceſſeſſors.\f* Iſaac, whom Sara shal bring forth to thee at this time an other yeare.
\v 22 And when he had leaft of ſpeaking with him, God aſcended from Abraham.
\v 23 And Abraham tooke Iſmael his ſonne, and al the homebred of his houſe: and al whom he had bought, al the males of al the men of his houſe: and he circumciſed the flesh of their prepuce forthwith the very ſame day, as God had commanded him.
\v 24 Abraham was nyntie and nyne yeares old, when he circumciſed the flesh of his prepuce.
\v 25 And Iſmael his ſonne was ful thirtene yeares old at the time of his circumciſion.
\v 26 The ſelfſame day was Abraham circumciſed and Iſmael his ſonne.
\v 27 And \f e \fr 17.27 \ft Conformitie in Religion conſerueth peace in euery familie. \+it Tho. Anglus in hunc locum\+it*.\f* al the men of his houſe, as wel the homebred, as the bought ſeruantes and ſtrangers were circumciſed togeather.
\c 18
\cd Angels intertained as gheſts by Abraham 10. tel when Sara shal beare a ſonne, wherat she laughing, they cōfirme that they had ſaid. 16. They alſo fortel the deſtruction of Sodom. 22. for which Abraham prayed ſix times.
\p
\v 1 AND God appeared to him in the vale of mambre as he ſat in the dore of his tent, in the verie heat of the day.
\v 2 And when he had lifted vp his eyes, there appeared to him three men ſtanding nere vnto him: whom after he had ſene, he ranne to meete them from the dore of his tent, and adored to the ground.
\v 3 And He ſaid \f a \fr 18.3 \ft Abraham ſaw three, and adored one. profeſſing three diuine perſons and one God. \+it S. Aug. li. 16. c. 29. ciuit\+it*.\f* Lord, if I haue found grace in thy ſight, goe not paſt thy ſeruant: \x - \xt Heb. 13.\x*
\v 4 but I wil fetch a little water, and wash ye your feete, and reſt ye vnder the tree.
\v 5 And I wil ſet a morſel of bread, and ſtrengthen your hart, afterward you ſhal paſſe: for therfore are you come aſide to your ſeruāt. Who ſaid: Do as thou haſt ſpoken:
\v 6 Abraham made haſt into the tent to Sara, and ſaid to her: Make haſt, temper togeather three meaſures of floure, and make harth cakes.
\v 7 But him ſelfe ranne to the heard, and tooke from thence a calfe verie tender and verie good, and gaue it to a young man: who made haſt and boiled it.
\v 8 He tooke alſo butter and mylke, and the calfe which he had boyled, and ſet before them: but him ſelfe did ſtand beſide them vnder the tree.
\v 9 And when they had eaten, they ſaid to him: Where is Sara thy wife? He anſwered: Loe she is in the tent.
\v 10 To whom he ſaid: Returning I wil come to thee at this time, life accompaning, and Sara thy wife ſhal haue a ſonne. \x - \xt Rom. 9.\x*Which when Sara heard, she laughed behind the dore of the tent.
\v 11 And they were both aged, and farre entred in yeares, and it ceaſed to be with Sara after the maner of wemen.
\v 12 Who laughed ſecreatly ſaying: After I am waxen old, & my Lord is an old one, shal I geue my ſelfe to pleaſure?
\v 13 And our Lord ſaid to Abraham: Why did Sara laugh, ſaying: Shal I an old woman beare a child in deed?
\v 14 Is there any thing hard to God? According to appointment I wil returne to thee this verie ſelfe ſame time, life accompaynig, and Sara ſhal haue a ſonne.
\v 15 Sara denied, ſaying: I laughed not: being much afraid. But our Lord: \f b \fr 18.15 \ft Abraham laughing with admiration for ioy was not reprehended, but Sara laughing of diffidence was reprehended, by him that ſeeth the hart. \+it S. Aug. q. 36. in Gen. & li. 16. c. 31. ciuit\+it*.\f* It is not ſo, ſaith he: but thou didſt laugh.
\v 16 When the men therfore were riſen vp from thence, they turned their eyes againſt Sodome: and Abraham did goe with them, bringing them on the way.
\v 17 And our Lord ſaid: Can I conceale from Abraham the things which I wil doe:
\v 18 wheras he shal be into a nation great, and verie ſtrong, and in him are TO BE \sc BLESSED al the nations of the earth\sc*?
\v 19 For I know that he wil commande his children, and his houſe after him that they kepe the way of the Lord, and doe iudgement and iuſtice: that for Abrahams ſake the Lord may bring to effect al the things that he hath ſpoken vnto him. Therfore ſaid our Lord.
\v 20 The crye of Sodome, and Gomorre is multiplied, and their ſinne is aggrauated excedingly.
\v 21 I wil deſcend, and ſee whether they haue in acte accomplished the crye that is come to me: or whether it be not ſo, that I may know.
\v 22 And they turned them ſelues from thence, and went their way to Sodome: but Abraham as yet ſtood before our Lord.
\v 23 And approching he ſaid: what! wilt thou deſtroy the iuſt with the wicked?
\v 24 \f c \fr 18.24 \ft what a wall are iuſt men to their countrie? their faith ſaueth vs, their iuſtice defendeth vs from deſtruction. \+it S. Amb. li. 1. de Abraham. c. 6\+it*.\f* If there ſhalbe fiftie iuſt perſons in the citie, shal they perish withal? and wilt thou ſpare that place for fiftie iuſt, if they be therin?
\v 25 Be it farre from thee, that thou doe this thing, and that thou kil the iuſt with the wicked, and that the iuſt be in like caſe as the wicked, this is not beſeeming thee: which iudgeſt al the earth, no thou wilt not do this iudgement.
\v 26 And our Lord ſaid to him: If I shal find in Sodome fiftie iuſt perſons within the citie, I wil ſpare the whole place for their ſake.
\v 27 And Abraham anſwered, and ſaid: Becauſe I haue once begunne, I wil ſpeake to my Lord, wheras I am duſt and ashes.
\v 28 What if there shal be fiue leſſe then fiftie iuſt perſons? wilt thou for fortie fiue deſtroy the whole citie? And he ſaid: I wil not deſtroy it, if I shal finde fiue and fourtie.
\v 29 And againe he ſaid vnto him: But if fourtie shalbe found there, what wilt thou doe? He ſaid: I wil not ſtrike it for fourties ſake.
\v 30 Lord, ſaith he, be not angrie I beſech thee, if I ſpeake: what if thirtie shal be found there? He anſwered: I wil not doe it, if I shal find thirtie there.
\v 31 Becauſe, ſaith he, I haue once begunne, I wil ſpeake to my Lord: What if twentie shalbe founde there? He ſaid: I wil not deſtroy it for twenties ſake.
\v 32 I beſeech thee, ſaith he, be not angrie Lord, if I ſpeake yet once more: What if tenne shalbe found there? And he ſaid: I wil not deſtroy it for tennes ſake.
\v 33 And our Lord departed after that he ceaſed to ſpeake vnto Abraham: and Abraham returned into his place.
\c 19
\cd Lot receiuing Angels in his houſe, is abuſed by the Sodomites. 12. He with his wife (26. who for looking back is turned into a ſtatua of ſalt) and his two daughters are deliuered. 24. Sodome and Gomorre are burned. 31. Lot lieth vnwitting with both his daughters, begat of them Moab and Ammon, of whom came the Moabites and Ammonites.
\p
\v 1 AND the two \f a \fr 19.1 \ft Abraham & Lot by hoſpitalitie merited to receiue Angels in ſteed of men. Heb. 13.\f* angels came to Sodome at euen, and Lot ſitting in the gates of the citie. Who when he had ſene them, roſe vp and went to meete them: and adored proſtrate vnto the ground,
\v 2 and ſaid: I beſech you, my Lords, turne into the houſe of your ſeruant, and lodge there: wash your feet, and in the morning you shal go forth on your way. Who ſaid: No, but we wil abide in the ſtreat.
\v 3 He compelled them earneſtly to turne in vnto him: and when they were entred into his houſe, he made them a banquet, and baked vnleauened bread, and they did eate.
\v 4 And before they went to bed, the men of the citie beſet the houſe from young to old, al the people togeather.
\v 5 And they called Lot, and ſaid to him: Where are the men that came in to thee at night? bring them forth hither that we may know them.
\v 6 Lot going forth to them, and shutting the dore after him, ſaid:
\v 7 Doe not ſo, I beſech you, my brethren, doe not commit this euil.
\v 8 I haue two daughters, which as yet haue not knowen man: I wil bring them forth to you, and abuſe you them as it shal pleaſe you, ſo that you do no euil to theſe men, becauſe they are entred vnder the shadowe of my roofe.
\v 9 But they ſaid: Get thee backe thither. And againe: Thou cameſt in, ſaid they, as a ſtranger, what to be a iudge? Thy ſelfe therfore we wil afflict more then theſe. And they did violence to Lot exceadingly: and it was euen nowe at the point that they would break the dores.
\v 10 And behold the men put forth their hand, and drew in Lot vnto them, and shut the dore:
\v 11 and them, that were without, they ſtroke with blyndnes from the leaſt to the greateſt, ſo that they could not find the dore.
\v 12 And they ſaid to Lot: Haſt thou here anie of thine? ſonne in law, or ſonnes, or daughters, al that are thine, bring them out of this citie:
\v 13 for we wil deſtroy this place, for that \f b \fr 19.13 \ft This ſinne crieth to heauen for reuenge.\f* their crye is waxen lowde before our Lord, who hath ſent vs to deſtroy them.
\v 14 Therfore Lot went forth, and ſpake to his ſonnes in lawe that were to take his daughters, and ſaid: Ariſe: get you forth out of this place, becauſe our Lord wil deſtroy this citie. And he ſemed vnto them to ſpeake as it were in ieſt.
\v 15 And when it was morning, the angels vrged him, ſaying: Ariſe, take thy wife, and the two daughters which thou haſt: leaſt thou alſo perish withal in the wickednes of the citie.
\v 16 He lingring, they tooke his hand, and the hand of his wife, and of his two daughters, becauſe our Lord ſpared him.
\v 17 And they led him forth, and ſet him without the citie: and there they ſpake to him, ſaying: Saue thy life: looke not backe, neither ſtay thou in al the countrie about: but ſaue thy ſelfe in the mountaine, leſt thou alſo perish withal.
\v 18 And Lot ſaid to them: I beſeech thee my Lord,
\v 19 becauſe thy ſeruant hath fonnd grace before thee, and thou haſt magnified thy mercie, which thou haſt wrought with me, in that thou wouldeſt ſaue my life, and ſafe I can not be in the mountaine, leſt perhaps the euil catch me, and I dye.
\v 20 There is this citie hereby at hand, wherunto I may flee, a little one, and I ſhalbe ſafe in it: is it not a little one, and my life shalbe ſaued?
\v 21 And he ſaid to him: Behold alſo in this point I haue heard thy prayers, not to ouerthrow the citie for which thou haſt ſpoken.
\v 22 Make haſt, and be ſaued there: becauſe I can not doe any thing til thou enter in thither. Therfore the name of that citie was called \f 1 \fr 19.22 \ft \+it a litle one\+it*\f* Segor.
\v 23 The ſunne was riſen vpon the earth, & Lot entred into Segor.
\v 24 Therfore our Lord rained vpon Sodome and Gomorre brimſtone & fire from our Lord out of heauen:
\v 25 and he ſubuerted theſe cities, and al the countrie about, al the inhabitants of the cities, and al things that ſpring of the earth.
\v 26 And his wife \f c \fr 19.26 \ft Lots wife turned into ſalt, admonisheth the ſeruants of God to procede in vertue, & not to looke back to vice. \+it Luc. 17. S. Aug. li. 16. c. 30. ciuit\+it*.\f* looking behind her, was turned into a ſtatua of ſalt.
\v 27 And Abraham getting vp early in the morning, there where before he had ſtood with our Lord,
\v 28 beheld Sodome & Gomorre, and the whole land of that countrie: and he ſaw the cinders riſe vp from the earth as it were the ſmoke of a fornace.
\v 29 For when God ſubuerted the citties of that countrie, he \f d \fr 19.29 \ft Lot neither perfect, nor very wicked was deliuered for Abrahams ſake. \+it S. Aug. q. 45 in Gen\+it*.\f* remenbring Abraham, deliuered Lot out of the ſubuerſion of the cities wherein he had dwelt.
\v 30 And Lot aſcended out of Segor, and abode in the mountaine, his two daughters alſo with him (for he was afraid to abide in Segor) and he abode in a caue him ſelfe, and his two daughters with him.
\v 31 And the elder ſaid to the younger: Our father is old, and there is no man left on the earth, that may companie with vs after the maner of the whole earth.
\v 32 Come, let vs make him drunke with wine, and let vs lie with him, that we may preſerue ſeed of our father.
\v 33 They therfore made their father to drinke wine that night: and the elder went in, and lay with her father: but he perceaued not, neyther when his daughter lay downe, nor when she roſe vp.
\v 34 The next day alſo the elder ſaid to the younger: Behold I lay yeſternight with my father, let vs make him drinke wine alſo this night, and thou shalt lye with him, that we may ſaue ſeed of our father.
\v 35 They made their father drinke wine that night alſo, and the younger daughter went in, and lay with him: and neyther then truly did he perceaue when she lay downe, or when ſhe roſe vp.
\v 36 The two daughters therfore of Lot were with child by their father.
\v 37 And the elder bare a ſonne and ſhe called his name Moab: he is, the \f e \fr 19.37 \ft Moabites & Ammonites were two diſtinct nations. perhaps of the 72. See. p. 43.\f* father of the Moabites euen to this preſent day.
\v 38 The younger alſo bare a ſonne, and ſhe called his name Ammon, that is the ſonne of my people: he is the father of the Ammonites euen to this day.
\c 20
\cd Abraham ſeiorning in Geraris, his wife is taken into King Abimelechs houſe, but by Gods commandement is reſtored vntouched, 14. with great giftes, 17. and Abraham praying Abimelechs houſe is cured.
\p
\v 1 ABRAHAM remoued from thence into the ſouth countrie, and dwelt betwene Cades, and Sur: and he liued as a pilgrime in Gerara.
\v 2 And he ſaid of Sara his wife: She is \f a \fr 20.2 \ft See pag. 52.\f* my ſiſter. Abimelech therfore the king of Gerara ſent, and tooke her.
\v 3 And God came to Abimelech in a dreame by night, and ſaid to him: Loe thou shalt dye for the woman that thou haſt taken: for she hath a huſband.
\v 4 But Abimelech had not touched her, and he ſaid: Lord wilt thou kil a nation that is ignorant and iuſt?
\v 5 Did not he ſay to me: She is my ſiſter: and she ſay, He is my brother? in ſimplicitie of my hart, and cleanenes of my hands haue I done this.
\v 6 And God ſaid to him: And I do know that thou dideſt it with a ſincere hart: and therfore I kept thee that thou shouldeſt not ſinne againſt me, and I permitted not that thou shouldeſt touch her.
\v 7 Now therfore reſtore the wife to her huſband, becauſe he is a prophet: and he ſhal pray for thee, and thou ſhalt liue: but if thou wilt not reſtore her, know thou that dying thou shalt dye, thou and al things that are thine.
\v 8 And Abimelech forthwith riſing vp in the night, called al his ſeruantes: and he ſpake al theſe words to their eares, and al the men were ſore afraid.
\v 9 And Abimelech called alſo for Abraham, and ſaid to him: What haſt thou done to vs? what haue we offended againſt thee, that thou haſt brought vpon me and vpon my kingdom a great ſinne? that which thou oughteſt not to doe, thou haſt done to vs.
\v 10 And again expoſtulating, he ſaid: What ſaweſt thou, that thou dideſt this?
\v 11 Abraham anſwered: I thought with my ſelfe, ſaying: Perhaps there is not the feare of God in this place: and they wil kil me for my wife:
\v 12 howbeit otherwiſe alſo in verie deed she is my ſiſter, \f b \fr 20.12 \ft Not his fathers owne daughter, but of his progenie. \+it S. Aug. li. 22 cont. Fauſt c. 35. & li. 16. c. 19. & 30. de ciuit\+it*.\f* the daughter of my father, and not the daughter of my mother, and I tooke her to wife.
\v 13 And after that God brought me out of my fathers houſe, I ſaid to her: This mercie thou shalt doe with me: In euerie place, which we shal come vnto, thou shalt ſay that I am thy brother.
\v 14 Therfore Abimelech \f c \fr 20.14 \ft Kinges of the earth eſteming the Church only for a laudable people of God, ſeeke to ſubiect her to them ſelues: but knowing her to be the inuiolable ſpouſe of Chriſt, ſubiect them ſelues to her, and offer to her moſt honorable giftes. \+it S. Aug. li. 22. cont. Fauſt. c. 38\+it*.\f* tooke ſhepe & oxen, and ſeruants, and handmayds, and gaue to Abraham: and reſtored to him Sara his wife,
\v 15 and ſaid: The land is before you, dwel wherſoeuer it shal pleaſe thee.
\v 16 And to Sara he ſaid: Behold I haue geuen thy brother a thouſand peeces of ſiluer, this shal ſerue thee for a veile of thine eyes to al that are with thee, and whither ſoeuer thou shalt goe, remenber alſo thou waſt taken.
\v 17 And Abraham praying, God healed Abimelech and his wife, and his handmaids, and they bare children:
\v 18 for our Lord had cloſed vp euerie matrice of the houſe of Abimelech for Sara Abrahams wife.
\c 21
\cd Iſaac is borne. 4. circumciſed, 8. and weaned. 9. Agar and Iſmael are put forth of Abrahams houſe, 15. but after deſolation are nourished, and proſper in the deſert. 22. King Abimelech and Abraham make a league confirming it with oath. \f - \ft The ſixth part of this book.\f*
\p
\v 1 AND \f 1 \fr 21.1 \ft Of the progeny and other bleſſings of Abraham, Iſaac, and Jacob.\f* our Lord viſited Sara, as he had promiſed: and fulfilled the things which he ſpake.
\v 2 And she conceaued and bare a ſonne in her old age, at the time that God had foretold her.
\v 3 And Abraham called the name of his ſonne, which Sara bare him, \f a \fr 21.3 \ft Abraham and Sara laughed (he admiring, she doubting) at the joyful promiſe of a ſon, and therefore he is called Iſaac, which ſignifieth laughter. \+it St. Auguſtine li. 16. c. 31. civit\+it*.\f* Iſaac:
\v 4 and he circumciſed him the eight day, as God had commanded him,
\v 5 when he was an hundred yeares old: for at this age of his father, was Iſaac borne.
\v 6 And Sara ſaid: Laughter God hath made to me: whoſoeuer shal heare of it, wil laugh with me.
\v 7 And again she ſaid: Who that shal heare of it would beleue Abraham, that Sara gaue ſucke to a ſonne, which she bare him now being an old man?
\v 8 The child therfore grewe, and was weined: and Abraham made a great feaſt in the day of his weining.
\v 9 And when Sara had ſenne the ſonne of Agar the Ægyptian playing with Iſaac her ſonne, she ſaid to Abraham:
\v 10 Caſt out this handmaid, and her ſonne: for the ſonne of the handmaid shal not be heire with my ſonne Iſaac.
\v 11 Abraham tooke this greuouſly for his ſonne.
\v 12 To whom God ſaid: Let it not ſeme greuous to thee for the boy, and for thy handmaid: al things that Sara shal ſay to thee, heare her voice: becauſe \f b \fr 21.12 \ft See chap. 17. v. 21.\f*\f 1 \fr 21.12 \ft Rom. 9. Heb. 11.\f* in Iſaac shal ſeed de called to thee. \ef - \fr 21.12 \fq Let it not. \ft ¹ It ſemed ſo hard, inhumaine, and vniuſt to caſt Agar and Iſmael out of the houſe, that Abraham would not haue done it, onles God had commanded him in this to heare the voice of Sara. But when he knew Gods wil therin, not reſpecting flesh and bloud, nor diſputing further of the lawfulnes of the fact, ſent them both away, commending them to Gods protection: and ſo by this ſeparation the familie was made quiet. ² It repreſented alſo a notable Myſterie of the Synagog of the Iewes & Church of Chriſt. As S. Auguſtin ³ doth excellently expound the ſame, according to S. Paul (Gal. 4.) That Iſmaels playing with Iſaac was perſecution, becauſe it tended to peruert him, ſhewing it to be a greater iniurie, to delude and deceiue anie, in drawing them to new and particular companies, then corporally to perſecute them: ⁴ and that the Church, and Catholique Princes punish heretikes for their good, ⁵to make them returne to the truth, or finally to ceaſe from ſeducing others. ⁶ \+it Ecce libera afflixit ancillam, & non illam vocat perſecutionem Apoſtolus, ludit ſeruus cum domino, & perſecutionem vocat. Afflictio iſta non vocatur perſecutio, & luſio illa vocatur perſecutio\+it*. Behold the freewoman afflicted the handmaide, and the Apoſtle calleth it not perſecution, the ſeruant playeth with the maiſter, and he calleth it perſecution. ⁷This affliction is not called perſecution, and that play is called perſecution. Againe, ⁸he ſayeth of Sara, \+it Illuſionem vidit in illo luſu, animaduertit ſerui ſuperbiam, diſplicuit illi, eiecit foras\+it*. She ſaw foule play in that play, she noted the ſeruants pride, it diſpleaſed her, she caſt him forth of the dores. S. Hierom ⁹ alſo teacheth that the ſpiritual neuer perſecuteth the carnal: but ſpareth him as his ruſtical brother, knowing that he may in time be profitable. \fq ¹ \ft Separation to be made in families, when iust cause requireth. \fq ² \ft Mystical sense. \fq ³ \ft \+it li. 15. c. 2. civit.\+it* \fq ⁴ \ft \+it li. 15. c. 2. ciuit\+it*. \fq ⁵ \ft The true Church doth not persecute but iustly punisheth offenders. \fq ⁶ \ft \+it Tract. 11. in Ioan\+it*. \fq ⁷ \ft Heretikes and other infidels do persecute when either by word, or sword they impugne the truth. \fq ⁸ \ft \+it Tract. 12\+it*. \fq ⁹ \ft \+it in 4. Gal\+it*\ef*
\v 13 But the ſonne alſo of the handmaid I wil make into a great nation, becauſe he is thy ſeede.
\v 14 Abraham therfore roſe vp in the morning, and taking bread and a bottle of water, put it vpon her shoulder, and deliuered the boy and diſmiſt her. Who went away, and wandred in the wilderneſſe of Berſabee.
\v 15 And when the water in the bottle was ſpent, ſhe caſt the boy vnder one of the trees, that were there.
\v 16 And ſhe went her way, and ſate ouer againſt a great way of as farre as a bowe can caſt. for she ſaid: I wil no ſee the child dying: and ſitting ouer againſt, she lifted vp her voice and wept.
\v 17 And God heard the voice of the boy: and an angel of God called Agar from heauen, ſaying: What doeſt thou Agar? feare not: for God hath heard the voice of the boy, from the place wherin he is.
\v 18 Ariſe, take vp the boy, and hold his hand: for into a great nation wil I make him.
\v 19 And God opened her eyes: who ſeing a wel of water, went, and filled the bottle, and gaue the boy to drinke.
\v 20 And God was with him: who grew, and abode in the wildernes, and became a young man archer.
\v 21 And he dwelt in the wildernes of Pharan, and his mother tooke a wife for him out of the land of Ægypt.
\v 22 The ſame time ſaid Abimelech, and Phicol the general of his armie to Abraham: God is with thee in al things which thou doeſt.
\v 23 Sweare therfore by God, not to hurt me, and my poſteritie, and my ſtocke: but according to the mercie, that I haue done thee, thou shalt doe to me, and to the land wherin thou haſt liued a ſtranger.
\v 24 And Abraham ſaid: I wil ſweare.
\v 25 And he rebuked Abimelech for the wel of water, which his ſeruants had taken away by force.
\v 26 And Abimelech anſwered: I knew not who did this thing: yea and thy ſelfe dideſt not tel me, and I heard not of it but to day.
\v 27 Abraham therfore tooke sheepe and oxen, and gaue to Abimelech: and both of them made a league.
\v 28 And Abraham ſet ſeuen ewe lambes of the flocke apart.
\v 29 To whom Abimelech ſaid: What meane theſe ſeuen ewe lambes, which thou haſt made to ſtand apart?
\v 30 But he ſaid: Seuen ewe lambes shalt thou take at my hand: that they may be a teſtimonie for me, that I digged this wel.
\v 31 Therfor was that place called \f 1 \fr 21.31 \ft \+it wel of oath\+it*\f* Berſabee: becauſe there both did ſweare.
\v 32 And they made a league for the wel of oath.
\v 33 And Abimelech aroſe, and Phicol the general of his armie, and they returned to the land of the Paleſtines. But Abraham planted a groue in Berſabee, and called therupon the name of our Lord God eternal.
\v 34 And he was a ſeiorner in the land of the Paleſtines manie dayes.
\c 22
\cd The faith and obedience of Abraham is proued in his redines to ſacrifice his ſonne Iſaac. 11. He is ſtayed from the act by an Angel. 16. Former promiſes are renewed to him. 20. And his brother Nachor hath alſo much iſſue.
\p
\v 1 WHICH \x - \xt Judith. 8.\x* things being done, \f 2 \fr 22.1 \ft The third prophecie in the office before Maſſe on Eaſter eue. And the firſt on whitſuneue.\f* God tempted Abraham, and ſaid to him; Abraham, Abraham. But he anſwered: Here I am. \ef - \fr 22.1 \fq Tempted. \ft ¹ ² God tempteth none to euil (as S. Iames teacheth) but by trial and experiment maketh men knowen to the world, as here Abrahams faith and ſingular obedience were manifeſted, when he doubted not \+it To offer his only begotten in Sacrifice, accounting that God is able to raiſe vp euen from the dead\+it*. ³ VVherupon he receiued his ſonne againe \+it in parable\+it*, that is, in figure and myſterie of Chriſt dead and aliue againe. ⁴ Iſaac alſo in this action ſignified the diuinitie of Chriſt which ſuffered not, and the ramme among the briers figured his humanitie, that ſhould be offered in Sacrifice to God. \fq ¹ \ft God tempteth not to euil, but by experience maketh knowen what vertue is in men. \fq ² \ft \+it Iac. 1. 13. Heb. 11. 19.\+it* \fq ³ \ft \+it S. Aug. li. 16. c. 32. ciuit. Theod. q. 72. in Gen\+it*. \fq ⁴ \ft Isaac figured Chists diuinitie, the Ramme his humanitie.\ef*
\v 2 \x - \xt Heb. 11\x* He ſaid to him: \f a \fr 22.2 \ft This hiſtorie is clere, and a moſt notorious example of perfect obedience.\f* Take thy only begotten ſonne, whom thou loueſt, Iſaac, and goe into the Land of viſion: and there thou ſhalt offer him for an holocauſt vpon one of the mountaines which I wil shew thee.
\v 3 Therfore Abraham riſing vp in the night, ſadled his aſſe: taking with him two young men, and Iſaac his ſonne: and when he had cut wood for the holocauſt, he went his way to the place which God had commanded him.
\v 4 And the third day, lifting vp his eyes, he ſawe the place afarre of:
\v 5 and he ſaid to his young men: Tarie you here with the aſſe: I and the boy going with ſpede as farre as yonder, after we haue adored, wil returne to you.
\v 6 He tooke alſo the wood of the holocauſt, and laid it vpon Iſaac his ſonne: and himſelfe caried in his hands fire and a ſword. And as they went on together,
\v 7 Iſaac ſaid to his father: My father. And he anſwered: What wilt thou ſonne? Behold, ſaith he, fire & wood: where is the victime of the holocauſt?
\v 8 And Abraham ſaid: God wil prouide vnto himſelfe the victime of the holocauſt, my ſonne. They went on therfore together:
\v 9 and they came to the place which God had shewed him, wherin he builed an altar, and laid the wood in order vpon it: and when he had bound Iſaac his ſonne, he laid him on the altar vpon the pile of wood.
\v 10 And he ſtretched forth his hand, and caught the ſword, for to ſacrifice his ſonne.
\v 11 And behold an angel of our Lord from heauen cried, ſaying: Abraham, Abraham. Who anſwered: Here I am.
\v 12 And he ſaid to him: Stretch not forth thy hand vpon the boy, neither doe thou any thing to him: now haue I knowen that thou feareſt God, and haſt not ſpared thine onlie begotten ſonne for my ſake.
\v 13 Abraham lifted vp his eyes, and ſaw behind his backe a ramme amongſt the briers ſticking faſt by the hornes, which he tooke and offered an holocauſt in ſtede of his ſonne.
\v 14 And he called the name of that place, \f b \fr 22.14 \ft It is a gratful and religious thing, by naming of places to conſerue the memorie of Gods benefites, that poſteritie may know them. \+it S. Chriſoſt. ho. 48. in Gen\+it*.\f* Our Lord ſeeth. Wherupon euen to this day it is ſaid, In the mountaine our Lord wil ſee.
\v 15 And the angel of our Lord called Abraham the ſecond time from heauen, ſaying:
\v 16 By my owne ſelfe haue I ſworne, ſaith the Lord: becauſe thou haſt done this thing, and haſt not ſpared thine onlie begotten ſonne for my ſake:
\v 17 I wil bleſſe thee, and I wil multiplie thy ſede as the ſtarres of heauen, and as the ſand that is by the ſea shore: thy ſede ſhal poſſeſſe the gates of his enemies,
\v 18 and in \sc thy ſede SHAL BE BLESSED al the nations of the earth\sc*, becauſe thou haſt obeyed my voice.
\v 19 Abraham returned to his young men; and they went to Berſabee together, and he dwelt there.
\v 20 Theſe things ſo being done, it was \f c \fr 22.20 \ft Nachors progenie is here mentioned to shew whence Rebecca came, whom Iſaac maried.\f* told Abraham that Melca alſo had borne children to Nachor his brother,
\v 21 Hus the firſtbegotten, & Buz his brother, and Camuel the father of the Sirians,
\v 22 and Caſed, and Azau. Pheldas alſo & Iedlaph,
\v 23 and Bathuel, of whom was borne Rebecca: theſe eight did Melcha beare, to Nachor Abrahams brother.
\v 24 And his concubine, named Roma, bare Tabee, and Gaham, and Tahas, and Maacha.
\c 23
\cd Sara dying Abraham ſolemnizeth her funeral. 4. byeth a field with a duble caue of Ephron, 15. for four hundreth ſicles. 19. and there burieth her.
\p
\v 1 AND Sara liued an hundred twentie ſeuen yeares.
\v 2 And she died in the citie of Arbee which is Hebron, in the land of Chanaan: And Abraham came to mourne, and weepe for her.
\v 3 And after that he was riſen vp from \f a \fr 23.3 \ft A cleare example of religious office in burying the dead, See 2. Reg. 1. and 2. Paral. 35.\f* the funeral obſequies, he ſpake to the children of Heth, ſaying:
\v 4 I am a ſtranger and pilgrime among you: geue me the right of a ſepulchre with you, that I may burie my dead.
\v 5 The children of Heth anſwered, ſaying:
\v 6 My lord heare vs, thou art a prince of God among vs: in our principal ſepulchres burie thou thy dead: and no man can let thee but that in his owne monument thou mayeſt burie thy dead.
\v 7 Abraham roſe vp, and \f b \fr 23.7 \ft Adoration vſed for reuerence done to men. \+it See alſo c. 27 v. 29. c. 33. v. 3. 7 and S. Aug. q. 61. in Gen\+it*.\f* adored the people of the land, to wit the children of Heth:
\v 8 and he ſaid to them: If it pleaſe your ſoule that I burie my dead, heare me, and be interceſſors for me to Ephron the ſonne of Seor:
\v 9 that he geue me the duble caue, which he hath in the vttermoſt part of his field: for money to the worth therof let him deliuer it to me before you for poſſeſſion of a ſepulchre.
\v 10 And Ephron dwelt in the middeſt of the children of Heth. And Ephron made anſwer to Abraham in the hearing of al that went in at the gate of the citie, ſaying:
\v 11 No, it shal not be ſo, my lord, but thou rather harken to that which I doe ſay: The field I deliuer to thee, and the caue that is therin, in the preſence of the children of my people, burie thy dead.
\v 12 Abraham adored before the people of the land.
\v 13 And he ſpoke to Ephron, his people ſtanding round about: I beſeech thee to heare me: I wil geue money for the field: take it, and ſo I wil burie my dead in it.
\v 14 And Ephron anſwered:
\v 15 My lord, heare me. The ground which thou deſireſt, is worth foure hundred ſicles of ſiluer: this is the price betwen me and thee: but how much is this? burie thy dead.
\v 16 Which when Abraham had heard, he weyed the money, that Ephron had asked, in the hearing of the children of Heth, foure hundred ſicles of ſiluer of common currant money.
\v 17 And the field that before time was Ephrons, wherin was the duble caue, looking towards Mambre, aſwel it, as the caue, and al the trees therof in al the lymits therof round about:
\v 18 was made ſure to Abraham for a poſſeſſion, in the ſight of the children of Heth, and of al that went in at the gate of his citie.
\v 19 And ſo Abraham buried Sara his wife in the duble caue of the field, that looked towards Mambre, this is Hebron in the land of Chanaan.
\v 20 And the field was made ſure to Abraham, and the caue, that was in it, for a poſſeſſion to burie in, of the Children of Heth.
\c 24
\cd Abrahams ſeruant adiured and ſent by him into Meſopotamia, to ſeke a wife for Iſaac, 12. prayeth to God for a ſigne, findeth Rebecca, 34. and demanding her for this purpoſe, 50. with her parents, 58. and her owne conſent, she goeth with him, 67. is maried to Iſaac: who therby is comforted for the death of his mother.
\p
\v 1 AND Abraham was old, and of manie dayes: and our Lord had bleſſed him in al things.
\v 2 And he ſaid to the elder ſeruant of his houſe, which was ruler ouer al that he had: Put thy hand vnder my thighe,
\v 3 that I may adiure thee by our Lord, God of heauen and earth, that thou \f a \fr 24.3 \ft In chooſing a wife a vertuous ſtock and familie eſpecially true faith and religion, are before al other things to be conſidered & preferred. \+it S. Amb. li. 1. c. 9. de Abrah. S. Chriſoſt. ho. 48. in Gen\+it*.\f* take not a wife for my ſonne, of the daughters of the Cananites, among whom I dwel:
\v 4 but that thou goe vnto myne owne countrie and kindred, and thence take a wife for my ſonne Iſaac.
\v 5 The ſeruant anſwered: If the woman wil not come with me into this land, whether muſt I bring thy ſonne backe againe to the place, from whence thou dideſt come forth?
\v 6 And Abraham ſaid: Beware thou neuer bring my ſonne backe againe thither.
\v 7 Our Lord God of heauen, which tooke me out of my fathers houſe, and out of my natiue countrie, which ſpake to me, and ſware to me, ſaying: To thy ſede wil I geue this land: he shal ſend his angel before thee, and thou shalt take from thence a wife for my ſonne:
\v 8 but if the woman wil not folow thee, thou shalt not be bound by the oath: only bring not my ſonne thither againe.
\v 9 The ſeruant therfore put his hand vnder the thigh of Abraham his lord, and ſware to him vpon this worde.
\v 10 And he tooke tenne camels of his lords heard, and departed, of al his goods carying ſomething with him; and ſetting forward went on into Meſopotamia to the citie of Nachor.
\v 11 And when he had made the camels lye downe without the towne beſide a wel of water at euen, at the time when women are wont to come forth to drawe water, he ſaid:
\v 12 O Lord God of my lord Abraham, mete me to day, I beſeech thee, and doe mercifully with my maiſter Abraham.
\v 13 Behold I ſtand nigh to the fountaine of water, and the daughters of the inhabiters of this citie, wil come forth to drawe water.
\v 14 Therfore the maid, to whom I shal ſay: Bowe downe thy tankard that I may drinke: and she shal anſwere, Drinke, yea to thy camels alſo wil I geue drinke: she it is, whom thou haſt prouided for thy ſeruant Iſaac: and by this I shal vnderſtand, that thou haſt delt mercifully with my maiſter. \ef - \fr 24.14 \fq The maide to whom I shal ſay. \ft Obſeruation of ſpeaches called ominous, which are interpreted to ſignifie good or euil luck, ¹ are ſometimes ſuperſticious, & ſuggeſted by euil ſpirites, who now & then telling, or inſinuating ſome truth, get credite, and ſo allure men to attend to ſuch vaine, vncertaine, and vnlawful ſignes, as S. Auguſtin teſtifieth (li. 2. de Gen. ad lit. c. 17. & li. 12. c. 22.) ² Neuertheles ſuch ſignes are ſometimes lawfully obſerued, & deſired from God, as the ſame Doctor diſputeth (li. queſt. ſuper Gen q. 53) and S. Chriſoſtome teacheth more clerly (ho. 45. in Gen) likewiſe S. Theodoret (q. 73. in Gen) But whoſoeuer wil not erre in particular caſes, muſt folow either expreſſe Scripture, or the iudgement of the Church, ³ ⁴which is alwayes directed by the ſpirite of truth. And touching this prayer of Abrahams ſeruant, and his deſire of this determinate ſigne, to know the maide, whom God had prouided to be Iſaachs wife, the fathers generally hold that it was religious, deuout, and diſcrete. ⁵For he being careful of his maſters buſines, and not truſting his owne iudgement, but relying vpon that Abraham had ſaid, \+it Our Lord shal ſend his Angel before thee\+it*, commended ſo weightie a cauſe to God by prayer, the Angel ſuggeſting both to him to deſire, and to the maide to performe (as the euent ſheweth) ſuch qualities and vertues in her, as were moſt agreable to the great charitie and hoſpitalitie dayly practiſed in Abrahams houſe, moſt conuenient and neceſſarie (as he diſcretly conſidered) for that familie, and good of manie. The like obſeruations were approued in Gedeon, ⁶and Ionathas. ⁷And to pray for ſuch ſignes in ſome caſe, or for manifeſt miracles is alſo approued by the Apoſtles example praying God \+it To shevv by lote vvhich of the tvvo he had choſen to the Apoſtleship, in place of Iudas\+it*. ⁸ And that he would extend his hand to cures, & ſignes, & wonders, to be done by the name of his holie ſonne \+sc IESVS\+sc*. \fq ¹ \ft Ominous speaches sometimes supersticious. \fq ² \ft Some times lawful. \fq ³ \ft Holie scripture and the Church are iudges of doubtful obseruations. \fq ⁴ \ft \+it Ioan. 14.\+it* \fq ⁵ \ft Eliezers prayer, for a particular signe, was lawful, deuout, and discrete. \fq ⁶ \ft \+it 1. Reg 14\+it*. \fq ⁷ \ft \+it Act. 1. & 4\+it*. \fq ⁸ \ft \+it Iudic. 7.\+it*\ef*
\v 15 Neyther had he yet ended theſe wordes within him ſelfe, & behold Rebecca came forth, the daughter of Bathuel, the ſonne of Melcha wife to Nachor the brother of Abraham, hauing a tankard on her shoulder:
\v 16 a paſſing comely maide, & moſt beautiful virgin, & not knowen to man: and she was gone downe to the fountaine, and had filled her tankard, and came backe.
\v 17 And the ſeruant ranne to mete her, and ſaid: Geue me a little water to drinke of thy tankard.
\v 18 Who anſwered: Drinke my lord. And quickly she let downe the tankard vpon her arme, and gaue him drinke.
\v 19 And when he had druncke, she added: but for thy camels alſo I wil drawe water, til al doe drinke.
\v 20 And powring out the tankard into the troughes, she ranne backe to the wel to drawe water: and being drawen gaue it to al the camels.
\v 21 But he muſing beheld her with ſilence, deſirous to know whether our Lord had made his iourney proſperous, or not.
\v 22 And after that the camels had drunck, the man plucked forth golden earelettes, weying two ſicles: and as manie braceletts of tenne ſicles weight.
\v 23 And he ſaid to her: whoſe daughter art thou? shew me: is there anie place in thy fathers houſe to lodge?
\v 24 Who anſwered: I am the daughter of Bathuel, the ſonne of Melcha, whom she bare to Nachor.
\v 25 And she added, ſaying: Of ſtrawe alſo and hay we haue good ſtore, and a large place to lodge in.
\v 26 The man bowed himſelfe, and adored our Lord,
\v 27 ſaying: Bleſſed be the Lord God of my lord Abraham, that hath not taken away his mercie & truth from my lord, and hath brought me the ſtreight way into the houſe of my lords brother.
\v 28 The maide therfore ranne, and reported into \f b \fr 24.28 \ft Her father hauing perhaps manie wiues and euerie one a ſeueral houſe ſhe went to her mothes houſe.\f* her mothers houſe al things that she had heard.
\v 29 And Rebecca had a brother named Laban, who in al haiſt went forth to the man, where the fountaine was.
\v 30 And when he had ſeene the eareletts and braceletts in his ſiſters hands, and had heard al her words reporting: Theſe words ſpake the man vnto me: he came to the man which ſtoode beſide the camels, and nighe to the fountaine of water:
\v 31 and ſaid to him: Come in, thou bleſſed of our Lord: Why ſtandeſt thou without? I haue prepared the houſe, and a place for the camels.
\v 32 And he brought him in into his lodging: and he vnharneſſed the camels, and gaue ſtrawe and hay, and water to wash his feet, and of the men that were come with him.
\v 33 And bread was ſet before him. Who ſaid: I wil not eate, til I ſpeake my meſſage. He anſwered him: Speake.
\v 34 And he ſaid: I am the ſeruant of Abraham:
\v 35 and our Lord hath bleſſed my lord wonderfully, and he is magnified: and he hath geuen him sheepe, and oxen, ſiluer and gold, men ſeruants and wemen ſeruants, camels, and aſſes.
\v 36 And Sara my lordes wife hath borne my lord a ſonne in her old age, and he hath geuen him al things that he had.
\v 37 And my lord adiured me ſaying: Thou ſhalt not take a wife for my ſonne of the Chananites, in whoſe land I dwel:
\v 38 but thou shalt goe to my fathers houſe, and of mine owne kinred shalt thou take a wife for my ſonne:
\v 39 but I anſwered my Lord: What if the woman wil not come with me?
\v 40 Our Lord, ſaith he, in whoſe ſight I walke, wil ſend his angel with thee, and wil direct thy way: and thou shalt take a wife for my ſonne of myne owne kinred, and of my fathers houſe.
\v 41 Thou shalt be innocent from my curſe, when thou shalt come to my kinne, and they wil not geue her thee.
\v 42 I came therfore to day to the wel of water, and ſaid: O Lord God of my lord Abraham, if thou haſt directed my way, wherin I now walke,
\v 43 behold I ſtand beſides the wel of water, and the virgin, that shal come forth to drawe water, when she shal heare me ſay: Geue me a litle water to drinke of thy tankard:
\v 44 and ſhe shal ſay to me: Drinke both thou and for thy camels I wil drawe alſo: that is the woman, which our Lord hath prepared for my maiſters ſonne.
\v 45 And whileſt I pondered theſe things ſecretly with my ſelfe, Rebecca appeared comming with a tankard, which she caried vpon her shoulder: and she went downe to the fountaine, & drew water. And I ſaid to her: Geue me a litle to drinke.
\v 46 Who ſpedelie let downe the tankard from her shoulder, and ſaid to me: Drinke both thou, and to thy camels I wil geue drinke. I dranke, and ſhe watered the cammels.
\v 47 And I asked her, and ſaid: Whoſe daughter art thou? who anſwered: I am the daughter of Bathuel, the ſonne of Nachor, whom Melcha bare him. I hoong therfore earelettes to adorne her face, and I put braceletts vpon her hands.
\v 48 And proſtrate I adored our Lord, bleſſing the Lord God of my lord Abraham, who hath brought me the ſtraight way to take the daughter of my lords brother for his ſonne.
\v 49 Wherfore if you doe according to mercie and truth with my lord, shew me: but if it pleaſe you otherwiſe, that alſo tel me, that I may goe to the right hand, or to the left.
\v 50 And Laban and Bathuel anſwered: From our Lord the word hath proceded: we can not ſpeake any other thing with thee beſides his pleaſure.
\v 51 Behold Rebecca is before thee, take her and goe thy waies, and let her be the wife of thy lords ſonne, as our Lord hath ſpoken.
\v 52 Which when Abrahams ſeruant heard, falling downe he adored our Lord to the grounde.
\v 53 And taking forth veſſel of ſiluer, and gold, and garments, gaue them to Rebecca for a preſent. To her brothers alſo, and to her mother he offred giftes.
\v 54 A banket was made, and eating and drinking togeather they lodged there. And in the morning, the ſeruant aroſe, and ſaid: Diſmiſſe me, that I may goe to my lord.
\v 55 And her brother, and mother anſwered: Let the maide tarie at the leaſt tenne days with vs, and after she shal depart.
\v 56 Stay me not, ſaid he, becauſe our Lord hath directed my way: diſmiſſe me that I may goe on to my lord.
\v 57 And they ſaid: Let vs cal the maid, and \f c \fr 24.57 \ft As children ought not to mary without their parents good liking: ſo the parties owne conſent is moſt neceſſarie. S Amb. Epiſt. 43.\f* aske her wil.
\v 58 And being called, when she was come, they asked: Wilt thou goe with this man? who ſaid: I wil goe.
\v 59 They diſmiſſed her therfore, and her nurce, and Abrahams ſeruant, and his companie,
\v 60 wishing proſperitie to their ſiſter, and ſaying: Thou art our ſiſter, encreaſe thou into thouſand thouſands, and thy ſeed poſſeſſe the gates of their enemies.
\v 61 Therfore Rebecca, and her maides being ſet vpon camels, folowed the man: who with ſpeed returned vnto his lord; and
\v 62 the ſame time Iſaac walked along the way, that leadeth to the wel of the Liuing and the ſeing, ſo called: for he dwelt in the ſouth countrie;
\v 63 and he was gone forth to \f d \fr 24.63 \ft Suach ſignifieth to ſpeake conſideratly with hart or mouth. Here S. Ambroſe (li. 1. c. 1. de Iſaac) and S. Aug. (q. 69. in Gen) vnderſtand it of mental prayer.\f* meditate in the field, the day nowe being wel ſpent: and when he had caſt vp his eyes, he ſaw camels coming a farre of.
\v 64 Rebecca alſo, when she ſaw Iſaac, lighted of the camel,
\v 65 and ſaid to the ſeruant: Who is that man which cometh towards vs along the field? And he ſaid to her. The ſame is my lord. But she quickly taking her cloake, couered her ſelfe.
\v 66 And the ſeruant told Iſaac al things that he hed done.
\v 67 Who brought her into the tent of Sara his mother, and tooke her to wife: and he loued her ſo much, that it did moderate the ſorrowe, which was chanced of his mothers death.
\c 25
\cd Abraham hauing manie children by his wife Cetura, died at the age of 175. yeares: 12. Iſmael alſo hauing twelue ſonnes dukes, died. 19. Iſaac praying for his barren wife, she hath Eſau and Iacob twinnes. 30. Eſau ſelleth his firſt birth right to Iacob for a meſſe of potage.
\p
\v 1 AND Abraham maried an other wife named Cethura:
\v 2 which bare him Zamran, and Iecſan, and Madan, and Madian, and Ieſboc, and Sue.
\v 3 Iecſan alſo begat Saba and Dadan. The Children of Dadan were Aſſurim, and Latuſim, and Loomim.
\v 4 But alſo of Madian was borne Epha, and Opher, and Henoch, and Abida, and Eldaa: al theſe were the children of Cetura.
\v 5 And Abraham gaue al his poſſeſſions to Iſaac:
\v 6 and to the children of his concubines he gaue gifts, and ſeparated them from Iſaac his ſonne, whileſt himſelfe yet liued, to the eaſt countrie. \ef - \fr 25.6 \fq To the children of his concubines. \ft ¹ S Auguſtin (li. 16. c. 34. de ciuit) sheweth that both Agar and Cetura, being Abrahams lawful wiues (for ſo they are called in holie Scripture) are alſo called concubines, becauſe they had not like priuileges to Sara, whoſe ſonne was ſole heyre to his father, and the children of the others had only giftes (or mouable goods) not attayning to the promiſed kingdom. And al this for myſterie ſake. For Iſmael ſignified the carnal people before Chriſt, the children of Cetura prefigured Heretikes, ²who ſuppoſe themſelues to pertaine to the new Teſtament, but are ſeparated no leſſe then the Iewes from Chriſts Kingdom. And albeit there was alſo an other particular reaſon, why Agar was called concubine, becauſe she was a ſecond wife, the firſt then liuing, yet this lerned father ſaith, he did not ſee, why Cetura being maried after the death of Sara, should be called concubine, but only for this Myſterie. \fq ¹ \ft VVhy Agar & Cetura being lawful wiues are called concubines. \fq ² \ft Their children signified Pagaines & Heretikes.\ef*
\v 7 And the days of Abrahams life were a hundred ſeuentie and fiue yeares.
\v 8 And decaying dyed in \f a \fr 25.8 \ft The life of the iuſt hath fulnes of dayes though it be otherwiſe short; the daies of the wicked are void of fruict, be they manie or few. \+it S. Ambroſe li. de Abraham\+it*.\f* a good old age, and hauing liued a great time, and being ful of days: and was gethered to his people.
\v 9 And there buried him Iſaac and Iſmael his ſonnes in the duble caue, which was ſituated in the field of Ephron the ſonne of Seor the Hethite, ouer againſt Mambre,
\v 10 which he had bought of the children of Heth: there was he buried, and Sara his wife.
\v 11 And after his death God bleſſed Iſaac his ſonne, who dwelled beſide the wel of the Liuing and ſeing ſo named.
\v 12 Theſe are the generations of Iſmael the ſonne of Abraham, whom Agar the Ægyptian bare him, Saraes ſeruant: and
\v 13 theſe are the names of his children according to their calling and generations. The firſt begotten of Iſmael Nabaioth, then Cedar, and Adbeel, and Mabſam,
\v 14 Maſma alſo, and Duma, and Maſſa,
\v 15 Hadar, and Thema, and Iethur, and Naphis, and Cedma.
\v 16 Theſe are the ſonnes of Iſmael: and theſe are their names by their caſtles and townes, twelue princes of their tribes.
\v 17 And the yeares of Iſmaels life came to an hundred thirtie ſeauen, and decaying died, and was put vnto his people.
\v 18 And he dwelt from Heuila euen to Sur, which looketh towards Ægypt, as they enter to the Aſſirians, before the face of al his bretheren died he.
\v 19 Theſe alſo are the generations of Iſaac the ſonne of Abraham: Abraham begat Iſaac:
\v 20 who when he was fortie yeares old, tooke to wife Rebecca the daughter of Bathuel the Syrian of Meſopotamia, ſiſter to Laban.
\v 21 And Iſaac beſought our Lord for his wife, becauſe she was barren: who heard him, and made Rebecca to conceaue. \ef - \fr 25.21 \fq Heard him. \ft Notwithſtanding Gods aſſured promiſe, that Iſaach should haue iſſue (Gen. 21. v. 12) yet he prayeth inſtantly for the ſame. And Moyſes here attributeth Rebeccas conceiuing to Iſaachs prayer. ¹ wherby we ſee that Gods forſeing, predeſtinating, and promiſing exclude not, but in dede include ſecondarie cauſes, and ordinarie meanes, by which his eternal wil and pleaſure is fulfilled. For as God did forſee that Rebecca should haue children, ſo he did forſee, that Iſaac ſhould pray for it, and obtayne it; and the one was as ſure to come to paſſe as the other. And the ſame conſequence is true concerning eternal life as S. Gregorie teacheth. li. 1. c. 8. Dialog. \fq ¹ \ft Gods predestination and forseing include, & not exclude the meanes by which his wil is done.\ef*
\v 22 But the little ones ſtrugled in her wombe; who ſaid: If it ſhould be ſo with me, what nede was there to conceaue? And she \f b \fr 25.22 \ft S. Auguſtin (q. 72. in Gen) diſputeth but could not decide, whether Rebecca went to ſome Prieſt, or Prophet, or Altar, or whither els, or only retyred to priuate prayer.\f* went to conſult our Lord.
\v 23 Who anſwering ſaid: Two nations are in thy wombe, and two peoples shal be diuided out of thy wombe, and one people shal ouercome the other, and the elder shal ſerue the younger. \ef - \fr 25.23 \fq The elder shal ſerue the younger. \ft ¹ As before (c. 17. v. 21. &. c. 21. v. 12.) the couenant and great promiſes made to Abrahams ſede, are declared to pertaine only to Iſaac, and not to Iſmael, nor to the other brothers: ſo the ſame belong not to Eſau, the elder, but only to Iacob the younger ſonne of Iſaac, the Holie Ghoſt ſaying, \+it The elder shal ſerue the younger\+it*. And withal ſignifieth, (ſaith S. Auguſtin) ² that the elder people of the Iewes ſhal ſerue the younger Chriſtian people. For although it may be vnderſtood literally to be fulfilled, in that the Idumeans coming of Eſau, were ſubdued by King Dauid coming of Iacob; ³yet it is more conueniently beleeued, that this prophecie tended to a greater thing And what is this, but that which is euidently fulfilled in the Iewes and Chriſtians? An other great document of grace S. Paul geathereth vpon this Myſterie: that the twinnes being not yet borne, nor hauing done any thing good or euil, without anie good merites, the younger is elected, the elder reprobate. ⁴ ⁵For doubtles (ſaith S. Auguſtin) touching original ſinne they were both equal, and concerning proper ſinne, neither of them had anie at al. By which example he sheweth Gods mere mercie in the elect, and iuſtice in the reprobate. as is more largely noted in the Engliſh New Teſtament, vpon the ninth chapter to the Romanes. \fq ¹ \ft The covenant made to Abraham pertained only to Isaac and Jacob, not to the rest of his issue. \fq ² \ft \+it li. 16. c. 35. civit.\+it* \fq ³ \ft \+it The couenant made to Abraham pertaned only to Isaac, and Iacob, not to the rest of his issue\+it*. \fq ⁴ \ft li 16. c. 35. ciuit. \fq ⁵ \ft \+it 2. Reg. 8. Psal. 59\+it*.\ef*
\v 24 Now her time was come to be deliuered, and behold twinnes were found in her wombe.
\v 25 He that came forth firſt, was read, and al hearie in manner of a skinne: and his name was called Eſau. Immediatly the other coming forth, held his brothers plant in his hand: and therfore he called him Iacob.
\v 26 Threeſcore yeares old was Iſaac, when the litle ones were borne vnto him.
\v 27 Who being growne vp, Eſau became a man cunning in hunting, and a huſband man: but Iacob \f c \fr 25.27 \ft Holie Scriprure premonisheth Iacobs ſinceritie, leſt in the Myſteries folowing he might be ſuſpected of falſe dealing S. Aug. li. 16. c. 37. ciuit.\f* a plaine man dwelled in tents.
\v 28 Iſaac loued Eſau, becauſe he did eate of his hunting: and Rebecca loued Iacob.
\v 29 And Iacob boyled broth: to whom Eſau being come faynt out of the field,
\v 30 ſaid: Geue me of this read broth, becauſe I am exceeding faint. For which cauſe his name was called Edom.
\v 31 To whom Iacob ſaid: Sel me thy firſt-birth-right. \ef - \fr 25.31 \fq Sel me thy firſt birth right. \ft Iacob inſtructed by his mother, that God had choſen him in place of his brother Eſau (for to her God had reueled that the elder should ſerue the younger) ¹ did lawfully vſe this oportunitie to get Eſaus grant of the right pertaining to the firſt borne, but Eſau in ſelling it ſinned, ſhewing himſelfe an intemperate prophane man. Heb. 12. \fq ¹ \ft Iacob lawfully bought but Esau sinned in selling the first-birth-right.\ef*
\v 32 He anſwered, Loe I dye, what wil the firſt birth right auaile me?
\v 33 Iacob ſaid: Sweare therfore to me. Eſau ſware to him, and ſould his firſt-birth-right.
\v 34 And ſo taking bread and the rice broth, did eate, and drinke, and went his way; little eſteeming that he had ſold his firſt birth right.
\c 26
\cd Iſaac by reaſon of famine goeth into Gerara, 3. where God reneweth to him the promiſes made to Abraham. 9. King Abimelech blameth him for calling his wife his ſiſter. 15. the people enuying his wealth, quareleth for his welles 26. At laſt Abimelech maketh league with Iſaac.
\p
\v 1 AND when a famine was riſen in the land, after that ſterilitie, that had chanced in the dayes of Abraham, Iſaac went to Abimelech king of the Paleſtines into Gerara.
\v 2 And our Lord appeared to him, and ſaid: \f a \fr 26.2 \ft God by Abrahams examplar life inuited the Ægyptians to true religion: now commandeth Iſaac to ſtay in Gerara to the like end. \+it S. Theod. q. 76. in Gen\+it*.\f* Goe not downe into Egypt, but reſt in the land which I shal tel thee.
\v 3 And ſeiourne in it, and I wil be with thee, and wil bleſſe thee: for to thee and to thy ſeed, I wil geue al theſe countries, accomplishing the oath which I ſware to Abraham thy father.
\v 4 And I wil multiplie thy ſeed as the ſtarres of heauen: and I wil geue to thy poſteritie al theſe countries: and in thy ſeed \sc SHAL BE BLESSED al the nations of the earth\sc*,
\v 5 for becauſe Abraham obeyed my voice, and kept my preceptes and commandements, and obſerued my ceremonies & lawes. \ef - \fr 26.5 \fq My ceremonies. \ft ¹ Theſe were not the ſame ceremonies and lawes which were afterwards preſcribed by God, and deliuered by Moyſes, but other obſeruances by which Abraham and other holie Patriarches before him, ſerued God with certaine external worſhip, differing from the rites of the Gentiles, eſpecially from Enos time (Gen. 4.) and ſo forward. \fq ¹ \ft External ceremonies in the law of nature.\ef*
\v 6 Therfore Iſaac abode in Gerara.
\v 7 Who when he was asked by the men of that place, concerning his wife, anſwered: She is \f b \fr 26.7 \ft See pag 52.\f* my ſiſter. for he was afraid to confeſſe that ſhe was married to him, thinking leſt peraduenture they would kil him becauſe of her beautie.
\v 8 And when verie manie days were paſſed, and he abode there, Abimelech the king of the Paleſtines looking forth through a windowe, ſawe him ſporting with Rebecca his wife.
\v 9 And calling for him, he ſaid: It is euident that she is thy wife: why dideſt thou faine her to be thy ſiſter? He anſwered: I feared leſt I should die for her.
\v 10 And Abimelech ſaid: Why haſt thou deceaued vs? ſome man of the people might haue lyen with thy wife, & thou haddeſt brought vpon vs \f c \fr 26.10 \ft Adulterie a great ſinne alſo among Painims.\f* a great ſinne. And he commanded al the people, ſaying:
\v 11 He that ſhal touch this mans wife, dying shal dye.
\v 12 And Iſaac ſowed in that land, and he found that ſame yeare an hundred fold: and our Lord bleſſed him. \ef - \fr 26.12 \fq An hundred fold. \ft For this increaſe of wealth the King and people at firſt enuied Iſaac, but afterwards perceiuing that God almightie, whom he ſerued, ſo bleſſed him, the reſt of the land remaining barren, they ſought to make league with him (v. 28) ¹ Euen ſo the Kings and nations of the world, firſt enuying and perſecuting Chriſts Church, at leingth ſeing it ſtil proſperous became with al humilitie children of the ſame Church, and ſeruants of Chriſt, being ouercome not by force of armes but by patience, & peacable endeuours of thoſe whom they moſt hated. VVherof excellently ſaith S. Leo (Ser. 1. in Natali. Apoſt) Although Rome renowmed by manie victories, dilated her Empyre by land and by ſea, yet was it leſſe, that martial trauel ſubdued, then that which Chriſtian peace hath obtained. The Bishops of Rome hauing larger Iuriſdiction ſpiritual, then euer the Roman Cœſars had temporal Dominion. \fq ¹ \ft Christian fortitude preuaileth more by suffering, then by forcible resisting.\ef*
\v 13 And the man was made rich, and he went proſpering and encreaſing, til he was made exceeding great:
\v 14 and he had alſo poſſeſſions of sheep and of heards, and a verie great familie. For this the Paleſtines enuying him,
\v 15 ſtopped at that time al the welles, that the ſeruants of his father Abraham had digged; filling them vp with water:
\v 16 in ſo much that Abimelech himſelfe ſaid to Iſaac: Depart from vs, becauſe thou art become mightier then we a great deale.
\v 17 And departing, to come to the Torrent of Gerara, and to dwel there:
\v 18 againe he digged other welles, which the ſeruants of his father Abraham had digged, and which, after his death, the Philiſtines had ſtopped vp of old: and he called them by the ſame names, which his father before had called them.
\v 19 And they digged in the \f 1 \fr 26.19 \ft The chanel where ſometimes a vehement ſtreame runneth, ſometimes none at al.\f* Torrent, and found liuing water:
\v 20 but there alſo the paſtors of Gerara made a brawle againſt the paſtors of Iſaac, ſaying: It is our water. for which cauſe he called the name of the wel, by occaſion of that which had hapned, \f 1 \fr 26.20 \ft \+it VVrangling\+it*.\f* Calumnie.
\v 21 And they digged alſo an other: & for that they brawled likewiſe, and he called the name of it, Enmitie.
\v 22 Going foreward from thence he digged an other wel, for which they contended not: therfore he called the name therof, Latitude, ſaying: Now hath our Lord dilated vs, and made vs to encreaſe vpon the earth.
\v 23 And he went vp from that place vnto Berſabee,
\v 24 where our Lord appeared to him that ſame night, ſaying: I am the God of Abraham thy father, do not feare, becauſe I am with thee: I wil bleſſe thee, and multiplie thy ſeed for my ſeruant Abrahams ſake.
\v 25 Therfore he builded there an altar: and hauing called vpon the name of our Lord, he pitched his tent: and commanded his ſeruants that they ſhould digge a wel.
\v 26 To the which place when there were come from Gerara Abimelech, and Ocozath his freind, and Phicol chieffe captaine of his ſouldiers,
\v 27 Iſaac ſpake to them: Why are ye come to me a man whom you hated, and haue thruſt our from you?
\v 28 Who anſwered: We ſaw that the Lord is with thee, and therfore we ſaid: Let there be an oath betwen vs, and \f d \fr 26.28 \ft So nations of the world firſt enuyed the Church of Chriſt, but after made peace with it.\f* let vs make a league,
\v 29 that thou do vs no harme, as we alſo haue touched nothing of thine, neither haue we done that which might hurt thee: but with peace haue we diſmiſt thee encreaſed with the bleſſing of the Lord.
\v 30 Therfore he made them a feaſt, and after they had eaten and drunken
\v 31 ariſing in the morning, they ſware one to an other: and Iſaac diſmiſſed them peaceably into their place.
\v 32 And behold the ſame day came the ſeruants of Iſaac telling him of a wel, which they had digged, and ſaying: We haue found water.
\v 33 Wherupon he called it Abundance: and the name of the citie was geuen Berſabee, euen vnto this preſent day.
\v 34 But Eſau being fourtie yeares old married wiues, Iudith the daughter of Beeri the Hethite, and Baſemath the daughter of Elon of the ſame place:
\v 35 both which had \f e \fr 26.35 \ft Eſau by marrying againſt his parents wil, made breach from them.\f* offended the mind of Iſaac and Rebecca.
\c 27
\cd Iacob by his mothers counſail getteth his fathers bleſſing in place of Eſau, 42. And by her is aduiſed (for auoiding Eſaus wrath, who threatned to kil him) to flie to his vncle Laban, in Haran of Meſopotamia.
\p
\v 1 AND \f 1 \fr 27.1 \ft The Epiſtle on Saturday the ſecond week in Lent.\f* Iſaac was old, and his eyes were dimme, and he could not ſee: and he called Eſau his elder ſonne, and ſaid to him: my ſonne? Who anſwered Here I am.
\v 2 To whom his father: Thou ſeeſt, quoth he, that I am old, and know not the day of my death.
\v 3 Take thy inſtruments, thy quiuer, and bowe, and goe abrode: and when thou haſt taken any thing by hunting,
\v 4 make me broth therof, as thou knoweſt I like, and bring that I may eate: and my ſoule may bleſſe thee before I dye.
\v 5 Which when Rebecca had heard, and he was gone into the field to fulfil his fathers commandement,
\v 6 she ſaid to her ſonne Iacob: I heard thy father talking with Eſau thy brother, and ſaying to him:
\v 7 Bring me of thy hunting, and make me meates that I may eate, and bleſſe thee in the ſight of our Lord before I dye.
\v 8 Now therfore my ſonne, aſſent to my counſel:
\v 9 and go thy way to the flocke, bring me two kiddes of the beſt, that I may make of them meat for thy father, ſuch as he gladly eateth:
\v 10 which when thou haſt brought in, and he hath eaten, he may bleſſe thee before he dye.
\v 11 To whom he anſwered: Thou knoweſt that Eſau my brother is an hearie man, and I am ſmooth:
\v 12 if my father shal feele me, and perceaue it, \f a \fr 27.12 \ft Iacob ſecure in conſcience that the right of firſt-birth belonged to him, yet feared to geue occaſion of offence to his father.\f* I feare leſt he wil thinke I would haue deluded him, and I shal bring vpon me a curſe for a bleſſing.
\v 13 To whom his mother ſaid: This curſe, my ſonne, light vpon me: only heare thou my voice, and go, fetch me the things which I haue ſaid.
\v 14 He went, and brought, and gaue them to his mother. She dreſſed meats, euen as she knew his father liked.
\v 15 And she did on him the garments of Eſau verie good, which she had at home with her:
\v 16 and the litle skinnes of the kidds she put about his hands, and couered the bare of his necke.
\v 17 And she gaue him the broth, and deliuered him bread that she had baked.
\v 18 Which when he had caried in, he ſaid: My father? But he anſwered: I heare. Who art thou my ſonne?
\v 19 And Iacob ſaid: I am thy firſt begotten Eſau: I haue done as thou dideſt command me: ariſe, ſit, and eate of my hunting, that thy ſoule may bleſſe me. \ef - \fr 27.19 \fq I am thy firſt begotten Eſau. \ft Iacob was not by nature the firſt begotten; but by Gods ordinance, & by couenant made with Eſau, ¹had right to the preeminence, and priuilegies belonging to the firſt borne. So he did not lie, but ſpake a truth, meaning that he was that ſonne, to whom by diuine election the firſt-birth-right was dew, which his father ſuppoſed to pertaine to Eſau. But becauſe ſome ſcorners of Chriſtian doctrin (like to the old Manichees) vſe to ſay, that Catholique Doctors, and Schoolmen excuſe, and condemne whom they liſt by ſuch gloſſes, let ſuch reprouers vnderſtand, that both moderne and ancient Catholique writers auow this defence of the holie Patriarch Iacob, not by priuate ſpirite, but by the moſt true and proper ſenſe of holie Scripture it ſelfe. VVhere it may appeare, if they wil axamine the text, ²that Iacob in al this procurement of his fathers bleſſing, neither did aniething vniuſtly, nor ſaid anie thing falſly. ³ Firſt it was reueled to his mother (chap. 25. v. 23.) \+it That the elder\+it* (of her twinnes) \+it should ſerue the younger\+it*. ⁴ Secondly, holie Scripture teſtifieth in the ſame chapter (v. 27.) \+it That Iacob vvas a plaine\+it* (or ſincere) \+it Man\+it*, void of vniuſt dealing. ⁵ Thirdly, for more quiet enioying that right, which God had ordained for him, he procured his brothers conſent and confirmation (v. 33) ⁶ Fourtly though he was ſecure in conſcience that the bleſſing was dew to him, yet he feared (v. 12.) leſt he might geue occaſion of offence to his father, to whom this myſterie was not yet reueled. ⁷ Fiftly Iſaac perceiuing at laſt Gods wil, that Iacob ſhould be preferred, was neither offended with him, nor reuoked his bleſſing, as vnlawfully ſurpriſed, but condeſcending therto, ratified that he had done, ſaying (v. 33.) \+it I haue bleſſed him, and he shal be bleſſed\+it*. ⁸ Sixtly, God himſelfe from this time forwards often appeared to Iacob, and with great promiſes, and manie temporal and ſpiritual benefites, declared his ſingular loue to him. ⁹ Seuently, theſe three Patriarches Abraham, Iſaac, and Iacob are ſpecial renowmed Sainctes of the old Teſtament: yea the Lord and Creator of al would peculiarly be called (Exodi. 3.) the God of Abraham, Iſaac, and Iacob. Moyſes praying inſtantly for Gods mercie and clemencie towards the people (Exodi. 32.) beſought him to remember Abraham Iſaac, and Iacob his ſeruants: and ſo in both old and new Teſtament theſe three are often mentioned as chiefe Princes in the Kingdom of Heauen. Al which ſhewe the great vertues and holines of them al. ¹⁰ And touching this fact of Iacob, where (if euer aniewhere) might ſeme to be ſome great ſinne. S. Auguſtin at large proueth that he did not herein ſinne at al: \+it That vvhich Iacob did\+it* (ſaith he, li. cont. mendacium c. 10.) \+it By his mothers inſtruction to deceiue his father, if it be diligently conſidered, vvas no lie, but a myſterie, and therfore for the familiar counſail of the Holie Ghoſt, vvhich his mother had receiued, he is excuſed from ſinne\+it*. The ſame he confirmeth. q. 74. in Gen. li. 16. c. 37. de ciuit. & li. 22. c. 34 cont. Fauſt. The ſame alſo teach S. Chriſoſtom ho. 53 in Gen. S. Hierom Epiſt. 125. S. Theodoret. q. 79. &. 80. in Gen. S. Gregorie, ho. 6. in Ezechielem. S. Bede, Iſidorus, Innocentius 3. Rupertus and others vpon this place, al agreing abſolutly that euerie lie is a ſinne, ¹¹declare that Iacob lied not, but ſtil ſpake the truth, confirming their expoſition by other like places of Scripture. As when our Sauiour ſaid of S. Iehu Baptiſt (Math. 11.) \+it He is Elias\+it* meaning that he was Elias in ſpirite not in perſon. So Iacob ſaid truly that he was Eſau, not meaning in perſon but in right of the firſt borne, by Gods ordinance: Eſau alſo hauing condeſcended therto by couenant and oath. In that alſo he deceiued his father, was no ſinne. For it was a lawful and good deceipt, ¹²ſuch a one (ſaith S. Chriſoſtom) ¹³ as Hieremie ſpeaketh of, ¹⁴ \+it Lord thou haſt deceiued me, and I am deceiued\+it*, ſo Iſaac was deceiued, not as we commonly cal deceipt, but to his owne and others good, by Gods diſpoſition. \fq ¹ \ft Iacob did not lie, nor sinne in saying he was Esau. &c. \fq ² \ft He is proued innocent by the text. \fq ³ \ft 1. \fq ⁴ \ft 2. \fq ⁵ \ft 3. \fq ⁶ \ft 4. \fq ⁷ \ft 5. \fq ⁸ \ft 6. \fq ⁹ \ft 7. \fq ¹⁰ \ft The Fathers proue his innocencie in this fact. \fq ¹¹ \ft Euerie lie is a sinne. \fq ¹² \ft Some deceipt good. \fq ¹³ \ft \+it Ho. 6. ad Col\+it*. \fq ¹⁴ \ft \+it Hier. 20\+it*.\ef*
\v 20 And againe Iſaac to his ſonne: How couldeſt thou, ſaid he, find it ſo quickly, my ſonne? Who anſwered: \f b \fr 27.20 \ft It was truly Gods wil, but not in that ſence as Iſaac vnderſtood it.\f* It was the wil of God that that which I would came quickly in my way:
\v 21 And Iſaac ſaid: Come hither, that I may feele thee my ſonne, and may proue whether thou be my ſonne Eſau, or no.
\v 22 He came nere to his father, and when he had felt him, Iſaac ſaid: The voice verely, is the voice of Iacob: but the hands, are the hands of Eſau.
\v 23 And he knew him not, becauſe his hearie hands had made him like vnto the elder. Bleſſing him therfore, \ef - \fr 27.23 \fq Knew him not. \ft S. Damaſus demanding of S. Hierom, ¹what might be the reaſon why God would ſuffer his holie ſeruant Iſaac not to know Iacob, but to be deceiued, and through ignorance to bleſſe whom he would not, declareth that it happened not only to Iacob, but alſo to manie other like holie men, to be ignorant of manie things, and to be deceiued in error of opinion: ² and that this error was profitable to Iſaac and his houſe For if he had geuen this bleſſing (which was a ſpiritual Iuriſdiction) to Eſau, as he purpoſed, he had committed a noxious error in dede, by preferring a bloudie man, one that was readie, if he could, to haue killed his brother. (v. 41.) omitting him, that was ſincere and very vertuous, and had done his owne wil, not Gods wil therin. But why would not God reuel his wil to Iſaac (as he had commanded a farre greatter thing to Abraham to ſacrifice the ſame Iſaac) that he might wittingly haue bleſſed Iacob by Gods commandment? The Fathers do probably alleage this for one reaſon, that if Eſau, ³being a fierce and cruel man, had perceiued that his father had willingly preferred Iacob, he would haue bene incenſed againſt his father, conceiued and attempted euil againſt him. An other reaſon S. Chriſoſtom ⁴ and Theodoret do yeld, that by this ſtrange maner of imparting this bleſſing, ⁵ it might more manifeſtly appeare to be Gods wil and ordinance, and not to procede from mans affection, that Iacob should be preferred. \fq ¹ \ft \+it Epist. 125\+it*. \fq ² \ft It was good that Isaac knew not Iacob, when he blessed him. \fq ³ \ft Good in respect of Esau. \fq ⁴ \ft \+it Ho. 53. q. 79. in Gen\+it*. \fq ⁵ \ft More to Gods glorie, and Iacobs commendation.\ef*
\v 24 he ſaid: Art thou my ſonne Eſau? He anſwered: I am.
\v 25 But he ſaid: Bring me the meats of thy hunting, my ſonne, that my ſoule may bleſſe thee. Which when they were brought and he had eaten, he offred him wine alſo, which after he had drunke,
\v 26 he ſaid to him: Come nere me, and geue me a kiſſe, my ſonne.
\v 27 He came nere, and kiſſed him. And immediatly as he felt the fragrant ſauoure of his garments, bleſſing him, he ſaid: Behold the ſauoure of my ſonne is as the ſauoure of a plentiful field, which our Lord hath bleſſed.
\v 28 God geue thee of the deaw of heauen, and of the fatnes of the earth abundance of corne and wine.
\v 29 And let peoples ſerue thee, and tribes adore thee: be thou lord of thy brethren, and thy mothers children bowe they before thee. He that shal curſe thee, be he curſed: and he that shal bleſſe thee, be he replenished with bleſſings.
\v 30 Iſaac had ſcarce ended his wordes, and Iacob now gone forth abroad, but Eſau came,
\v 31 and brought in to his father meates made of his hunting, ſaying: Ariſe my father, and eate of thy ſonnes hunting: that thy ſoule may bleſſe me.
\v 32 And Iſaac ſaid to him: Why! who arth thou? Who anſwered: I am thy firſt begotten ſonne Eſau.
\v 33 Iſaac was amazed and aſtonied cxceadingly: and marueling more then a man can beleue, ſaid: Who is he then that euen now brought me veniſon that he had taken, and I did eate of al thinges before thou cameſt? and I haue bleſſed him, \f c \fr 27.33 \ft Iſaac now knowing it to be Gods wil, ratified that he had done.\f* and he shal be bleſſed.
\v 34 Eſau hauing heard his fathers wordes, roared out with a great crye: and being diſmaied, ſaid: Bleſſe me alſo, my father.
\v 35 Who ſaid: Thy brother came deceiptfully and tooke thy bleſſing.
\v 36 But he ſaid again: Rightly is his name called Iacob: for he hath ſupplanted me loe the ſecond time: my firſt-birth-right he tooke before, and now the ſecond time he hath ſtollen my bleſſing. And againe to his father he ſaid: Haſt thou not reſerued me alſo a bleſſing?
\v 37 Iſaac anſwered: I haue appointed him thy Lord, and al his brethren I haue made ſubiect to his ſeruice: with corne and wine I haue eſtablished him, and for thee, my ſonne, what ſhal I doe more after this?
\v 38 To whom Eſau ſaid: Haſt thou one only bleſſing, father? I beſech thee bleſſe me alſo. And when he wept that he howled againe,
\v 39 Iſaac being moued, ſaid to him: In \f d \fr 27.39 \ft worldlings bleſſing conſiſteth in tranſitorie welth.\f* the fat of the earth, and in the deaw of heauen from aboue,
\v 40 shal thy bleſſing be. Thou shalt liue by the ſworde, and shalt ſerue thy brother: and \f e \fr 27.40 \ft The Idumeans being ſubdued by king Dauid (2. Reg. 8.) reuolted from king Ioram, and had a king of their owne (4. Reg. 8.) they were againe ſubdued by Hircanus (teſte Ioſepho. li. 13. Antiq.) but againe Herod an Idumean raigned in Iewrie Math. 2. Luc. 1.\f* the time ſhal come, when thou ſhalt shake of, and looſe his yoake from thy necke.
\v 41 Eſau therfore alwaies hated Iacob for the bleſſing wherwith his father had bleſſed him: and he ſaid in his hart: The daies wil come of the mourning of my father, and I wil kil Iacob my brother.
\v 42 Theſe things were told to Rebecca: who ſending & calling Iacob her ſonne, ſaid to him: Behold Eſau thy brother threatneth to kil thee.
\v 43 Now therfore, my ſonne, heare my voice, and get thee vp and flye to Laban my brother into Haran:
\v 44 and thou shalt dwel with him a few daies, til the furie of thy brother be aſſwaged,
\v 45 and his indignation ceaſe, and he forget thoſe things, which thou haſt done to him: afterward I wil ſend, and bring thee from thence hither. Why shal I be depriued of both ſonnes in one day?
\v 46 And Rebecca ſaid to Iſaac: I am wearie of my life for the daughters of Heth: if Iacob take a wife of the ſtocke of this land, I liſt not liue.
\c 28
\cd Iacob with his fathers bleſſing, and admonition not to take a wife of Chanaan, but of the daughters of his vncle Laban, goeth into Meſopotamia: (6. Eſau in the meane time marieth a third wife, his vncle Iſmaels daughter) 11. Iacob ſeeth in ſlepe a ladder reaching to heauen, Angels aſcending and deſcending, and our Lord leyning theron renewed the promiſes made to Abraham and Iſaac. 16. And he awayking maketh a vow.
\p
\v 1 ISAAC therfore called Iacob, and bleſſed him, and commanded him ſaying: Take not a wife of the ſtocke of Chanaan:
\v 2 but goe, and make a iourney into Meſopotamia of Syria; to the houſe of Bathuel thy mothers father, and take thee a wife thence of the daughters of Laban thin vncle.
\v 3 And God almightie bleſſe thee, and make thee encreaſe, and multiplie thee: that thou maieſt be into multitudes of peoples.
\v 4 And \f a \fr 28.4 \ft Iſaac againe confirmeth the bleſſings of Abraham to Iacob, and his ſede, omitting Eſau: yea and God repeteth the ſame. v. 13.\f* geue he thee the bleſſings of Abraham, and to thy ſeed after thee: that thou mayeſt poſſeſſe the land of thy perigrination, which he promiſed to thy grandfather.
\v 5 And when Iſaac had diſmiſt him, taking his iourney he came to Meſopotamia of Syria to Laban the ſonne of Bathuel the Syrian, brother to Rebecca his mother.
\v 6 And Eſau ſeing that his father had bleſſed Iacob, and had ſent him into Meſopotamia of Syria, to marry a wife thence; and that after the bleſſing he had commanded him, ſaying: Thou shalt not take a wife of the daughters of Chanaan:
\v 7 and that Iacob obeying his parents was gone into Syria:
\v 8 hauing tryal alſo that his father did not willingly ſee the daughters of Canaan:
\v 9 he went to Iſmael, and tooke to wife beſides them, which he had before, Maheleth the daughter of Iſmael Abrahams ſonne, ſiſter to Nabaioth.
\v 10 \f 1 \fr 28.10 \ft The Epiſtle in a votiue Maſſe for trauelers.\f* Therfore Iacob being departed from Berſabee, went on to Haran.
\v 11 And when he was come to a certaine place, and would reſt in it after ſunne ſet, he tooke one of the ſtones that lay there, and putting it vnder his head, ſlept in the ſame place. \ef - \fr 28.11 \fq Tooke of the ſtones. \ft ¹ Iacob traueling into a ſtrange countrie went in ſuch poore ſtate, the better to hide his departure from Eſau, who otherwiſe might haue killed him by the way. It was alſo thus diſpoſed by God, that Iacobs faith and confidence might, to his greatter merite, be exerciſed: and that Gods prouidence might more manifeſtly appeare, as it did in his returne after twentie yeares, when with moſt gratful mind he recounted Gods benefites ſaying (Gen. 32) VVith my ſtaffe I paſſed ouer this Iordan, and now with two troupes I do returne. \fq ¹ \ft VVhy Iacob traueled in poore state.\ef*
\v 12 \x - \xt Sap. 10.\x* And he ſaw in his ſleepe a ladder ſtanding vpon the earth, and the top therof tooching heauen: the angels alſo of God aſcending and deſcending by it, \ef - \fr 28.12 \fq A ladder. \ft He that was in temporal diſtreſſe, was maruelouſly comforted ſpiritually, ¹by ſeing a ladder that reached from the earth to heauen; Angels paſſing vp and downe the ſame, and the Sonne of God leaning vpon it, as he that reigneth both in heauen and earth, who in particular promiſed him, and his ſede that whole land, that he and his ſede should be bleſſed, yea that in \+it His Sede\+it* al nations ſhould be bleſſed, ²and that he would kepe and protect him where ſoeuer he went. How al this was performed is briefly reherſed in the booke of wiſdom. chap. 10. \fq ¹ \ft A notable example of Gods comforth to the afflicted. \fq ² \ft Al nations beleuing in Christ are blessed in him.\ef*
\v 13 and our Lord leyning vpon the ladder ſaying to him: I am the Lord God of Abraham thy father, and the God of Iſaac: the Land, wherin thou ſleepeſt, I wil geue to thee and to thy ſeed.
\v 14 And thy ſeed shal be as the duſt of the earth: thou ſhalt be dilated to the Weſt, and to the Eaſt, & to the North, and to the South: and IN \sc THEE and thy ſeed al the tribes of the earth SHAL BE BLESSED\sc*.
\v 15 And I wil be thy keeper whither ſo euer thou goeſt, and wil bring thee backe into this land: neither wil I leaue thee, til I ſhal haue accomplished al things which I haue ſaid.
\v 16 And when Iacob was awaked out of ſleepe, he ſaid: In dede our Lord is in this place, and I wiſt not.
\v 17 And trembling he ſaid: How terrible is this place! this is none other but the houſe of God, and the gate of heauen.
\v 18 And Iacob ariſing in the morning; tooke the ſtone, which he had laid vnder his head, and erected it for a title, powring oyle vpon the toppe. \ef - \fr 28.18 \fq Erected it, powring oyle. \ft To erect a ſtone, and powre oyle vpon it, was nowiſe ſuperſtitious in Iacob. Neither did he lerne it of Idolaters: for he abhorred and deteſted al idolatrical obſeruances. But as S. Iuſtinus Martyr S. Clement of Alexandria, Origen, Euſebius and others teſtiſie, idololatrical ſuperſtition did rather imitate true religious ¹ ceremonies. For the diuel alwayes affecting that honour, which he ſeeth done to God, perſwaded thoſe whom he ſeduced, and blinded with errors, to ſerue him in ſuch maner of external rites, as God was ſerued, ²that therby he might either haue like worship with God, as it happened among Painim Idolaters: or els depriue God of this kind of honour, as now we ſee Proteſtants reiect and pul downe conſecrated Altares, pretending them to be ſuperſtitious. VVherin they ſhew moſt groſſe ignorance, if in dede they ſo iudge of ignorance, and not of mere malice. For who is ſo ſimple, but he may ſee, that the chiefe ³ difference betwen Religion and Superſtition in external things, conſiſteth in the perſons to whom they are done, & in the intention of the doers, & by the ſame differcnee of perſons ciuil honour is alſo diſtinguiſhed, from both religious and ſuperſtitious. As he that kneeleth to God, religiouſly honoreth God. Kneeling to the ſunne, moone, or other falſe-Gods, ſuperſtitiouſly honoreth the diuel, & kneeling to the King, ciuilly honoreth the King, Iacob without doubt did al to Gods onlie honour. And that which he did in this place, is now vſed in the Catholique Church. For ſo Rabanus a diligent obſeruer and writer of Eccleſiaſtical Rites, Ceremonies, and Cuſtomes touching the vſe of holie oyle witneſſeth (li. 1. c. 45. Inſtitut. cleric.) that the Altar being firſt ſprinkled with water, is annointed with Chriſme, to the example of the Patriarch Iacob, who after that dreadful viſion, erected a ſtone for a title (or monument) powring oyle theron, and calling that place \+it The houſe of God\+it*. S. Cyprian alſo writing of Chriſme, mentioneth the two ſortes of holie oyle vſed in the Church; ⁴one of ſimple oyle conſecrated by a Biſhop, which is vſed for Catechumes before Baptiſme, perſons poſſeſſed, and the ſick; the other is made of oyle and balme, alſo conſecrated by a Biſhop, and this is vſed in Baptiſme, Confirmation, and in conſecrating Altares, Kings, and Prieſts. \fq ¹ \ft Erecting and annointing of Altares is a religious office being done to Gods honour. \fq ² \ft The Church lerneth not rites of Idolaters, but they of the Church. \fq ³ \ft Difference in religious, superstitious, & ciuil honour consisteth in the persons, & intentions. \fq ⁴ \ft Two sortes of holie oyle.\ef*
\v 19 And he called the name of the citie \f 1 \fr 28.19 \ft \+it Houſe of God\+it*.\f* Bethel, which before was called Luza.
\v 20 And he vowed a vowe, ſaying: If God shal be with me, and shal keepe me in the way, by the which I walke, and shal geue me \f b \fr 28.20 \ft To whom ynough is not ynough, to him nothing is ynough Aulus Gell.\f* bread to eate, and rayment to put on, \ef - \fr 28.20 \fq Vovved. \ft It can not be vnderſtood that Iacob here vowed, or promiſed only to ſerue God, as the Soueraigne Lord of al creatures, for to that he was bond, whether he should proſper temporally or no; but that he vowed particular godlie workes, ¹ to which he was not otherwiſe obliged. As here he expreſſeth two things. Preſuppoſing before al, that the Lord Omnipotent shal be his God, he addeth, firſt \+it And this ſtone, vvhich I haue erected for a title, shal be called the houſe of God\+it*. wherby he promiſed the building of a Church, performed at his returne (chap. 35.) Secondly he added, \+it And of al things vvhich thou shalt geue me I vvil offer tithes to thee\+it*. And this Likwiſe was of free deuotion. ² For tithes alſo in the law of nature were dew to Prieſts, and by inferior Prieſts to the chiefe Prieſt, as Abraham gaue tithes to Melchiſedech. And ſo al his tithes were dew to his father, and after his father him ſelfe was chiefe: yet he promiſed them to God, that is, to offer them in Sacrifice, and beſtow them in other vſes pertaining to Gods ſeruice. \fq ¹ \ft Vowes are properly of things which are not otherwise commanded. \fq ² \ft \+it Gen. 14\+it*.\ef*
\v 21 and I shal be returned proſperouſly to my fathers houſe, the Lord ſhal be my God,
\v 22 and this ſtone, which I haue erected for a title, shal be called the Houſe of God: and of al things that thou ſhalt geue to me, I wil offer tithes to thee.
\c 29
\cd Iacob intertained by Laban, 15. ſerueth him ſeuen yeares for Rachel, 23. but firſt receiuing Lia, 27. ſeuen dayes after receiueth alſo Rachel, and ſerueth for her ſeuen yeares more. 31. She remaining barren, Lia beareth foure ſonnes.
\p
\v 1 IACOB therfore going on his iourney, came into the Eaſt countrie.
\v 2 And he ſawe a wel in the field, and three flockes of sheepe lying beſide it: for of it the beaſts were watered, and the mouth therof was cloſed with a great ſtone.
\v 3 And the maner was when al the sheepe were geathered togeather they did rowle of the ſtone, and after the sheepe were refreshed they put it on the mouth of the wel againe.
\v 4 And he ſaid to the sheperds: Brethren, whence are you? Who anſwered: Of Haran.
\v 5 And he asked them, ſaying: Know you Laban the ſonne of Nachor? They ſaid: We do knowe him.
\v 6 Is he in health? quoth he: He is in health, ſay they: And behold Rachel his daughter cometh with his flocke.
\v 7 And Iacob ſaid: There is yet much day remaining, neither is it time to bring the flockes into the foulds againe: firſt geue the sheep drinke, and ſo bring them backe to feede.
\v 8 Who anſwered: We can not, til al the cattel be gethered together: and we remoue the ſtone from the wels mouth, that we may water the flocks.
\v 9 They were yet ſpeaking, and behold Rachel came with her fathers ſheepe: for she fed the flocke.
\v 10 whom when Iacob had ſeene, and knew her to be his coſen germaine, and that they were the sheepe of Laban his vncle: he remoued the ſtone, wherwith the wel was cloſed.
\v 11 And hauing watered the flocke, he \f a \fr 29.11 \ft S. Auguſtin (q. 87. in Gen) commendeth familiar kiſſing of kinsfolke and frendes as a laudable cuſtome in ſome countries. It is no where more ciuil & modeſt then in England.\f* kiſſed her: and lifting vp his voice wept,
\v 12 and he told her that he was her fathers brother, and the ſonne of Rebecca: but she in haſt went and told her father.
\v 13 Who when he heard that Iacob his ſiſters ſonne was come, he ranne forth to mete him: and embracing him, and hartely kiſſing him, brought him into his houſe. And when he had heard the cauſes of his iourney,
\v 14 he anſwered: Thou art my bone and my flesh. And after the dayes of one moneth were expired,
\v 15 he ſaid to him: becauſe thou art my brother, shalt thou ſerue me \f 1 \fr 29.15 \ft \+it VVithout vvages\+it*?\f* gratis? Tel me what wages wilt thou take.
\v 16 He had in dede two daughters, the name of the elder was Lia: and the younger was called Rachel.
\v 17 But Lia was bleare eyed: Rachel wel fauored, and of a bewtiful countenance.
\v 18 Whom Iacob louing, ſaid: I wil ſerue thee for Rachel thy younger daughter, ſeauen yeares.
\v 19 Laban anſwered: It is better that I geue her to thee then to an other man, tary with me.
\v 20 Iacob therfore ſerued for Rachel ſeuen yeares: and they ſemed a few dayes becauſe of the greatnes of his loue.
\v 21 And he ſaid to Laban: geue me my wife: becauſe now the time is complete, that I may companie with her.
\v 22 Who hauing bid a great number of his freinds to the feaſt, made the mariage.
\v 23 And at night \f b \fr 29.23 \ft Laban greuouſly offended, neither could Lia be excuſed, but Iacob was innocent in this fact.\f* he brought in Lia his daughter to him,
\v 24 geuing his daughter a handmaid, named Zelpha. With whom when Iacob had compained after the maner, when morning was come he ſaw Lia:
\v 25 and he ſaid to his father in lawe: What is it that thou dideſt meane to doe? did not I ſerue thee for Rachel? why haſt thou deceiued me?
\v 26 Laban anſwered: It is not the cuſtome in this place, that we beſtowe the younger in mariage firſt.
\v 27 Make vp the \f c \fr 29.27 \ft After ſeuen daies he had Rachel who was his firſt ſpouſe. \+it S. Hierom Tradit. Heb. S. Aug. q. 89. in Gen\+it*.\f* weeke of dayes of this match: and I wil geue the this ſame alſo, for the worke that thou shalt ſerue me other ſeauen yeares.
\v 28 He yelded to his pleaſure: and after the weeke was paſt, he maried Rachel to wife:
\v 29 to whom her father had deliuered Bala for to be her ſeruant.
\v 30 And hauing at length obteined the mariage that he wished, he preferred the loue of the later before the former, ſeruing with him other ſeauen yeares.
\v 31 And our Lord ſeing that he deſpiſed Lia, opned her wombe, her ſiſter remaining barren.
\v 32 Who conceaued and bare a ſonne, and called his name Ruben, ſaying: Our Lord ſaw mine affliction: now my huſband wil loue me.
\v 33 And againe she conceaued and bare a ſonne, and ſaid: For becauſe our Lord heard that I was contemned, he hath geuen this alſo to me: and she called his name Simeon.
\v 34 And she conceaued the third time, and bare an other ſonne: and ſaid: Now alſo my huſband wil be ioyned to me, for becauſe I haue borne him three ſonnes: and therfore she called his name, Leui.
\v 35 The fourth time she conceaued and bare a ſonne, and ſaid: Now wil I confeſſe to our Lord. And for this she called him Iuda: And she left bearing.
\c 30
\cd Rachel yet barren, deliuereth her handmaide to Iacob, who beareth two ſonnes. 9. Lia ceaſing to beare geueth her handmaid alſo, and she beareth two more. 17. Then Lia beareth other two ſonnes and one daughter. 22. Rachel beareth Ioſeph. 25. Iacob deſirous to returne home, is hyred to ſtay for a certaine part of the ſtockes increaſe. 43. Wherby he becometh exceeding rich.
\p
\v 1 AND Rachel ſeing ſhe was vnfruitful, \f a \fr 30.1 \ft Not properly enuie, but griefe & lawful emulation. \+it S\+it*. Aug. li. 22. c. 54. cont. Fauſt.\f* enuied her ſiſter, and ſaid to her huſband: Geue me children, otherwiſe I shal dye.
\v 2 With whom Iacob being angrie anſwered: Am I as God, who hath depriued thee of the fruite of thy wombe?
\v 3 But she ſaid: I haue here my ſeruant Bala: Companie with her, that she may beare vpon my knees, and I may haue children of her.
\v 4 And she gaue him Bala vnto \f b \fr 30.4 \ft Of pluralitie of wiues ſee pag. 62.\f* mariage: who,
\v 5 when her huſband had compained with her, conceaued and bare a ſonne.
\v 6 And Rachel ſaid: Our Lord hath iudged for me, and hath heard my voice, geuing me a ſonne. and therfore she called his name, Dan.
\v 7 And againe Bala conceauing bare an other,
\v 8 for whom Rachel ſaid: God hath compared me with my ſiſter, and I haue preuailed: and she called him Nepthali.
\v 9 Lia perceauing that she had left bearing, deliuered Zelpha her handmaid to her huſband.
\v 10 Who conceauing and bringing forth a ſonne,
\v 11 she ſaid: Happely. And therfore called his name Gad.
\v 12 Zelpha alſo bare an other.
\v 13 And Lia ſaid: This is for my bleſſednes: for wemen wil cal me bleſſed. Therfore she called him, Aſer.
\v 14 And Ruben going forth in the time of wheat harueſt into the field, found mandragores: which he brought to his mother Lia. And Rachel ſaid: Geue me part of thy ſonnes \f c \fr 30.14 \ft Holie Scripture (ſaith S. Auguſtin) would neuer haue mentioned ſuch womanlie deſires, but to admonish vs to ſeke great miſteries therin. li. 22. c. 56. cont. Fauſtum.\f* mandragores.
\v 15 She anſwered: Doeſt thou thinke it a ſmal matter, that thou haſt taken my huſband from me, vnleſſe thou take alſo my ſonnes mandragores? Rachel ſaid: For thy ſonnes mandragores let him ſleepe with thee this night.
\v 16 And when Iacob returned at euen from the field, Lia went out to meete him, and ſaid: Companie with me, becauſe with wages I haue hired thee for my ſonnes mandragores. And he ſlept with her that night.
\v 17 And God heard her prayers: and she conceaued and bare the fifth ſonne,
\v 18 and ſaid: God hath geuen me a reward, becauſe I gaue my handmaid to my huſband. And she called his name Iſſachar.
\v 19 Againe Lia conceauing, bare the ſixt ſonne,
\v 20 and ſaid: God hath endowed me with a good dowrie: this turne alſo my huſband wil be with me, for becauſe I haue borne him ſix ſonnes, and therfore she called his name, Zabulon.
\v 21 After whom she bare a daughter, named Dina.
\v 22 Our Lord alſo remembring Rachel, heard her, and opened her wombe.
\v 23 Who conceaued, and bare a ſonne, ſaying: God hath taken away my reproch.
\v 24 And she called his name, Ioſeph, ſaying: Our Lord adde to me an other ſonne.
\v 25 And when Ioſeph was borne, Iacob ſaid to his father in lawe: Diſmiſſe me that I may returne into my countrie, and to my land.
\v 26 Geue me my wiues, and my children, for whom I haue ſerued thee, that I may depart: thou knoweſt the ſeruice that I haue ſerued thee.
\v 27 Laban ſaid to him: Let me finde grace in thy ſight: I haue learned by experience, that God hath bleſſed me for thy ſake:
\v 28 appoint thee wages which I shal geue thee.
\v 29 But he anſwered: Thou knoweſt how I haue ſerued thee, and how great thy poſſeſſion hath benne in my hands.
\v 30 Thou hadeſt a ſmal thing before I came to thee, and now thou art made rich: and our Lord hath bleſſed thee at my comming in. It is reaſon therfore that once I prouide alſo for mine owne houſe.
\v 31 And Laban ſaid: What ſhal I geue thee? But he ſaid: I wil nothing: but if thou wilt doe that which I demande, I wil fede, and kepe thy sheepe again.
\v 32 Goe round about al thy flockes, and ſeparate al the shepe of diuers colours, of ſpeckled flyſe: and what ſoeuer shal be ruſſet and ſpotted, and of diuers colours, aſwel in the shepe, as in the goates, shal be my wages.
\v 33 And my iuſtice shal anſwer for me to morowe, before thee when the time of the bargaine shal come: and al that shal not be of diuers colours, and ſpotted, and ruſſet, aſwel in the shepe as in the goates, shal accuſe me of theft.
\v 34 And Laban ſaid: I like wel that thou demandeſt.
\v 35 And he ſeparated the ſame day the shee goates, and the shepe, and the he goates, and the rammes of diuers colours, and ſpotted: and al the flocke of one coloure, that is of white and blacke flyſe, he deliuered in the hand of his ſonnes.
\v 36 And he put a ſpace of three dayes iourney betwixt him and his ſonne in lawe, who fed the reſt of his flocke.
\v 37 Iacob therfore \f d \fr 30.37 \ft Iacob did iuſtly vſe this meanes to recouer that which Laban withheld from him, being due for the dowrie of his wiues, and recompence for his ſeruice. \+it Rupert. li. 7. c. 39. in Gen\+it*.\f* taking grene roddes of the poplare, and of the almond, and of the plaine trees, in part pilled them: and when the barkes were taken of, in the parts that were pilled, there appeared whitnes: but the parts that were whole, remayned grene: and by this meanes the colour was made diuers.
\v 38 And he put them in the troughes, where the water was poured out: that when the flockes should come to drinke, they might haue the roddes before their eyes, and in the ſight of them conceaue.
\v 39 And it came to paſſe that in the verie heate of the ramming, the shepe beheld the roddes, and brought forth ſpotted, and of diuers colours, and ſpeckled.
\v 40 And Iacob diuided the flocke, and put the roddes in the troughes before the eyes of the rammes: and al the white and the blacke were Labans: and the reſt, Iacobs, when the flockes were ſeparated one from the other.
\v 41 Therfore when the ewes went to ramme, in the prime time, Iacob put the roddes in the troughes of water before the eyes of the rammes, and of the ewes, that in looking vpon them they might conceaue:
\v 42 but when the later comming was, and the laſt conceauing, he did not put them. And thoſe that were late warde, became Labans: and they of the prime time, Iacobs.
\v 43 And the man was enriched beyond meaſure, and he had manie flockes, wemen ſeruantes and men ſeruants, camels and aſſes.
\c 31
\cd Iacob by Gods commandment parteth ſecretly with al he hath towards his father. 21. Laban purſueth him. 26. expoſtulating why he went in ſecrete maner. 30. eſpecially chargeth him with ſtelling his goddes. 31. Iacob excuſeth himſelfe, not knowing that Rachel had taken away the Idols. 34. and she deludeth his diligent ſearching for them. 36. Then Iacob expoſtulateth with Laban for this vnkindnes. 43. Finally they make a league and depart ech to his owne countrie.
\p
\v 1 AFTER he heard the wordes of Labans ſonnes ſaying: Iacob hath taken al that was our fathers, and being enriched of his ſubſtance, is become great:
\v 2 and perceauing alſo Labans countenance, that it was not towards him as yeſterday and the other day,
\v 3 eſpecially our Lord ſaying to him: Returne into the land of thy fathers, and to thy kinred, and I wil be with thee.
\v 4 He ſent, and called Rachel and Lia into the field, where he fed the flockes,
\v 5 and ſaid to them: I ſee your fathers countenance that it is not towards me as yeſterday and the other day: and the God of my father hath bene with me.
\v 6 And your ſelues knowe that I haue ſerued your father to the vttermoſt of my power.
\v 7 Yea your father alſo hath circumuented me, and hath changed my wages tenne times: and yet God hath not ſuffred him to hurt me.
\v 8 If at any time he ſaid: They of diuers colours shal be thy wages: al the sheepe brought forth young of diuers colours, But when he ſaid contrarie: Thou shalt take al the white ones for thy wages: al the flockes brought forth white ones.
\v 9 And God hath taken your fathers ſubſtance, and geuen it to me.
\v 10 For after the time came of the ewes conceauing, I lifted vp mine eyes, and ſawe in my ſleepe the males aſcending vpon the females of diuers colours, and the ſpotted, and the ſpeckled.
\v 11 And the angel of God ſaid to me in ſleepe: Iacob? And I anſwered: Here I am.
\v 12 Who ſaid: Lyft vp thine eyes, and ſee al the males aſcending vpon the females, them of diuers colours, the ſpotted and the ſpeckled. For I haue ſeene al things that Laban hath done to thee.
\v 13 I am the God of Bathel, where thou dideſt \f e \fr 31.13 \ft Annointing of Altars, and free vowes are gratful offices to God. See chap. 28.\f* annointe the ſtone, and dideſt vowe the vowe vnto me. Now therfore ariſe; and goe out of this land, returning into the land of thy natiuitie.
\v 14 And Rachel and Lia anſwered; Haue we any thing left in the goods, and heritage of our fathers houſe?
\v 15 Hath he not reputed vs as ſtrangers, and ſould vs, and eaten vp the price of vs?
\v 16 But God hath taken our fathers riches, and deliuered them to vs, and to our children: wherfore do al things, that God hath commanded thee.
\v 17 Iacob therfore roſe vp, and ſetting his children, and wiues vpon camels, went his way.
\v 18 And he tooke al his ſubſtance, and flockes, and whatſoeuer he had gotten in Meſopotamia, and went forward to Iſaac his father into the land of Chanaan.
\v 19 At that time Laban was gone to sheare his sheepe, and Rachel ſtole the \f 1 \fr 31.19 \ft \+it Teraphim\+it*.\f* idols of her father. \ef - \fr 31.19 \fq Idols. \ft ¹ Images of falſe goddes (as theſe were) are moſt properly called idols. And ſo the hebrew word \+it Teraphim\+it* is here rightly tranſlated idols. which in other places ſignifieth other things. As The ſtatua which Michol put in Dauids bed, ²couering the head therof with a hearie goates skinne, to deceiue Saules ſeriants who ſought Dauids death, is called \+it Teraphim\+it*, and may there be tranſlated a ſtatua, image, or ſimilitude, but not an idol. ³Againe. Oſee the Prophet fortelling the lamentable ſtate of the Iſraelites, ⁴ſayeth, they shal be long without King, prince, ſacrifice, altar, ephod, and \+it Teraphim\+it*, ⁵which laſt word in the Proteſtants Engliſh Bibles remaineth vntranſlated VVhere if they had tranſlated \+it Images\+it*. (as here they doe) it would proue, that ſome images pertaine to true religion, the want wherof is lamented among other principal things. ⁶ Theſe idols Rachel ſtole from her father, to withdraw him from idolatrie. as S. Baſil (in lib. Prouerb.) S. Gregorie Nazianzen. (orat. de Paſchate) and Theodoret. (q. 89. in Gen.) expound it. And in this, ſaith Theodoret, ſhe was a right figure of the Catholique Church, which depriueth idolaters of their idols. It is probable alſo by her baſe vſing of them, that ſhe held them not for goddes, when ſhe put them vnder the camels litter, and ſate vpon them. Finally that ſhe reſerued them, and did not caſt them away, nor burne, nor burie them, argueth that they were perhaps of precious mettal, or other matter, which ſhe might turne to profite: ⁷ and that lawfully in part of recompence, that she and her ſiſter, had no other dowrie, but rather were ſold to Iacob. VVho alſo had ſuffered much iniurie at their fathers handes. \fq ¹ \ft Images of false goddes are idols. \fq ² \ft \+it 1. Reg. 19.\+it* \fq ³ \ft Some images are neither religious nor superstitious. \fq ⁴ \ft \+it Osee. 3.\+it* \fq ⁵ \ft Some are religious. \fq ⁶ \ft Rachel tooke away her fathers Idols, for his good. \fq ⁷ \ft She kept them in recompence of wrongs.\ef*
\v 20 And Iacob would not confeſſe to his father in lawe that he fled.
\v 21 And when he was gone aſwel him ſelfe as al things that were his right, and hauing paſſed the riuer was marching on to Mount Galaad,
\v 22 it was told Laban the third day that Iacob fled.
\v 23 Who, taking his brethren vnto him, purſued him ſeuen dayes: and he ouertoke him in the Mount Galaad.
\v 24 And he ſaw in his ſleepe God ſaying vnto him: Take hede thou ſpeake not roughly anie thing againſt Iacob.
\v 25 And Iacob had now pitched his tent in the mountaine: and when he with his brethren had ouertaken him, he pitched his tent in the ſame Mount Galaad.
\v 26 And he ſaid to Iacob: Why dideſt thou ſo, that vnwitting to me thou wouldeſt carie away my daughters as captiues with the ſword?
\v 27 Why wouldeſt thou flee without my knowledge, and not tel me, that I might haue brought thee on the way with ioy, and ſongues, and timbrels, and cithernes?
\v 28 Thou haſt not ſuffered me to kiſſe my ſonnes and daughters: thou haſt donne foolishly; now alſo in dede,
\v 29 my hand is able to requite thee euil: but the God of your father ſaid vnto me yeſterday: Take hede thou ſpeake not any thing againſt Iacob roughly.
\v 30 Suppoſe, thou diddeſt deſire to goe to thy freinds, and hadeſt a longing to thy fathers houſe: why dideſt thou ſteale my goods?
\v 31 Iacob anſwered: In that I departed vnwitting to thee, I feared leſt thou wouldeſt take away thy daughters by force.
\v 32 But wheras thou chargeſt me with theft: with whom ſoeuer thou ſhalt find thy goddes, let him be ſlaine before our brethen. ſearch, what ſoeuer of thy things thou ſhalt finde with me, and take away. Saying this, he knew not that Rachel had ſtollen the idols.
\v 33 Laban therfore hauing gone into the tent of Iacob, and of Lia, and of both the hand-maides, found them not. And when he was entred into Rachels tent,
\v 34 ſhe in haſt hid the idols vnder the camels litter, and ſatte therupon: and when he had ſought al the tent, and found nothing,
\v 35 she ſaid: Let not my lord be angrie that I can not riſe vp before thee, becauſe according to the cuſtome of wemen it is now chanced to me. ſo his carefulnes in ſeeking was deluded.
\v 36 And Iacob being \f f \fr 31.36 \ft Iacob in this iuſt expoſtulation was angrie & ſinned not. Pſal. 4.\f* angrie ſaid in chiding maner: For what fault of myne, and for what offence of my part haſt thou ſo chaffed after me,
\v 37 and ſearched al my houshould ſtuffe? What haſt thou found of al the ſabſtance of thy houſe? lay it here before my brethren, and thy brethren, and let them iudge betwen me & thee.
\v 38 Haue I therfore bene with thee twentie yeares? thy ewes and goates were not barren, the wethers of thy flocke I did not eate:
\v 39 neyther that which the beaſt had caught did I shew to thee, I made good al the damage: whatſoeuer perished by theft, thou dideſt exact it of me:
\v 40 day and night was I parched with heate, and with froſt, & ſleepe did flye from myne eyes.
\v 41 And in this ſorte haue I ſerued thee in thy houſe twentie yeares, fourtene for thy daughters, and ſix for thy flockes: thou haſt changed alſo my wages tenne times.
\v 42 Vnles the God of my father Abraham, and the feare of Iſaac had holpe me, peraduenture now thou haddeſt ſent me away naked: God beheld my affliction and the laboure of my hands, and rebuked thee yeſterday.
\v 43 Laban anſwered him: The daughters are mine and the children, and thy flockes, and al things that thou ſeeſt are mine: what can I do to my daughters, and nephews?
\v 44 Come therfore, let vs enter in league: that it may be for a teſtimonie betwen me and thee.
\v 45 Iacob therfore tooke a ſtone, and erected it for a title:
\v 46 and he ſaid to his brethren: Bring hither ſtones. Who gethering them together made a heape, and they did eate vpon it:
\v 47 Which Laban called The witneſſe heape: and Iacob called The hillock of teſtimonie, either of them according to the proprietie of his language.
\v 48 And Laban ſaid: This heape shal be a witnes betwen me and thee this day, and therfore the name therof was called Galaad, that is, The witnes heape.
\v 49 Our Lord behold and iudge betwen vs when we shal be departed one from the other,
\v 50 if thou shalt afflict my daughters, and if thou bring in other wiues ouer them: none is witnes of our talke but God, who is preſent and beholdeth.
\v 51 And he ſaid againe to Iacob: Behold this heape, and the ſtone which I haue erected betwen me and thee,
\v 52 shal be a witnes: this heape, I ſay, and the ſtone be they for a teſtimonie, if either I shal paſſe beyond it going towards thee, or thou shalt paſſe beyond it, thinking harme to me.
\v 53 The God of Abraham, and the God of Nachor iudge betwen vs, the God of their father. Iacob therfore ſware by the feare of his father Iſaac:
\v 54 and after he had offred victimes in the mountaine, he called his brethren to eate bread. Who when they had eaten, lodged there:
\v 55 but Laban ariſing in the night, kiſſed his ſonnes, and daughters, and bleſſed them: and returned vnto his place.
\c 32
\cd Angels mete Iacob by the way. 3. He ſendeth meſſengers and giftes to pacifie his brother Eſau. 24. Wreſtling with an Angel is not ouercome, in fine the Angel benummeth his thiegh, bleſſeth him, and fortelleth that he shal be called Iſrael.
\p
\v 1 IACOB alſo went on his iourney that he had begunne: and the Angels of God met him.
\v 2 Whom when he had ſeene, he ſaid: Theſe are the Campes of God, and he called the name of that place Mahanaim, that is, Campes.
\v 3 And he ſent alſo meſſengers before him to Eſau his brother into the land of Seir, into the countrie of Edom:
\v 4 and he commanded them, ſaying: Thus ſpeake ye vnto my lord Eſau: This ſaith thy brother Iacob: I haue ſoiourned, and haue bene with Laban vntil this preſent day.
\v 5 I haue oxen, and aſſes, and sheepe, and men ſeruants, and wemen ſeruants: and now I ſend a leagacie to my lord, that I may finde grace in thy ſight.
\v 6 And the meſſengers returned to Iacob, ſaying: We came to Eſau thy brother, and behold he cometh with ſpede to mete thee with foure hundred men.
\v 7 Iacob feared exceedingly: & being ſore affraid diuided the people that was with him, the flockes alſo and the ſhepe and the oxen, and the camels, into two troupes, \ef - \fr 32.7 \fq Feared exceedingly. \ft ¹ ² Iuſtly may we meruel, why Iacob ſo often aſſured by Gods promiſes, confirmed by his manie bleſſings, protected in al former dangers, accompained the night before with armies of Angels, indued alſo with al vertues, and namely with perfect charitie (which expelleth feare) was for al this ſo vehemently afeard! S. Auguſtin anſwereth, ³that he neither diſtruſted in God, nor did anie vnlawful thing: but did his owne endeuour wiſely and confidently, leſt by preſuming or deſparing he ſhould rather haue tempted God, then truſted in him. ⁴ The cauſes of his feare were in reſpect of him ſelfe and his brother. For conſidering Gods former promiſes, benefites & protections were not to be preſumed as abſolute ſignes of his perpetual loue but conditional, if him ſelfe perſeuered ſincerly in Gods ſeruice. And ſeing \+it The iuſt man knovveth not vvhether he be vvorthie of loue, or of hatred\+it*, ⁵ ⁶he might doubt, leſt by his twentie yeares conuerſation among Infidels in Meſopotamia, he had contracted ſome ſinnes, for which God might ſuffer him to fall into calamitie and affliction. And though he was in dede ſtil more and more vertuous, and conſequently in Gods more fauour and protection: yea ſo much the more, by how much leſſe he preſumed of his owne good ſtate and merites: yet by the vehement apprehending of his brothers inclination to reuenge, ⁷the greatnes of the occaſion by procuring the firſt-birth-right, and his fathers bleſſing from him, the newes of his ſpeedie coming towards him with foure hundred men, the natural ſituation of the place, where Eſau might eſcaly inuiron him, and (as he humbly thought) his owne vnworthines, he was poſſeſſed with natural feare (ſuch as happeneth to conſtant men) and was ſore afflicted for the tender care of his familie. But reflecting vpon Gods goodnes, he prudently diſpoſed of his people and flockes, and beſought God to protect him and his, by prayer qualified with requiſite conditions, to wit, with ⁸ humilitie, not asking for his owne but for \+it Abraham and Iſaacs\+it* ſake, and for Gods owne promiſe, acknowledging himſelfe to be \+it leſſe then Gods mercies\+it* towards him, with ⁹ gratitude recounting great benefites receiued, ſaying, \+it VVith my ſtaffe I paſſed ouer this Iordan, and novv vvith tvvo troupes I do returne\+it*, with ¹⁰ confidence in that God had ſaid, he \+it VVould dilate his ſede as the ſand of the ſea\+it*, and with ¹¹ meeknes in \+it ſending giftes and good vvordes\+it* to Eſau. Thus finally he pacified him, and ſo his owne feare was turned into ioy. \fq ¹ \ft Iacobs feare was iust, and without fault. \fq ² \ft \+it 1. Ioan. 4.\+it* \fq ³ \ft \+it q. 102. in Gen\+it*. \fq ⁴ \ft The causes of his feare. \fq ⁵ \ft The humble conceit of himself. \fq ⁶ \ft \+it Eccl. 9.\+it* \fq ⁷ \ft Esaus inclination & meanes to reuenge. \fq ⁸ \ft Iacobs prayer qualified with Humilitie. Gratitude. Confidence. Meeknes. \fq ⁹ \ft Gratitude, \fq ¹⁰ \ft Confidence, \fq ¹¹ \ft Meekness.\ef*
\v 8 ſaying: If Eſau come to one troupe, and ſtrike it, the other troupe that remaineth, shal be ſaued.
\v 9 And Iacob ſaid: O God of my father Abraham, and God of my father Iſaac: O Lord that dideſt ſay to me: Returne into thy land, and into the place of thy natiuitie, and I wil doe thee good.
\v 10 I am inferiour to al thy mercies, and thy truth that thou haſt fulfilled to thy ſeruant. With my ſtaffe I paſſed ouer this Iordain: and now with two troupes I doe returne.
\v 11 Deliuer me from the hand of my brother Eſau, becauſe I am ſore affraid of him: leſt perhaps he come, and ſtrike the mother with the children.
\v 12 Thou dideſt ſay that thou wouldeſt do good to me, and dilate my ſeed as the ſand of the ſea, which for multitude can not be numbred.
\v 13 And when he had ſlept there that night, he ſeparated of thoſe things which he had, giftes to his brother Eſau,
\v 14 she goates two hundred, he goates twentie, ewes two hundred, and rammes twentie,
\v 15 thirtie milch camels with their coltes, fourtie kine, and twentie bulles, twentie she aſſes, and their foles ten.
\v 16 And he ſent by the handes of his ſeruants, euerie flocke by it ſelfe, and he ſaid to his ſeruants: Goe before me, and let there be a ſpace betwen flocke and flocke.
\v 17 And he commanded the former, ſaying: If thou mete my brother Eſau, and he aske thee, whoſe art thou? or whither goeſt thou? or whoſe are theſe that thou doeſt folowe?
\v 18 thou shalt anſwere: Iacobes thy ſeruant, he hath ſent them for gifts to my lord Eſau: himſelfe alſo commeth after vs.
\v 19 In like maner he gaue commandements to the ſecond, and the third, and to al that folowed the flocks, ſaying: With the ſelfe ſame words ſpeake ye to Eſau, when you shal finde him.
\v 20 And ye shal adde: Iacob alſo thy ſeruant himſelfe foloweth on after vs; for he ſaid: I wil pacifie him with the gifts that goe before, and afterward I wil ſee him, perhaps he wil be gracious vnto me.
\v 21 The giftes therfore went before him, but himſelfe lodged that night in the campe.
\v 22 And when he was riſen early he tooke his two wiues, and his hand-maides as manie, with his eleuen ſonnes, and paſſed ouer the ford Iaboc.
\v 23 And when he had ſet ouer al things that appertained to him,
\v 24 he taried alone: and behold a man wraſteled with him til morning. \ef - \fr 32.24 \fq A man wraſtled. \ft ¹ This wreſtling with an Angel aſſumpting a bodie in forme of a man was corporal, as the effect ſhewed in Iacobs ſinow ſhrunck vp, which made him to halt. v. 25. & 31. ² It was alſo ſpiritual, as appeareth by his earneſt prayer, vrging and at laſt obtayning the Angels bleſſing. S. Dionyſ. c. 4. cæl. Hierer. S. Greg. prefat. in Iob. Theodoret, q. 91. in Gen. \fq ¹ \ft Iacob wrestled with an Angel corporally & spiritually. \fq ² \ft \+it Osee. 12\+it*.\ef*
\v 25 Who when he ſaw that he could not ouercome him, he touched the ſinowe of his thighe, and forthwith it shranke.
\v 26 And he ſaid to him: Let me goe for it is breake of day. He anſwered: I wil not let thee goe, vnleſſe thou bleſſe me.
\v 27 He therfore ſaid: What is thy name? He anſwered: Iacob.
\v 28 But he, no, thy name, quoth he, \f a \fr 32.28 \ft The changing of his name here promiſed, is performed chap. 35. S. Hieron. Tradit. Heb.\f* shal not be called Iacob, but Iſrael: for if thou haſt bene ſtrong againſt God, how much more shalt thou preuaile againſt men?
\v 29 Iacob asked him: Tel me by what name art thou called? He anſwered: Why doeſt thou aske my name? and bleſſed him in the ſame place.
\v 30 And Iacob called the name of the place Phanuel, ſaying: I haue ſene God face to face, and my ſoule was made ſafe.
\v 31 And immediatly the ſunne roſe to him, after that he was paſt Phanuel; but he halted on his foote.
\v 32 For which cauſe the children of Iſrael eate not the ſinowe, that shrunke in Iacobes thighe, vnto his preſent day: becauſe he touched the ſinowe of his thighe, and it ſhrunke.
\c 33
\cd Iacob ſeing Eau come with a great troupe of men, feareth harme, but is most curteouſly entertained by him. 10. He hardly perſwadeth Eſau to take giftes, 13. and to returne home. 17. So Iacob coming by Socoth to Salem, there byeth a field, pitcheth his tents, and erecteth an Altar.
\p
\v 1 AND Iacob lifting vp his eyes, ſaw Eſau coming, and with him foure hundred men: and he diuided the children of Lia and of Rachel, and of the two handmaides:
\v 2 and he put both the handmaids & their children foremoſt: and Lia, and her children in the ſecond place: and Rachel, and Ioſeph laſt.
\v 3 And himſelfe going foreward adored proſtrate to the grownd ſeuen times, vntil his brother came nere.
\v 4 Eſau therfore running to mete his brother, embraced him: and claſping him faſt about the necke, and kiſſing him wept.
\v 5 And caſting vp his eyes, he ſaw the wemen and their litle ones, and ſaid: What meane theſe? And do they perteyne to thee? He anſwered: They are the litle ones which God hath geuen to me thy ſeruant.
\v 6 And the handmaides and their children coming nere, bowed themſelues.
\v 7 Lia alſo with her children came nere: and when they had adored in like maner, laſt Ioſeph and Rachel adored.
\v 8 And Eſau ſaid: What are the troupes that I did mete? He anſwered: That I might find grace before my lord.
\v 9 But he ſaid: I haue plentie, my brother, be thy things to thy ſelfe.
\v 10 And Iacob ſaid: Do not ſo I beſech thee, but if I haue found grace in thin eyes, take a litle preſent at my hands: for ſo haue I ſeene thy face, as if I should haue ſeene \f a \fr 33.10 \ft Iacob ſeing Gods hand in this change of his brothers mind, not of flaterie, but ſincerly acknowledged his benignitie, as Gods countenance towards him.\f* the countenance of God: be gracious to me,
\v 11 and take the bleſſing, which I haue brought thee, and which God hath geuen me, who geueth al thinges. Scarſe at his brothers great inſtance, taking it,
\v 12 he ſaid: Let vs march on together, and I wil accompanie thee in thy iourney.
\v 13 And Iacob ſaid: My lord thou knoweſt that I haue with me litle ones, and ſheepe, and kine with young: which if I cauſe to ouerlaboure themſelues in going, in one day al the flockes wil die.
\v 14 It may pleaſe my lord to goe before his ſeruant: and I wil folow ſoftly after him, as I ſhal ſee my litle ones to be able, vntil I come to my lord in Seir.
\v 15 Eſau anſwered: I beſech thee, that of my people at the leaſtwiſe, which is with me, there may remaine ſome to accompanie thee in the way. It is not needful, ſaid he, this only I haue nede of, that I may finde grace (my lord) in thy ſight.
\v 16 Eſau therfore returned that day the ſame way, that he came into Seir.
\v 17 And Iacob cometh into Socoth: where hauing built a houſe, and pitched his tents, he called the name of that place Socoth, that is, Tabernacles.
\v 18 And he paſſed into Salem a citie of the Sichimites, which is in the land of Chanaan, after he returned from Meſopotamia of Siria: and he dwelt beſide the towne.
\v 19 And he bought that part of the field, wherin he had pitched his tents, of the children of Hemor, the father fo Sichem for an hundred lambes.
\v 20 And erecting an altar there, on it he called vpon the moſt mightie God of Iſrael.
\c 34
\cd For rauishing Dina, the Sichimetes (being firſt circumciſed) are ſlaine by Simeon and Leui her brothers. 27. The rest of Iacobs ſonnes ſpoile the citie. 30. Iacob blameth them, fearing harme may come by this fact.
\p
\v 1 AND \f 1 \fr 34.1 \ft O Dina (ſaith S. Bernard) what nede was there to ſee wemen of a ſtrange countrie? \+it Tract. de gradib. humilitatis\+it*.\f* Dina the daughter of Lia went forth to ſee the wemen of that countrie.
\v 2 whom when Sichem had ſeene the ſonne of Hemor the Heuite, the prince of that land, he was in loue with her: and he tooke her away, and lay with her, by force rauishing the virgin.
\v 3 And his ſoule was faſt kint vnto her, and whereas she was ſad, he comforted her with ſweete wordes.
\v 4 And going to Hemor his father, he ſaid: Take me this wench to be my wife.
\v 5 Which when Iacob had heard, his ſonnes being abſent, and occupied in feeding of the cattle, he held his peace til they returned.
\v 6 And when Hemor Sichems father was come forth to ſpeake vnto Iacob,
\v 7 behold his ſonnes came out of the field: and hearing what had paſſed, they were paſſing wrath, becauſe he had done a foule thing in Iſrael, and committed an vnlawful fact, in rauishing Iacobs daughter.
\v 8 Hemor therfore ſpake to them: The ſoule of my ſonne Sichem is faſtned to your daughter; Geue her vnto him to wife:
\v 9 and let vs contract mariages one with an other: geue vs your daughters, and take you our daughters.
\v 10 And dwel with vs: the land is at your commandement, tille, occupie, and poſſeſſe it.
\v 11 Yea and Sichem alſo ſaid to her father and to her brethren: Let me finde grace in your ſight: and what ſoeuer you shal appointe I wil geue:
\v 12 raiſe the dowrie, and require giftes, and I shal gladly geue, what you shal demande: only geue me this wench to wife.
\v 13 Iacobs ſonnes anſwered Sichem & his father \f a \fr 34.13 \ft They offended by falſly pretending religion, and by exceſſe in reuenge, & therfore are reproued by their father. v. 30. & chap. 49 v 5. Otherwiſe their zeale was iuſt to punish ſo foule a fault. \+it Iudith. 9\+it*.\f* in guile, being wrath for the deflouring of their ſiſter:
\v 14 We can not doe that which you demande, nor geue our ſiſter to an vncircumciſed perſon: which with vs is an vnlawful & abhominable thing.
\v 15 But in this order we may be confederate, if you wil be like to vs, and al the man ſex among you be circumciſed:
\v 16 then wil we geue and take mutually your daughters, and ours: and we wil dwel with you, and wil be one people:
\v 17 but if you wil not be circumciſed, we wil take our daughter, and depart.
\v 18 The offer pleaſed Hemor, and Sichem his ſonne:
\v 19 neither did the young man make delay, but forthwith fulfilled that which was demanded: for he loued the wench exceedingly, and he was the greateſt man in al his fathers houſe.
\v 20 And going into the gate of the citie, they ſpake to the people:
\v 21 Theſe men are men of peace, and are willing to dwel with vs: let them occupie in the land, and til it, which being large and wide doth lacke men to tille it: their daughters we shal take to wife, and ours we wil geue to them.
\v 22 One thing there is for the which ſo great a good is differred: If we circumciſe our men ſexe, folowing the rite of the nation.
\v 23 And their ſubſtance, and cattle, and al things that they poſſeſſe, shal be ours: only in this let vs condeſcend, and dwelling togeather, we shal make one people.
\v 24 And they al aſſented, and circumciſed al the man ſex.
\v 25 And behold the third day, when the griefe of the woundes is moſt paineful: Iacobs two ſonnes, Simeon and Leui the brothers of Dina, taking their ſwordes, entred into the citie boldly: and killing al the man ſex,
\v 26 murdred withal Hemor and Sichem, taking away Dina their ſiſter out of Sichems houſe.
\v 27 When they were gone forth, the other ſonnes of Iacob ranne in vpon them that were ſlaine: and ſpoiled the citie in reuenge of the rape.
\v 28 And waſting al things that were in their houſes, and fildes, their ſheepe and heardes, and aſſes,
\v 29 their little ones alſo, and their wiues they led away captiue.
\v 30 Which things when they had boldly atcheiued, Iacob ſaid to Simeon and Leui: You haue trubled me, and made me odious to the Chananites, and Pherezites the inhabiters of this land. we are few: they being gethered together wil ſtrike me; and I, and my houſe ſhal be deſtroyed.
\v 31 They anſwered: What ſhould they abuſe our ſiſter as a ſtrumpet?
\c 35
\cd Iacob purging his whole familie of idols, goeth by Gods commandment into Bethel, 7. There buildeth an Altar. 8. Debora dieth. 9. God appearing againe to Iacob bleſſeth him, and changeth his name into Iſrael. 16. Rachel bearing Beniamin dieth, and is buried in Bethleem, 22. Ruben lyeth with Bala. 23. Iſraels twelue ſonnes are recited. 28. Iſaac dieth at the age of 180. yeares. and his ſonnes Eſau and Iacob burie him.
\p
\v 1 IN THE meane time God ſpake to Iacob: Ariſe, and goe vp to Bethel, and dwel there, and make an altar to God that appeared to thee when thou diddeſt flie from Eſau thy brother.
\v 2 And Iacob hauing called together al his houſe, ſaid: Caſt away the ſtrange goddes that are among you, and be clenſed and change your garments. \ef - \fr 35.2 \fq Caſt away the ſtrange goddes. \ft ¹ Iacob preparing to performe his vow of building a houſe & altar to God, firſt extirpateth al Idolatrie from amongſt his people; and then by Sacrifice appeaſeth Gods wrath prouoked how ſoeuer and ſpecially by Simeon and Leui killing the Sichemites. Duly conſidering that what people or perſon deſireth Gods protection & bleſſings, muſt firſt be pure in Religion, and clenſed from ſinnes: \+it Quia ei nulla nocebit adnerſitas, ſi nulla dominetur iniquitas\+it*: becauſe no aduerſitie shal hurt him, if no iniquitie haue dominion ouer him. orat. fer. 6. poſt cineres. \fq ¹ \ft Clensing from sinne is the first office of the seruants of God.\ef*
\v 3 Ariſe, and let vs goe vp into Bethel, that we may make there an altar vnto God: who heard me in the day of my tribulation, and accompained me in my iourney.
\v 4 They gaue to him therfore al the ſtrange goddes that they had, and the earelets which were in their eares: but he buried them vnder the \f 1 \fr 35.4 \ft \+it An execrable tree\+it*.\f* terebinth, that is behind the citie of Sichem.
\v 5 And when they were departed, \f a \fr 35.5 \ft God (when it pleaſeth him) maketh the weake ſtronger then the mightie; and few more terible then manie. \+it S. Chriſoſt. ho. 59. S. Aug. q. 112. in Gen\+it*.\f* the terror of God inuaded al the cities rounde about, and they durſt not purſew them going away.
\v 6 And Iacob came to Luza, which is in the land of Chanaan, ſurnamed Bethel: he and al the people that was with him.
\v 7 And he builded there an altar, and called the name of that place, The houſe of God: for there God appeared to him when he fled from his brother.
\v 8 The ſame time died Debora the nurſe of Rebecca, and was buried at the foote of Bethel vnder an oke: and the name of that place was called, The oke of weeping.
\v 9 And God appeared again to Iacob after he returned from Meſopotamia of Siria, and he bleſſed him,
\v 10 ſaying: Thou \f b \fr 35.10 \ft The name of ſupplanter not ſufficiently expreſſing his valure, he is alſo called Iſrael. See the Annotation.\f* shalt not be called any more Iacob, but Iſrael shal be thy name. And he called him Iſrael, \ef - \fr 35.10 \fq Called him Iſrael. \ft As the Patriarch now performeth his vow to God: ſo God fulfilleth his promiſe, geuing him a new name. ¹ For \+it Iacob a ſupplanter\+it*, ſignifying too ſmal force & praiſe for ſuch a champion, God therfore honored him with the name of \+it Iſrael\+it*. That is, \+it One that ſeeth and contemplateth God\+it*, as moſt ancient writers expound it. Alſo \+it A prince, or valiant with God\+it*, as S. Hierom ſheweth Tradit. Heb. For \+it Iſra\+it* in Hebrew ſignifieth \+it To dominier, or rule ouer\+it*, and \+it El\+it* ſignifieth God And ſo this name geuen to him teſtifieth that he, by Gods gift and grace, was valiant euen againſt an Angel repreſenting God, much more againſt men, and other aduerſaries. Others interprete it, \+it The right one of God\+it*, as witneſſeth the ſame S. Hierom both here, and in his commentaries in 44. Iſaie. Al do importe a great excellencie in this Patriarch. And the ſucceſſe of things confirmeth the ſame. Particularly in that not onlie ſome one of his ſonnes (as in the iſſue of Abraham and Iſaac, the reſt being excluded) but his whole progenie of twelue ſonnes, ²making twelue Tribes, were participant of the peculiar bleſſings, in their offſpring poſſeſſing the promiſed land, and excedingly increaſing became the moſt principal nation in the world, the ſelected people of God, called by the name and title of \+it the children of Iſrael\+it*. Of whom not only Moyſes, but al the old Teſtament moſt ſpecially treateth, and of whom and in whom the promiſed Meſſias, the Redemer of mankind ſhould be borne. \fq ¹ \ft The name Israel signifieth special prerogatiues in the Patriarch Iacob. \fq ² \ft Al his twelue sonnes in their posteritie were heires of the promised land.\ef*
\v 11 and ſaid to him: I am God almightie, encreaſe thou and multiplie: Of thee shal be nations and peoples of nations, kinges shal come forth of thy loynes.
\v 12 And the land which I gaue to Abraham and Iſaac, I wil geue to thee, and to thy ſeede after thee.
\v 13 And he departed from him.
\v 14 But he erected a title of ſtone, in the place where God had ſpoken vnto him: offering vpon it liquide offeringes, and powring oile on it:
\v 15 and calling the name of that place, Bethel.
\v 16 And being gone forth from thence, he came in the ſpring time to the land which leadeth to Ephrata: wherin when Rachel was in trauaile,
\v 17 becauſe of difficultie in her trauaile, ſhe beganne to be in danger, and the midwife ſaid vnto her: Feare not, for thou shalt haue alſo this a ſonne.
\v 18 And her ſoule departing for paine, and death now at hand, she called the name of her ſonne Benoni, that is the ſonne of my paine: but his father called him Beniamin, that is the ſonne of the right hand.
\v 19 Rachel therfore died, and was buried in the hye way that leadeth to Ephrata, this ſame is Bethleem. \x - \xt Math. 2.\x*
\v 20 And Iacob erected a title ouer her ſepulchre: This is the litle of Rachels monument, vntil this preſent day.
\v 21 Departing thence, he pitched his tent beyond the Flocke tower.
\v 22 And when he dwelt in that countrie: Ruben went, and \f c \fr 35.22 \ft For this fact Ruben was excluded from the chiefe dignitie among his bretheren. Gen. 49.\f* ſlept with Bala his fathers concubine: which thing he was not ignorant of. And the ſonnes of Iacob were twelue.
\v 23 The ſonnes of Lia: Ruben the firſt begotten, and Simeon, and Leui, and Iudas, and Iſſachar, and Zabulon.
\v 24 The ſonnes of Rachel: Ioſeph and Beniamin.
\v 25 The ſonnes of Bala Rachels handmaid: Dan and Nepthali.
\v 26 The ſonnes of Zelpha Lias handmaid: Gad and Aſer: theſe are the ſonnes of Iacob, that were borne to him in Meſopotamia of Siria.
\v 27 He came alſo to Iſaac his father in Mambre, the citie of Arbee, this is Hebron: wherin Abraham and Iſaac ſoiourned.
\v 28 And the dayes of Iſaac were complete an hundred eyghtie yeares.
\v 29 And ſpent with age he died, and was put to his people, being old and ful of dayes: and Eſau and Iacob his ſonnes buryed him.
\c 36
\cd Eſau with his wiues and children parteth from Iacob. 9. His genealogie is recited, with their habitations.
\p
\v 1 AND theſe are the generations of Eſau, the ſame is Edom. \ef - \fr 36.1 \fq The generations of Eſau. \ft As before Moyſes deſcribed ¹ the genealogies of Cain, of Iapheth and Cham, of Nachor, of Iſmael, and other ſonnes of Abraham, ſo here he recordeth an other collateral progenie of Eſau, that the difference and diſtinction of them, and the ſelected people of God might be more conſpicuous, becauſe contraries oppoſed are ſene more clerly. ² And ſo the Churches ſucceſſion and perpetual light, compared with the interrupted and obſcure companies, shineth the brighter. For albeit in thoſe other generations there might be manie faithful and iuſt perſons, among the infidels and wicked, and of ſome we are aſſured (as of Lot and Iob) yet faith and religion decayed, and was extinguished in their carnal children, and only continued in the right line from Adam to Iacob, whoſe twelue ſonnes were fathers and beginners of twelue Tribes, and in them the ſame true Religion was ſtil conſerued and publikly profeſſed, as in the onlie knowne viſible Church, til the comming of Chriſt. as S. Auguſtin clerly ſheweth in his excellent worke of the Citie of God: eſpecially in the 15. and 16. bookes, in manie chapters. \fq ¹ \ft \+it chap. 4. 10. 22. 25\+it*. \fq ² \ft By comparison of interrupted companies the continual succession of the Church is more glorious.\ef*
\v 2 Eſau tooke wiues of the daughters of Chanaan: Ada the daughter of Elon the Hetheite, and Oolibama the daughter of Ana daughter of Sebeon the Heueite: \ef - \fr 36.2 \fq Ada the daughter of Elon. \ft ¹ In the 26. chap. (v. 34.) Eſaus two wiues, of which he tooke in Chanaan are called Iudith the daughter of Beeri the Hetheite, and Baſemath the daughter of Elon of the ſame place, and here the ſame two wiues are named Ada the daughter of Elon the Hetheite, and Oolibama the daughter of Sebeon the Heueite. VVhich neither agree in names nor countrie. Againe his third wife Iſmaels daughter, here named Baſemath, in 28. chap v. 9. is called Maheleth. For reconciliation of which and other like difficulties, or ſeeming contradictions, albeit he lerned expoſitors ſay, that either theſe perſons had diuers names, or one was true and natural father, an other legal, or adoptiue, for there were ſuch alſo before the law of Moyſes, as appeareth in the hiſtorie of Thamar: ² yet it were hard to geue a determinate ſolution of this difficultie. VVhich example, with manie others (by vs omitted in theſe briefe annotations) conuince the Proteſtants preſumptuous error, holding that Scriptures are eaſie to be vnderſtood. \fq ¹ \ft One place of Scripture seemeth contrarie to an other but is not. \fq ² \ft Holie Scriptures not easie to be vnderstood.\ef*
\v 3 Baſemath alſo the daughter of Iſmael ſiſter of Nabaioth.
\v 4 And Ada bare Eliphaz: Baſemath bare Rahuel:
\v 5 Oolibama bare Iehus and Ihelon and Coree. theſe are the ſonnes of Eſau, that were borne to him in the land of Chanaan.
\v 6 And \f a \fr 36.6 \ft The ſeparation of Eſau from Iacob.\f* Eſau tooke his wiues and ſonnes and daughters, and euerie ſoule of his houſe, and his ſubſtance, and catle, and al that he could haue in the land of Chanaan: and he went into an other countrie, and departed from his brother Iacob. \ef - \fr 36.6 \fq Departed from his btother. 8. Dvvelt in Mount Seir. \ft ¹ Here is an other difficultie (though not ſo intricate as the former) how Eſau now parted into Mount Seir, ſeing he dwelled there, when his brother Iacob came from Meſopotamia. chap. 22. v. 3. VVhich S. Auguſtin (q. 119. in Gen.) ſolueth ſaying: Eſau firſt dwelt in Seir after he was diſapointed of his fathers bleſſing, but dwelt againe with his father, after Iacobs returne from Meſopotamia, and now went to Seit againe after his fathers death. \fq ¹ \ft Esaus last parting from Iacob. (c)\ef*
\v 7 For they were exceding rich, and could not dwel together: neither was the land of their peregrination able to beare them, for the multitude of flockes.
\v 8 And Eſau dwelt in Mount Seir, he is Edom.
\v 9 And theſe are the generations of Eſau the father of Edom in mount Seir,
\v 10 and theſe are the names of his ſonnes: Eliphaz the ſonne of Ada the wife of Eſau: Rahuel alſo the ſonne of Baſemath his wife.
\v 11 And Eliphas had ſonnes: Theman, Omar, Sepho, and Gathan, and Cenes.
\v 12 And Thamna was the concubine of Eliphas the ſonne of Eſau: which bare to him Amalech. theſe are the ſonnes of Ada the wife of Eſau.
\v 13 And the ſonnes of Rahuel: were Nahath & Zara, Samma and Meza. theſe were the ſonnes of Baſemath the wife of Eſau.
\v 14 Theſe alſo were the ſonnes of Oolibama, the daughter of Ana, the daughter of Sebeon, the wife of Eſau, which ſhe bare to him, Iehus, and Ihelon, and Coree.
\v 15 Theſe were dukes of the ſonnes of Eſau: the ſonnes of Eliphaz the firſt-begotten of Eſau: duke Theman, duke Omar, duke Sepho, duke Cenes,
\v 16 duke Coree, duke Gatham, duke Amalech, theſe are the ſonnes of Eliphaz in the land of Edom, and theſe are the ſonnes of Ada.
\v 17 Theſe alſo were the ſonnes of Rahuel, the ſonne of Eſau: duke Nahath, duke Zara, duke Zamma, duke Meza. and theſe are be the dukes of Rahuel, in the Land of Edom: theſe be the ſonnes of Baſemath the wife of Eſau.
\v 18 And theſe were the ſonnes of Oolibama the wife of Eſau: duke Iehus, duke Ihelon, euke Coree. theſe be the dukes of Oolibama, the daughter of Ana, and wife of Eſau.
\v 19 Theſe are the ſonnes of Eſau, and theſe are the dukes of them: the ſame is Edom.
\v 20 Theſe are the ſonnes of Seir the horreite, the inhabiters of the land: Lotan, and Sobal, and Sebeon, and Ana,
\v 21 and Diſon, and Eſer, and Diſan. Theſe are dukes of the Horreite, the ſonnes of Seir in the Land of Edom.
\v 22 And Lotan had ſonnes: Hori and Heman. and the ſiſter of Lotan, was Thamna.
\v 23 And theſe were the ſonnes of Sobal: Aluan and Manahat, and Ebal, and Sepho and Onam.
\v 24 And theſe were the ſonnes of Sebeon: Aia and Ana. This is Ana that found the hot waters in the wildernes, when he fed the aſſes of Sebeon his father:
\v 25 and he had a ſonne Diſon, and a daughter Oolibama.
\v 26 And theſe were the ſonnes of Diſon: Hamdan, and Eſeban, and Iethram, and Charan.
\v 27 Theſe alſo were the ſonnes of Eſer: Balaan, and Zauan, and Acan.
\v 28 And Diſan had ſonnes: Hus, and Aram.
\v 29 Theſe were dukes of the Horreites: duke Lotan, duke Sobal, duke Sebeon, duke Ana,
\v 30 duke Diſon, duke Eſer, duke Diſan: theſe were dukes of the Horreites that ruled in the Land Seir.
\v 31 And the Kinges that ruled in the land of Edom, before that the children of Iſrael had a king, were theſe:
\v 32 Bela the ſonne of Beor, and the name of his citie Denaba.
\v 33 And Bela died, and \f b \fr 36.33 \ft By the common opinion of Latin and Greke fathers, this was holie. Iob as we ſhal diſcuſſe when we come to his booke againſt the hebrew doctors, who ſay Iob was of Nachors race.\f* Iobab the ſonnne of Zara of Boſra reigned in his ſteed.
\v 34 And when Iobab was dead, Huſam of the land of the Themans reigned in his ſteed.
\v 35 He alſo being dead, there reigned in his ſteed Adad the ſonne of Badad, that ſtroke Madian in the countrie of Moab: and the name of his citie was Auith.
\v 36 And when Adad was dead, there reigned for him Semla of Maſreca.
\v 37 He alſo being dead, Saul of the riuer Rohoboth, reigned in his ſteed.
\v 38 And when he alſo was dead, Balanan the ſonne of Achobor ſucceeded into the kingdome.
\v 39 This man alſo being dead Adar reigned in his place, and the name of his citie was Phau: and his wife was called Meetabel, the daughter of Matred, daughter of Mezaab.
\v 40 Theſe therfore be the names of the dukes of Eſau in their kinreds, and places, and callings: duke Thamna, duke Alua, duke Ietheth,
\v 41 duke Oolibama, duke Ela, duke Phinon,
\v 42 duke Cenez, duke Theman, duke Mabſer,
\v 43 duke Magdiel, duke Hiram: theſe are the dukes of Edom dwelling in the land of their empire, the ſame is Eſau the father of the Idumeians.
\c 37
\cd Ioſeph informing his father of his brethrens faults. 5. and telling his dreames, is by them more hated. 13. being ſent to viſite them, 18. they firſt thinke to kil him, 26. but by Iudas counſel ſel him to the Iſmaelites. 29. vnwiting to Ruben. 33. his father lamenteth ſuppoſing him to be ſlaine by ſome wild beaſt. 36. He is ſold againe to Putiphar in Ægypt. \f - \ft The ſeventh part of this book. How Joſeph was ſold into Ægypt, and there advanced.\f*
\p
\v 1 AND Iacob dwelt in the land of Chanaan, wherin his father ſoiourned.
\v 2 And \f a \fr 37.2 \ft Theſe things folowing happened to Iacob, in his generations, that is in his children. \+it See S. Chriſoſt. ho. 23. in Gen\+it*.\f* theſe are his generations: Ioſeph when he was ſixtene yeares old, fed the flock with his brethren being yet a boy: and he was with the ſonnes of Bala and Zelpha his fathers wiues: and he accuſed his brethren to his father of \f b \fr 37.2 \ft That for il life they were infamous, the hebrew word \+it dibba\+it* ſignifieth \+it infamie\+it*.\f* a moſt wicked crime.
\v 3 And Iſrael loued Ioſeph aboue al his ſonnes, becauſe he had begotten him in his old age: and he made him a coate of diuers coloures. \ef - \fr 37.3 \fq In his old age. \ft ¹ This being one cauſe why Iacob loued Ioſeph aboue al his other ſonnes, for that he was the youngeſt of the eleuen (for Beniamin the twelfth was yet an infant) it is alleaged in holie Scripture (ſaith S. Chriſoſtom. Epiſt. ad Olympiam) as leaſt offenſiue to his bretheren. For a more ſpecial cauſe was, for his mother Rachels ſake, but moſt principal cauſe of al was, for his great vertues, and mature iudgement; for which God alſo preferred him aboue them al, and now forſhewed the ſame by viſions in ſleepe. VVhich they enuying and meaning to preuent, did in dede vnwitting cooperate therto, Gods prouidence turning their euil worke to infinite good. ²As the ſame holie Ioſeph truly interpreteth it to them, after their fathers death. when they iuſtly feared reuenge, for ſo gteat and inhumane iniuries done vnto him. chap. 50. v. 20. \fq ¹ \ft The least offensiue cause is alleaged, why Iacob loued Ioseph aboue his bretheren. \fq ² \ft God turneth evil to good effect. \+it S. Aug. l. 14. c. 27. civit.\+it* Grave for hell corruptly translated.\ef*
\v 4 And his brethren ſeing that he was loued of his father, more then al his ſonnes, they hated him, neither could they ſpeake any thing to him peaceably.
\v 5 It chanced alſo that he reported to his brethren a dreame, that he had ſeene: which occaſion was the ſeed of greater hatred.
\v 6 And he ſaid to them: Heare my dreame which I haue ſeene:
\v 7 \f 1 \fr 37.7 \ft (Liturgical note) The Epiſtle on Friday, in the ſecond week of Lent.\f* I thought we bounde ſheaues in the field: and my ſheafe aroſe as it were, and ſtood, and your sheaues ſtanding about did adore my sheafe.
\v 8 His brethren anſwered: What shalt thou be our king? or shal we be ſubiect to thy dominion? This occaſion of his dreames and wordes miniſtred nourishment to the enuie and hatred.
\v 9 He ſawe alſo an other dreame, which telling his brethren, he ſaid: I ſawe in a dreame, as it were the ſunne, and the moone, and eleuen ſtarres adore me.
\v 10 Which when he had reported to his father, and brethren, his father rebuked him, and ſaid: What meaneth this dreame that thou haſt ſeene? why ſhal I and thy mother, and thy brethren adore thee vpon the earth?
\v 11 His brethren therfore enuyed him: but \f c \fr 37.11 \ft Brothers eaſily enuie eech other: but the parents are glad of their childrens aduancement.\f* his father conſidered the thing with himſelfe.
\v 12 And when his brethren abode in Sichem, feeding their fathers flockes,
\v 13 Iſrael ſaid to him: Thy brethren feed sheepe in Sichem: come, I wil ſend thee to them. Who anſwering,
\v 14 I am readie; he ſaid to him: Goe, and ſee if al things be wel with thy brethren, and the sheepe: and bring me word againe what they doe. Being ſent therfore from the Vale of Hebron, he came to Sichem:
\v 15 and a man found him there wandring in the field, and asked what he ſought.
\v 16 But he anſwered: \f d \fr 37.16 \ft So Chriſt, & al good Paſtors.\f* I ſeeke my brethren, ſhew me where they fede the flockes.
\v 17 And the man ſaid to him: They are departed from this place: for I heard them ſay: Let vs goe into Dothain. Ioſeph therfore went forward after his brethren, and found them in Dothain.
\v 18 Who when they had ſeene him a farre of, before he came nighe them, they deuiſed to kil him:
\v 19 and ſpake among them ſelues: Behold the dreamer commeth,
\v 20 come, let vs kil him, and caſt him into an old ceſterne: and we wil ſay A naughtie wild beaſt hath deuoured him: \f e \fr 37.20 \ft So the Iewes thinking to preuent Chriſts exaltation co-operated vnwitting therto \+it Proſper. li. de promiſs. Dei\+it*.\f* and then it shal appeare what his dreames doe profite him.
\v 21 And Ruben hearing this, endeuored to deliuer him out of their hands, and ſaid:
\v 22 Do not take away his life, neyther sheed ye blood: but caſt him into this ceſterne, that is in the wilderneſſe, and keepe your handes harmeles: and he ſaid this, deſirous to deliuer him out of their handes, and to reſtore him to his father.
\v 23 As ſoone therfore as he came vnto his brethren, forthwith they ſtripped him out of his ſide coate, and of diuers colours.
\v 24 And caſt him into the old ceſterne, that had not water.
\v 25 And ſitting to eate bread, they ſaw Iſmaelites wayfaring men coming from Galaad, and their camels carying ſpices, and roſen, and mirrh into Ægypt.
\v 26 Iudas therfore ſaid to his brethren: What auaileth it vs if we kil our brother, and conceale his bloode?
\v 27 It is better that he be ſold to the Iſmaelites, and that our handes be not polluted: for he is our brother and our flesh. His brethren aſſented to his wordes.
\v 28 And when the Madianite marchants paſſed by, they drawing him out of the ceſterne, ſold him to the Iſmaelites, for \f f \fr 37.28 \ft Some read \+it thirtie\+it*: And as the reading is diuers, ſo Chriſt whom Ioſeph ſignified is more & leſſe eſtimed of diueres. S. Aug. Ser. 81. de temp.\f* twentie peeces of ſiluer, who brought him into Ægypt.
\v 29 And Ruben returning to the ceſterne, findeth not the boy:
\v 30 and renting his garments went to his brethren, and ſaid: The boy doth not appeare, and whither shal I goe?
\v 31 And they tooke his coate, and dipped it in the blood of a kidde, which they had killed:
\v 32 ſending ſome that should carie it to their father, and should ſay: This we haue founde: ſee whether it be thy ſonnes coate, or no.
\v 33 Which when the father acknowledged, he ſaid: It is my ſonnes coate, a naughtie wild beaſt hath eaten him, a beaſt hath deuoured Ioſeph.
\v 34 And tearing his garments, did on ſackcloth, mourning his ſonne a great time.
\v 35 And al his children being gethered together to aſwage their fathers ſorowe, he would not take comforte, but ſaid: I wil deſcend vnto my ſonne into hel, mourning. And whileſt he perſeuered in weeping, \ef - \fr 37.35 \fq Into hel mourning. \ft Proteſtants denying more places for ſoules after this life, then Heauen for the iuſt, and Hel for the wicked, tranſlate the hebrew word \+it sheol, graue\+it* for \+it hel\+it*. Becauſe if they ſhould grant that Iacob, or other holie fathers of the old Teſtament deſcended into hel, they muſt confeſſe ſome other hel, then where the damned are tormented, whither no Chriſtian wil ſay that thoſe fathers went. If they contended only about the ſenſe and meaning of the text, it were more tolerable, for therin they ſpeake, according to their erronious opinion, as they thinke. ¹ But knowing as ſome of them doe, that \+it Hel\+it* is the true word of the text, ²there is no ſinceritie nor moral honeſtie in putting \+it graue\+it*, in place therof. And that they know it, the ſecond table of the Bible, printed at London 1602. witneſſeth, noting for a common place, that in the 37. chap. of Geneſis. v. 35. \+it Hel is taken for graue\+it*, thereby confeſſing, that the true Engliſh word of the holie Scripture in that place is \+it Hel\+it*, but that they would haue it to ſignifie graue. VVherupon anie reaſonable man would thinke to finde the word \+it Hel\+it* in the text, with ſome gloſſe to shew that graue were to be vnderſtood. But in al their Editions, alſo in that which was printed the yeare next folowing, 1603 wherto the ſame table is adioyned, they reade \+it graue\+it*, and not \+it hel\+it* in that place, though in ſome ³ other places, they much diſagree in tranſlating the ſame word. ⁴ As for the ſenſe, it can not be that Iacob ment the graue: for when he ſaid he would goe to his ſonne, he ſuppoſed him to be deuoured by a wild beaſt, and not buried in a graue. And therfore muſt neceſſarily meane, that he would goe where he thought the ſoule of his ſonne to be. VVhich was neither in heauen, for then he would rather haue aſcended thither ioyful, then deſcended to anie place mourning; neither did he meane the hel of the dammed, for that had bene deſperation; but to a lowe place, where the iuſt ſoules then remained in reſt, which was called Limbus Patrum, or Abrahams boſome. ⁵That is, ſaith S. Auguſtin, in his anſwere to Bishop Euodius (Epiſt. 99.) \+it ſecreta cuiuſdam quietis habitatio\+it*. The habitation of a certaine ſecret reſt. \fq ¹ \ft Willful corruption. \fq ² \ft \+it See. S. Hiero. Ep. 119. S. Aug. li. 20. c. 15. civit.\+it* \fq ³ \ft \+it Nu. 16. 2. Reg. 22. Job. 17. Psal. 15. 27. 85.\+it* \fq ⁴ \ft Iacob spake of hel not of graue. \fq ⁵ \ft Abrahams bosome.\ef*
\v 36 the Madianites ſold Ioſeph in Ægypt to Phutiphar an Eunuch of Pharoes maiſter of the ſouldiars.
\c 38
\cd Iudas hauing three ſonnes by a Chananite. 6. marieth the firſt, and after his death, the ſecond to Thamar. 10. VVho alſo dying, he delayeth to match the third VVith her. 15. But him ſelfe begetteth of her (taking her for a harlote) two ſonnes twinnes, Phares and Zara.
\p
\v 1 THE \f a \fr 38.1 \ft Moyſes inſerteth here this hiſtorie, becauſe Chriſt ſhould be borne of the genealogie of Iudas & Phares. Mat. 1.\f* ſame time Iudas going downe from his brethren, turned in to a man an Odollamite, named Hiras.
\v 2 And he ſawe there the daughter of a man of Chanaan, called Sue: and taking her to wife, he did companie with her.
\v 3 Who conceaued, and bare a ſonne, and called his name Her.
\v 4 And conceauing a childe againe, she called her ſonne after he was borne, Onan.
\v 5 She bare alſo the third: whom she called Sela. after whoſe birth, she ceaſed to beare any more.
\v 6 And Iudas gaue a wife to Her his firſt begotten, named Thamar.
\v 7 Alſo Her the firſt begotten of Iudas, was wicked in the ſight of our Lord: and was ſlaine of him.
\v 8 Iudas therfore ſaid to Onan his ſonne: companie with thy brothers wife, and be ioyned to her, that thou mayeſt rayſe ſeede to thy brother. \ef - \fr 38.8 \fq Raiſe ſede to thy brother. \ft ¹ By this it appeareth, that in the law of nature, when a maried man died without iſſue, his brother might lawfully marie the widow: whoſe firſt ſonne ſhould be counted and called the ſonne and heyre of his vncle dead before. The ſame was eſtabliſhed by the law of Moyſes (Deut. 25) VVhich being now abrogated, it remaineth in the Churches powre to conſtitute a law in this behalf, ²and conſequently to diſpenſe in the ſame, ſo farre as is agreable with the law of nature. VVherof ſee more. Leuit. 18. \fq ¹ \ft How a man might marie his brothers wife in the law of nature. The Churches decree is now our rule. \fq ² \ft The Church's decree is now our rule.\ef*
\v 9 He knowing that the children should not be borne to himſelfe, companying with his brothers wife, shed his ſeede vpon the ground, leſt children might be borne in his brothers name.
\v 10 And therfore our Lord ſtroke him, becauſe he did a deteſtable thing.
\v 11 For the which cauſe Iudas ſaid to Thamar his daughter in lawe: Be a widowe in thy fathers houſe, til Sela my ſonne growe vp: for he feared leſt he alſo might dye, as his brethren. Who went her way and dwelt in her fathers houſe.
\v 12 And after many dayes were come and gone: the daughter of Sue the wife of Iudas died: who after his mourning hauing receiued conſolation, went vp to the shearers of his sheepe, himſelfe and Hiras his shepheard of his flocke, the Odolamite, into Thamnas.
\v 13 And it was told \f b \fr 38.13 \ft Thamar ſinned deſiring to be a mother without lawful mariage: and · Iudas ſinned lying with a ſuppoſed harlot \+it S. Aug. li. 22. c 61. 62. & 63 cont. Fauſt.\+it*\f* Thamar that her father in law came vp into Thamnas to sheare his sheepe.
\v 14 Who putting of the garments of her widowhood, tooke a veile: and changing her habite, ſate in the croſſe way, that leadeth to Thamnas: becauſe Sela was growne, and she had not taken him to her huſband.
\v 15 Whom when Iudas had ſeene, he ſuppoſed her to be an harlot: for she had couered her face, leſt she should be knowen.
\v 16 And going vnto her, he ſaid: Suffer me to lye with thee: for he knew her not to be his daughter in law. Who anſwering: What wilt thou geue me that thou maieſt enioy my companie?
\v 17 He ſaid: I wil ſend thee a kid out of the flockes. And when she ſaid againe: I wil ſuffer that thou wilt, if thou geue me a pledge, til thou ſend that which thou doeſt promiſe;
\v 18 Iudas ſaid: What wilt thou to be geuen thee for a pledge? She anſwered: Thy ring, and bracelet, and ſtaffe which thou holdeſt in thy hand. The woman therfore by once companying conceaued,
\v 19 and riſing she went her way: and putting of the apparel which she had taken, put on the garments of her widowhood.
\v 20 And Iudas ſent a kid by his shephard the Odolamite, that he might receiue the pledge againe, which he had geuen to the woman: who when he had not found her,
\v 21 he asked the men of that place: Where is the woman that ſate in the croſſe way? Al making anſwere: There was no harlot in this place.
\v 22 He returned to Iudas, and ſaid to him: I haue not found her: yea the men alſo of that place ſaid vnto me, that there neuer ſate harlot there.
\v 23 Iudas ſaid: Let her take it to her, ſurely she can not charge vs with a lye, I ſent the kid which I promiſed: and thou dideſt not find her.
\v 24 And behold after three moneths they told Iudas, ſaying: Thamar thy daughter in law hath played the harlot, and her bellie ſemeth to ſwel. And Iudas ſaid: Bring her forth \f c \fr 38.24 \ft Adultrie puniſable by death, in the law of nature.\f* that she may be burnt.
\v 25 Who when she was led to execution, she ſent to her father in law, ſaying: By that man, whoſe theſe things are, haue I conceaued: looke whoſe the ring is, and the bracelet, and the ſtaffe.
\v 26 Who acknowledging the giftes, ſaid: She is iuſter then I: becauſe I did not geue her to Sela my ſonne. But he knew her no more.
\v 27 And when she was readie to be brought to bed, there appeared twinnes in her bellie: and in the verie deliuerie of the infants, one put forth the hand, wherin the midwife tyed a ſkarlet ſtring, ſaying:
\v 28 This shal come forth the former.
\v 29 But he drawing backe his hand, the other came forth: and the woman ſaid: Why is the partition diuided for thee? and for this cauſe called his name Phares.
\v 30 Afterward his brother came forth, in whoſe hand was the ſkarelet ſtring: whom she called Zara.
\c 39
\cd Ioſeph being in great credite with his maiſter, hath the whole charge of his houſe. 7. Contemning his myſtris ſolicitation to incontinencie, 13. is falſly accuſed by her to his maiſter: 20. and caſt into priſon, 21. Where againe he getteth credite, and hath the charge of al the priſoners.
\p
\v 1 THERFORE Ioſeph was brought into Ægypt, and Putiphar an Eunuch of Pharao, prince of his armie, a man of Ægypt bought him, at the hand of the Iſmaelites, by whom he was brought.
\v 2 And \f a \fr 39.2 \ft Ioſeph endued with al vertues was a ſpecial mirrhor of chaſtitie. \+it S. Amb. li. de Ioſeph. c. 1\+it*.\f* our Lord was with him, and he was a man, that in al things did proſperouſly: and he dwelt in his maiſters houſe,
\v 3 who knewe verie wel that our Lord was with him, and that al thinges which he did, were directed by him in his hand.
\v 4 And Ioſeph found grace before his maiſter, and miniſtred to him: by whom being made ruler ouer al his thinges, he gouerned the houſe committed to him, and al thinges that were deliuered vnto him:
\v 5 \f 1 \fr 39.5 \ft The foure cardinal vertues reigned in him.\f* and our Lord bleſſed the houſe of the Ægyptian for Ioſephes ſake, and multiplied as wel in houſes, as in landes al his ſubſtance.
\v 6 Neither knew he any other thing, but the bread which he did eate. And Ioſeph was of beautiful countenance, and comely fauored to behold.
\v 7 After manie dayes therfore his maiſtreſſe caſt her eyes on Ioſeph, and ſaid: Sleepe with me.
\v 8 Who \f b \fr 39.8 \ft Temperance.\f* in no wiſe aſſenting to that wicked act, ſaid to her: Behold, my maiſter hauing deliuered al thinges vnto me, knoweth not what he hath in his owne houſe:
\v 9 neither is there any thing which is not in my power, or that he hath not deliuered to me, beſide thee, that art his wife: \f c \fr 39.9 \ft Iuſtice.\f* how therfore can I do this wicked thing, and ſinne againſt my God?
\v 10 With theſe kinde of wordes \f d \fr 39.10 \ft Fortitude.\f* day by day both the woman was importune vpon the young man: and he refuſed the aduoutrie.
\v 11 And it chanced on a certaine day, that Ioſeph went into the houſe, and did ſome buſineſſe without anie man with him:
\v 12 and she catching the ſkirte of his garment, ſaid: Sleepe with me. who \f e \fr 39.12 \ft Prudence.\f* leauing the cloke in her hand, fled, and went forth abroad.
\v 13 And when the woman ſawe the garment in her handes, and her ſelfe to be contemned,
\v 14 she called to her the men of her houſe, and ſaid to them: See he hath brought in an Hebrew, to delude vs: he came vpon me, for to lie with me: and when I had cried out,
\v 15 and he heard my voice, he left the cloake that I held, and fled forth.
\v 16 For an argument therfore of her credite, she reſerued the cloake, and shewed it to her huſband returning home,
\v 17 and ſaid: There came vnto me the Hebrew ſeruant, whom thou dideſt bring hither, for to delude me:
\v 18 and when he heard me crie, he left the cloke which I held, and fled forth.
\v 19 His maiſter hearing theſe thinges, and geuing ouer light credite to his wiues wordes, was very wrath:
\v 20 and deliuered Ioſeph into priſon, where the kinges priſoners were kept, and he was there shut vp.
\v 21 And \f f \fr 39.21 \ft God is more ſpecially with his ſeruants in affliction then in proſperitie. \+it S. Amb. li. de Ioſeph. c. 5\+it*.\f* our Lord was with Ioſeph, and hauing mercie vpon him gaue him grace in the ſight of the chiefe of the priſon.
\v 22 Who deliuered in his hand al the priſoners that were kept in cuſtodie: and whatſoeuer was done, was vnder him.
\v 23 Neyther did himſelfe knowe any thing, hauing committed al things to him: for our Lord was with him, and directed al his workes.
\c 40
\cd Ioſeph interpreteth the dreames of two Eunuches priſoners 12. that the one should be reſtored to his office, 16. the other be hanged, 20. The third day the euent declareth the interpretations to be true, but Ioſeph is forgotten.
\p
\v 1 THESE thinges being ſo done, it chanced that two Eunuches, the cupbearer of the king of Ægypt, and his baker, offended againſt their lord.
\v 2 And Pharao being wrath againſt them (for the one was chiefe of the cupbearers, the other chiefe baker)
\v 3 he ſent them into the priſon of the captaine of the ſouldiers, in the which Ioſeph alſo was priſoner.
\v 4 But the keeper of the priſon deliuered them to Ioſeph, who alſo miniſtred to them: ſome litle time was paſſed, and they were kept in cuſtodie.
\v 5 And they ſawe ech of them both a dreame in one night, according to an interpretation agreing to them ſelues:
\v 6 to whom when Ioſeph was entred in the morning, and ſaw them ſad,
\v 7 he asked them, ſaying: Why is your countenance ſadder to day then it was woont?
\v 8 Who anſwered: We haue ſeene a dreame, & there is no bodie to interprete it to vs. And Ioſeph ſaid to them: Why doth not interpretation belong to God? Tel me what you haue ſeene. \ef - \fr 40.8 \fq Doth not interpretation belong to God. \ft Dreames do come of diuers cauſes. ¹ Some of natural complexion, or diſpoſition, wherby Philoſophers or Phiſitions may probably iudge of the ſtate of mans bodie. Some are rather effects of things paſt, then ſignes of anie thing to come. Of which ſorte the wiſe man ſaith: \+it Dreames do folovv manie cares\+it*. (Eccle. 5.) ² Some are ſuggeſted by euil ſpirites, either to flatter worldlings with great pretenſes, or to terrifie weake mindes with dangers and afflictions, or to vexe and truble thoſe in ſleepe, whom they can not eaſely moue waking. as S. Gregorie diſcourſeth (li. 8. Moral in cap 7 Iob) ³ Some dreames are of God, as in Iacob, Ioſeph, theſe Eunuches, Pharao, Nabuchodonoſor, ⁴and others both good and euil men. But to diſcerne, and aſſuredly to iudge of ſome dreames, whether they be from God, by holie Angels, or illuſions of euil ſpirites, is a ſpecial gift of God, as alſo the interpretation therof belongeth to God, as Ioſeph here teſtifieth. VVhoſoeuer therfore wil be ſecure muſt relie either vpon expreſſe Scripture, or iudgement of the Church, ⁵as in ominous ſpeaches was noted before (chap. 24.) Otherwiſe the general rule is, not to obſerue dreames. Deut. 18. \fq ¹ \ft Some dreames are natural. \fq ² \ft Some are illusions of euil spirites. \fq ³ \ft Some are from God. Dan. 4 \fq ⁴ \ft \+it Dan. 4.\+it* \fq ⁵ \ft Holie Scripture and the Church are iudges of doubtful dreames.\ef*
\v 9 The chiefe of the cupbearers firſt told his dreame: I ſaw before me a vine,
\v 10 wherin were three branches, growing by litle and litle into buddes, and after the bloſſomes the grapes waxed ripe:
\v 11 and the cup of Pharao in my hand: and I tooke the grapes, and wrong them into the cup which I held, and I gaue the cup to Pharao.
\v 12 Ioſeph anſwered: This is the interpretation of the dreame: The three branches, are yet three dayes:
\v 13 after the which Pharao wil remember thy ſeruice, and wil reſtore thee to thy old degree: and thou shalt geue him the cup according to thyne office, as before thou haddeſt wont to doe.
\v 14 Only remember me, when it shal be wel with thee, and doe me this mercie: to put Pharao in mind that he take me out of this priſon:
\v 15 becauſe I was taken away by ſtealth, out of the land of the hebrewes, and here an innocent was I caſt into the lake.
\v 16 The maiſter of the bakers ſeing that he had wiſely reſolued the dreame, he ſaid: And I alſo ſaw a dreame, That I had three baskettes of meale vpon my head:
\v 17 and that in one basket that was the higher, I caried al meates that are made by the art of baking, and that the birds did eate out of it.
\v 18 Ioſeph anſwered: This is the interpretation of the dreame: The three basketts, are yet three dayes:
\v 19 after the which Pharao wil take thy head from thee, and hang thee \f a \fr 40.19 \ft Death on the croſſe was moſt cruel, & moſt ignominious. Cicero. 7. Ver: yet ſuffered by Chriſt, and by him made glorious. \+it Sap. 2. Philip. 2\+it*.\f* on the croſſe, and the foules shal teare thy flesh.
\v 20 The third day after this was the birth day of Pharao: who making a great feaſt to his ſeruantes, at the banket he remembred the maiſter of the cupbearers, and the chiefe of the bakers.
\v 21 And he reſtored the one into his place, to reach him the cuppe,
\v 22 the other he hanged on a gibbet, that the truth of the interpreter might be approued.
\v 23 And yet notwithſtanding the chiefe of the cupbearers, proſperous thinges ſucceeding, forgat his interpreter.
\c 41
\cd Pharao dreaming of fat & leane kine. 5. alſo of ful and thinne eares of corne, 8. no other being able to interprete, 9. Ioſeph is remembred. 25. Who interpreting the ſame. 38. is made ruler ouer al Ægypt. 50. marieth, and hath two ſonnes, Manaſſes and Ephraim.
\p
\v 1 AFTER two yeares \f a \fr 41.1 \ft Pharao his dreames, and his Eunuches were prophetical. For by them God fore-shewed things to come: v 25. yet they were no prophets, · but Ioſeph: who had the gift to interpret them. \+it S. Aug li. 12. c. 9. de Gen. ad lit. S. Greg. li. 11. Moral. in c. 13. Iob.\+it*\f* Pharao ſaw a dreame. He thought he ſtood vpon a riuer,
\v 2 out of the which came vp ſeuen kine, faire and fat exceedingly: and they fed in marish places.
\v 3 Other ſeuen alſo came vp out of the riuer, foule, and caryan leane: and they fed on the very banke of the riuer, in grene places:
\v 4 and they deuoured them, that had the marueylous beautie and good ſtate of bodies. Pharao after he waked,
\v 5 ſlept againe, and ſaw an other dreame: Seuen eares of corne grew forth vpon one ſtalke ful and faire:
\v 6 there ſprang alſo other eares as many, thinne and blaſted with aduſtion,
\v 7 deuouring al the beautie of the former. Pharao awaking vp after his reſt,
\v 8 and when morning was come, being frighted with feare, he ſent to al the interpreters of Ægypt, and to al the wiſe men: and they being called for, told them his dreame, neither was there anie that could interprete it.
\v 9 Then at length the maiſter of the cupbearers remembring himſelfe, ſaid: I confeſſe my ſinne:
\v 10 The king being angrie with his ſeruantes, commanded me and the chiefe of the bakers to be caſt into the priſon of the captaine of the ſouldiers:
\v 11 where in one night both of vs ſaw a dreame portending things to come.
\v 12 There was there a young man an hebrew, ſeruant to the ſame captaine of the ſouldiers: to whom telling our dreames,
\v 13 we heard whatſoeuer afterward the euent of the thing proued to be ſo. for I was reſtored to my office: and he was hanged vpon a gibbet.
\v 14 Forthwith at the kinges commandment, Ioſeph being brought out of the priſon they polled him: and changing his apparel, brought him vnto him.
\v 15 To whom he ſaid: I haue ſeene dreames, and there is not anie that can expound them: which I haue heard, thou doeſt moſt wiſely interprete.
\v 16 Ioſeph anſwered: Without me, God shal anſwere proſperous thinges to Pharao.
\v 17 Pharao therfore told that he had ſeene: Me thought I ſtoode vpon the banke of the riuer,
\v 18 and ſeuen kine came vp out of the banke of the riuer, exceeding faire, and ful of flesh: which grazed on greene places in a marish paſture.
\v 19 And behold, there folowed theſe, other ſeuen kine, ſo paſſing il fauored and leane, that I neuer ſaw the like in the land of Ægypt,
\v 20 which hauing deuoured and conſumed the former,
\v 21 gaue no token of their fulnes: but with the like leanenes and deformitie, looked heauelie. Awaking, and fallen againe into a deepe ſleepe,
\v 22 I ſawe a dreame: Seuen eares of corne grew forth vpon one ſtalke, ful and verie faire.
\v 23 Other ſeuen alſo thinne and blasted, with aduſtion, ſprang of the ſtalke:
\v 24 which deuoured the beautie of the former: I told the dreame to the coniecturers, and there is no man that can declare it.
\v 25 Ioſeph anſwered: The kinges dreame is one: God hath shewed to Pharao \f b \fr 41.25 \ft Theſe things came to paſſe by Gods particular prouidence. \+it Pſalm. 4. God called\+it* (or cauſed) \+it a famine vpon the land\+it*.\f* the thinges that he wil doe.
\v 26 The ſeuen faire kine, and the ſeuen ful eares: be ſeuen yeres of plentifulnes: and both conteine the ſelfe ſame meaning of the dreame.
\v 27 Alſo the ſeuen leane and thinne kine, that came vp after them, and the ſeuen thinne eares, and blaſted with the burning winde: are ſeuen yeares of famine to come.
\v 28 Which ſhal be fulfilled in this order.
\v 29 Behold there shal come ſeuen yeares of great fertilitie in the whole Land of Ægypt:
\v 30 after which shal folowe other ſeuen yeares of ſo great ſterilitie, that al the abundance before shal be forgotten: for the famine shal conſume al the land,
\v 31 and the greatnes of the ſcarſitie, shal deſtroy the greatnes of the plentie.
\v 32 And in that thou dideſt ſee the ſecond time a dreame perteining to the ſame thing: it is a token of the certeintie, for that the worde of God shal come topaſſe, and be fulfilled ſpedely.
\v 33 Now therfore let the king prouide a wiſe man and induſtrious, and make him ruler ouer the Land of Ægypt:
\v 34 that he may appointe ouerſeers ouer al countries: and gether into barnes the fifth part of the fruites, during the ſeuen yeares of the fertilitie,
\v 35 that now preſently shal enſewe: and let al the corne be laid vp, vnder Pharaoes handes, and let it be reſerued in the cities.
\v 36 And let it be in a readines, againſt the famine of ſeuen yeares to come, which shal oppreſſe Ægypt, and the land shal not be conſumed with ſcarſitie.
\v 37 The counſel pleaſed Pharao, and al his ſeruants:
\v 38 and he ſpake to them: Can we find ſuch an other man, that is ful of the ſpirite of God?
\v 39 He ſaid therfore to Ioſeph: Becauſe God hath shewed thee al things that thou haſt ſpoken, can I find a wiſer and one like vnto thee?
\v 40 Thou shalt be ouer my houſe, and at the commandment of thy mouth al the people shal obey: only in the throne of the kingdome I wil goe before thee.
\v 41 And againe Pharao ſaid to Ioſeph: Behold, I haue appointed thee ouer the whole land of Ægypt.
\v 42 And he tooke his ring from his owne hand, and gaue it into his hand: and he put vpon him a ſilke roabe, and put a chaine of gold about his necke.
\v 43 And he made him goe vp into his ſecond chariot, the cryer proclayming that al should bowe their knee before him, and that they should know he was made gouernour ouer the whole Land of Ægypt. \ef - \fr 41.43 \fq Made gouernour. \ft ¹ It is eaſie in the eyes of God, ſuddenly to enrich the poore. For who would haue thought (ſaith Philo) ² that in one day a boudman should be made a lord, a poore priſoner the chiefe of the nobilitie, an vnder gaolor the viceroy, or kings deputie, for a common priſon to haue a kinglie court of his owne, from extreme ignominie, to aſcend into ſo hiegh a roome of dignitie! \fq ¹ \ft Holie Ioseph suddenly aduanced. \fq ² \ft \+it Eccl. 11. li. de Ioseph.\+it*\ef*
\v 44 And the king ſaid to Ioſeph: I am Pharao: without thy commandment no man shal moue hand or foote in al the land of Ægypt.
\v 45 And he turned his name, and called him in the Ægyptian togue the Sauiour of the world. And he gaue him to wife Aſeneth the daughter of Putiphar \f c \fr 41.45 \ft \+it Cohen\+it* ſignifieth prieſt; as not only the latin, but alſo the 70. & Philo and Ioſephus here tranſlate though ſometimes it ſignifieth \+it prince\+it*. as the Chaldey paraphraſis interpreteth, wherby it is probable that this Putiphar was both a prieſt, and a prince.\f* prieſt of Heliopolis. Ioſeph therfore went forth to the land of Ægypt \ef - \fr 41.45 \fq Sauiour of the vvorld. \ft In the original text the new name and title geuen by Pharao to Ioſeph is expreſſed by theſe two wordes, \+it Saphnath pahanaach\+it*: the former \+it ſiphnath\+it* in Hebrew ſignifieth a ſecrete or hidden thing, of \+it ſaphan\+it* to hide: but the ſignification of the other word \+it pahanaach\+it*, is more vncertaine, being found no where els in the holie Bible. The Rabins do commonly interprete them both together, \+it The man to vvhom ſecretes are reueled\+it*, or, \+it The reueler of ſecretes\+it*. ¹ and ſo this name agreeth wel to Ioſeph, in reſpect of the gift of interpreting dreames. But beſides his interpreting, he alſo gaue moſt wiſe counſel, that tended to the ſaftie of manie, which, it is like, Pharao ment to expreſſe by this new name. And S Hierom, who doubtles with great diligence, and no leſſe iudgement, ſearched the true ſignification therof, ſaith, that albeit this name in Hebrew ſoundeth \+it the finder out of ſecrets\+it*, yet ſeing it was impoſed by an Ægyptian (who knew no Hebrew) the reaſon therof muſt be had of the ſame tongue; and theſe two wordes in the Ægyptian language are interpreted ² \+it The Sauiour of the world\+it*: for that he deliuered the world from the iminent ruine of famine Thus ſaith S. Hierom And ſo moſt aptly the figure anſwereth to Chriſt, ³the true Sauiour of the world. \fq ¹ \ft Ioseph truly called the reueler of secrets. \fq ² \ft But more honorably, the Sauiour of the world Therin a figure of Christ. \fq ³ \ft Therein a figure of Christ.\ef*
\v 46 (and he was thirtie yeares old when he ſtood in the ſight of king Pharao) and did circuite al the countries of Ægypt.
\v 47 And the fertilitie of the ſeuen yeares came: and the corne being bound vp into ſheaues was gethered togeather into the barnes of Ægypt.
\v 48 Al the abundance alſo of graine was laid vp in euerie citie.
\v 49 And there was ſo great abundance of wheat, that it became equal to the ſand of the ſea, and the plentie exceeded meaſure.
\v 50 And there were borne vnto Ioſeph two ſonnes before the famine came: whom Aſeneth the daughter of Putiphar prieſt of Heliopolis bare him.
\v 51 And he called the name of the firſt begotten \f 1 \fr 41.51 \ft Obliuion.\f* Manaſſes, ſaing: God made me to forget al my labours, & my fathers houſe.
\v 52 The name alſo of the ſecond he called \f 1 \fr 41.52 \ft Fruitful or Grovving.\f* Ephraim, ſaing: God hath made me to encreaſe in the land of my pouertie.
\v 53 Therfore when the ſeuen yeares of the plentifulnes, that had bene in Ægypt were paſſed:
\v 54 the ſeuen yeares of ſcarſitie beganne to come, which Ioſeph foretold: and in the whole world the famine preuailed, but in al the land of Ægypt there was bread.
\v 55 The which being in hunger, the people cried to Pharao, deſiring foode. To whom he anſwered: Goe ye to Ioſeph: and whatſoeuer he ſhal ſay to you, that doe ye.
\v 56 And the famine dayly encreaſed in al the land: and Ioſeph opened al the barnes, and ſold to the Ægyptians: for them alſo the famine had opreſſed.
\v 57 And al prouinces came into Ægypt, to buy victuales, and to moderate the miſerie of the ſcarſitie.
\c 42
\cd Iacob ſendeth tenne of his ſonnes to bye corne in Ægypt. 7. where Ioſeph knowing them, they not knowing him, with hard ſpeaches putteth them in priſon. 18. At laſt Simeon remayning in cuſtodie, til Beniamin be brought, the reſt are diſmiſſed, 25. with their money, vnknowen to them, in their ſackes.
\p
\v 1 AND Iacob hearing, that victuales were ſold in Ægypt, he ſaid to his ſonnes: Why neglect ye?
\v 2 I haue heard that wheat is ſold in Ægypt: Goe ye downe, and bye vs neceſſaries, that we may liue, and not be conſumed with lacke.
\v 3 Therfore the tenne brethren of Ioſeph going downe, to bye corne in Ægypt,
\v 4 Beniamin being kept at home by Iacob, who ſaid to his brethren: Leſt perhaps he take any harme in the iourney:
\v 5 entred into the land of Ægypt with others that went to bye. for the famine was in the land of Chanaan.
\v 6 And Ioſeph was prince in the land of Ægypt, and at his pleaſure corne was ſold to the people. And when his brethren had adored him,
\v 7 and he knewe them, he ſpake as it were to ſtrangers ſomewhat roughly, asking them: From whence came you? Who anſwered: From the Land of chanaan, that we may bye neceſſaries to liue. \ef - \fr 42.7 \fq ſome vvhat roughly. \ft Joſeph afflicted his brethren to bring them into conſideration of their former faults, and to true contrition ¹. Without which, though injuries be remitted by men, yet the offenders are not abſolued before God. Therefore he louing them and hating their ſinne, by affliction brought them to vnderſtanding. VVho being at laſt truly penitent, he acknowledged and moſt louingly embraced them, and prouided for them in their neceſſitie. \+it S. Aug. ſer. 82. de temp.\+it* And this example S. Gregorie (ho. 22. in Ezech.) applieth to the inſtruction of Paſtors of ſouls: that they procure true repentance before abſolution of ſins ²: \+it Ne ſi inordinate culpa dimittitur, is qui eſt culpabilis in reatu grauius aſtringatur: Lest, if the fault be diſorderly remitted, he that is faultie be more grievouſly entangled in guilt of conſcience\+it*: therefore with much diſcretion ſeueritie in ſhew, and clemencie in mind are required. \fq ¹ \ft Contrition necessarie for the remission of sinnes. \fq ² \ft Disorderlie remission is hurtful.\ef*
\v 8 And yet himſelfe knowing his brethren, was not knowen of them.
\v 9 And remembring the dreames, which ſome times he had ſeene, he ſaid to them: You are ſpies: to view the weaker parts of the land you are come. \ef - \fr 42.9 \fq You are ſpies. \ft ¹ To the ſame purpoſe he calleh them ſpies. After the maner of examiners calling ſuſpected perſons, as they may ſeme to be: therby to trie what they would anſwer. Al for their holſome penance, and withal to procure them afterwards more compaſſion among the Ægyptians. S. Aug. q. 139. ſuper. Gen. \fq ¹ \ft Ioseph calleth his brothers spies for their good.\ef*
\v 10 Who ſaid: It is not ſo, my lord, but thy ſeruantes are come to bye victuals.
\v 11 We are al the ſonnes of one man: we are come as men of peace, neither do thy ſeruantes goe about anie euil.
\v 12 To whom he anſwered: It is otherwiſe: you came to conſider the vndefenſed partes of this land.
\v 13 But they: We thy ſeruantes, ſay they, are twelue brethren, the ſonnes of one man in the Land of Chanaan: the youngeſt is with our father, the other is not liuing.
\v 14 This is it, quoth he, that I ſaid: You are ſpies.
\v 15 Now preſently I wil take a trial of you: by the health of Pharao you shal not depart hence, vntil your youngeſt brother do come. \ef - \fr 42.15 \fq By the health of Pharao. \ft ¹ Joſeph in ſwearing by Pharao's health, honoured God, the giver and conſeruer of health, life, power, dignitie, and all that was in Pharao. Whereby we ſee that this maner of ſwearing was lawful. As now likewiſe Chriſtians lawfully ſweare not only by God, but alſo by his creatures, ſaying: As God shal helpe them, and his holie Goſpels: ſo it be with due circumſtances and requiſite conditions, namely with thoſe which the Prophet Hieremie mentioneth ², \+it in truth, in iudgement, and in iuſtice\+it*: not falſe, rash, nor of an vniuſt thing: nor in frequent and common talke (a moſt bad cuſtome) for ſo thou muſt neither ſweare by God himſelf, nor (as our Sauiour teacheth) ³ by heauen, nor by the earth, nor by Hieruſalem, nor by thy head, nor anie thing els: ſignifying that in ſome caſe, and due maner we may ſweare by any of theſe. ⁴ And in this particular Joſeph did rather name Pharao in his oath then God, as wel to conceale himſelf as yet from his brethren, as to ſtrike more terror in their hearts by naming his Maſter the King, in whom he had more intereſt then they. \fq ¹ \ft It is lawful to sweare by creatures. \fq ² \ft \+it Hier. 4.\+it* \fq ³ \ft \+it Mat. 5.\+it* \fq ⁴ \ft In some case more conuenient then to name God expresly.\ef*
\v 16 Send you one of you to bring him: and you shal be in priſon, til the thinges be proued which you haue ſaid, whether they be true or falſe: \f a \fr 42.16 \ft If theſe things which ye ſay be proued falſe, ye are to be held as ſpies, for your lying. \+it S Aug. q. 139. ſuper Gen\+it*.\f* otherwiſe by the health of Pharao you are ſpies.
\v 17 Therfore he put them in priſon three dayes.
\v 18 And the third day bringing them out of priſon, he ſaid: Doe as I haue ſaid, and you shal liue: for I feare God.
\v 19 If you be men of peace, let one of your brethren be bounde in priſon: and goe ye your wayes, and carie the corne that you haue bought, vnto your houſes,
\v 20 and bring your youngeſt brother to me, that I may proue your ſayings to be true, and that you die not. They did as he had ſaid,
\v 21 and they talked one to an other: Worthely do we ſuffer theſe thinges, becauſe we haue ſinned againſt our brother, ſeing the diſtreſſe of his ſoule, whileſt he beſought vs, and we heard not: therfore is this tribulation come vpon vs.
\v 22 Among whom Ruben one of them, ſaid: Why, did not I ſay to you: Sinne not againſt the boy: and you heard me not? Loe his blood is required.
\v 23 And they knew not that Ioſeph vnderſtood: becauſe he ſpake to them by an interpreter.
\v 24 And he turned away himſelfe a litle while, and wept: and returning he ſpake to them.
\v 25 And taking Simeon, and binding him in their preſence, he commanded his ſeruantes that they ſhould fil vp their ſackes with wheat, and put euerie mans money againe in their bagges, geuing them beſides for to eate on the way: who did ſo.
\v 26 But they carying their corne vpon their aſſes, tooke their iourney.
\v 27 And one of them opening his ſacke, to geue his beaſt prouender in the inne, beholding the money in the ſackes mouth,
\v 28 he ſaid to his brethren: My money is geuen me againe, behold it is in the ſacke. And being aſtonyed, and trubled amongeſt themſelues, they ſaid: What is this, that God hath done vnto vs?
\v 29 And they came to Iacob their father into the land of Chanaan, and they told him al thinges that had chanced vnto them, ſaying:
\v 30 The lord of the land ſpake to vs roughly, and thought vs to be ſpies of the prouince,
\v 31 to whom we anſwered: We are men of peace, neither do we attempt any treachery.
\v 32 We are twelue brethren borne of one father: one is not liuing, the youngeſt is with our father in the Land of Chanaan.
\v 33 Who ſaid to vs: Thus shal I trie that you be men of peace: Leaue one of your brethren with me, and take ye prouiſion neceſſarie for your houſes, and goe your wayes,
\v 34 and bring your youngeſt brother to me, that I may know you are not ſpies: and you may receiue this felowe againe, that is kept in priſon: and afterwardes may haue licence to bye what thinges you wil.
\v 35 This being ſaid, when they powred out their corne, euerie man found his money tied in the mouth of the ſackes: and al being aſtonyed together,
\v 36 their father Iacob ſaid: You haue made me to be without children, Ioſeph is not liuing, Simeon is kept in bondes, and Beniamin you wil take away: al theſe euils are fallen vpon me.
\v 37 To whom Ruben anſwered: Kil my two ſonnes, if I bring him not againe to thee: deliuer him into my hand, and I wil reſtore him vnto thee.
\v 38 But he ſaid: My ſonne shal not go downe with you: his brother is dead, and he alone is remayning: if any aduerſitie shal chance to him in the land to the which you goe, you shal bring downe \f b \fr 42.38 \ft Myn old age or, me an old man. S. Aug. q. 142.\f* my hoare heares with ſorowe vnto hel. \ef - \fr 42.38 \fq Vnto hel. \ft ¹ To that place where ſoules remaine, as before. chap. 37. v. 35. For this phraſe, of \+it bringing vnto hel\+it*, and \+it deſcending into hel\+it*, vſually ſpoken in the old Teſtament, of al ſortes of ſoules both iuſt and wicked, ſignifieth that al went that time to hel, that is, to a lowe place, farre diſtant from heauen. But ſome to reſt, and ſome to paine. VVherupon S. Hierom ſaith: Hel is a place. wherin ſoules are included, either in reſt, or in paines, according to the qualitie of their deſerts. \fq ¹ \ft Diuers mansions in hel.\ef*
\c 43
\cd The famine preſſing, the land, Iacob willith his ſonnes to goe againe int. Ægypt to bye more corne: 3. Who refuſing to goe without Beniamin. 11. he is ſent with them, and preſents and duble money, leſt the former were brought back by error. 16. Ioſeph ſeing Beniamin, 23. deliuereth Simeon, and intertaineth them al at dinner.
\p
\v 1 IN THE meane time the famine did oppreſſe al the land very ſore.
\v 2 And the prouiſion being ſpent, which they had bronght out of Ægypt, Iacob ſaid to his ſonnes: Goe againe, and by vs a little victual.
\v 3 Iudas anſwered: That ſame man denounced vnto vs vnder atteſtation of an oath, ſaing: You shal not ſee my face, vnleſſe you bring your youngeſt brother with you.
\v 4 If therfore thou wilt ſend him with vs, we wil goe forward together, and wil bye neceſſaries for thee:
\v 5 but if thou wilt not, we wil not goe: for the man, as we haue often ſaid, denounced vnto vs ſaing: You shal not ſee my face without your youngeſt brother.
\v 6 Iſrael ſaid to them: You haue done this to my miſerie, in that you told him that you had an other brother alſo.
\v 7 But they anſwered: The man asked vs in order our progenie: if our father liued: if we had a brother: and we anſwered him conſequently to that which he demanded: could we know that he would ſay: Bring hither your brother with you?
\v 8 Iudas alſo ſaid to his father: Send the boy with me, that we may ſet forwarde, and may liue: leſt we and our litle ones die.
\v 9 I take vpon me the boy: require him of my hand, vnleſſe I bring him againe, and reſtore him to thee, I wil be \f a \fr 43.9 \ft Guilt of ſinne is a greater bond then the life of Rubens ſonnes; which he offered, & Iacob yelded not therto: & yet granted to this offer of Iudas.\f* guiltie of ſinne againſt thee for euer.
\v 10 If delay had not bene made, we had bene come now the ſecond time.
\v 11 Therfore Iſrael their father ſaid to them: If it muſt nedes be ſo; do that you wil: take of the beſt fruites of the land in your veſſels, and carie to the man for preſents, a courteſie of roſen, and of honey, and of incenſe, of mirhe, of terebinth, and of almondes.
\v 12 Duble money alſo carie with you: and recarie that you founde in your ſackes, leſt perhaps it was done by an errour:
\v 13 but take alſo your brother, and goe to the man.
\v 14 And my God almightie make him fauorable vnto you: and ſend backe with you your brother, whom he keepeth, and this Beniamin: as for me I shal be deſolate without children.
\v 15 The men therfore tooke the preſentes, and the duble money, and Beniamin: and went downe inro Ægypt, and ſtood before Ioſeph.
\v 16 Whom when he had ſeene, and Beniamin withal, he commanded the ſteward of his houſe; ſaing: Bring in the men into the houſe, and kil victimes, and prepare a feaſt: becauſe they ſhal eate with me at noone.
\v 17 He did that which was commanded him, and brought the men into the houſe.
\v 18 And there being ſore afraied, they ſaid one to an other: becauſe of the money, which we caried backe the firſt time in our ſackes, we are brought in: that he may turne vpon vs \f 1 \fr 43.18 \ft \+it Calumniam\+it*.\f* a falſe accuſation, and forceibly bring both vs, and our aſſes into bondage.
\v 19 Wherfore in the verie dore ſtepping to the ſteward of the houſe,
\v 20 they ſpake: Sir we deſire thee to heare vs. Now once before we came downe to bye prouiſion:
\v 21 which being bought, when we were come to the Inne, we opened our ſackes, and found our money in the mouthes of the ſackes: which we haue now brought againe in the ſame weight.
\v 22 But we haue brought other money beſides, to bye the thinges that are neceſſarie for vs: our conſcience is not priuie, who put it in our bagges.
\v 23 But he anſwered: Peace be with you, feare you not: your God, and the God of your father hath geuen you treaſures in your ſackes. for the money, which you gaue me, I haue for good. And he brought forth Simeon vnto them.
\v 24 And being brought into the houſe, he fetched water, and they waſhed their feete, and he gaue prouender to their aſſes.
\v 25 But they made readie the preſentes, til Ioſeph should come in at noone: for they had heard that they should eate bread there.
\v 26 Therfore Ioſeph came in to his houſe, and they offered him preſentes holding them in their handes, and \f b \fr 43.26 \ft They now adore him, whom they ſold, leſt they ſhould adore him. S. Greg. ho. 22. in Ezech.\f* they adored proſtrate to the ground.
\v 27 But he curteouſly reſaluting them, asked them ſaying: Is the old man your father in health, of whom you told me? Is he yet liuing?
\v 28 Who anſwered: Thy ſeruant our father is in health, he is yet liuing. And bowing themſelues, they adored him.
\v 29 And Ioſeph lifting vp his eyes, ſawe Beniamin his brother of the ſame mother, and ſaid: Is this your young brother, of whom you told me? And againe: God, ſaith he, be merciful vnto thee my ſonne.
\v 30 And he made haſt becauſe his hart was moued vpon his brother, and teares brake forth: and entring into his chamber he wept.
\v 31 And when he had washed his face, comming forth againe, he refrained himſelfe, and ſaid: Set bread on the table.
\v 32 Which being ſet downe, to Ioſeph apart, and to his brethren apart, to the Ægyptians alſo that did eate with him apart (for it is vnlawful for the Ægyptians to eate with the Hebrewes, and \f c \fr 43.32 \ft See Exodi. 8. v. 26.\f* they thinke ſuch a feaſt prophane),
\v 33 they ſate before him, the firſt begotten according to his firſt-birth, and the youngeſt according to his age. And they maruailed out of meaſure,
\v 34 taking the portions that they receiued of him: and the greater portion came to Beniamin, ſo that it exceeded \f d \fr 43.34 \ft Euerie one hauing fiue portions, Beniamin had duble. Ioſephus li. 1. Antiq.\f* by fiue partes. And they dranke and were inebriated with him. \ef - \fr 43.34 \fq Inebriated. \ft ¹ Not that they did eate or drinck to much, or fel into exceſſe, but competently. As the earth is ſaid to be inebriated with raine (Pſal. 64.) being ſufficiently watered, and ſo made fructful, not drowned, nor ouerflowed, for ſo it is vnfructful. S. Hierom. Tradit. Heb. S. Aug. q. 144. ſuper Gen. \fq ¹ \ft Moderation to be vsed in feasting.\ef*
\c 44
\cd Ioſeph cauſeth their ſackes to be filled with corne, and their money to be put againe therin, and in Beniamins ſack alſo a ſiluer cuppe, 4. and when they were parted, ſending after them, chargeth them with theft. 12. And the cuppe being fond in Beniamins ſack, they al much afflicted returne to Ioſeph. 17. Who threatning to kepe Beniamin, 18. Iudas intreateth, 32. and finally offereth him ſelfe to ſeruitude for him.
\p
\v 1 AND Ioſeph commanded the ſteward of his houſe, ſaing: Fil their ſackes with corne, as much as they can hold: and put the money of euerie one in the top of his ſack.
\v 2 And in the ſackes mouth \f a \fr 44.2 \ft By this Ioſeph tried his bretherens affection, whether they would intrete for Beniamin, or ſuffer him to be captiue: as they had before ſold him ſelfe to captiuitie. \+it Theod. q. 105. in Gen\+it*.\f* of the younger put my ſiluer cup, and the price which he gaue for the wheat. And it was ſo done.
\v 3 And when the morning roſe, they were diſmiſt with their aſſes.
\v 4 And they were now departed out of the citie, and had gone forward a little way: then Ioſeph ſending for the ſteward of his houſe, ſaid: Ariſe, quoth he, and purſew the men: and ouertaking them ſay to them: Why haue you rendred euil for good?
\v 5 The cup which you haue ſtollen, is that wherin my lord doth drinke, and wherin he is wont to diuine: you haue done a verie euil thing.
\v 6 He did as he had commanded. And hauing ouertaken them, he ſpake to them in the ſame order.
\v 7 Who anſwered: Why doth our lord ſpeake ſo, as though thy ſeruautes had committed ſo haynous a fact?
\v 8 The money, that we found in the top of our ſackes, we recaried to thee from the land of Chanaan: and how foloweth it, that we haue ſtollen out of thy lordes houſe, gold or ſiluer?
\v 9 With whom ſoeuer of thy ſeruantes that shal be found, which thou ſeekeſt, let him die, and we wil be the bondmen of our lord.
\v 10 Who ſaid to them: Be it done according to your ſentence: with whom ſoeuer it shal be found, be he my bondman, and you shal be guiltles.
\v 11 Therfore in haſte taking downe their ſackes vpon the ground, euerie man opened.
\v 12 Which when he had ſearched, beginning from the elder euen to the youngeſt, he found the cup in Beniamins ſack.
\v 13 But they \f b \fr 44.13 \ft O torments of mercie! he vexeth whom he loueth. \+it S. Greg. ho. 22. in Ezech\+it*.\f* renting their garments, and loading their aſſes againe, returned into the towne.
\v 14 And Iudas foremoſt with his brethren entred in to Ioſeph (for he was not yet gone out of the place) and they fel togeather before him on the ground.
\v 15 To whom he ſaid: Why would you doe ſoe? know you not that there is not the like to me in the ſcience \f c \fr 44.15 \ft Ioſeph being in dede a prophet, knowing more then al ſorcerers in Ægypt ſpoke of himſelfe as he was eſtemed in that place. \+it S. Aug. q. 145. ſuper Gen\+it*.\f* of diuining.
\v 16 To whom Iudas ſaid: What shal we anſwere, my lord? or what shal we ſpeake, or be able iuſtly to pretend? God hath found the iniquitie of thy ſeruantes: loe we are al bondmen to my lord, both we, & he, with whom the cup was found.
\v 17 Ioſeph anſwered: God forbid that I should ſo doe: he that ſtole the cup, the ſame be my bondman: and goe you free vnto your father.
\v 18 And Iudas approching nearer, ſaid boldly: I beſeech thee my lord, let thy ſeruant ſpeake a word in thine eares, and be not angrie with thy ſeruant: for after Pharao thou art,
\v 19 my lord. Thou dideſt aske thy ſeruantes the firſt time: Haue you a father or a brother?
\v 20 and we anſwered thee my lord: We haue a father an old man, and a little boy, that was borne in his old age; whoſe brother by the mother is dead: and his mother hath him only, and his father loueth him tenderly.
\v 21 And thou ſaidſt to thy ſeruants: Bring him hither to me, and I wil ſet myn eyes on him.
\v 22 We ſuggeſted to my lord: The boy can not leaue his father: for if he leaue him, he wil die.
\v 23 And thou ſaideſt to thy ſeruants: Vnleſſe your youngeſt brother come with you, you shal no more ſee my face.
\v 24 Therfore when we were gone vp to thy ſeruant our father, we told him al things that my lord did ſpeake.
\v 25 And our father ſaid: Goe againe, and bye vs a litle wheate.
\v 26 To whom we ſaid: We can not goe: if our youngeſt brother shal goe downe with vs, we wil ſet forward together: otherwiſe he being abſent, we dare not ſee the mans face.
\v 27 Wherunto he anſwered: You know that my wife bare me two.
\v 28 One went forth, and you ſaid: A beaſt did deuour him: and hitherto he appeareth not.
\v 29 If you take this alſo, and ought befal him in the way, you shal bring downe \f d \fr 44.29 \ft See pag. 130.\f* my hoare hayres with ſorow vnto hel.
\v 30 Therfore if I shal enter to thy ſeruant our father, and the boy be wanting (wheras his life dependeth vpon the life of him)
\v 31 and he shal ſee that he is not with vs, he wil dye, & thy ſeruants shal bring downe his hoare hayres with ſorow vnto hel.
\v 32 Let me be thy proper ſeruant, that did take him into my protection and promiſed ſaying: Vnleſſe I bring him againe I wil be guilty of ſinne againſt my father for euer.
\v 33 I therfore thy ſeruant wil tary in ſteed of the childe in the ſeruice of my lord, and let the childe goe vp with his brethren.
\v 34 For I can not returne to my father, the childe being abſent; leſt I ſtand by a witnes of the calamitie, that shal oppreſſe my father.
\c 45
\cd Ioſeph manifeſteth himſelf to his bretheren. Who being much terified he comforteth them, and weeping embraceth euerie one. 16. The bruite wherof coming to Pharao, he congratulating commendeth Ioſeph to cal his father with al his familie into Ægypt. 21. So the eleuen brothers are ſent away with giftes and prouiſion for Iacobs iourney. 26. Al which the father vnderſtanding is reuiued in ſpirite.
\p
\v 1 IOSEPH could no longer refrayne manie ſtanding by in preſence: wherupon he commanded that al should goe forth, and no ſtranger should be preſent at their acknowledging one of another.
\v 2 And he lifted vp his voice with weeping, which the Ægyptians heard, and al the houſe of Pharao.
\v 3 And he ſaid to his Brethren: I am Ioſeph: is my father yet liuing? His brethren could not anſwer him being teribly aſtonyed out of meaſure.
\v 4 To whom gently he ſaid: Come hither to me. And when they were come nere him, I am, quoth he, Ioſeph, your brother, whom you ſolde into Ægypt.
\v 5 Be not affraid, neither let it ſeeme to you a hard caſe that you did ſel me into theſe countries: for \f a \fr 45.5 \ft Gods prouidence turned their euil dealing to the good of the whole familie. \+it chap. 50. v. 20\+it*.\f* God ſent me before you into Ægypt for your preſeruation.
\v 6 For it is two yeares ſince the famin begain to be vpon the earth, and yet fiue yeares remaine, wherin there can be neither earing nor reaping.
\v 7 And God ſent me before, that you may be preſerued vpon the earth, and may haue victuals to liue.
\v 8 Not by your counſail, but by the wil of God was I ſent hither: who hath made me as it were a father to Pharao, and lord of his whole houſe, and prince in al the land of Ægypt.
\v 9 Make haſt and get ye vp to my father, and you shal ſay to him: Thy ſonne Ioſeph willeth thus: God hath made me lord of the whole land of Ægypt: come downe to me, tary not.
\v 10 And thou shalt dwel in the land of Geſſen: and thou shalt be nere me thou and thy ſonnes, and thy ſonnes children, thy shepe, and thy heardes. and al things that thou doſt poſſeſſe.
\v 11 And there I wil fede thee (for yet there are fiue yeares of famine remayning) leſt both thou perish, and thy houſe, & al things that thou doſt poſſeſſe.
\v 12 Behold, your eyes, and the eyes of my brother Beniamin doe ſee, that it is my mouth that ſpeaketh vnto you.
\v 13 Report to my father my whole glorie and al things that you haue ſeene in Ægypt: make haſt, and bring him to me.
\v 14 And falling vpon the neck of his brother Beniamin, embracing him he wept: he alſo in like maner weeping vpon his neck.
\v 15 And Ioſeph kiſſed al his brethren, and wept vpon euerie one: after which things they were bold to ſpeake vnto him.
\v 16 And it was heard of, and very famouſly reported abrode in the kings courte: The brethren of Ioſeph are come: and \f b \fr 45.16 \ft Ioſephs prudent proceding before he made him ſelfe knowen to his brethren and them to Pharao, procured al this ioy & fauour towards them in Ægypt.\f* Pharao was glad, and al his familie.
\v 17 And he ſpake to Ioſeph that he should command his brethren, ſaying: Loading your beaſts goe into the Land of Chanaan,
\v 18 and take thence your father and kinne, and come to me: and I wil geue you al the good things of Ægypt, that you may eate the marow of the land.
\v 19 Geue commandement alſo that they take waynes out of the land of Ægypt, for the carage of their litle ones and wyues: and ſay: Take vp your father, and make haſt to come with al ſpede.
\v 20 Neither doe you leaue any thing of your houshould ſtuffe: for al the riches of Ægypt, shal be yours.
\v 21 And the ſonnes of Iſrael did as it was commanded them. To whom Ioſeph gaue waynes according to Pharaos commandement: and victuals for the way.
\v 22 He bad alſo to be brought vp for euery one two robes: but to Beniamin he gaue three hundred peeces of ſiluer with fiue robes of the beſt:
\v 23 ſending to his father as much money and rayment, adding beſides them he aſſes that should carie of al the riches of Ægypt, and as many shee aſſes, carying wheat for the iourney and bread.
\v 24 Therfore he diſmiſſed his brethren, and when they were departing he ſaid to them: Be not angrie in the way. \ef - \fr 45.24 \fq Be not angrie in the way. \ft Trauelers in iourney are eaſily prouoked to anger and brawling: eſpecially if they auoide not probable occaſions. ¹Therfore Ioſeph admoniſheth his brothers to beware therof, leſt in talking of him, and how they had ſold him to ſtrangers, ſome of them might accuſe others, and excuſe themſelues, and ſo fal into new offences. S. Chris. ho. 64. in Gen. \fq ¹ \ft Occasion of sinne to be auoided.\ef*
\v 25 Who going vp out of Ægypt, came into the land of Chanaan to their father Iacob.
\v 26 And they told him ſaying: Ioſeph thy ſonne is liuing: and he ruleth in al the Land of Ægypt. Which when Iacob heard, awaking as it were out of a heauie ſleepe, notwithſtanding did not beleeue them.
\v 27 They on the contrarie ſide reported the whole order of the thing. And when he ſawe the waynes and al things that he had ſent, his ſpirit reuiued,
\v 28 and he ſaid: It ſufficeth me if Ioſeph my ſonne be liuing yet: I wil goe, and ſee him before I dye.
\c 46
\cd Iſrael warranted in a viſion from God, goeth into Ægypt with al his famimlie, 8. Who are here recited. 28. Ioſeph meeting him in Geſſen, aduiſeth him to tel Pharao, that they are shepheards by their trade of life. \f - \ft The eighth and laſt part of this book.\f*
\p
\v 1 AND \f 1 \fr 46.1 \ft Of Jacob and his progenies going into Ægypt. Of his and Joſeph's death.\f* Iſrael taking his iourney, with al things that he had, came to the wel of the oath, and killing there victimes to the God of his father Iſaac. \ef - \fr 46.1 \fq Came to the wel of oath. \ft ¹ In this holie place (called \+it Berſebee\+it*, that is, \+it vvel of oath\+it*, where Abraham and Iſaac had confirmed by oath, their league with the Kings of the countrie, and erected Altares, Iacob alſo conſulted God, about his going into Ægypt, and was commanded to goe with al that he had. \fq ¹ \ft God reueleth his wil in holie places.\ef*
\v 2 He heard him by a viſion of the night calling him, and ſaying vnto him: Iacob, Iacob. To whom he anſwered: Loe here I am.
\v 3 God ſaid to him: I am the moſt mightie God of thy father: feare not, goe downe into Ægipt, for into a great nation wil I make thee there.
\v 4 I wil goe downe with thee thither, and thence wil I bring thee returning: Ioſeph alſo shal put his handes vpon thine eyes.
\v 5 And Iacob roſe vp from the wel of the oath: and his ſonnes tooke him vp, with their little ones and wiues in the waynes, which Pharao had ſent to carie the old man,
\v 6 and al that he had poſſeſſed in the Land of Chanaan: and he came into Ægypt with al his ſede,
\v 7 his ſonnes, and nephewes, daughters, and al his progenie together.
\v 8 And theſe are the names of the children of Iſrael, that entred into Ægypt, him ſelfe with his children. His firſt-begotten Ruben.
\v 9 The ſonnes of Ruben: Henoch and Phallu and Heſron and Charmi.
\v 10 The ſonnes of Simeon: Iamuel and Iamin and Ahod, and Iachin and Sohar, and Saul the ſonne of Chananiteſſe.
\v 11 The ſonnes of Leui: Gerſon and Caath and Merari.
\v 12 The ſonnes of Iuda: Her and Onan & Sela and Phares and Zara. And Her and Onan died in the land of Chanaan. And there were ſonnes borne to Phares: Heſron and Hamul.
\v 13 The ſonnes of Iſſachar: Thola and Phua and Iob and Semron.
\v 14 The ſonnes of Zabulon: Sared & Elon and Iahelel.
\v 15 Theſe are the ſonnes of Lia, which she bare \f a \fr 46.15 \ft That is, She bare their fathers in Meſopotamia. \+it S. Aug. q. 151. in Gen\+it*.\f* in Meſopotamia of Syria with Dina his daughter. Al the ſoules of his ſonnes and daughters, are thirtie three.
\v 16 The ſonnes of Gad: Sephion and Haggi and Siuni and Eſebon and Heri and Arodi and Areli.
\v 17 The ſonnes of Aſer: Iamne and Ieſua and Ieſſui and Beria, Sara alſo their ſiſter. The ſonnes of Beria: Heber and Melchiel.
\v 18 theſe be the ſonnes of Zelpha, whom Laban gaue to Lia his daughter. and theſe she bare to Iacob ſixtene ſoules.
\v 19 The ſonnes of Rachel Iacobs wife: Ioſeph and Beniamin.
\v 20 And there were ſonnes borne to Ioſeph, in the Land of Ægypt, which Aſeneth the daughter of Putiphar prieſt of Heliopolis bare to him: Manaſſes and Ephraim.
\v 21 The ſonnes of Beniamin: Bela and Bechor and Aſbel and Gera and Naaman and Echi and Ros and Mophim and Ophim and Ared.
\v 22 theſe be the ſonnes of Rachel, which ſhe bare to Iacob: al the ſoules, fourtene.
\v 23 The ſonnes of Dan: Huſim.
\v 24 The ſonnes of Nepthali: Iaziel and Guni and Ieſer and Sallem.
\v 25 Theſe be the ſonnes of Bala, whom Laban gaue to Rachel his daughter: and theſe she bare to Iacob: al the ſoules, ſeuen.
\v 26 Al the ſoules, that entred with Iacob into Ægypt, and that came out of his thighe, beſides his ſonnes wiues, ſixtie ſix. \ef - \fr 46.26 \fq Sixtie ſix. 27. Seuentie. \ft ¹ The difficultie in theſe two verſes, concerning the number of Iſraelites, that were at firſt in Ægypt with Iacob, is eaſily explicated, that iuſt ſixtie ſix, of his owne iſſue, came in with him; and himſelfe being counted maketh ſixtie ſeuen, adding alſo Ioſeph (who was there before) and his two ſonnes Manaſſes and Ephraim (borne there) they were in al ſeuentie. But a far greater difficultie remaineth: for beſides theſe ſeuentie perſons, the Septuagint Greeke Interpreters number and name fiue more; to wit, a ſonne and a nephew of Manaſſes (the firſt called \+it Machir\+it*, the other \+it Galaad\+it*,) and two ſonnes of Ephraim (called \+it Sutalaam\+it*, and \+it Taam\+it*,) and one nephew (named \+it Edem\+it*) which number of ſeuentie fiue. S. Steuen alſo citeth, ²folowing the vulgar knowen Scripture of the Septuagint, rather then the Hebrew text. Now in what ſenſe, theſe fiue could be ſaid to haue entred into Ægypt with Iacob, being not then borne; may ſome of them not borne during Iacobs life, for Iacob liued but ſeuentene yeares in Ægypt (chap 47. v. 28) and Ioſeph being maried but nine yeares before (for this was the ſecond deare yeare, after the ſeuen plentiful yeares) his ſonnes could not excede ſeuen or eight yeares, when Iacob came to Ægypt; and ſo being but 24. or 25. yeares old at his death, could not then be grandfathers: how therfore theſe fiue named by the Septuagint, and ſome others, not then borne, of the lines of Phares and Beniamin, recited here by Moyſes, could be ſaied to come with Iacob into Ægypt, S. Auguſtin findeth ſo inſoluble, that he doubteth not to affirme, ſome great hidden myſterie to be vnderſtood by the Septnagint Interpreters, in theſe numbers, ³not otherwiſe perhaps explicable according to the letter. \fq ¹ \ft A difficultie how manie Israelites came at first into Ægypt. \fq ² \ft \+it Act. 7.\+it* \fq ³ \ft Numbers mystical, sometimes not explicable in the literal sense.\ef*
\v 27 And the ſonnes of Ioſeph, that were borne to him in the land of Ægypt, two ſoules. Al the ſoules of the houſe of Iacob, that entred into Ægypt, were ſeuentie.
\v 28 And he ſent Iudas before him to Ioſeph, that he ſhould tel him, and he should come into Geſſen to meete him.
\v 29 Whither when he was come, Ioſeph adreſſing his chariot went vp to mete his father, vnto the ſame place: and ſeing him fel vpon his neck, and as they embraced he wept
\v 30 And his father ſaid to Ioſeph: Now wil I die with ioy, becauſe I haue ſeene thy face, and do leaue thee aliue.
\v 31 But he ſpake to his brethren, and to al his fathers houſe: I wil goe vp, and wil tel Pharao, and wil ſay to him: My brethren, and my fathers houſe, that were in the Land of Chanaan, are come to me:
\v 32 and the men are paſtours of sheepe, and their trade is to feede flockes: their cattel, and heardes, and al that they could haue, they haue brought with them.
\v 33 And when he shal cal you, and shal ſay: What is your trade?
\v 34 You shal anſwer; We thy ſernantes are paſtours, from our infancie vntil this preſent, both we and our fathers. And this you shal ſay, that you may dwel in the Land of Geſſen, becauſe he Ægyptians \f b \fr 46.34 \ft Ægyptians honoring ſhepe, goates, and kyne for goddes, deteſted them that did gouerne, kil, or eate thoſe cattel.\f* deteſt al paſtours of sheepe.
\c 47
\cd Iacob with his ſonnes being come into Geſſen, Pharao granteth them the ſame place to dwel in. 13 The famine forceth the Ægyptians to ſel al their goods, landes, and poſſeſſions to the King, 22. except the Prieſts part, to whom the king aloweth neceſſarie foode, without paying for it. 27. After ſeuentene yeares Iacob adiureth Ioſeph, to burie him amongſt his anceſters.
\p
\v 1 IOSEPH therfore going in told Pharao, ſaing: My father & brethren, their sheepe and heardes, & al thinges that they poſſeſſe, are come out of the Land of Chanaan: & behold they ſtay in the Land of Geſſen.
\v 2 The vtmoſt alſo of his brethren fiue perſons he preſented before the king:
\v 3 whom he asked: What trade haue you? They anſwered: We thy ſeruantes are paſtours of sheepe, both we, and our fathers.
\v 4 We are come to ſoiourne in the land, becauſe there is no graſſe for thy ſeruantes flockes, the famine being very ſore in the land of Chanaan: and we deſire thee to command that we thy ſeruantes may be in the Land of Geſſen.
\v 5 And the King therfore ſaid to Ioſeph: Thy father and thy brethren are come to thee.
\v 6 The Land of Ægypt, is in thy ſight: make them to dwel in the beſt place, and deliuer them the Land of Geſſen. And if ſo be thou knowe that there are induſtrious men among them, appoint them maiſters of my cattel.
\v 7 After this Ioſeph brought in his father to the King, and ſet him before him: who bleſſing him,
\v 8 and being asked of him: How manie be the dayes of the yeares of thy life?
\v 9 He anſwered: The dayes of the pilgrimage of my life are an hundred thirtie yeares, \f a \fr 47.9 \ft Euerie mans life is ſhorte, & repleniſhed with manie miſeries. \+it Iob. 14\+it*.\f* few, and euil, and they are not come to the dayes of my fathers, in which they were pilgrimes.
\v 10 And bleſſing the king, he went forth.
\v 11 But Ioſeph gaue poſſeſſion to his father and his brethren in Ægypt, in the beſt place of the land, in Rhameſſes, as Pharao had commanded.
\v 12 And he nourished them, and al his fathers houſe, alowing victuales to euerie one.
\v 13 For in the whole world there wanted bread, and famine oppreſſed the land, eſpecially of Ægypt and Chanaan.
\v 14 Out of which he gethered together al the money for the ſelling of corne, and brought it in vnto the kings treaſure.
\v 15 And when the byers wanted money, al Ægypt came to Ioſeph, ſaying: Geue vs bread: why die we before thee, our money failing?
\v 16 To whom he anſwered: Bring your cattel, and for them I wil geue you victuales, if you haue not to pay.
\v 17 Which when they had brought, he gaue them ſuſtenance for horſes, and sheepe, and oxen, and aſſes: and he ſuſtayned them that yeare for the exchange of the cattel.
\v 18 And they came the ſecond yeare, and ſaid to him: We wil not conceale from our lord, that our money fayling, our cattel withal haue fayled: neither art thou ignorant, that we haue nothing beſides our bodies and land.
\v 19 Why therfore shal we die in thy ſight? both we and our land wil be thyne: bye vs to be the kings bondmen, and geue vs ſede, leſt for default of tillers the land be turned into a wildernes.
\v 20 Ioſeph therfore bought al the Land of Ægypt, euerie man ſelling his poſſeſſions for the greatnes of the famine. And he brought it vnder Pharaos handes,
\v 21 and al the people therof from the fardeſt ends of Ægypt, euen to the vttermoſt coaſts therof,
\v 22 ſauing the land of the Prieſts, which the king had deliuered them: \f b \fr 47.22 \ft The prieſts, of Ægypt being not forced to laboure for their liuing, found out the Mathematiques, as witneſſeth Ariſtotle. \+it in princ. Metaph\+it*.\f* to whom alſo a certaine alowance of victuals was geuen out of the cōmon barnes, and therfore they were not driuen to ſel their poſſeſſions. \ef - \fr 47.22 \fq Sauing the land of the Prieſts. \ft ¹ Let them heare which now liue (ſaith S. Chryſoſtom) ² what great care men had in times paſt of the prieſts of idols: and let them learne at leaſt to yeeld like honour to true prieſts, to whom the miniſterie of al diuine offices is committed. ³ For if the Ægyptians, in their errors, had ſo great care of Idols, thincking them to be more honored, if their miniſters were reſpected, how great condemnation doe they not deſerue, that now diminiſh that, which pertaineth to the prouiſion of prieſts? ⁴ Doe yee not know that the honour pertaineth to God himſelf? Regard not therfore him to whom the honour is exhibited. For it is not for his cauſe to whom thou doeſt it, but for his ſake whoſe prieſt he is, that of him thou maieſt abundantly receiue rewards. VVherfore he ſaid: \+it He that shal doe it to one of theſe, hath done it for me\+it*: & \+it He that receiueth a prophet, in the name of a prophet, shal receiue the revvard of a prophet\+it*. ⁵VVil our Lord reward thee according to the worthines or meannes of his miniſters? According to thine owne alacritie, he either crowneth or condemneth. &c. I ſay not this for the prieſts ſakes, but for yours, deſiring to gaine you in al things. For in lieu of that litle you geue, you ſhal receiue immortal rewards, and vnſpeakeable good. Let vs conſider theſe things, and haſte to ſerue them, not looking vpon the coſt, but vpon the gaine, and increaſe that riſeth therof. &c. For whatſoeuer you beſtow vpon Gods prieſts, he accounteth it as beſtowed on himſelf. And he that ſo beſtoweth, ſhal not only receiue like retrbution, but manifold greater: our merciful God, alwayes of the abundance of his mercie, exceeding the things which are done by vs. Let vs not therfore be worſe then infidels, who for the error of idols gaue ſo much to their ſeruants; for how much error and truth do differ, ſo much difference is there, betwen theirs and Gods Prieſts. Thus much and ſomething more writeth S. Chriſoſtom vpon this place. \fq ¹ \ft The immunitie and care of Priests in the law of nature. \fq ² \ft \+it Ho. 65. in Gen\+it*. \fq ³ \ft Yea amongst Infidels. \fq ⁴ \ft Much more amongst Christians, Priests ought to be respected. \fq ⁵ \ft \+it Math. 25. & 10\+it*.\ef* \ef - \fr 47.22 \fq Prieſts. \ft The Hebrew word ¹ \+it Cohenim\+it* is here vniuerſally tranſlated \+it Prieſts\+it*, in al languages and Editions: which (chap. 40. v. 45.) ſome tranſlate \+it Prince\+it*: and more probably (2. Reg. 8. v. vlt.) where Dauids ſonnes are called \+it Cohenim\+it*: who were in dede Princes, and not properly Prieſts. But in this preſent place it ſignifieth thoſe, to whom Pharao alowed particular prouiſion in the time of dearth, which al vnderſtand of Prieſts, and not of Princes. \fq ¹ \ft \+it Cohen\+it* in some place signifieth Prince, but is here translated \+it Priest\+it*, in al the English Bibles.\ef*
\v 23 Ioſeph therfore ſaid to the people: Behold as you ſee, Pharao poſſeſſeth both you and your land: take ſede, and ſowe the fields,
\v 24 that you may haue corne. The fifth part you shal geue to the king: the other foure I am content you shal haue for ſede, and for foode to your families and your children.
\v 25 Who anſwered: Our life is in thy hand: only let our lord haue a reſpect vnto vs, and we wil gladly ſerue the king.
\v 26 From that time vntil this preſent day in the whole land of Ægypt, the fifth part is paied to the kings, and it became as it were a lawe, ſauing the land of the prieſts, which was free from this condition.
\v 27 Iſrael therfore dwelt in Ægypt, that is, in the Land of Geſſen, and poſſeſſed it: and was increaſed, and multiplied exceedingly.
\v 28 And he liued in it ſeuenteene yeares: and al the dayes of his life came to an hundred fourtie ſeuen yeares.
\v 29 And when he ſawe that the day of his death approched, he called his ſonne Ioſeph, and ſaid to him: If I haue found grace in thy ſight, put thy hand vnder my thigh: and thou ſhalt doe me this mercie and truth, not to bury me in Ægypt:
\v 30 but I wil ſleepe with my fathers, and take me away out of this land, and burie me in the ſepulchre of my anceſters. To whom Ioſeph anſwered: I wil doe that thou haſt commanded. \ef - \fr 47.30 \fq I vvil ſleepe vvith my fathers. \ft ¹ Albeit neither the lack of burial, nor anie crueltie nor contumelie vſed againſt dead bodies, can annoy the iuſt, for \+it thoſe that kil mens bodies, can aftervvards doe them noe more harme\+it*: ²yet it is both a lawful natural deſire, and a ſpiritual comfort and profit, to be buried in ſpecial places, where their owne frends, or holie perſons are buried, or where God is more ſpecialy ſerued, Sacrifice, and other prayers offered. And ſo both Iacob and Ioſeph deſired to reſt in the land of Chanaan, where their parents were buried and where Chriſt ſhould be borne and redeeme mankind. ³ But wordlie pompe and honour of funerals, are rather the comfort of the liuing, then the reliefe of the departed. as S. Auguſtin teacheth, in Pſal. 115. For in the ſight of men, the troupe of ſeruants (ſaith the ſame S. Auguſtin lib. 1. c. 13. de ciuit.) made ſolemne and glorious exequies to the rich glutton, that was cloathed in ſilk, and fared delicately in his life, but in the ſight of God, the Angels miniſterie made far more excellent to poore Lazarus, though they caried not his bodie into a marble tombe, but his ſoule into Abrahams boſome. \fq ¹ \ft Special place of burial lawfully desired, and spiritually profitable. \fq ² \ft \+it Luc. 12.\+it* \fq ³ \ft But pompe auaileth not the dead.\ef*
\v 31 And he ſaid: Sweate then to me. Who ſwearing, Iſrael adored God, turning to the beds head. \ef - \fr 47.31 \fq To the beds head. \ft S. Paul alleaging this place ſaith: \+it Iacob adored the top of\+it* (Ioſeph) \+it his rod\+it*, ¹folowing the ² Septuagint, who for the ſame Hebrew word (being without points, that is, without vowels) in this place ſay, \+it rod\+it*, and in the next chapter (v. 2.) interpret \+it bed\+it*. For \+it Matteh\+it* ſignifieth \+it a rod\+it*, and \+it Mittah, a bed\+it*. The Latin therfore tranſlating \+it bed\+it*, as the Hebrew is pointed, and the Septuagint, and S. Paule reading \+it rod\+it*, both are true, and both together expreſſe the whole action, that Iacob taking Ioſephs \+it rod\+it* into his hand, and turning to \+it the beds head\+it*, leaned on the top of the rod, and adored not only God, the Lord and geuer of al good, but alſo his ſonne Ioſeph now the chiefe ruler and Prince of Ægypt. as S. Auguſtin expoundeth. q 162. in Gen And herein ſaith S. Chriſoſtom (ho. 66) Ioſephs dreame was fulfilled, that the ſunne and moone should adore him. ³ The like ſaith Theodoret (q. 108. in Gen) And Procopius addeth that Iacob adoring Ioſephs rod, adored alſo Chriſts kingdome, prefigured by the ſame rod. But how adoration of creatures redoundeth to the honour of God, more is noted vpon the ſaid place of S. Paul. Heb. 11. \fq ¹ \ft \+it Heb. 11\+it*. \fq ² \ft The Septuagint are not contrarie to the Hebrew and Latin text, but supplie that was omitted. \fq ³ \ft Adoration of God and creatures is not repugnant.\ef*
\c 48
\cd Ioſeph viſiteth his father being ſick. 5. Who adopteth his two ſonnes Manaſſes and Ephraim, 13. and bleſſeth them, preferring the younger before the elder, contrarie to Ioſephs mind. 22. And geueth a portion of land to Ioſeph, aboue his brethren.
\p
\v 1 THESE things being ſo done, it was told Ioſeph that his father was ſicke: who, taking his two ſonnes Manaſſes and Ephraim, went forward.
\v 2 And it was told the old man: Behold thy ſonne Ioſeph cometh to thee. Who being ſtrengthned ſate on his bed.
\v 3 And Ioſeph being entred in to him, he ſaid: God almightie appeared to me in Luza, which is in the Land of Chanaan: and He bleſſed me,
\v 4 and ſayd: I wil increaſe, and multiplie thee, and make thee into multitudes of peoples: and I wil geue thee this land, and to thy ſede after thee for an euerlaſting poſſeſſion.
\v 5 Thy two ſonnes therfore, which were borne to thee in the Land of Ægypt before I came hither to thee, ſhalbe myne: Ephraim and Manaſſes, as Ruben and Simeon ſhalbe reputed to me.
\v 6 But the reſt begotten of thee after them, shalbe thyne, and shal be called by the name of their brethren in their poſſeſſions.
\v 7 For vnto me, when I came out of Meſopotamia, Rachel dyed in the land of Chanaan in the very iourney, and it was ſpring time: and I entered into Ephrata, and buried her by the way ſide to Ephrata, which by an other name is called Bethleem. \x - \xt Mat. 2.\x*
\v 8 And ſeeing his ſonnes he ſaid to him: Who are theiſe?
\v 9 He anſweted: They are my ſonnes, whom God hath geuen me in this place. Bring them, quoth he, to me, that I may bleſſe them.
\v 10 For Iſraels eyes were dymme by reaſon of very great age, and he could not ſee clearly. And when they were ſet beſide him, kiſſing and embracing them,
\v 11 he ſaid to his ſonne: I am not defrauded of thy ſight: moreouer God hath shewed me thy ſede.
\v 12 And when Ioſeph had taken them from his fathers lappe, he adored proſtrate vnto the ground.
\v 13 And he ſet Ephraim on his right hand, that is, on the left hand of Iſrael: but Manaſſes on his owne left hand, to wit, on his fathers right hand, and put them nere to him.
\v 14 Who ſtretching forth his right hand, put it vpon the head of Ephraim the younger brother: \f a \fr 48.14 \ft By this he made a croſſe, prefiguring the Croſſe of Chriſt. Iſidor in hunc locum.\f* and the left vpon the head of Manaſſes, that was the elder, changing handes. \ef - \fr 48.14 \fq Streatching forth his right hand. \ft ¹ ² As nature hath made the right hand readier to moue, ſtronger to worke and reſiſt, and apter to frame and faſhion anie thing, ſo generally we vſe it more then the left. And when we vſe both handes at once, we ordinarily applie the right hand to the greater, and more excellent effect, both in ſpiritual and corporal things. As in confirmation of fidelitie or freindſhip, in bleſſing, writing, fieghting, playing, and in moſt others things, we vſe the right hand, either only or chiefly. So the Patriarch Iacob laide his right hand vpon Ephraim, knowing by prophetical ſpirite, ³ that he ſhould be preferred, before his elder brother Manaſſes. Literally fulfilled in Ioſue, Ieroboam, and other chief Princes of Ephraims iſſue. And myſtically in the Gentils, being later called of God, and yet preferred before the Iewes. \+it S. Cypri li. 1. c. 21. aduer. Iudeos: S. Amb. li. de Benedict. Patriarch. c. 1. S. Aug. li. 16 c. 42. de ciuit. &c\+it*. \fq ¹ \ft The right hand also in spiritual things, preferred before the left. \fq ² \ft \+it Arist li. 2. de calo. textu. 8. & de inces. animal. ca. 4\+it*. \fq ³ \ft The younger brother preferred signified the Gentiles before the Iewes.\ef* \ef - \fr 48.14 \fq Changing handes. \ft The myſterie, of the Gentils excelling the Iewes in time of grace, often prefigured by preferring the younger brother before the elder (Abel before Cain; Abraham before Nachor; Iſaac before Iſmael; Iacob himſelf before Eſau; and now Ephraim before Manaſſes) is here further repreſented by Iacobs forming of a croſſe, ¹ ²with his armes laied one ouer the other, when he bleſſed his two nephewes: who otherwiſe might haue laied his right hand firſt vpon one, and then vpon the other; or haue cauſed them to change places; but he wittingly croſſed his armes, and \+it changed his handes\+it*; or according to the Hebrew, \+it made his handes vnderſtand\+it*, that is, by his handes made it to be vnderſtood, not only that the younger ſhould be in place of the elder, Ephraim before Manaſſes, and much more the Gentiles before the Iewes, but alſo that this greater Myſterie ſhould be effected by Chriſt dying on a Croſſe. For what els could the verie croſſing of his armes, ſo wittingly and purpoſly done, ſignifie, but the forme and figure of Chriſts Croſſe? As els where the wood, which young Iſaac caried on his back vnto the mountaine prefigured the matter or ſubſtance of the ſame Croſſe Al accomplished when Chriſt was crucified: wherby the Iewes were ſcandalized, and the Gentiles called and ſaued. Our Sauiour himſelfe fortelling, that \+it he being exalted\+it* (to wit vpon the Croſſe) \+it vvould dravv al vnto himſelf\+it*. ³And S. Paul teaching that Chriſt \+it faſtned the hand vvriting, that vvas againſt vs; vpon the Croſſe\+it*. ⁴ \fq ¹ \ft The forme of the Crosse prefigured by Iacob crossing his armes. \fq ² \ft \+it Procop. Isidor. in Gen\+it*.* \fq ³ \ft \+it Joan. 12.\+it* \fq ⁴ \ft \+it Collos. 2.\+it*\ef*
\v 15 And Iacob bleſſed the ſonnes of Ioſeph, and ſaid: God, in whoſe ſight my fathers Abraham and Iſaac haue walked, God that feedeth me from my youth vntil this preſent day:
\v 16 The Angel that deliuereth me from al euils, bleſſe theſe children: and be my name called vpon them, the names alſo of my fathers Abraham, and Iſaac, and grow they into a multitude vpon the earth. \ef - \fr 48.16 \fq The Angel that delinereth me. \ft It is euident by this plaine text, that Iacob was deliuered from euiles by an Angel, ¹and that he inuocated the ſame Angel to bleſſe his nephewes, S. Baſil \+it (li. 3. cont. Eunom. in initio\+it*) sheweth by this place amongſt others, that an Angel is preſent with euerie one, \+it as a pedagogue, and paſtour, directing his life\+it*. S. Chryſoſtom alſo \+it (ho. 7. in laud. S. Pauli\+it*) citeth this place in teſtimonie, that proper Angels are deputed to protect men. Yet Proteſtants ſay, ²that \+it this Angel muſt be vnderſtood of Chriſt\+it*: remitting their gloſſe to the. 31. ch v. 13. and 32. v. 1 of Geneſis, where it can not be proued. ³ But the ancient Fathers teach the patronage & Inuocation of Angels grounded in holie Scripture. Namely in this place, and manie other places in the old Teſtament. Alſo Mat. 18. Act. 12. 1. Cor. 11. & the like. For example, S. Iuſtinus Martyr in explic. qq. neceſſ. q. 30. affirming it for a knowen truth, declareth that thoſe Angels, which receiue the charge of guarding men, continew the ſame office either to both ſoule and bodie, or to the ſoule after it is parted from the bodie, S. Cyril of Alexandria (lib. 4. cont. Iulian. prope init.) shewing how God vſeth the miniſterie of holie Angels, for mens ſaluation, ſaith: \+it Hi noxias a nobis abigunt feras\+it* &c. Theſe (Angels) driue away noyſome wildbeaſts from vs: and reskew thoſe that are caught, from their crueltie, and teach what ſoeuer is laudable, to make our paſſage free, and not peſtered, when with vs they glorifie one ſoueraigne God. S. Chryſoſtom (ho. 60, in Math. 18) S. Hierome vpon the ſame place, S. Ambroſe, in Pſal. 38. S Auguſtin li. 83. qq. q. 79. & li. Soliloq. c. 27. S. Gregorie li. 4. c. 31. in 3 Iob S. Bernard ſer. 5. Dedicat. Eccles. & ſer. 12. in Pſal. 90. & others, ſo vniuerſally teach the ſame, that Caluin (li. Inſtit. c. 14. ſect. 38.) dare not denie it, and yet wil nedes doubt of it. \fq ¹ \ft Protection & Inuocation of Angels. \fq ² \ft \+it Bible 1603\+it*. \fq ³ \ft Proued by ancient Fathers.\ef* \ef - \fr 48.16 \fq be my name called vpon them. \ft This place hath two good literal ſenſes. For firſt it importeth, that Ephraim and Manaſſes were made participant among the Tribes, of the bleſſings of Abraham, Iſaac, and Iacob. Secondly, that God would bleſſe them, for Abraham, Iſaac, and Iacobs ſake: ¹ſo Moyſes praying for the whole people (Exodi. 32.) beſought God to remember, Abraham, Iſaac, and Iſrael, and God was therwith pacified. \fq ¹ \ft God for his Saints sake sheweth fauour to their frends.\ef*
\v 17 And Ioſeph ſeing that his father had put his right hand vpon the head of Ephraim, tooke it heauily: and taking his fathers hand he went about to lift it from Ephraims head, and to remoue it vpon the head of Manaſſes.
\v 18 And he ſaid to his father; It is not conuenient father ſo to be: becauſe this is the firſt begotten put thy right hand vpon his head.
\v 19 Who refuſing, ſaid: I know my ſonne, I know: and this ſame in dede shal be into peoples, and shal be multiplied: but his younger brother ſhal be greater then he: and his ſede shal growe into nations.
\v 20 And he bleſſed them at that time, ſaying: In thee shal Iſrael be bleſſed, and it shalbe ſaid: God do vnto thee as vnto Ephraim, and as vnto Manaſſes. And he ſette Ephraim before Manaſſes.
\v 21 And he ſaid to Ioſeph his ſonne: Behold I dye, and God wil be with you, and wil bring you backe into the land of your fathers.
\v 22 I doe geue thee one portion aboue thy brethren, which I tooke out of the hand of the Amorrhean with my ſword and bowe.
\c 49
\cd Iacob replenished with the ſpirite of prophecie, a litle before his death, fortelleth his ſonnes manie things, that shal happen to their poſteritie. Chargeth ſome of them with faultes past, bleſſeth euerie one. 29. Appointeth where to burie him. 32. and dyeth.
\p
\v 1 AND Iacob called his ſonnes, and ſaid to them: Come together, that I \f a \fr 49.1 \ft Theſe are predictions not al bleſſings. \+it S. Amb. li. de Benedict Patriar\+it*.\f* may shew you the things that shal come to you in the laſt dayes.
\v 2 Come together, and heare you ſonnes of Iacob, heare ye Iſrael your father:
\v 3 Ruben my firſt begotten, thou art my ſtrength, and the begining of my ſorow: former in giftes, greater in empyre.
\v 4 Thou art poured out as water, \f b \fr 49.4 \ft A prophecie not an imprecation. S. Aug. li. 16. c. 12. cont. Fauſt.\f* encreaſe thou not: becauſe thou diddeſt aſcend thy fathers bed, and diddeſt defile his coutch. \ef - \fr 49.4 \fq Be cauſe thou dideſt aſcend thy fathers bed. \ft ¹ For this crime of inceſt Ruben was depriued of his firſt-birth-right. VVho being by order of birth \+it former in guiftes\+it*, wherby he ſhould haue had duble portion; \+it and greater in Empire\+it*, wherby he ſhould haue bene Prince or Lord ouer his bretheren, the former prerogatiue was geuen to Ioſeph, whoſe two ſonnes wete heades of two Tribes, the other was geuen to the Tribe of Iuda, in Dauid and his poſteritie. He was alſo depriued of his prerogatiue in Prieſthood, which was after annexed to the Tribe of Leui, wherupon the Chaldee paraphraſis ſpeaketh thus to Ruben: It belonged to thee to haue receaued three better lettes then thy brethren, Prieſthood, Beſt portion, and the Kingdome: But becauſe thou haſt ſinned, the duble portion is geuen to Ioſeph, the Kingdome to Iudas, and Prieſthood to Leui. \fq ¹ \ft Ruben for his sinne was put from the prerogatiues of the first borne.\ef*
\v 5 Simeon and Leui brethren: veſſels of iniquitie warring. \ef - \fr 49.5 \fq Veſſelles of iniquitie. \ft ¹ ² Albeit Simeon and Leui were moued with iuſt zeale to puniſh the foule crime committed by Sichem, againſt their ſiſter and whole familie: yet in their maner of reuenging were manie ſinnes worthely condemned by Iacob, both immediatly after the fact, and here at his death. For before the ſlaughter they committed there greate ſinnes; in that they raſhly did it vnknowne to their fathertherby putting him & themſelues in extreme danger, if God had not maruelouſly protected them; in falſly pretending agreement and league with the Sichamites, which they ment not to performe; and in ſacrilegiouſly abuſing the Sacrament of Circumciſion, making it a cloke to deceiue their ennemies. In the fact alſo they committed other foure groſſe crimes: cruelly killing thoſe, that offered other abundant ſatisfaction; murthering others that were altogether innocent.; ſacking and deſtroying the citie, and carying away wemen and children captiue. Myſtically S. Ambroſe, Ruffinus, Iſidorus, and others vnderſtand this prophecie, of the Scribes and the Prieſtes, ³deſcending of Simeon and Leui, who were moſt eagre againſt our Sauiour, as himſelf more plainely fortold, ſaing: \+it The ſonne of man shalbe betrayed to the chiefe Prieſtes, and to the Scribes, and they shal condemne him to death\+it*. ⁴ Their furie was curſed aboue al furie, \+it Becauſe\+it* (as Iacob here ſaith) \+it it vvas ſtubborne\+it*, or obſtinate, for they did not only condemne Chriſt to death in their wicked councel, ⁵but alſo vrged and preſſed Pilat, endeuoringe to ſaue him, & ſturred vp the people to crye: \+it Take him avvay, Crucifie him\+it*. ⁶Yea their \+it indignation\+it* was ſo \+it hard\+it*, that they preferred Barabbas before Chriſt. \fq ¹ \ft The zeale of Simeon & Leui was commendable: but their maner of reuenge, was manie wayes faultie. \fq ² \ft \+it Iudith. 9\+it*. \fq ³ \ft The Priests & Scribes furie, obstinacie, & hard harts against Christ. \fq ⁴ \ft \+it Mat. 20\+it*. \fq ⁵ \ft \+it Ioan. 11.\+it* \fq ⁶ \ft \+it Ioan. 18.\+it*\ef*
\v 6 Into their counſel come not my ſoule, and in their congregation be not my glorie: becauſe in their furie they ſlew a man, and in their wilfulnes they vndermined a wall.
\v 7 Curſed be their furie, becauſe it is ſtubborne: and their indignation, becauſe it is hard: I wil diuide them in Iacob, and wil diſperſe them in Iſrael.
\v 8 Iudas, thee thy brethren shal praiſe: thy hand shalbe in the neck of thyne enemies: thy fathers children shal adore thee.
\v 9 A lions whelp Iudas: to the pray my ſonne thou didſt aſcend: taking thy reſt thou didſt lye as a lion, and as it were a lyoneſſe, who shal raiſe him vp?
\v 10 \f 1 \fr 49.10 \ft That theſe are moſt profound Myſteries, iſ eaſie to conceiue, but moſt hard to vnderſtand them. In ſome the Patriarch recounteth things paſt in his life, fortelling the effects therof to come. Other things he forsheweth pertayning to the diuiſion of the Land of Chanaan, others to the times of the Iudges, of the Kings, of the Captiuitie, of Deliuerie from thence, of Chriſt, of Antichriſt, and of the end of this world. Of al which diuers ancient fathers haue written large commentaries, & godlie treatiſes.\f* \sc The ſcepter shal not BE TAKEN away from Iudas, and a duke out of his thigh, til he doe come that is to be ſent, and the ſame shal be the expectation of the gentiles\sc*. \ef - \fr 49.10 \fq The ſcepter shal not be taken avvay. \ft Here the Patriarch Iacob fortelleth the time, when the promiſed Meſſias should come into the world, by this ſigne that the ſcepter ſhould not be taken from Iuda, til the ſame Redeemer of mankind were at hand. ¹Not that the regal ſcepter should remaine in the Tribe of Iuda, from Iacobs death til Chriſtes comming: for that Kingdome beganne firſt in Dauid, aboue ſix hundred yeares after Iacobs death, and after the captiuitie of Babylon the highe Prieſts of the Tribe of Leui did gouerne alſo the ſtate, & not only the Church, other ſix hundred yeares. But the ſenſe is, that of the Tribe of Iuda ſhould riſe moſt gloriouſe Kinges, whoſe crowne and Kingdome ſhould remaine with the Iewish Nation, vntil their expected Meſſias should drawe nere, and then be taken from them by the Gentiles. VVhich was downe by Herod, whoſe father was an Idumean, his mother an Arabique. Thus the Ancient fathers with one accorde vnderſtood this prophecie. S. Iuſtinus Martyr. \+it Ser. cum Triphone\+it*. Euſebius Ceſarien. lib. Hiſt. Eccles. cap 6. S. Athanaſius. \+it lib. de Incarnat\+it*. S. Ambroſe \+it lib. de Benedict. Patriarch c. 4\+it*. S. Chryſoſtom. \+it Ho. 67. in Gen\+it*. S. Auguſtin lib. 18. c. 45. de ciuit. Theodoretus. q. vlt. in Gen. \fq ¹ \ft Taking away of the regal scepter from the Iewes a signe of Christs coming.\ef*
\v 11 Tying to the vineyard his colt, and to the vine, o my ſonne, his the aſſe. He shal wash his ſtole in wine, and in the bloud of the grape his cloke. \ef - \fr 49.11 \fq He shal vvash his ſtole in vvyne. \ft ¹ By wine, and bloud of the grape, what other thing is ſhewed (ſaith S. Cyprian Epiſt ad Cœcil. 63) ² but the wine of the Chalice of our Lordes bloud? Likewiſe Tertullian (lib 4. contra Marcionem) expoundeth the ſtole to ſignifie Chriſts flesh, and the wine his bloud. In al which booke his drift is to shewe that Chriſt did not deſtroy the olde Teſtament, but fulfilled the figures and prophecies therof. And not that Chriſt gaue his bodie in figure only, as our aduerſaries alleadge him. \fq ¹ \ft Our Lords real presence in the B. Sacrament prophecied. \fq ² \ft \+it li. 2. Ep. 3\+it*.\ef*
\v 12 His eyes are more beautiful then wine, and his teeth whiter then milke.
\v 13 Zabulon shal dwel in the shore of the ſea, and in the road of shippes reaching as farre as Sidon.
\v 14 Iſſachar a ſtrong aſſe lying at reſt betwen the borders.
\v 15 He ſaw reſt that it was good: and the earth that it was very good: and he put vnder his shoulder to cary, and became ſeruing vnder tributes.
\v 16 Dan shal iudge his owne people as alſo an other tribe in Iſrael.
\v 17 Be Dan a ſnake in the way, a ſerpent in the path, byting the hooſſes of the horſe, that his ryder may fal backward. \ef - \fr 49.17 \fq Be Dan a ſnake in the vvay. \ft ¹ This prophecie moſt ancient Fathers vnderſtood of AntiChriſt. namely S. Irenæus, \+it lib. 5. aduerſ. Hæres\+it*: S. Hyppolitus Martyr \+it Orat. de conſummat. ſæculi\+it*. S. Ambroſe. \+it c. 7 de Benedict. Patriarch\+it*. S. Auguſtin. q. 12. \+it in Ioſue\+it*. Proſper, \+it lib. de promiſs. & prædict. Dei. P. 4\+it*. Theodoret. q. \+it vlt. in Gen\+it*. S. Gregorie, \+it lib. 30. Moral. c. 18\+it*, and many others vpon the 7. chap. of the Apocalips. where they ſuppoſe S. Ioan did omitt Dan from amongſt the Elect of the Iſraelitical Tribes, in deteſtation of Antichriſt, to be borne of that Tribe. And certayne it is, that the Iewes wil receiue, and folowe him for their Meſſias. ²as our Sauiour himſelfe ſaith; VVhich maketh it very probable, that he shal be a Iewe borne, elſe they would not ſo eaſily admitt him. \fq ¹ \ft Ancient Fathers suppose that Antichrist shalbe of the tribe of Dan. \fq ² \ft \+it Ioan. 5.\+it*\ef*
\v 18 I wil expect \sc THY SALVATION\sc* o Lord.
\v 19 Gad, the gyrded shal fight before him: and himſelf shal be gyrded backward.
\v 20 Aſer, his bread is fat, and he shal geue daynties to kinges.
\v 21 Nepthali, a hart let forth, & geuing ſpeeches of beautie.
\v 22 Ioſeph a child encreaſing, encreaſing and comelie to behold: the daughters courſed to and for vpon the wall. \ef - \fr 49.22 \fq Ioſeph a childe encreaſing. \ft ¹ Ioſeph was in manie reſpectes a figure of Chriſt, eſpecially in that he was loued of his father before al his bretheren, ſolde by his brethrn to the Gentiles of enuie, and for money, aduanced to dignitie and authoritie, the deliuerer of Ægypt from famine, and called Sauiour of the world; al performed in Chriſt, the true Childe encreaſing. \fq ¹ \ft Ioseph in manie things prefigured Christ.\ef*
\v 23 But the dart men did exaſperate him, & brawled, and enuied him.
\v 24 His bowe ſate vpon the ſtrong, & the bands of his armes and his handes were diſſolued, by the handes of the mightie of Iacob: thence came forth a paſtour, the ſtone of Iſrael.
\v 25 The God of thy father shal be thy helper, & the Almightie shal bleſſe thee with the bleſſinges of heauen from aboue, with the bleſſinges of the deapth that lieth beneath, with the bleſſings of the pappes and of the wombe.
\v 26 The bleſſinges of thy father were ſtrengthned with the bleſſings of his fathers: vntil the deſire of the eternal hilles came: be they vpon the head of Ioſeph, & vpon the crowne of the Nazarite among his brethren.
\v 27 Beniamin \f c \fr 49.27 \ft This prophecie S. Auguſtin vnderſtandeth of S. Paul, of the tribe of Beniamin; who was firſt a perſecutor, and after an Apoſtle of Chriſt. \+it ſer. 14. de ſanctis\+it*.\f* a rauening woolfe, in the morning shal eate the pray, and in the euening shal diuide the ſpoile.
\v 28 Al theſe in the tribes of Iſrael twelue: theſe things ſpake their father to them, and he bleſſed euerie one, with their proper bleſſings
\v 29 And he commanded them, ſaying: I am geathered vnto my people: burie you me with my fathers in the duble caue, which is in the field of Ephron the Hethite,
\v 30 againſt Mambre in the Land of Chanaan, which Abraham bought with the field of Ephron the Hethite for a poſſeſſion to burie in.
\v 31 There they buried him, & Sara his wife: there was Iſaac buried with Rebecca his wife: there alſo Lia doth lie buried.
\v 32 And when he had finished the precepts wherwith he inſtructed his ſonnes, he plucked vp his feet vpon the bed, and died: & he was put vnto his people.
\c 50
\cd Ioſeph cauſeth his fathers bodie to be embawmed; 3. the dayes of mourning being expired, 6. with Pharaos leaue, Ioſeph with the ancients of Ægypt, al his brethren, and elder ſorte of Iſraelites goe, and ſolemnly burie the bodie in Chanaan. 14. After their returne, his brethren fearing, leſt Ioſeph wil now reuenge former iniuries, he freely forgeueth al. 22. At the age of 110. yeares, adiuring the poſteritie to carie his bones into Chanaan he dieth, and is put in a coffin.
\p
\v 1 WHICH Ioſeph ſeeing, fel vpon his fathers face weeping and kiſſing him.
\v 2 And he commanded his ſeruantes the phyſitians, that they should embawme his father with ſpices.
\v 3 Who fufilling his commandements, there paſſed fourtie dayes: for this was the maner of corſes embawmed, and Ægypt mourned him ſeuentie daies.
\v 4 And the mourning-time being expired, Ioſeph ſpake to the familie of Pharao: If I haue found grace in your ſight, ſpeake in the eares of Pharao:
\v 5 for ſo much as my father did adiure me, ſaing: Behold I die, in my ſepulchre \f a \fr 50.5 \ft Iacob digged a ſepulcher for himſelfe, though it be not heretofore mentioned when he did it. \+it S. Aug. q. 170. in Gen\+it*.\f* which I digged for my ſelfe in the land of Chanaan, thou shalt burie me. I wil goe vp therfore, and burie my father, and returne.
\v 6 And Pharao ſaid to him: Goe vp and burie thy father as thou waſt adiured.
\v 7 Who going vp, there went with him al the ancients of Pharaos houſe, and al the elders of the Land of Ægypt:
\v 8 the houſe of Ioſeph with his brethren, ſauing their little ones, and the flockes and heards, which they had left in the Land of Geſſen.
\v 9 He had alſo in his traine chariotts and horſemen: and it became no ſmal multitude.
\v 10 And they came to the floore of Atad, which is ſituate beyond Iordaine: where celebrating the exequies with great and vehement mourning, they ſpent ful ſeuen dayes.
\v 11 Which when the Inhabiters of the Land of Chanaan had ſeene, they ſaid: This is a great mourning vnto the Ægyptians. And therfore the name of that place was called, The mourning of Ægypt.
\v 12 Therfore the ſonnes of Iacob did as he commanded them:
\v 13 and carying him into the Land of Chanaan, they buried him in the duble caue, which Abraham had bought with the field for a poſſeſſion to burie in of Ephron the Hethite againſt Mambre.
\v 14 And Ioſeph returned into Ægypt with his brethren, and with al the traine, his father being buried.
\v 15 After whoſe death, his brethren fearing, and talking one with an other: Leſt perhaps he be mindful of the iniurie which he ſuffered, and requite vs al the euil that we haue done,
\v 16 they aduertiſed him ſaing: Thy father commanded vs before he died,
\v 17 that we ſhould ſay thus much to thee in his wordes: I beſech that thou forget the wicked fact of thy brethren, and the ſinne & malice which they haue exerciſed againſt thee: we alſo deſire thee, that to the ſeruants of the God of thy father thou remit this iniquitie. Whom when Ioſeph had heard he wept.
\v 18 And his brethren came to him: and \f b \fr 50.18 \ft This word \+it adoring\+it* often in holie Scripture ſignifieth ciuil honour: as here it can haue no other ſenſe.\f* adoring proſtrate on the ground they ſaid: We are thy ſeruantes.
\v 19 To whom he anſwered: Feare not: can we reſiſt the wil of God?
\v 20 You thought euil againſt me: but God turned that into good, that he might exalt me, as preſently you ſee, and might ſaue many peoples. \ef - \fr 50.20 \fq You thought euil. \ft ¹ This plaine diſtinction sheweth, that ſinne is wholly of the ſinner; and that God hath no part therin, but turneth it to good. For thoſe things which Ioſephs brethren did againſt him, were occaſions of his aduancement in Ægypt, through the omnipotent wiſdome of God. VVhoſe propertie is, out of euerie euil to draw good. S. Chriſoſt. ho. 67. in Gen. S. Aug. \+it Enchirid. c. 11. & li. 14. c. 27. de ciuit\+it*. \fq ¹ \ft Mans wil, not God the cause of sinne.\ef*
\v 21 Feare not: I wil feed you & your litle ones, and he comforted them, and ſpake gently & mildly.
\v 22 And he dwelt in Ægypt, with al his fathers houſe: and liued an hundred and tenne yeares. And he ſawe the children of Ephraim vnto the third generation. Alſo the children of Machir the ſonne of Manaſſes were borne in Ioſephs knees
\v 23 Which thinges being done, he ſpake to his brethren: After my death God wil viſite you, and wil make you goe vp out of this land, to the land which he ſware to Abraham, Iſaac, and Iacob.
\v 24 And when he had adiured them and ſaid: God wil viſite you, carie my bones with you out of this place: \ef - \fr 50.24 \fq Carie my bones with you. \ft For the ſame reaſons Ioſeph would be finally buried in Chanaan, for which Iacob deſired to be there buried (chap. 47.) but Ioſeph would not preſently be caried thither, ¹leſt it might haue geuen offence to the Ægyptians; or at leaſt haue diminished their fauoure towardes his brethren: and withal he would confirme his brethren in their hope of returning, ſeing he was content, that his bodie should expect in Ægypt, til the whole Nation ſhould returne into Chanaan. \fq ¹ \ft Ioseph, for his brethrés sake, differred his desired burial in Chanaan.\ef*
\v 25 he died, being an hundred and tenne yeares old. And being embawmed with ſpices, was put in a coffin in Ægypt.
